const DATA=[{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"I","loc":"London. The Parliament-house.","id":0},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum. Enter DUKE of YORK, EDWARD, RICHARD, NORFOLK, MONTAGUE, WARWICK, and Soldiers.","id":1},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["I wonder how the king escap'd our hands."],"id":2,"m":"I wonder how the king slipped through our fingers."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["While we pursued the horsemen of the North,","He slyly stole away and left his men,","Whereat the great Lord of Northumberland,","Whose warlike ears could never brook retreat,","Cheer'd up the drooping army; and himself,","Lord Clifford, and Lord Stafford, all abreast,","Charg'd our main battle's front, and breaking in,","Were by the swords of common soldiers slain."],"id":3,"m":"While we were chasing the northern cavalry, he quietly crept away and abandoned his men. Then the great Lord Northumberland, whose fighting spirit could never stand a retreat, rallied the sagging army; and he himself, Lord Clifford and Lord Stafford charged side by side into the front of our main force, broke into it, and were killed by ordinary soldiers' swords."},{"t":"sp","who":"EDWARD","lines":["Lord Stafford's father, Duke of Buckingham,","Is either slain or wounded dangerously;","I cleft his beaver with a downright blow.","That this is true, father, behold his blood."],"id":4,"m":"Lord Stafford's father, the Duke of Buckingham, is either dead or badly wounded. I split his visor with a straight downward blow. Here's his blood on my sword, father, to prove it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Showing his bloody sword.","id":5},{"t":"sp","who":"MONTAGUE","lines":["And, brother, here's the Earl of Wiltshire's blood,"],"id":6,"m":"And here, brother, is the Earl of Wiltshire's blood,"},{"t":"sd","text":"To York, showing his.","id":7},{"t":"sp","who":"MONTAGUE","cont":1,"lines":["Whom I encounter'd as the battles join'd."],"id":8,"m":"whom I fought when the armies met."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Speak thou for me, and tell them what I did."],"id":9,"m":"Let this speak for me and tell them what I did."},{"t":"sd","text":"Throwing down the Duke of Somerset's head.","id":10},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Richard hath best deserv'd of all my sons.—","But is your grace dead, my Lord of Somerset?"],"id":11,"m":"Richard has earned the most praise of all my sons. But are you really dead, my Lord of Somerset?"},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["Such hope have all the line of John of Gaunt!"],"id":12,"m":"May all of John of Gaunt's descendants end the same way!"},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Thus do I hope to shake King Henry's head."],"id":13,"m":"That's how I hope to shake King Henry's head off."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["And so do I.—Victorious Prince of York,","Before I see thee seated in that throne","Which now the house of Lancaster usurps,","I vow by heaven these eyes shall never close.","This is the palace of the fearful king,","And this the regal seat; possess it, York,","For this is thine, and not King Henry's heirs'."],"id":14,"m":"So do I. Victorious Prince of York, I swear by heaven I won't close my eyes until I see you sitting on the throne that the house of Lancaster has stolen. This is the timid king's palace and this is the royal seat. Take it, York; it belongs to you, not to King Henry's heirs."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Assist me, then, sweet Warwick, and I will;","For hither we have broken in by force."],"id":15,"m":"Help me, then, dear Warwick, and I will, since we've broken in here by force."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["We'll all assist you; he that flies shall die."],"id":16,"m":"We'll all help you. Anyone who runs away will die."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Thanks, gentle Norfolk.—Stay by me, my lords;—","And, soldiers, stay and lodge by me this night."],"id":17,"m":"Thank you, good Norfolk. Stay by me, my lords; and soldiers, stay and camp here with me tonight."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["And when the king comes, offer him no violence,","Unless he seek to thrust you out perforce."],"id":18,"m":"And when the king comes, don't use violence on him unless he tries to throw you out by force."},{"t":"sd","text":"They retire.","id":19},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["The queen this day here holds her parliament,","But little thinks we shall be of her council.","By words or blows here let us win our right."],"id":20,"m":"The queen is holding her parliament here today, but she has no idea we'll be sitting in on it. With words or with blows, let's win our rights here."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Arm'd as we are, let's stay within this house."],"id":21,"m":"Let's stay in this building armed as we are."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["The bloody parliament shall this be call'd,","Unless Plantagenet, Duke of York, be king,","And bashful Henry depos'd, whose cowardice","Hath made us bywords to our enemies."],"id":22,"m":"This will be called the bloody parliament unless Plantagenet, Duke of York, becomes king and timid Henry is deposed; his cowardice has made us a joke among our enemies."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Then leave me not, my lords; be resolute.","I mean to take possession of my right."],"id":23,"m":"Then don't desert me, my lords; stand firm. I mean to take what's mine."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Neither the king, nor he that loves him best,","The proudest he that holds up Lancaster,","Dares stir a wing if Warwick shake his bells.","I'll plant Plantagenet, root him up who dares.—","Resolve thee, Richard; claim the English crown."],"id":24,"m":"Neither the king, nor his most loyal friend, nor the proudest man who supports Lancaster dares flap a wing while Warwick shakes his falcon bells. I'll plant Plantagenet here; let anyone who dares try to pull him up. Make up your mind, Richard: claim the English crown."},{"t":"sd","text":"Warwick leads York to the throne, who seats himself.","id":25},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. Enter KING HENRY, CLIFFORD, NORTHUMBERLAND, WESTMORELAND, EXETER, and the rest.","id":26},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["My lords, look where the sturdy rebel sits,","Even in the chair of state! belike he means,","Back'd by the power of Warwick, that false peer,","To aspire unto the crown and reign as king.—","Earl of Northumberland, he slew thy father;","And thine, Lord Clifford; and you both have vow'd revenge","On him, his sons, his favourites, and his friends."],"id":27,"m":"My lords, look where the bold rebel sits, right on the throne! It seems he means, with the backing of that traitor Warwick, to grab the crown and rule as king. Earl of Northumberland, he killed your father, and yours, Lord Clifford; you have both sworn revenge on him, his sons, his favourites and his friends."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["If I be not, heavens be reveng'd on me!"],"id":28,"m":"If I don't take it, let heaven take revenge on me!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["The hope thereof makes Clifford mourn in steel."],"id":29,"m":"The hope of revenge is why Clifford mourns in armour instead of black."},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["What! shall we suffer this? let's pluck him down;","My heart for anger burns; I cannot brook it."],"id":30,"m":"What! Are we going to put up with this? Let's drag him down. I'm burning with anger; I can't stand it."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Be patient, gentle Earl of Westmoreland."],"id":31,"m":"Be patient, good Earl of Westmoreland."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["Patience is for poltroons, such as he;","He durst not sit there had your father liv'd.","My gracious lord, here in the parliament","Let us assail the family of York."],"id":32,"m":"Patience is for cowards like him. He wouldn't dare sit there if your father were alive. My gracious lord, let's attack the York family right here in parliament."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Well hast thou spoken, cousin; be it so."],"id":33,"m":"Well said, cousin; let's do it."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Ah, know you not the city favours them,","And they have troops of soldiers at their beck?"],"id":34,"m":"Ah, don't you know the city of London is on their side, and they have troops of soldiers ready at a word?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["But when the duke is slain, they'll quickly fly."],"id":35,"m":"But once the duke is dead, they'll soon run."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Far be the thought of this from Henry's heart,","To make a shambles of the parliament-house!","Cousin of Exeter, frowns, words, and threats","Shall be the war that Henry means to use.—"],"id":36,"m":"Henry would never dream of turning the parliament house into a slaughterhouse! Cousin Exeter, frowns, words and threats are the only war Henry means to wage."},{"t":"sd","text":"They advance to the duke.","id":37},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","cont":1,"lines":["Thou factious Duke of York, descend my throne,","And kneel for grace and mercy at my feet;","I am thy sovereign."],"id":38,"m":"You rebellious Duke of York, get down from my throne and kneel at my feet for pardon and mercy. I am your sovereign."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["I am thine."],"id":39,"m":"I am yours."},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["For shame, come down; he made thee Duke of York."],"id":40,"m":"Shame on you, come down. He made you Duke of York."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["'T was my inheritance, as the earldom was."],"id":41,"m":"It was my inheritance, just as the earldom was."},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["Thy father was a traitor to the crown."],"id":42,"m":"Your father was a traitor to the crown."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Exeter, thou art a traitor to the crown","In following this usurping Henry."],"id":43,"m":"Exeter, you're the traitor to the crown, following this usurper Henry."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["Whom should he follow, but his natural king?"],"id":44,"m":"Who else should he follow but his rightful king?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["True, Clifford; and that's Richard, Duke of York."],"id":45,"m":"Quite right, Clifford, and that's Richard, Duke of York."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["And shall I stand, and thou sit in my throne?"],"id":46,"m":"And am I to stand while you sit on my throne?"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["It must and shall be so.","Content thyself."],"id":47,"m":"It must and will be so. Accept it."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Be Duke of Lancaster; let him be king."],"id":48,"m":"Be Duke of Lancaster and let him be king."},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["He is both king and Duke of Lancaster;","And that the Lord of Westmoreland shall maintain."],"id":49,"m":"He is both king and Duke of Lancaster, and the Lord of Westmoreland will defend that."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["And Warwick shall disprove it. You forget","That we are those which chas'd you from the field,","And slew your fathers, and with colours spread","March'd through the city to the palace gates."],"id":50,"m":"And Warwick will prove it false. You forget that we're the ones who chased you off the battlefield, killed your fathers, and marched through the city to the palace gates with our flags flying."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Yes, Warwick, I remember it to my grief;","And, by his soul, thou and thy house shall rue it."],"id":51,"m":"Yes, Warwick, I remember it, to my sorrow; and by my father's soul, you and your family will regret it."},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["Plantagenet, of thee, and these thy sons,","Thy kinsmen, and thy friends, I'll have more lives","Than drops of blood were in my father's veins."],"id":52,"m":"Plantagenet, I'll take more lives from you, your sons, your relatives and your friends than there were drops of blood in my father's veins."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["Urge it no more; lest that instead of words","I send thee, Warwick, such a messenger","As shall revenge his death before I stir."],"id":53,"m":"Say no more, or instead of words I'll send you, Warwick, a messenger that will avenge his death before I move from this spot."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Poor Clifford! how I scorn his worthless threats!"],"id":54,"m":"Poor Clifford! How I despise his empty threats!"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Will you we show our title to the crown?","If not, our swords shall plead it in the field."],"id":55,"m":"Do you want us to show our claim to the crown? If not, our swords will argue it on the battlefield."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["What title hast thou, traitor, to the crown?","Thy father was, as thou art, Duke of York;","Thy grandfather, Roger Mortimer, Earl of March.","I am the son of Henry the Fifth,","Who made the Dauphin and the French to stoop,","And seiz'd upon their towns and provinces."],"id":56,"m":"What claim do you have to the crown, traitor? Your father was Duke of York, as you are; your grandfather was Roger Mortimer, Earl of March. I am the son of Henry the Fifth, who made the Dauphin and the French bow down and seized their towns and provinces."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Talk not of France, sith thou hast lost it all."],"id":57,"m":"Don't talk about France, since you've lost all of it."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["The lord protector lost it, and not I;","When I was crown'd I was but nine months old."],"id":58,"m":"The Lord Protector lost it, not me. I was only nine months old when I was crowned."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["You are old enough now, and yet, methinks, you lose.—","Father, tear the crown from the usurper's head."],"id":59,"m":"You're old enough now, and still you seem to be losing. Father, tear the crown off the usurper's head."},{"t":"sp","who":"EDWARD","lines":["Sweet father, do so; set it on your head."],"id":60,"m":"Dear father, do it; put it on your own head."},{"t":"sp","who":"MONTAGUE","lines":["Good brother, as thou lov'st and honourest arms,","Let's fight it out and not stand cavilling thus."],"id":61,"m":"Good brother, as you love and respect the profession of arms, let's fight it out instead of standing here quibbling."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Sound drums and trumpets, and the king will fly."],"id":62,"m":"Sound the drums and trumpets, and the king will run."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Sons, peace!"],"id":63,"m":"Quiet, sons!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Peace thou, and give King Henry leave to speak."],"id":64,"m":"Quiet yourself, and let King Henry speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Plantagenet shall speak first; hear him, lords,","And be you silent and attentive too,","For he that interrupts him shall not live."],"id":65,"m":"Plantagenet will speak first. Listen to him, lords, and stay silent and attentive, because anyone who interrupts him will die."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Think'st thou that I will leave my kingly throne,","Wherein my grandsire and my father sat?","No! first shall war unpeople this my realm;","Ay, and their colours—often borne in France,","And now in England, to our heart's great sorrow—","Shall be my winding sheet.—Why faint you, lords?","My title's good, and better far than his."],"id":66,"m":"Do you think I'll give up my royal throne, where my grandfather and father sat? No! War will empty this kingdom of people first; and the flags so often carried in France, and now in England to our great sorrow, will be my burial shroud. Why are you losing heart, lords? My claim is good, far better than his."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Prove it, Henry, and thou shalt be king."],"id":67,"m":"Prove it, Henry, and you'll be king."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Henry the Fourth by conquest got the crown."],"id":68,"m":"Henry the Fourth won the crown by conquest."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["'T was by rebellion against his king."],"id":69,"m":"It was by rebellion against his king."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["[Aside] I know not what to say; my title's weak.—","Tell me, may not a king adopt an heir?"],"id":70,"m":"(Aside) I don't know what to say; my claim is weak. (Aloud) Tell me, can't a king adopt an heir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["What then?"],"id":71,"m":"What of it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["An if he may, then am I lawful king;","For Richard, in the view of many lords,","Resign'd the crown to Henry the Fourth,","Whose heir my father was, and I am his."],"id":72,"m":"If he can, then I'm the lawful king; for Richard the Second, in front of many lords, handed the crown to Henry the Fourth, whose heir was my father, and I am his."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["He rose against him, being his sovereign,","And made him to resign his crown perforce."],"id":73,"m":"Henry rebelled against him, his sovereign, and forced him to give up his crown."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Suppose, my lords, he did it unconstrain'd,","Think you 't were prejudicial to his crown?"],"id":74,"m":"Suppose, my lords, he gave it up freely: do you think that would cancel his line's right to the crown?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["No; for he could not so resign his crown","But that the next heir should succeed and reign."],"id":75,"m":"No; he couldn't give up his crown in a way that stopped the next heir from succeeding and reigning."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Art thou against us, Duke of Exeter?"],"id":76,"m":"Are you against us, Duke of Exeter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["His is the right, and therefore pardon me."],"id":77,"m":"The right is on his side, so forgive me."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Why whisper you, my lords, and answer not?"],"id":78,"m":"Why are you whispering, my lords, instead of answering?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["My conscience tells me he is lawful king."],"id":79,"m":"My conscience tells me he is the lawful king."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["[Aside] All will revolt from me and turn to him."],"id":80,"m":"(Aside) They'll all desert me and go over to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Plantagenet, for all the claim thou lay'st,","Think not that Henry shall be so depos'd."],"id":81,"m":"Plantagenet, whatever claim you make, don't think Henry will be deposed so easily."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Depos'd he shall be, in despite of all."],"id":82,"m":"He will be deposed, whatever anyone does."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Thou art deceiv'd; 't is not thy southern power,","Of Essex, Norfolk, Suffolk, nor of Kent,","Which makes thee thus presumptuous and proud,","Can set the duke up in despite of me."],"id":83,"m":"You're mistaken. It isn't your southern army from Essex, Norfolk, Suffolk or Kent, which makes you so arrogant, that can put the duke on the throne against my will."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["King Henry, be thy title right or wrong,","Lord Clifford vows to fight in thy defence.","May that ground gape and swallow me alive,","Where I shall kneel to him that slew my father!"],"id":84,"m":"King Henry, whether your claim is right or wrong, Lord Clifford swears to fight for you. May the ground open and swallow me alive where I kneel to the man who killed my father!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["O Clifford, how thy words revive my heart!"],"id":85,"m":"O Clifford, how your words lift my heart!"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Henry of Lancaster, resign thy crown.—","What mutter you, or what conspire you, lords?"],"id":86,"m":"Henry of Lancaster, give up your crown. What are you muttering and plotting, lords?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Do right unto this princely Duke of York,","Or I will fill the house with armed men,","And over the chair of state where now he sits","Write up his title with usurping blood."],"id":87,"m":"Give this princely Duke of York his rights, or I'll fill this hall with armed men and write his claim above the throne where he sits in the usurper's blood."},{"t":"sd","text":"He stamps, and the soldiers show themselves.","id":88},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["My Lord of Warwick, hear but one word:","Let me for this my lifetime reign as king."],"id":89,"m":"My Lord of Warwick, hear just one thing: let me reign as king for the rest of my life."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Confirm the crown to me, and to mine heirs,","And thou shalt reign in quiet while thou liv'st."],"id":90,"m":"Settle the crown on me and my heirs, and you can reign in peace as long as you live."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["I am content; Richard Plantagenet,","Enjoy the kingdom after my decease."],"id":91,"m":"I agree. Richard Plantagenet, have the kingdom after my death."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["What wrong is this unto the prince your son!"],"id":92,"m":"What a wrong this does to the prince your son!"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["What good is this to England and himself!"],"id":93,"m":"What a good thing this is for England and for him!"},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["Base, fearful, and despairing Henry!"],"id":94,"m":"Low, cowardly, hopeless Henry!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["How hast thou injur'd both thyself and us!"],"id":95,"m":"How you've harmed yourself and us!"},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["I cannot stay to hear these articles."],"id":96,"m":"I can't stay to hear the terms of this deal."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Nor I."],"id":97,"m":"Nor can I."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["Come, cousin, let us tell the queen these news."],"id":98,"m":"Come, cousin, let's take this news to the queen."},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["Farewell, faint-hearted and degenerate king,","In whose cold blood no spark of honour bides."],"id":99,"m":"Goodbye, faint-hearted, unworthy king, with not a spark of honour in your cold blood."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Be thou a prey unto the house of York,","And die in bands for this unmanly deed!"],"id":100,"m":"May you fall prey to the house of York and die in chains for this unmanly act!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["In dreadful war mayst thou be overcome,","Or live in peace abandon'd and despis'd!"],"id":101,"m":"May you be defeated in terrible war, or live in peace, abandoned and despised!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Northumberland, Clifford, and Westmoreland.","id":102},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Turn this way, Henry, and regard them not."],"id":103,"m":"Turn this way, Henry, and ignore them."},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["They seek revenge, and therefore will not yield."],"id":104,"m":"They want revenge, so they won't give in."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Ah, Exeter!"],"id":105,"m":"Ah, Exeter!"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Why should you sigh, my lord?"],"id":106,"m":"Why are you sighing, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Not for myself, Lord Warwick, but my son,","Whom I unnaturally shall disinherit.—","But be it as it may, I here entail","The crown to thee, and to thine heirs for ever;","Conditionally, that here thou take an oath","To cease this civil war, and whilst I live","To honour me as thy king and sovereign,","And neither by treason nor hostility","To seek to put me down and reign thyself."],"id":107,"m":"Not for myself, Lord Warwick, but for my son, whom I am unnaturally going to disinherit. But let it be: I here settle the crown on you and your heirs for ever, on condition that you swear an oath here to end this civil war, to honour me as your king and sovereign while I live, and never to try to overthrow me and reign yourself, by treason or by force."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["This oath I willingly take and will perform."],"id":108,"m":"I take this oath willingly and will keep it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Coming from the throne.","id":109},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Long live King Henry!—Plantagenet, embrace him."],"id":110,"m":"Long live King Henry! Plantagenet, embrace him."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["And long live thou, and these thy forward sons!"],"id":111,"m":"And long live you, and these eager sons of yours!"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Now York and Lancaster are reconcil'd."],"id":112,"m":"Now York and Lancaster are reconciled."},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["Accurs'd be he that seeks to make them foes!"],"id":113,"m":"Cursed be anyone who tries to make them enemies!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Sennet. The Lords come forward.","id":114},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Farewell, my gracious lord; I'll to my castle."],"id":115,"m":"Goodbye, my gracious lord; I'm going to my castle."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["And I'll keep London with my soldiers."],"id":116,"m":"And I'll hold London with my soldiers."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["And I to Norfolk with my followers."],"id":117,"m":"And I'll go to Norfolk with my followers."},{"t":"sp","who":"MONTAGUE","lines":["And I unto the sea from whence I came."],"id":118,"m":"And I'll go back to the sea where I came from."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt York and his Sons, Warwick, Norfolk, Montague, Soldiers, and Attendants.","id":119},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["And I, with grief and sorrow, to the court."],"id":120,"m":"And I'll go to court, with grief and sorrow."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter QUEEN MARGARET and the PRINCE OF WALES.","id":121},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["Here comes the queen, whose looks bewray her anger.","I'll steal away."],"id":122,"m":"Here comes the queen; her face shows her anger. I'll slip away."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Exeter, so will I."],"id":123,"m":"Exeter, so will I."},{"t":"sd","text":"Going.","id":124},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Nay, go not from me; I will follow thee."],"id":125,"m":"No, don't leave me; I'll follow you."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Be patient, gentle queen, and I will stay."],"id":126,"m":"Calm down, dear queen, and I'll stay."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Who can be patient in such extremes?","Ah, wretched man! would I had died a maid,","And never seen thee, never borne thee son,","Seeing thou hast prov'd so unnatural a father!","Hath he deserv'd to lose his birthright thus?","Hadst thou but lov'd him half so well as I,","Or felt that pain which I did for him once,","Or nourish'd him as I did with my blood,","Thou wouldst have left thy dearest heart-blood there","Rather than have made that savage duke thine heir","And disinherited thine only son."],"id":127,"m":"Who can be calm in such extremes? Ah, miserable man! I wish I'd died a virgin and never seen you or borne you a son, since you've turned out such an unnatural father! Has he done anything to deserve losing his birthright like this? If you had loved him half as much as I do, or felt the pain I felt for him once, or fed him with my blood as I did, you would have given your own heart's blood rather than make that savage duke your heir and disinherit your only son."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE","lines":["Father, you cannot disinherit me.","If you be king, why should not I succeed?"],"id":128,"m":"Father, you can't disinherit me. If you're king, why shouldn't I succeed you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Pardon me, Margaret;—pardon me, sweet son;","The Earl of Warwick and the duke enforc'd me."],"id":129,"m":"Forgive me, Margaret; forgive me, dear son. The Earl of Warwick and the duke forced me."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Enforc'd thee! art thou king, and wilt be forc'd?","I shame to hear thee speak. Ah, timorous wretch!","Thou hast undone thyself, thy son, and me,","And given unto the house of York such head","As thou shalt reign but by their sufferance.","To entail him and his heirs unto the crown,","What is it but to make thy sepulchre","And creep into it far before thy time?","Warwick is chancellor and the lord of Calais;","Stern Falconbridge commands the narrow seas;","The duke is made protector of the realm;","And yet shalt thou be safe? such safety finds","The trembling lamb environed with wolves.","Had I been there, which am a silly woman,","The soldiers should have toss'd me on their pikes","Before I would have granted to that act.","But thou prefer'st thy life before thine honour;","And seeing thou dost, I here divorce myself,","Both from thy table, Henry, and thy bed,","Until that act of parliament be repeal'd","Whereby my son is disinherited.","The northern lords that have forsworn thy colours","Will follow mine if once they see them spread;","And spread they shall be to thy foul disgrace","And utter ruin of the house of York.","Thus do I leave thee.—Come, son, let's away:","Our army is ready; come, we'll after them."],"id":130,"m":"Forced you! Are you a king and you let yourself be forced? I'm ashamed to hear you speak. Ah, timid wretch! You've ruined yourself, your son and me, and given the house of York so much power that you'll reign only because they allow it. To settle the crown on him and his heirs is just digging your own grave and crawling into it long before your time. Warwick is Chancellor and lord of Calais; stern Falconbridge controls the Channel; the duke is made Protector of the realm; and you think you'll be safe? That's the safety of a trembling lamb surrounded by wolves. If I'd been there, silly woman that I am, the soldiers would have tossed me on their spears before I agreed to that act. But you value your life above your honour; and since you do, I here separate myself from your table and your bed, Henry, until that act of parliament that disinherits my son is repealed. The northern lords who have abandoned your flag will follow mine once they see it unfurled; and it will be unfurled, to your shame and the complete ruin of the house of York. So I leave you. Come, son, let's go. Our army is ready; come, we'll go after them."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Stay, gentle Margaret, and hear me speak."],"id":131,"m":"Wait, gentle Margaret, and hear me speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Thou hast spoke too much already; get thee gone."],"id":132,"m":"You've said too much already; go away."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Gentle son Edward, thou wilt stay with me?"],"id":133,"m":"My dear son Edward, you'll stay with me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Ay, to be murther'd by his enemies."],"id":134,"m":"Yes, to be murdered by his enemies."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE","lines":["When I return with victory from the field","I'll see your grace; till then I'll follow her."],"id":135,"m":"When I come back victorious from the battlefield I'll see your grace; until then I'll follow her."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Come, son, away! we may not linger thus."],"id":136,"m":"Come, son, away! We can't hang about like this."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Queen Margaret and the Prince.","id":137},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Poor queen! how love to me and to her son","Hath made her break out into terms of rage!","Reveng'd may she be on that hateful duke","Whose haughty spirit, winged with desire,","Will cost my crown, and like an empty eagle","Tire on the flesh of me and of my son.","The loss of those three lords torments my heart;","I'll write unto them, and entreat them fair.—","Come, cousin, you shall be the messenger."],"id":138,"m":"Poor queen! How her love for me and her son has made her burst out in rage! May she get her revenge on that hateful duke, whose proud ambition, flying on desire, will cost me my crown and, like a starving eagle, tear at my flesh and my son's. Losing those three lords torments me. I'll write to them and ask them kindly to return. Come, cousin, you'll be the messenger."},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["And I, I hope, shall reconcile them all."],"id":139,"m":"And I hope I'll reconcile them all."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":140},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"II","loc":"Sandal Castle.","id":141},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter EDWARD, RICHARD, and MONTAGUE.","id":142},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Brother, though I be youngest, give me leave."],"id":143,"m":"Brother, I may be the youngest, but let me speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"EDWARD","lines":["No; I can better play the orator."],"id":144,"m":"No, I'm the better speaker."},{"t":"sp","who":"MONTAGUE","lines":["But I have reasons strong and forcible."],"id":145,"m":"But I have strong, convincing reasons."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter YORK.","id":146},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Why, how now, sons and brother! at a strife?","What is your quarrel? how began it first?"],"id":147,"m":"What's this, sons and brother? Arguing? What's the quarrel? How did it start?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EDWARD","lines":["No quarrel, but a slight contention."],"id":148,"m":"It's not a quarrel, just a small disagreement."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["About what?"],"id":149,"m":"About what?"},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["About that which concerns your grace and us—","The crown of England, father, which is yours."],"id":150,"m":"About something that concerns your grace and us: the crown of England, father, which is yours."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Mine, boy? not till King Henry be dead."],"id":151,"m":"Mine, boy? Not until King Henry is dead."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Your right depends not on his life or death."],"id":152,"m":"Your right doesn't depend on whether he lives or dies."},{"t":"sp","who":"EDWARD","lines":["Now you are heir, therefore enjoy it now;","By giving the house of Lancaster leave to breathe,","It will outrun you, father, in the end."],"id":153,"m":"You're the heir now, so enjoy it now. If you give the house of Lancaster time to breathe, it will overtake you in the end, father."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["I took an oath that he should quietly reign."],"id":154,"m":"I swore an oath that he would reign in peace."},{"t":"sp","who":"EDWARD","lines":["But for a kingdom any oath may be broken;","I would break a thousand oaths to reign one year."],"id":155,"m":"But any oath can be broken for a kingdom. I'd break a thousand oaths to reign for one year."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["No; God forbid your grace should be forsworn."],"id":156,"m":"No; God forbid your grace should break your word."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["I shall be, if I claim by open war."],"id":157,"m":"I will be breaking it if I claim the crown by open war."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["I'll prove the contrary if you'll hear me speak."],"id":158,"m":"I'll prove the opposite if you'll hear me out."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Thou canst not, son; it is impossible."],"id":159,"m":"You can't, son; it's impossible."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["An oath is of no moment, being not took","Before a true and lawful magistrate","That hath authority over him that swears.","Henry had none, but did usurp the place;","Then, seeing 't was he that made you to depose,","Your oath, my lord, is vain and frivolous.","Therefore, to arms! And, father, do but think","How sweet a thing it is to wear a crown,","Within whose circuit is Elysium","And all that poets feign of bliss and joy.","Why do we linger thus? I cannot rest","Until the white rose that I wear be dyed","Even in the lukewarm blood of Henry's heart."],"id":160,"m":"An oath means nothing unless it's sworn before a true and lawful authority with power over the one who swears. Henry had no such power; he stole the place. So, since he was the one you swore to, your oath, my lord, is empty and worthless. So, to arms! And father, just think how sweet it is to wear a crown; inside its circle lies paradise and every bliss and joy the poets invent. Why are we hanging about? I can't rest until the white rose I wear is dyed in the warm blood of Henry's heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Richard, enough; I will be king, or die.—","Brother, thou shalt to London presently,","And whet on Warwick to this enterprise.—","Thou, Richard, shalt to the Duke of Norfolk,","And tell him privily of our intent.—","You, Edward, shall unto my Lord Cobham,","With whom the Kentishmen will willingly rise.","In them I trust; for they are soldiers,","Witty, courteous, liberal, full of spirit.—","While you are thus employ'd, what resteth more","But that I seek occasion how to rise,","And yet the king not privy to my drift,","Nor any of the house of Lancaster?"],"id":161,"m":"Enough, Richard; I will be king or die. Brother, go to London at once and spur Warwick on to this enterprise. Richard, go to the Duke of Norfolk and tell him our plan secretly. Edward, go to my Lord Cobham; the men of Kent will gladly rise with him. I trust them, for they're soldiers: clever, courteous, generous and full of spirit. While you're busy with this, all that's left for me is to find an opportunity to rise up, without the king or anyone of the house of Lancaster knowing my plan."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Messenger.","id":162},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","cont":1,"lines":["But stay.—What news? Why com'st thou in such post?"],"id":163,"m":"But wait. What news? Why have you come in such a hurry?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["The queen, with all the northern earls and lords,","Intend here to besiege you in your castle.","She is hard by with twenty thousand men,","And therefore fortify your hold, my lord."],"id":164,"m":"The queen, with all the northern earls and lords, intends to besiege you here in your castle. She's close by with twenty thousand men, so strengthen your defences, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Ay, with my sword. What! think'st thou that we fear them?—","Edward and Richard, you shall stay with me;","My brother Montague shall post to London.","Let noble Warwick, Cobham, and the rest,","Whom we have left protectors of the king,","With powerful policy strengthen themselves,","And trust not simple Henry nor his oaths."],"id":165,"m":"Yes, with my sword. What! Do you think we're afraid of them? Edward and Richard, you'll stay with me; my brother Montague will ride to London. Let noble Warwick, Cobham and the rest we left to guard the king strengthen themselves with clever policy, and not trust simple Henry or his oaths."},{"t":"sp","who":"MONTAGUE","lines":["Brother, I go; I'll win them, fear it not:","And thus most humbly I do take my leave."],"id":166,"m":"Brother, I'm going. I'll win them over, don't worry. And so I humbly take my leave."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":167},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter SIR JOHN and SIR HUGH MORTIMER.","id":168},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Sir John and Sir Hugh Mortimer, mine uncles,","You are come to Sandal in a happy hour;","The army of the queen mean to besiege us."],"id":169,"m":"Sir John and Sir Hugh Mortimer, my uncles, you've come to Sandal at a lucky moment. The queen's army means to besiege us."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR JOHN MORTIMER","lines":["She shall not need; we'll meet her in the field."],"id":170,"m":"She won't need to; we'll meet her in the field."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["What, with five thousand men?"],"id":171,"m":"What, with five thousand men?"},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Ay, with five hundred, father, for a need.","A woman-general! what should we fear?"],"id":172,"m":"Yes, with five hundred if need be, father. A woman general! What do we have to fear?"},{"t":"sd","text":"A march afar off.","id":173},{"t":"sp","who":"EDWARD","lines":["I hear their drums; let's set our men in order,","And issue forth and bid them battle straight."],"id":174,"m":"I can hear their drums. Let's put our men in order, march out and fight them at once."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Five men to twenty!—though the odds be great,","I doubt not, uncle, of our victory.","Many a battle have I won in France","Whenas the enemy hath been ten to one;","Why should I not now have the like success?"],"id":175,"m":"Five men against twenty! Though the odds are great, I don't doubt we'll win, uncle. I won many battles in France when the enemy outnumbered us ten to one; why shouldn't I have the same success now?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum. Exeunt.","id":176},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"III","loc":"Plains near Sandal Castle.","id":177},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarums. Enter RUTLAND and his TUTOR.","id":178},{"t":"sp","who":"RUTLAND","lines":["Ah! whither shall I fly to scape their hands?","Ah, tutor! look where bloody Clifford comes."],"id":179,"m":"Ah! Where can I run to escape them? Ah, tutor! Look, here comes bloody Clifford."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter CLIFFORD and Soldiers.","id":180},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["Chaplain, away! thy priesthood saves thy life.","As for the brat of this accursed duke","Whose father slew my father, he shall die."],"id":181,"m":"Chaplain, go! Your priesthood saves your life. But this cursed duke's brat, whose father killed my father, will die."},{"t":"sp","who":"TUTOR","lines":["And I, my lord, will bear him company."],"id":182,"m":"And I'll die with him, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["Soldiers, away with him!"],"id":183,"m":"Soldiers, take him away!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TUTOR","lines":["Ah, Clifford, murder not this innocent child,","Lest thou be hated both of God and man."],"id":184,"m":"Ah, Clifford, don't murder this innocent child, or you'll be hated by God and man."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit, forced off by Soldiers.","id":185},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["How now! is he dead already? Or is it fear","That makes him close his eyes?—I'll open them."],"id":186,"m":"What's this? Is he dead already? Or is it fear that makes him shut his eyes? I'll open them."},{"t":"sp","who":"RUTLAND","lines":["So looks the pent-up lion o'er the wretch","That trembles under his devouring paws;","And so he walks, insulting o'er his prey,","And so he comes to rend his limbs asunder.—","Ah, gentle Clifford, kill me with thy sword,","And not with such a cruel threat'ning look.","Sweet Clifford, hear me speak before I die:","I am too mean a subject for thy wrath;","Be thou reveng'd on men, and let me live."],"id":187,"m":"That's how a caged lion looks at the trembling wretch under its devouring paws; that's how it paces, gloating over its prey; and that's how it comes to tear the limbs apart. Ah, gentle Clifford, kill me with your sword, not with such a cruel threatening look. Sweet Clifford, hear me before I die: I'm too lowly a target for your anger. Take your revenge on men, and let me live."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["In vain thou speak'st, poor boy; my father's blood","Hath stopp'd the passage where thy words should enter."],"id":188,"m":"You're wasting your words, poor boy. My father's blood has blocked the way your words would enter."},{"t":"sp","who":"RUTLAND","lines":["Then let my father's blood open it again;","He is a man, and, Clifford, cope with him."],"id":189,"m":"Then let my father's blood open it again. He's a man; fight him, Clifford."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["Had I thy brethren here, their lives and thine","Were not revenge sufficient for me.","No; if I digg'd up thy forefathers' graves","And hung their rotten coffins up in chains,","It could not slake mine ire nor ease my heart.","The sight of any of the house of York","Is as a fury to torment my soul;","And till I root out their accursed line","And leave not one alive, I live in hell.","Therefore—"],"id":190,"m":"If I had your brothers here, their lives and yours would not be revenge enough for me. No; if I dug up your ancestors' graves and hung their rotting coffins up in chains, it couldn't quench my anger or ease my heart. The sight of anyone from the house of York is like a Fury tormenting my soul, and until I root out their cursed family and leave not one alive, I'm living in hell. So—"},{"t":"sp","who":"RUTLAND","lines":["O, let me pray before I take my death!—","To thee I pray; sweet Clifford, pity me!"],"id":191,"m":"O, let me pray before I die! I pray to you: sweet Clifford, pity me!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["Such pity as my rapier's point affords."],"id":192,"m":"The only pity you'll get is my sword's point."},{"t":"sp","who":"RUTLAND","lines":["I never did thee harm; why wilt thou slay me?"],"id":193,"m":"I never did you any harm; why will you kill me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["Thy father hath."],"id":194,"m":"Your father did."},{"t":"sp","who":"RUTLAND","lines":["But 't was ere I was born.","Thou hast one son; for his sake pity me,","Lest in revenge thereof, sith God is just,","He be as miserably slain as I.","Ah, let me live in prison all my days,","And when I give occasion of offence,","Then let me die, for now thou hast no cause."],"id":195,"m":"But that was before I was born. You have a son; for his sake pity me, or in revenge for this, since God is just, he may be killed as miserably as I am. Ah, let me live in prison all my life, and if I ever give you cause for offence, then let me die; but now you have no reason."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["No cause?","Thy father slew my father; therefore, die. [Clifford stabs him]"],"id":196,"m":"No reason? Your father killed my father; so die. (He stabs him.)"},{"t":"sp","who":"RUTLAND","lines":["Dii faciant laudis summa sit ista tuae! [Dies]"],"id":197,"m":"May the gods grant that this be the height of your glory! (He dies.)"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["Plantagenet! I come, Plantagenet!","And this thy son's blood cleaving to my blade","Shall rust upon my weapon till thy blood","Congeal'd with this, do make me wipe off both."],"id":198,"m":"Plantagenet! I'm coming, Plantagenet! And your son's blood sticking to my blade will rust on my weapon until your blood, dried together with it, makes me wipe both off."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":199},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"IV","loc":"Another part of the field.","id":200},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum. Enter YORK.","id":201},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["The army of the queen hath got the field.","My uncles both are slain in rescuing me;","And all my followers to the eager foe","Turn back and fly like ships before the wind,","Or lambs pursu'd by hunger-starved wolves.","My sons—God knows what hath bechanced them;","But this I know,—they have demean'd themselves","Like men born to renown by life or death.","Three times did Richard make a lane to me,","And thrice cried 'Courage, father! fight it out!'","And full as oft came Edward to my side","With purple falchion painted to the hilt","In blood of those that had encount'red him;","And when the hardiest warriors did retire","Richard cried 'Charge! and give no foot of ground!'","And cried 'A crown, or else a glorious tomb!","A sceptre, or an earthly sepulchre!'","With this, we charg'd again; but, out, alas!","We budg'd again, as I have seen a swan","With bootless labour swim against the tide","And spend her strength with overmatching waves."],"id":202,"m":"The queen's army has won the field. Both my uncles are killed trying to rescue me, and all my followers turn their backs on the eager enemy and run, like ships before the wind or lambs chased by starving wolves. My sons: God knows what has happened to them; but I do know they have behaved like men born to win fame in life or death. Three times Richard cut a path through to me and three times shouted, 'Courage, father! Fight it out!' And just as often Edward came to my side with his sword stained red to the hilt with the blood of the men who had fought him. And when the toughest soldiers fell back, Richard shouted, 'Charge! Don't give an inch!' and 'A crown, or else a glorious tomb! A sceptre, or a grave on earth!' With that, we charged again; but, alas, we were pushed back again, like a swan I've seen swimming uselessly against the tide and wearing herself out against waves too strong for her."},{"t":"sd","text":"A short alarum within.","id":203},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","cont":1,"lines":["Ah, hark! the fatal followers do pursue,","And I am faint and cannot fly their fury;","And were I strong, I would not shun their fury.","The sands are number'd that make up my life;","Here must I stay, and here my life must end.—"],"id":204,"m":"Ah, listen! The deadly pursuers are coming, and I'm too weak to escape their fury; and if I were strong, I wouldn't run from it. The sands of my life are counted out; here I must stay, and here my life must end."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter QUEEN MARGARET, CLIFFORD, NORTHUMBERLAND, and Soldiers.","id":205},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","cont":1,"lines":["Come, bloody Clifford, rough Northumberland,","I dare your quenchless fury to more rage.","I am your butt, and I abide your shot."],"id":206,"m":"Come, bloody Clifford, rough Northumberland; I dare your endless fury to rage even more. I'm your target, and I'll stand and take your shots."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Yield to our mercy, proud Plantagenet."],"id":207,"m":"Surrender to our mercy, proud Plantagenet."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["Ay, to such mercy as his ruthless arm","With downright payment show'd unto my father.","Now Phaethon hath tumbled from his car,","And made an evening at the noontide prick."],"id":208,"m":"Yes, the kind of mercy his ruthless arm showed my father, paid in full. Now Phaethon has fallen from his chariot and turned noon into evening."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["My ashes, as the phoenix, may bring forth","A bird that will revenge upon you all;","And in that hope I throw mine eyes to heaven","Scorning whate'er you can afflict me with.","Why come you not?—what! multitudes, and fear?"],"id":209,"m":"My ashes, like the phoenix's, may produce a bird that will take revenge on you all; and in that hope I lift my eyes to heaven, scorning whatever torment you can give me. Why don't you come on? What! So many of you, and afraid?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["So cowards fight when they can fly no further;","So doves do peck the falcon's piercing talons;","So desperate thieves, all hopeless of their lives,","Breathe out invectives 'gainst the officers."],"id":210,"m":"That's how cowards fight when they can't run any further; that's how doves peck at the falcon's sharp claws; that's how desperate thieves with no hope of life hurl insults at the officers."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["O Clifford, but bethink thee once again,","And in thy thought o'errun my former time;","And, if thou canst for blushing, view this face,","And bite thy tongue, that slanders him with cowardice","Whose frown hath made thee faint and fly ere this."],"id":211,"m":"O Clifford, just think back and run over my past in your mind; and if you can look at this face without blushing, bite your tongue for calling a coward the man whose frown has made you faint and run before now."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["I will not bandy with thee word for word,","But buckle with thee blows, twice two for one."],"id":212,"m":"I won't trade words with you; I'll trade blows with you, four for every one."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Hold, valiant Clifford! for a thousand causes","I would prolong awhile the traitor's life.—","Wrath makes him deaf; speak thou, Northumberland."],"id":213,"m":"Stop, brave Clifford! For a thousand reasons I want to keep the traitor alive a little longer. Anger has made him deaf; you speak to him, Northumberland."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Hold, Clifford! do not honour him so much","To prick thy finger, though to wound his heart.","What valour were it, when a cur doth grin,","For one to thrust his hand between his teeth,","When he might spurn him with his foot away?","It is war's prize to take all vantages,","And ten to one is no impeach of valour."],"id":214,"m":"Stop, Clifford! Don't do him the honour of pricking your finger, even to wound his heart. What bravery is there in putting your hand between a snarling dog's teeth when you could kick it away? In war it's fair to take every advantage, and ten against one is no slur on courage."},{"t":"sd","text":"They lay hands on York, who struggles.","id":215},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["Ay, ay; so strives the woodcock with the gin."],"id":216,"m":"Yes, yes; that's how the woodcock struggles in the trap."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["So doth the cony struggle in the net."],"id":217,"m":"That's how the rabbit struggles in the net."},{"t":"sd","text":"York is taken prisoner.","id":218},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["So triumph thieves upon their conquer'd booty;","So true men yield, with robbers so o'ermatch'd."],"id":219,"m":"That's how thieves gloat over the loot they've captured; that's how honest men give in when robbers outnumber them."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["What would your grace have done unto him now?"],"id":220,"m":"What would your grace like done with him now?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Brave warriors, Clifford and Northumberland,","Come, make him stand upon this molehill here,","That raught at mountains with outstretched arms,","Yet parted but the shadow with his hand.—","What! was it you that would be England's king?","Was 't you that revell'd in our Parliament,","And made a preachment of your high descent?","Where are your mess of sons to back you now?","The wanton Edward and the lusty George?","And where's that valiant crook-back prodigy,","Dicky your boy, that with his grumbling voice","Was wont to cheer his dad in mutinies?","Or, with the rest, where is your darling Rutland?","Look, York; I stain'd this napkin with the blood","That valiant Clifford with his rapier's point","Made issue from the bosom of the boy,","And, if thine eyes can water for his death,","I give thee this to dry thy cheeks withal.","Alas, poor York! but that I hate thee deadly","I should lament thy miserable state.","I prithee, grieve to make me merry, York;","Stamp, rave, and fret, that I may sing and dance.","What, hath thy fiery heart so parch'd thine entrails","That not a tear can fall for Rutland's death?","Why art thou patient, man? thou shouldst be mad;","And I, to make thee mad, do mock thee thus.","Thou wouldst be feed, I see, to make me sport;","York cannot speak unless he wear a crown.—","A crown for York!—and, lords, bow low to him.—","Hold you his hands whilst I do set it on.—"],"id":221,"m":"Brave warriors, Clifford and Northumberland, come and make him stand on this molehill here, the man who reached for mountains with outstretched arms but only grasped their shadow. What! Was it you who wanted to be King of England? Was it you who made merry in our parliament and preached a sermon about your noble descent? Where's your pack of sons to back you now? The wanton Edward and the lusty George? And where's that brave hunchbacked freak, your boy Dicky, who used to cheer his dad on in rebellions with his growling voice? Or, along with the rest, where's your darling Rutland? Look, York: I stained this handkerchief with the blood that brave Clifford, with his sword's point, made flow from the boy's chest; and if your eyes can weep for his death, I give you this to dry your cheeks with. Alas, poor York! If I didn't hate you to death, I'd pity your miserable state. Please, grieve to make me merry, York; stamp, rave and fret, so I can sing and dance. What, has your fiery heart so dried up your insides that not a tear can fall for Rutland's death? Why are you so calm, man? You should be mad; and I'm mocking you like this to make you mad. I see you want to be paid to entertain me. York can't speak unless he wears a crown. A crown for York! And, lords, bow low to him. Hold his hands while I put it on."},{"t":"sd","text":"Putting a paper crown on his head.","id":222},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","cont":1,"lines":["Ay, marry, sir, now looks he like a king.","Ay, this is he that took King Henry's chair;","And this is he was his adopted heir.—","But how is it that great Plantagenet","Is crown'd so soon and broke his solemn oath?","As I bethink me, you should not be king","Till our King Henry had shook hands with Death.","And will you pale your head in Henry's glory,","And rob his temples of the diadem,","Now in his life, against your holy oath?","O, 't is a fault too too unpardonable.—","Off with the crown, and with the crown his head!","And whilst we breathe take time to do him dead."],"id":223,"m":"Yes, indeed, sir, now he looks like a king. Yes, this is the man who took King Henry's throne, and this is the one who was his adopted heir. But how is it that great Plantagenet is crowned so soon and has broken his solemn oath? As I remember, you weren't to be king until our King Henry had shaken hands with Death. And will you wrap your head in Henry's glory and rob his brows of the crown now, while he's alive, against your holy oath? O, it's a fault far too unforgivable. Off with the crown, and with the crown his head! And while we catch our breath, let's take time to kill him."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["That is my office, for my father's sake."],"id":224,"m":"That's my job, for my father's sake."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Nay, stay; let's hear the orisons he makes."],"id":225,"m":"No, wait; let's hear the prayers he says."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["She-wolf of France, but worse than wolves of France,","Whose tongue more poisons than the adder's tooth,","How ill-beseeming is it in thy sex","To triumph, like an Amazonian trull,","Upon their woes whom fortune captivates!","But that thy face is, vizard-like, unchanging,","Made impudent with use of evil deeds,","I would assay, proud queen, to make thee blush.","To tell thee whence thou cam'st, of whom deriv'd,","Were shame enough to shame thee, wert thou not shameless.","Thy father bears the type of King of Naples,","Of both the Sicils and Jerusalem,","Yet not so wealthy as an English yeoman.","Hath that poor monarch taught thee to insult?","It needs not, nor it boots thee not, proud queen;","Unless the adage must be verified,","That beggars mounted run their horse to death.","'T is beauty that doth oft make women proud;","But, God he knows, thy share thereof is small.","'T is virtue that doth make them most admir'd;","The contrary doth make thee wond'red at.","'T is government that makes them seem divine;","The want thereof makes thee abominable.","Thou art as opposite to every good","As the Antipodes are unto us,","Or as the south to the Septentrion.","O tiger's heart wrapp'd in a woman's hide!","How couldst thou drain the life-blood of the child,","To bid the father wipe his eyes withal,","And yet be seen to bear a woman's face?","Women are soft, mild, pitiful, and flexible;","Thou stern, obdurate, flinty, rough, remorseless.","Bid'st thou me rage? why, now thou hast thy wish:","Wouldst have me weep? why, now thou hast thy will;","For raging wind blows up incessant showers,","And when the rage allays the rain begins.","These tears are my sweet Rutland's obsequies,","And every drop cries vengeance for his death,","'Gainst thee, fell Clifford, and thee, false Frenchwoman."],"id":226,"m":"She-wolf of France, but worse than the wolves of France, whose tongue is more poisonous than an adder's fang! How unfitting it is for your sex to gloat like an Amazon whore over the sorrows of those whom fortune has taken prisoner! If your face weren't like a mask, unchanging and hardened shameless by habitual evil, I'd try to make you blush, proud queen. Just to tell you where you came from and who your family is would shame you, if you weren't shameless. Your father has the title of King of Naples, of both Sicilies and Jerusalem, yet he's not as rich as an English farmer. Did that poor monarch teach you to gloat? There's no need for it, and it does you no good, proud queen, unless it's to prove the saying that a beggar on horseback rides his horse to death. It's beauty that often makes women proud, but God knows you have little of it. It's virtue that makes them most admired; the lack of it makes you a freak. It's self-control that makes them seem divine; the lack of it makes you hateful. You're as opposite to every good thing as the other side of the world is to us, or as the south is to the north. O tiger's heart wrapped in a woman's skin! How could you draw the child's life-blood and tell the father to wipe his eyes with it, and still have the face of a woman? Women are soft, mild, compassionate and yielding; you are stern, hard, flinty, rough and pitiless. You tell me to rage? Well, now you have your wish. You want me to weep? Well, now you have your way; for raging wind whips up endless showers, and when the rage dies down the rain begins. These tears are my sweet Rutland's funeral rites, and every drop cries for revenge for his death, against you, cruel Clifford, and you, treacherous Frenchwoman."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Beshrew me, but his passion moves me so","That hardly can I check my eyes from tears."],"id":227,"m":"Curse me, but his grief moves me so much I can hardly hold back my tears."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["That face of his the hungry cannibals","Would not have touch'd, would not have stain'd with blood;","But you are more inhuman, more inexorable,","O, ten times more, than tigers of Hyrcania.","See, ruthless queen, a hapless father's tears;","This cloth thou dipp'dst in blood of my sweet boy,","And I with tears do wash the blood away.","Keep thou the napkin, and go boast of this;","And if thou tell'st the heavy story right,","Upon my soul, the hearers will shed tears,","Yea, even my foes will shed fast-falling tears","And say 'Alas! it was a piteous deed!'—","There, take the crown, and with the crown my curse;","And in thy need such comfort come to thee","As now I reap at thy too cruel hand!—","Hard-hearted Clifford, take me from the world;","My soul to heaven, my blood upon your heads!"],"id":228,"m":"Even starving cannibals wouldn't have touched that face of his or stained it with blood; but you are more inhuman, more pitiless, ten times more than the tigers of Hyrcania. See, ruthless queen, an unlucky father's tears. You dipped this cloth in my sweet boy's blood, and I'm washing the blood away with tears. Keep the handkerchief and go and boast about this; and if you tell the sad story truthfully, I swear the listeners will weep; yes, even my enemies will shed streams of tears and say, 'Alas, it was a pitiful deed!' There, take the crown, and with the crown my curse; and when you're in need, may you get the same comfort I'm now getting from your cruel hand! Hard-hearted Clifford, take me out of this world; my soul to heaven, my blood on your heads!"},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Had he been slaughter-man to all my kin,","I should not, for my life, but weep with him,","To see how inly sorrow gripes his soul."],"id":229,"m":"Even if he had butchered all my family, I couldn't, to save my life, help weeping with him, seeing how deeply sorrow grips his soul."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["What! weeping-ripe, my Lord Northumberland?","Think but upon the wrong he did us all,","And that will quickly dry thy melting tears."],"id":230,"m":"What! On the verge of tears, Lord Northumberland? Just think of the wrong he did us all, and that will soon dry your melting tears."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["Here's for my oath, here's for my father's death."],"id":231,"m":"This is for my oath; this is for my father's death."},{"t":"sd","text":"Stabbing him.","id":232},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["And here's to right our gentle-hearted king."],"id":233,"m":"And this is to avenge our gentle-hearted king."},{"t":"sd","text":"Stabbing him.","id":234},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Open thy gate of mercy, gracious God!","My soul flies through these wounds to seek out thee."],"id":235,"m":"Open your gate of mercy, gracious God! My soul flies out through these wounds to find you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Dies.","id":236},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Off with his head, and set it on York gates;","So York may overlook the town of York."],"id":237,"m":"Cut off his head and set it on the gates of York, so York can look out over the town of York."},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. Exeunt.","id":238},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"I","loc":"A plain near Mortimer's Cross in Herefordshire.","id":239},{"t":"sd","text":"A march. Enter EDWARD and RICHARD, with their Power.","id":240},{"t":"sp","who":"EDWARD","lines":["I wonder how our princely father scap'd,","Or whether he be scap'd away or no","From Clifford's and Northumberland's pursuit.","Had he been ta'en, we should have heard the news;","Had he been slain, we should have heard the news;","Or had he scap'd, methinks we should have heard","The happy tidings of his good escape.—","How fares my brother? why is he so sad?"],"id":241,"m":"I wonder how our princely father got away, or whether he escaped Clifford and Northumberland's pursuit at all. If he'd been captured we'd have heard the news; if he'd been killed we'd have heard the news; and if he'd escaped, I think we'd have heard the happy news of his escape. How is my brother? Why is he so sad?"},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["I cannot joy until I be resolv'd","Where our right valiant father is become.","I saw him in the battle range about,","And watch'd him how he singled Clifford forth.","Methought he bore him in the thickest troop","As doth a lion in a herd of neat;","Or as a bear, encompass'd round with dogs,","Who having pinch'd a few and made them cry,","The rest stand all aloof and bark at him.","So far'd our father with his enemies;","So fled his enemies my warlike father.","Methinks 'tis pride enough to be his son.—","See how the morning opes her golden gates","And takes her farewell of the glorious sun.","How well resembles it the prime of youth,","Trimm'd like a younker prancing to his love!"],"id":242,"m":"I can't be happy until I know what has become of our brave father. I saw him ranging about in the battle and watched him single out Clifford. I thought he carried himself in the thick of the troops like a lion among a herd of cattle, or like a bear surrounded by dogs: after he has bitten a few and made them yelp, the rest keep their distance and bark at him. That's how our father dealt with his enemies; that's how his enemies fled from my warlike father. I think it's honour enough to be his son. Look how the morning opens her golden gates and says goodbye to the glorious sun. How like it is to the springtime of youth, dressed up like a young gallant prancing off to his girl!"},{"t":"sp","who":"EDWARD","lines":["Dazzle mine eyes, or do I see three suns?"],"id":243,"m":"Are my eyes dazzled, or do I see three suns?"},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Three glorious suns, each one a perfect sun;","Not separated with the racking clouds,","But sever'd in a pale clear-shining sky.","See, see! they join, embrace, and seem to kiss,","As if they vow'd some league inviolable;","Now are they but one lamp, one light, one sun.","In this the heaven figures some event."],"id":244,"m":"Three glorious suns, each a perfect sun; not separated by drifting clouds, but apart in a pale, clear, shining sky. Look, look! They join, embrace and seem to kiss, as if they were swearing some unbreakable alliance. Now they're only one lamp, one light, one sun. Heaven is foretelling some event in this."},{"t":"sp","who":"EDWARD","lines":["'T is wondrous strange, the like yet never heard of.","I think it cites us, brother, to the field,","That we, the sons of brave Plantagenet,","Each one already blazing by our meeds,","Should, notwithstanding, join our lights together,","And overshine the earth, as this the world.","Whate'er it bodes, henceforward will I bear","Upon my target three fair shining suns."],"id":245,"m":"It's wonderfully strange; nobody's ever heard of such a thing. I think it calls us to the battlefield, brother, so that we, the sons of brave Plantagenet, each already shining by his own merits, should still join our lights together and outshine the earth, as this outshines the world. Whatever it means, from now on I'll carry three bright shining suns on my shield."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Nay, bear three daughters; by your leave I speak it,","You love the breeder better than the male.—"],"id":246,"m":"No, carry three daughters; with your permission, I'll say it: you like the breeders better than the males."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Messenger.","id":247},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","cont":1,"lines":["But what art thou, whose heavy looks foretell","Some dreadful story hanging on thy tongue?"],"id":248,"m":"But who are you? Your gloomy looks warn of some dreadful story hanging on your tongue."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["Ah, one that was a woeful looker-on","When as the noble Duke of York was slain,","Your princely father and my loving lord."],"id":249,"m":"Ah, someone who sadly watched when the noble Duke of York was killed, your princely father and my loving lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"EDWARD","lines":["O, speak no more, for I have heard too much!"],"id":250,"m":"O, say no more, I've heard too much!"},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Say how he died, for I will hear it all."],"id":251,"m":"Tell how he died; I'll hear all of it."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["Environed he was with many foes,","And stood against them as the hope of Troy","Against the Greeks that would have ent'red Troy.","But Hercules himself must yield to odds;","And many strokes, though with a little axe,","Hew down and fell the hardest-timber'd oak.","By many hands your father was subdu'd,","But only slaught'red by the ireful arm","Of unrelenting Clifford and the queen,","Who crown'd the gracious duke in high despite,","Laugh'd in his face, and when with grief he wept","The ruthless queen gave him, to dry his cheeks,","A napkin steeped in the harmless blood","Of sweet young Rutland, by rough Clifford slain.","And, after many scorns, many foul taunts,","They took his head, and on the gates of York","They set the same; and there it doth remain,","The saddest spectacle that e'er I view'd."],"id":252,"m":"He was surrounded by many enemies and stood against them as Hector, the hope of Troy, stood against the Greeks who would have entered Troy. But even Hercules must give way when outnumbered, and many strokes, even with a small axe, cut down the hardest oak. Your father was overpowered by many hands, but killed only by the angry arm of merciless Clifford and the queen. She crowned the gracious duke in mockery and laughed in his face, and when he wept with grief the ruthless queen gave him a handkerchief soaked in the innocent blood of sweet young Rutland, killed by rough Clifford, to dry his cheeks. And after many insults and foul taunts, they took his head and put it on the gates of York, and there it remains, the saddest sight I've ever seen."},{"t":"sp","who":"EDWARD","lines":["Sweet Duke of York! our prop to lean upon,","Now thou art gone, we have no staff, no stay.","O Clifford! boisterous Clifford! thou hast slain","The flower of Europe for his chivalry;","And treacherously hast thou vanquish'd him,","For hand to hand he would have vanquish'd thee.","Now my soul's palace is become a prison.","Ah, would she break from hence, that this my body","Might in the ground be closed up in rest!","For never henceforth shall I joy again,","Never, O, never, shall I see more joy!"],"id":253,"m":"Sweet Duke of York, our support to lean on! Now you're gone, we have no staff, no prop. O Clifford, violent Clifford! You've killed the flower of Europe's knighthood; and you beat him treacherously, for in single combat he would have beaten you. Now my body, the palace of my soul, has become a prison. Ah, if only my soul would break out, so my body could be shut away in the ground to rest! For I'll never be happy again; never, O never, will I see joy again!"},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["I cannot weep, for all my body's moisture","Scarce serves to quench my furnace-burning heart;","Nor can my tongue unload my heart's great burthen,","For selfsame wind that I should speak withal","Is kindling coals that fires all my breast","And burns me up with flames that tears would quench.","To weep is to make less the depth of grief;","Tears, then, for babes, blows and revenge for me!—","Richard, I bear thy name; I'll venge thy death,","Or die renowned by attempting it."],"id":254,"m":"I can't weep, for all the moisture in my body is hardly enough to cool my furnace-hot heart; nor can my tongue unload my heart's great burden, because the very breath I should speak with is fanning the coals that burn in my chest and consume me with flames that tears would put out. To weep is to make grief less deep. So tears are for babies; blows and revenge are for me! Richard, I bear your name; I'll avenge your death or die famous in the attempt."},{"t":"sp","who":"EDWARD","lines":["His name that valiant duke hath left with thee;","His dukedom and his chair with me is left."],"id":255,"m":"That brave duke left his name to you; his dukedom and his seat are left to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Nay, if thou be that princely eagle's bird,","Show thy descent by gazing 'gainst the sun;","For chair and dukedom, throne and kingdom say:","Either that is thine, or else thou wert not his."],"id":256,"m":"No, if you're really that princely eagle's chick, prove your descent by staring at the sun. Instead of seat and dukedom, say throne and kingdom: either that's yours, or you were never his son."},{"t":"sd","text":"March. Enter WARWICK and MONTAGUE, with their Army.","id":257},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["How now, fair lords! What fare? what news abroad?"],"id":258,"m":"What's this, fair lords? How are things? What news?"},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Great Lord of Warwick, if we should recount","Our baleful news, and at each word's deliverance","Stab poniards in our flesh till all were told,","The words would add more anguish than the wounds.","O valiant lord, the Duke of York is slain!"],"id":259,"m":"Great Lord of Warwick, if we told our terrible news and stabbed daggers into our flesh at every word until it was all told, the words would hurt more than the wounds. O brave lord, the Duke of York is dead!"},{"t":"sp","who":"EDWARD","lines":["O, Warwick, Warwick! that Plantagenet","Which held thee dearly as his soul's redemption","Is by the stern Lord Clifford done to death."],"id":260,"m":"O Warwick, Warwick! That Plantagenet, who loved you as dearly as the salvation of his soul, has been killed by stern Lord Clifford."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Ten days ago I drown'd these news in tears,","And now, to add more measure to your woes,","I come to tell you things sith then befallen.","After the bloody fray at Wakefield fought,","Where your brave father breath'd his latest gasp,","Tidings, as swiftly as the posts could run,","Were brought me of your loss and his depart.","I, then in London, keeper of the king,","Muster'd my soldiers, gather'd flocks of friends,","And very well appointed, as I thought,","March'd toward Saint Alban's to intercept the queen,","Bearing the king in my behalf along;","For by my scouts I was advertised","That she was coming with a full intent","To dash our late decree in parliament","Touching King Henry's oath and your succession.","Short tale to make, we at Saint Alban's met,","Our battles join'd, and both sides fiercely fought;","But, whether 't was the coldness of the king,","Who look'd full gently on his warlike queen,","That robb'd my soldiers of their heated spleen,","Or whether 't was report of her success,","Or more than common fear of Clifford's rigour,","Who thunders to his captives blood and death,","I cannot judge; but, to conclude with truth,","Their weapons like to lightning came and went,","Our soldiers',—like the night-owl's lazy flight,","Or like an idle thrasher with a flail—","Fell gently down, as if they struck their friends.","I cheer'd them up with justice of our cause,","With promise of high pay and great rewards,","But all in vain; they had no heart to fight,","And we in them no hope to win the day;","So that we fled: the king unto the queen;","Lord George your brother, Norfolk, and myself,","In haste, post-haste, are come to join with you;","For in the marches here, we heard, you were","Making another head to fight again."],"id":261,"m":"Ten days ago I drowned this news in tears; and now, to add to your sorrows, I've come to tell you what has happened since. After the bloody battle at Wakefield, where your brave father breathed his last, news of your loss and his death was brought to me as fast as messengers could ride. I was in London, guarding the king. I gathered my soldiers and crowds of friends and, well equipped as I thought, marched towards St Albans to cut off the queen, bringing the king along on my side; my scouts had told me she was coming with every intention of overturning our recent decree in parliament about King Henry's oath and your succession. To cut a long story short, we met at St Albans, our armies engaged and both sides fought fiercely. But whether it was the king's coolness, looking very gently on his warlike queen, that robbed my soldiers of their hot anger, or the news of her success, or an unusual fear of Clifford's cruelty, who thunders blood and death at his prisoners, I can't say; but to tell the truth, their weapons came and went like lightning, while our soldiers' weapons, like an owl's lazy flight or a lazy thresher's flail, fell gently, as if they were hitting their friends. I encouraged them with the justice of our cause and promises of high pay and great rewards, but it was no use. They had no heart to fight, and we had no hope of winning with them, so we ran: the king to the queen; Lord George your brother, Norfolk and I have rushed here at top speed to join you, since we heard you were here in the Welsh borders raising another army to fight again."},{"t":"sp","who":"EDWARD","lines":["Where is the Duke of Norfolk, gentle Warwick?","And when came George from Burgundy to England?"],"id":262,"m":"Where's the Duke of Norfolk, good Warwick? And when did George come from Burgundy to England?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Some six miles off the duke is with the soldiers;","And for your brother, he was lately sent","From your kind aunt, Duchess of Burgundy,","With aid of soldiers to this needful war."],"id":263,"m":"The duke is about six miles away with the soldiers; and as for your brother, he was recently sent by your kind aunt, the Duchess of Burgundy, with troops to help in this necessary war."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["'T was odds, belike, when valiant Warwick fled;","Oft have I heard his praises in pursuit,","But ne'er till now his scandal of retire."],"id":264,"m":"The odds must have been great if brave Warwick ran. I've often heard his praises in pursuit, but never till now the disgrace of his retreat."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Nor now my scandal, Richard, dost thou hear;","For thou shalt know, this strong right hand of mine","Can pluck the diadem from faint Henry's head","And wring the awful sceptre from his fist,","Were he as famous and as bold in war","As he is fam'd for mildness, peace, and prayer."],"id":265,"m":"You're not hearing of my disgrace now, Richard; for you'll learn that this strong right hand of mine can pluck the crown from feeble Henry's head and wrench the awesome sceptre from his fist, even if he were as famous and bold in war as he is famous for gentleness, peace and prayer."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["I know it well, Lord Warwick, blame me not;","'T is love I bear thy glories makes me speak.","But in this troublous time what's to be done?","Shall we go throw away our coats of steel","And wrap our bodies in black mourning-gowns,","Numbering our Ave-Maries with our beads?","Or shall we on the helmets of our foes","Tell our devotion with revengeful arms?","If for the last, say ay, and to it, lords."],"id":266,"m":"I know it well, Lord Warwick; don't blame me. It's my love for your glory that makes me speak. But what's to be done in these troubled times? Shall we throw away our steel armour, wrap ourselves in black mourning gowns and count our Hail Marys on our rosary beads? Or shall we show our devotion with vengeful arms on our enemies' helmets? If you choose the second, say yes, and let's get on with it, lords."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Why, therefore Warwick came to seek you out,","And therefore comes my brother Montague.","Attend me, lords. The proud insulting queen,","With Clifford and the haught Northumberland,","And of their feather many moe proud birds,","Have wrought the easy-melting king like wax.","He swore consent to your succession,","His oath enrolled in the parliament;","And now to London all the crew are gone,","To frustrate both his oath and what beside","May make against the house of Lancaster.","Their power, I think, is thirty thousand strong;","Now, if the help of Norfolk and myself,","With all the friends that thou, brave Earl of March,","Amongst the loving Welshmen canst procure,","Will but amount to five and twenty thousand,","Why, Via! to London will we march amain,","And once again bestride our foaming steeds,","And once again cry 'Charge upon our foes!'","But never once again turn back and fly."],"id":267,"m":"Why, that's why Warwick came to find you, and why my brother Montague is here. Listen, lords. The proud, insolent queen, with Clifford and the arrogant Northumberland and many more proud birds of their kind, have melted the soft king like wax. He swore to agree to your succession, and his oath is recorded in parliament; and now that whole gang has gone to London to cancel both his oath and anything else that works against the house of Lancaster. Their army is about thirty thousand strong, I think. Now, if Norfolk's help and mine, together with all the friends you can raise among the loyal Welsh, brave Earl of March, comes to just twenty-five thousand, then forward! We'll march at full speed to London, mount our foaming horses once again, and once again shout, 'Charge the enemy!' but never again turn back and run."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Ay, now, methinks, I hear great Warwick speak.","Ne'er may he live to see a sunshine day","That cries 'Retire,' if Warwick bid him stay."],"id":268,"m":"Yes, now I think I hear the great Warwick speaking. May the man who cries 'Retreat' when Warwick tells him to stand never live to see a sunny day."},{"t":"sp","who":"EDWARD","lines":["Lord Warwick, on thy shoulder will I lean;","And when thou fail'st—as God forbid the hour!—","Must Edward fall, which peril heaven forfend!"],"id":269,"m":"Lord Warwick, I'll lean on your shoulder; and if you fail, which God forbid, Edward must fall, a danger heaven prevent!"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["No longer Earl of March, but Duke of York.","The next degree is England's royal throne;","For King of England shalt thou be proclaim'd","In every borough as we pass along,","And he that throws not up his cap for joy","Shall for the fault make forfeit of his head.","King Edward,—valiant Richard,—Montague,—","Stay we no longer dreaming of renown,","But sound the trumpets and about our task."],"id":270,"m":"No longer Earl of March, but Duke of York. The next step up is England's royal throne; for you will be proclaimed King of England in every town we pass through, and anyone who doesn't throw his cap in the air for joy will lose his head for it. King Edward, brave Richard, Montague: let's stop dreaming of glory, sound the trumpets and get to work."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Then, Clifford, were thy heart as hard as steel,","As thou hast shown it flinty by thy deeds,","I come to pierce it, or to give thee mine."],"id":271,"m":"Then, Clifford, even if your heart is as hard as steel, as your flinty deeds have shown it, I'm coming to pierce it, or to give you mine."},{"t":"sp","who":"EDWARD","lines":["Then strike up, drums!—God and Saint George for us!"],"id":272,"m":"Then strike up, drums! God and Saint George for us!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Messenger.","id":273},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["How now! what news?"],"id":274,"m":"What's this? What news?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["The Duke of Norfolk sends you word by me,","The queen is coming with a puissant host,","And craves your company for speedy counsel."],"id":275,"m":"The Duke of Norfolk sends word through me that the queen is coming with a powerful army, and he asks you to come quickly to discuss it."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Why then it sorts; brave warriors, let's away."],"id":276,"m":"Why, then, it all fits. Brave warriors, let's go."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":277},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"II","loc":"Before York.","id":278},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. Enter KING HENRY, QUEEN MARGARET, the PRINCE OF WALES, CLIFFORD, and NORTHUMBERLAND, with drums and trumpets.","id":279},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Welcome, my lord, to this brave town of York.","Yonder's the head of that arch-enemy","That sought to be encompass'd with your crown;","Doth not the object cheer your heart, my lord?"],"id":280,"m":"Welcome, my lord, to this fine town of York. Over there is the head of that arch-enemy who tried to wear your crown. Doesn't the sight cheer your heart, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Ay, as the rocks cheer them that fear their wreck;","To see this sight, it irks my very soul.—","Withhold revenge, dear God! 't is not my fault,","Nor wittingly have I infring'd my vow."],"id":281,"m":"Yes, the way rocks cheer sailors afraid of shipwreck. It sickens my very soul to see it. Hold back your revenge, dear God! It's not my fault, and I haven't knowingly broken my vow."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["My gracious liege, this too much lenity","And harmful pity must be laid aside.","To whom do lions cast their gentle looks?","Not to the beast that would usurp their den.","Whose hand is that the forest bear doth lick?","Not his that spoils her young before her face.","Who scapes the lurking serpent's mortal sting?","Not he that sets his foot upon her back.","The smallest worm will turn, being trodden on,","And doves will peck in safeguard of their brood.","Ambitious York did level at thy crown,","Thou smiling while he knit his angry brows.","He, but a duke, would have his son a king,","And raise his issue like a loving sire;","Thou, being a king, blest with a goodly son,","Didst yield consent to disinherit him,","Which argu'd thee a most unloving father.","Unreasonable creatures feed their young;","And though man's face be fearful to their eyes,","Yet, in protection of their tender ones,","Who hath not seen them, even with those wings","Which sometime they have us'd with fearful flight,","Make war with him that climb'd unto their nest,","Offering their own lives in their young's defence?","For shame, my liege! make them your precedent.","Were it not pity that this goodly boy","Should lose his birthright by his father's fault,","And long hereafter say unto his child,","'What my great-grandfather and grandsire got,","My careless father fondly gave away?'","Ah, what a shame were this! Look on the boy,","And let his manly face, which promiseth","Successful fortune, steel thy melting heart","To hold thine own, and leave thine own with him."],"id":282,"m":"My gracious lord, this excessive leniency and harmful pity must be put aside. Who do lions look gently at? Not at the beast that would steal their den. Whose hand does the forest bear lick? Not the man who kills her cubs in front of her. Who escapes the hidden snake's deadly sting? Not the man who steps on its back. The smallest worm will turn when it's trodden on, and doves will peck to protect their young. Ambitious York aimed at your crown, while you smiled as he frowned angrily. He, only a duke, wanted his son to be king and tried to raise up his children like a loving father; you, a king blessed with a fine son, agreed to disinherit him, which showed you to be a most unloving father. Even animals without reason feed their young; and though they fear the sight of a man, who hasn't seen them use the very wings they sometimes flee with to attack someone climbing up to their nest, risking their own lives to defend their young? For shame, my lord! Let them be your example. Wouldn't it be a pity if this fine boy lost his birthright through his father's fault, and in years to come said to his own child, 'What my great-grandfather and grandfather won, my careless father foolishly gave away'? Ah, what a disgrace that would be! Look at the boy, and let his manly face, which promises good fortune, harden your melting heart to keep what's yours and leave it to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Full well hath Clifford play'd the orator,","Inferring arguments of mighty force.","But, Clifford, tell me, didst thou never hear","That things ill got had ever bad success?","And happy always was it for that son","Whose father for his hoarding went to hell?","I'll leave my son my virtuous deeds behind,","And would my father had left me no more;","For all the rest is held at such a rate","As brings a thousand-fold more care to keep","Than in possession any jot of pleasure.—","Ah, cousin York! would thy best friends did know","How it doth grieve me that thy head is here!"],"id":283,"m":"Clifford has played the speaker very well, presenting very powerful arguments. But, Clifford, tell me, haven't you ever heard that things got wrongly always turn out badly? And was it always lucky for the son whose father went to hell for his hoarding? I'll leave my son my good deeds, and I wish my father had left me nothing more; for everything else costs a thousand times more worry to keep than it gives even a scrap of pleasure to own. Ah, cousin York! I wish your best friends knew how it grieves me that your head is here!"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["My lord, cheer up your spirits;","our foes are nigh,","And this soft courage makes your followers faint.","You promis'd knighthood to our forward son;","Unsheathe your sword and dub him presently.—","Edward, kneel down."],"id":284,"m":"My lord, cheer up; our enemies are near, and this softness makes your followers lose heart. You promised to knight our eager son; draw your sword and dub him now. Edward, kneel."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Edward Plantagenet, arise a knight;","And learn this lesson,—draw thy sword in right."],"id":285,"m":"Edward Plantagenet, rise a knight; and learn this lesson: draw your sword only for what is right."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE","lines":["My gracious father, by your kingly leave,","I'll draw it as apparent to the crown,","And in that quarrel use it to the death."],"id":286,"m":"My gracious father, with your royal permission, I'll draw it as heir to the crown and use it in that cause to the death."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["Why, that is spoken like a toward prince."],"id":287,"m":"Why, that's spoken like a promising prince."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Messenger.","id":288},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["Royal commanders, be in readiness;","For with a band of thirty thousand men","Comes Warwick, backing of the Duke of York,","And in the towns, as they do march along,","Proclaims him king, and many fly to him.","Darraign your battle, for they are at hand."],"id":289,"m":"Royal commanders, be ready; Warwick is coming with an army of thirty thousand men, supporting the Duke of York, and in every town they march through he proclaims him king, and many go over to him. Set out your battle lines, for they're close."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["I would your highness would depart the field;","The queen hath best success when you are absent."],"id":290,"m":"I wish your highness would leave the battlefield. The queen does best when you're not there."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Ay, good my lord, and leave us to our fortune."],"id":291,"m":"Yes, my good lord, and leave us to our fortune."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Why, that's my fortune too; therefore I'll stay."],"id":292,"m":"Why, that's my fortune too, so I'll stay."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Be it with resolution then to fight."],"id":293,"m":"Then let it be with a firm resolve to fight."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE","lines":["My royal father, cheer these noble lords,","And hearten those that fight in your defence.","Unsheathe your sword, good father; cry 'Saint George!'"],"id":294,"m":"My royal father, encourage these noble lords and put heart into those fighting for you. Draw your sword, good father, and cry 'Saint George!'"},{"t":"sd","text":"March. Enter EDWARD, GEORGE, RICHARD, WARWICK, NORFOLK, MONTAGUE, and Soldiers.","id":295},{"t":"sp","who":"EDWARD","lines":["Now, perjur'd Henry, wilt thou kneel for grace","And set thy diadem upon my head,","Or bide the mortal fortune of the field?"],"id":296,"m":"Now, oath-breaking Henry, will you kneel for mercy and put your crown on my head, or face the deadly chances of battle?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Go, rate thy minions, proud insulting boy!","Becomes it thee to be thus bold in terms","Before thy sovereign and thy lawful king?"],"id":297,"m":"Go and scold your favourites, you proud, insolent boy! Is it right for you to speak so boldly to your sovereign and lawful king?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EDWARD","lines":["I am his king, and he should bow his knee.","I was adopted heir by his consent;","Since when, his oath is broke; for, as I hear,","You, that are king, though he do wear the crown,","Have caus'd him by new act of parliament","To blot out me and put his own son in."],"id":298,"m":"I am his king, and he should bow to me. I was made his adopted heir with his consent; and since then he has broken his oath, for I hear that you, who are the real king though he wears the crown, have made him cut me out by a new act of parliament and put his own son in."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["And reason, too;","Who should succeed the father but the son?"],"id":299,"m":"And with good reason. Who should succeed the father but the son?"},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Are you there, butcher?—O, I cannot speak!"],"id":300,"m":"Are you there, butcher? O, I can't speak for rage!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["Ay, crook-back; here I stand, to answer thee,","Or any he the proudest of thy sort."],"id":301,"m":"Yes, hunchback; here I stand to answer you, or the proudest man of your kind."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["'T was you that kill'd young Rutland, was it not?"],"id":302,"m":"It was you who killed young Rutland, wasn't it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["Ay, and old York, and yet not satisfied."],"id":303,"m":"Yes, and old York, and I'm still not satisfied."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["For God's sake, lords, give signal to the fight."],"id":304,"m":"For God's sake, lords, give the signal to fight."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["What say'st thou, Henry, wilt thou yield the crown?"],"id":305,"m":"What do you say, Henry? Will you give up the crown?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Why, how now, long-tongued Warwick! dare you speak?","When you and I met at Saint Alban's last,","Your legs did better service than your hands."],"id":306,"m":"Well, well, big-mouthed Warwick! Do you dare to speak? When you and I last met at St Albans, your legs served you better than your hands."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Then 't was my turn to fly, and now 't is thine."],"id":307,"m":"Then it was my turn to run, and now it's yours."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["You said so much before, and yet you fled."],"id":308,"m":"You said that before, and you still ran."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["'T was not your valour, Clifford, drove me thence."],"id":309,"m":"It wasn't your courage that drove me off, Clifford."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["No, nor your manhood that durst make you stay."],"id":310,"m":"No, nor was it your manliness that made you dare to stay."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Northumberland, I hold thee reverently.","Break off the parley; for scarce I can refrain","The execution of my big-swoln heart","Upon that Clifford, that cruel child-killer."],"id":311,"m":"Northumberland, I respect you. Break off this talk, for I can hardly stop my swollen heart from taking it out on that Clifford, that cruel child-killer."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["I slew thy father; call'st thou him a child?"],"id":312,"m":"I killed your father; do you call him a child?"},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Ay, like a dastard and a treacherous coward,","As thou didst kill our tender brother Rutland,","But ere sunset I'll make thee curse the deed."],"id":313,"m":"Yes, you killed him like a sneak and a treacherous coward, as you killed our young brother Rutland; but before sunset I'll make you curse the deed."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Have done with words, my lords, and hear me speak."],"id":314,"m":"Stop this talk, my lords, and hear me speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Defy them then, or else hold close thy lips."],"id":315,"m":"Defy them, then, or keep your mouth shut."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["I prithee, give no limits to my tongue;","I am a king, and privileg'd to speak."],"id":316,"m":"Please don't restrict my tongue. I'm a king and have the right to speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["My liege, the wound that bred this meeting here","Cannot be cur'd by words; therefore be still."],"id":317,"m":"My lord, the wound that caused this meeting can't be cured by words, so keep quiet."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Then, executioner, unsheathe thy sword.","By him that made us all, I am resolv'd","That Clifford's manhood lies upon his tongue."],"id":318,"m":"Then, executioner, draw your sword. By God who made us all, I'm sure Clifford's manhood is all in his tongue."},{"t":"sp","who":"EDWARD","lines":["Say, Henry, shall I have my right, or no?","A thousand men have broke their fasts to-day","That ne'er shall dine unless thou yield the crown."],"id":319,"m":"Tell me, Henry, will I have my right or not? A thousand men have eaten breakfast today who will never eat dinner unless you give up the crown."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["If thou deny, their blood upon thy head;","For York in justice puts his armour on."],"id":320,"m":"If you refuse, their blood is on your head, for York puts on his armour in a just cause."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE","lines":["If that be right which Warwick says is right,","There is no wrong, but every thing is right."],"id":321,"m":"If what Warwick says is right really is right, then nothing is wrong and everything is right."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Whoever got thee, there thy mother stands;","For, well I wot, thou hast thy mother's tongue."],"id":322,"m":"Whoever your father was, there stands your mother; I can tell you have your mother's tongue."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["But thou art neither like thy sire nor dam,","But like a foul misshapen stigmatic,","Mark'd by the destinies to be avoided,","As venom toads or lizards' dreadful stings."],"id":323,"m":"But you're like neither your father nor your mother; you're like a foul, deformed, branched creature, marked by fate to be avoided like poisonous toads or lizards' dreadful stings."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Iron of Naples hid with English gilt,","Whose father bears the title of a king,—","As if a channel should be call'd the sea,—","Sham'st thou not, knowing whence thou art extraught,","To let thy tongue detect thy base-born heart?"],"id":324,"m":"You're Naples iron covered with English gilding, whose father calls himself a king, as if a gutter were called the sea. Aren't you ashamed, knowing where you come from, to let your tongue reveal your low-born heart?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EDWARD","lines":["A wisp of straw were worth a thousand crowns","To make this shameless callat know herself.—","Helen of Greece was fairer far than thou,","Although thy husband may be Menelaus;","And ne'er was Agamemmon's brother wrong'd","By that false woman as this king by thee.","His father revell'd in the heart of France,","And tam'd the king, and made the dauphin stoop;","And, had he match'd according to his state,","He might have kept that glory to this day;","But when he took a beggar to his bed,","And grac'd thy poor sire with his bridal day,","Even then that sunshine brew'd a shower for him","That wash'd his father's fortunes forth of France","And heap'd sedition on his crown at home.","For what hath broach'd this tumult but thy pride?","Hadst thou been meek, our title still had slept;","And we, in pity of the gentle king,","Had slipp'd our claim until another age."],"id":325,"m":"A bunch of straw, the badge of a scold, would be worth a thousand crowns to make this shameless trollop know what she is. Helen of Greece was far more beautiful than you, though your husband may be a Menelaus; and Agamemnon's brother was never wronged by that unfaithful woman as much as this king has been by you. His father made merry in the heart of France, tamed its king and made the Dauphin bow; and if he had married according to his rank, he might have kept that glory to this day. But when he took a beggar to his bed and honoured your poor father with his wedding day, that sunshine brewed up a storm that washed his father's fortunes out of France and heaped rebellion on his crown at home. For what has started this uproar except your pride? If you'd been humble, our claim would still be asleep; and out of pity for the gentle king, we'd have let our claim pass to another generation."},{"t":"sp","who":"GEORGE","lines":["But when we saw our sunshine made thy spring,","And that thy summer bred us no increase,","We set the axe to thy usurping root;","And though the edge hath something hit ourselves,","Yet, know thou, since we have begun to strike,","We'll never leave till we have hewn thee down","Or bath'd thy growing with our heated bloods."],"id":326,"m":"But when we saw that our sunshine made your springtime, and your summer gave us no harvest, we took the axe to your usurping root; and though the edge has hit us a little too, know that since we've started to strike, we'll never stop until we've cut you down or watered your growth with our hot blood."},{"t":"sp","who":"EDWARD","lines":["And in this resolution I defy thee;","Not willing any longer conference,","Since thou deniest the gentle king to speak.—","Sound trumpets;—let our bloody colours wave,","And either victory or else a grave!"],"id":327,"m":"And with that resolve, I defy you. I want no more talk, since you won't let the gentle king speak. Sound the trumpets! Let our bloody flags wave: either victory or a grave!"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Stay, Edward."],"id":328,"m":"Wait, Edward."},{"t":"sp","who":"EDWARD","lines":["No, wrangling woman, we'll no longer stay;","These words will cost ten thousand lives this day."],"id":329,"m":"No, quarrelling woman, we'll wait no longer. These words will cost ten thousand lives today."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":330},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"III","loc":"A field of battle between Towton.","id":331},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarums. Excursions. Enter WARWICK.","id":332},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Forspent with toil, as runners with a race,","I lay me down a little while to breathe;","For strokes receiv'd, and many blows repaid,","Have robb'd my strong-knit sinews of their strength,","And, spite of spite, needs must I rest awhile."],"id":333,"m":"Exhausted by effort, like runners after a race, I'll lie down for a while to get my breath back. The blows I've taken, and the many I've returned, have drained my strong muscles of their strength, and like it or not, I must rest a while."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter EDWARD, running.","id":334},{"t":"sp","who":"EDWARD","lines":["Smile, gentle heaven, or strike, ungentle death!","For this world frowns and Edward's sun is clouded."],"id":335,"m":"Smile, kind heaven, or strike, unkind death! This world frowns, and Edward's sun is clouded over."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["How now, my lord? what hap? what hope of good?"],"id":336,"m":"What's this, my lord? What luck? What hope?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter GEORGE.","id":337},{"t":"sp","who":"GEORGE","lines":["Our hap is lost, our hope but sad despair;","Our ranks are broke and ruin follows us.","What counsel give you? whither shall we fly?"],"id":338,"m":"Our luck is gone, our hope is only sad despair. Our ranks are broken and ruin is chasing us. What do you advise? Where shall we run?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EDWARD","lines":["Bootless is flight, they follow us with wings;","And weak we are and cannot shun pursuit."],"id":339,"m":"Running is useless; they chase us as if on wings, and we're too weak to escape."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter RICHARD.","id":340},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Ah, Warwick, why hast thou withdrawn thyself?","Thy brother's blood the thirsty earth hath drunk,","Broach'd with the steely point of Clifford's lance;","And in the very pangs of death he cried,","Like to a dismal clangor heard from far,","'Warwick, revenge! brother, revenge my death!'","So, underneath the belly of their steeds","That stain'd their fetlocks in his smoking blood,","The noble gentleman gave up the ghost."],"id":341,"m":"Ah, Warwick, why have you pulled back? The thirsty earth has drunk your brother's blood, let out by the steel point of Clifford's lance; and in the very agony of death he cried, like a grim trumpet heard from far away, 'Warwick, revenge! Brother, avenge my death!' And so, under the bellies of their horses, whose legs were stained with his steaming blood, the noble gentleman died."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Then let the earth be drunken with our blood;","I'll kill my horse, because I will not fly.","Why stand we like soft-hearted women here,","Wailing our losses whiles the foe doth rage,","And look upon, as if the tragedy","Were play'd in jest by counterfeiting actors?","Here on my knee I vow to God above,","I'll never pause again, never stand still,","Till either death hath clos'd these eyes of mine,","Or fortune given me measure of revenge."],"id":342,"m":"Then let the earth get drunk on our blood. I'll kill my horse, because I will not run. Why are we standing here like soft-hearted women, wailing over our losses while the enemy rages, and watching as if the tragedy were being acted for fun by pretend actors? Here on my knee I swear to God above that I'll never pause again or stand still until death has closed my eyes or fortune has given me my share of revenge."},{"t":"sp","who":"EDWARD","lines":["O Warwick, I do bend my knee with thine,","And in this vow do chain my soul to thine!—","And, ere my knee rise from the earth's cold face,","I throw my hands, mine eyes, my heart to thee,","Thou setter-up and plucker-down of kings,","Beseeching thee, if with thy will it stands","That to my foes this body must be prey,","Yet that thy brazen gates of heaven may ope,","And give sweet passage to my sinful soul.—","Now, lords, take leave until we meet again,","Where'er it be, in heaven or in earth."],"id":343,"m":"O Warwick, I kneel with you and chain my soul to yours in this vow! And before my knee rises from the cold earth, I lift my hands, eyes and heart to you, God, who sets up and pulls down kings, begging you, if it's your will that this body must be prey to my enemies, at least to open your bronze gates of heaven and let my sinful soul pass sweetly in. Now, lords, let's say goodbye until we meet again, wherever it may be, in heaven or on earth."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Brother, give me thy hand;—and, gentle Warwick,","Let me embrace thee in my weary arms.","I, that did never weep, now melt with woe,","That winter should cut off our spring-time so."],"id":344,"m":"Brother, give me your hand; and, gentle Warwick, let me hug you in my tired arms. I, who never wept, now melt with sorrow that winter should cut short our spring like this."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Away, away! Once more, sweet lords, farewell."],"id":345,"m":"Away, away! Once more, sweet lords, farewell."},{"t":"sp","who":"GEORGE","lines":["Yet let us all together to our troops,","And give them leave to fly that will not stay,","And call them pillars that will stand to us;","And if we thrive, promise them such rewards","As victors wear at the Olympian games.","This may plant courage in their quailing breasts,","For yet is hope of life and victory.—","Forslow no longer; make we hence amain."],"id":346,"m":"But let's all go to our troops together. Let those who won't stay run, and call those who stand by us pillars; and if we win, promise them rewards like those the winners wear at the Olympic games. This may put courage in their sinking hearts, for there's still hope of life and victory. Don't delay any longer; let's go at full speed."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":347},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"IV","loc":"Another Part of the Field.","id":348},{"t":"sd","text":"Excursions. Enter RICHARD and CLIFFORD.","id":349},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Now, Clifford, I have singled thee alone.","Suppose this arm is for the Duke of York,","And this for Rutland; both bound to revenge,","Wert thou environ'd with a brazen wall."],"id":350,"m":"Now, Clifford, I've cut you out from the herd. Imagine this arm is for the Duke of York and this one for Rutland; both are bound to take revenge, even if you were surrounded by a wall of brass."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["Now, Richard, I am with thee here alone.","This is the hand that stabbed thy father York,","And this the hand that slew thy brother Rutland;","And here's the heart that triumphs in their death,","And cheers these hands that slew thy sire and brother","To execute the like upon thyself;","And so have at thee!"],"id":351,"m":"Now, Richard, I'm here alone with you. This is the hand that stabbed your father York, and this is the hand that killed your brother Rutland; and here's the heart that rejoices in their deaths and urges these hands, which killed your father and brother, to do the same to you. So, have at you!"},{"t":"sd","text":"They fight. Warwick enters; Clifford flies.","id":352},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Nay, Warwick, single out some other chase;","For I myself will hunt this wolf to death."],"id":353,"m":"No, Warwick, find some other prey; I'll hunt this wolf to death myself."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":354},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"V","loc":"Another Part of the Field.","id":355},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum. Enter KING HENRY.","id":356},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["This battle fares like to the morning's war,","When dying clouds contend with growing light,","What time the shepherd, blowing of his nails,","Can neither call it perfect day nor night.","Now sways it this way, like a mighty sea","Forc'd by the tide to combat with the wind;","Now sways it that way, like the selfsame sea","Forc'd to retire by fury of the wind.","Sometime the flood prevails, and then the wind,","Now one the better, then another best,","Both tugging to be victors, breast to breast,","Yet neither conqueror nor conquered;","So is the equal poise of this fell war.","Here on this molehill will I sit me down.","To whom God will, there be the victory!","For Margaret my queen, and Clifford too,","Have chid me from the battle, swearing both","They prosper best of all when I am thence.","Would I were dead! if God's good will were so;","For what is in this world but grief and woe?","O God! methinks it were a happy life,","To be no better than a homely swain;","To sit upon a hill, as I do now,","To carve out dials quaintly, point by point,","Thereby to see the minutes how they run,","How many make the hour full complete,","How many hours brings about the day,","How many days will finish up the year,","How many years a mortal man may live.","When this is known, then to divide the times;","So many hours must I tend my flock;","So many hours must I take my rest;","So many hours must I contemplate;","So many hours must I sport myself;","So many days my ewes have been with young;","So many weeks ere the poor fools will ean;","So many years ere I shall shear the fleece.","So minutes, hours, days, months, and years,","Pass'd over to the end they were created,","Would bring white hairs unto a quiet grave.","Ah, what a life were this! how sweet! how lovely!","Gives not the hawthorn bush a sweeter shade","To shepherds looking on their silly sheep","Than doth a rich embroider'd canopy","To kings that fear their subjects' treachery?","O, yes, it doth; a thousand-fold it doth!","And to conclude, the shepherd's homely curds,","His cold thin drink out of his leather bottle,","His wonted sleep under a fresh tree's shade,","All which secure and sweetly he enjoys,","Is far beyond a prince's delicates,","His viands sparkling in a golden cup,","His body couched in a curious bed,","When care, mistrust, and treason wait on him."],"id":357,"m":"This battle is like the struggle at dawn, when the fading clouds fight the growing light and the shepherd, blowing on his fingers, can't call it either full day or night. Now it sways this way, like a mighty sea forced by the tide to fight the wind; now it sways that way, like the same sea forced back by the fury of the wind. Sometimes the tide wins, then the wind; now one is ahead, then the other, both straining to win, chest to chest, yet neither conquers nor is conquered. That's how evenly balanced this cruel war is. I'll sit down here on this molehill. Let God give the victory to whoever he wants! For Margaret my queen, and Clifford too, have scolded me away from the battle, both swearing they do best when I'm not there. I wish I were dead, if it were God's will; for what is there in this world but grief and sorrow? O God! I think it would be a happy life to be nothing more than a simple shepherd; to sit on a hill as I'm doing now, carving sundials carefully, line by line, to watch the minutes run: how many make a whole hour, how many hours make a day, how many days complete a year, how many years a man may live. Once I knew that, I'd divide up my time: so many hours I must tend my flock; so many hours I must rest; so many hours I must think; so many hours I must play; my ewes have been pregnant so many days; so many weeks before the poor things give birth; so many years before I shear the fleece. So minutes, hours, days, months and years, spent in the way they were made for, would bring white hair to a quiet grave. Ah, what a life that would be! How sweet! How lovely! Doesn't the hawthorn bush give a sweeter shade to shepherds watching their innocent sheep than a rich embroidered canopy gives to kings who fear their subjects' treachery? O yes, it does, a thousand times sweeter! And in short, the shepherd's simple curds, his cold thin drink from his leather bottle, his usual sleep in the shade of a fresh tree, all of which he enjoys safely and sweetly, are far better than a prince's delicacies, his food served in a sparkling gold cup and his body laid in an elaborate bed, when worry, mistrust and treason wait on him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum. Enter a Son that hath killed his father, bringing in the dead body.","id":358},{"t":"sp","who":"SON","lines":["Ill blows the wind that profits nobody.","This man, whom hand to hand I slew in fight,","May be possessed with some store of crowns;","And I, that haply take them from him now,","May yet ere night yield both my life and them","To some man else, as this dead man doth me.—","Who's this?—O God! it is my father's face,","Whom in this conflict I unwares have kill'd.","O heavy times, begetting such events!","From London by the king was I press'd forth;","My father, being the Earl of Warwick's man,","Came on the part of York, press'd by his master;","And I, who at his hands receiv'd my life,","Have by my hands of life bereaved him.—","Pardon me, God, I knew not what I did;—","And pardon, father, for I knew not thee.—","My tears shall wipe away these bloody marks,","And no more words till they have flow'd their fill."],"id":359,"m":"It's an ill wind that blows nobody any good. This man, whom I killed in hand-to-hand combat, may have some money on him; and I, who perhaps take it from him now, may before nightfall give up both my life and the money to someone else, as this dead man does to me. Who is this? O God! It's my father's face, whom I've killed in this fight without knowing it. O terrible times, that produce such things! I was drafted out of London by the king; my father, being the Earl of Warwick's man, came to fight for York, drafted by his master; and I, who received my life from him, have taken his life with my own hands. Forgive me, God, I didn't know what I was doing; and forgive me, father, for I didn't know you. My tears will wash away these bloody marks, and I'll say no more until they've flowed their fill."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["O piteous spectacle! O bloody times!","Whiles lions war and battle for their dens,","Poor harmless lambs abide their enmity.","Weep, wretched man, I'll aid thee tear for tear;","And let our hearts and eyes, like civil war,","Be blind with tears and break o'ercharg'd with grief."],"id":360,"m":"O pitiful sight! O bloody times! While lions make war and fight over their dens, poor harmless lambs suffer from their hatred. Weep, poor man; I'll help you, tear for tear; and let our hearts and eyes, like a civil war, be blinded with tears and burst, overloaded with grief."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Father who has killed his son, with the body in his arms.","id":361},{"t":"sp","who":"FATHER","lines":["Thou that so stoutly hath resisted me,","Give me thy gold, if thou hast any gold,","For I have bought it with an hundred blows.—","But let me see;—is this our foeman's face?","Ah, no, no, no! it is mine only son!—","Ah, boy, if any life be left in thee,","Throw up thine eye; see, see what showers arise,","Blown with the windy tempest of my heart,","Upon thy wounds that kill mine eye and heart!—","O, pity, God, this miserable age!—","What stratagems, how fell, how butcherly,","Erroneous, mutinous, and unnatural,","This deadly quarrel daily doth beget!—","O boy, thy father gave thee life too soon,","And hath bereft thee of thy life too late!"],"id":362,"m":"You who resisted me so stubbornly, give me your gold if you have any, for I've paid for it with a hundred blows. But let me see: is this our enemy's face? Ah, no, no, no! It's my only son! Ah, boy, if there's any life left in you, lift up your eyes; see, see what showers rise, blown by the windy storm of my heart, onto your wounds, which kill my eyes and heart! O God, pity this miserable age! What cruel, butchering, unnatural, rebellious deeds this deadly quarrel produces every day! O boy, your father gave you life too soon, and took your life too late!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Woe above woe! grief more than common grief!","O that my death would stay these ruthful deeds!—","O pity, pity! gentle heaven, pity!—","The red rose and the white are on his face,","The fatal colours of our striving houses;","The one his purple blood right well resembles,","The other his pale cheeks, methinks, presenteth.","Wither one rose, and let the other flourish!","If you contend, a thousand lives must wither."],"id":363,"m":"Sorrow on sorrow! Grief beyond ordinary grief! O, if only my death would stop these pitiful deeds! O pity, pity, gentle heaven, pity! The red rose and the white are on his face, the deadly colours of our warring houses: his red blood looks just like one, and his pale cheeks, I think, show the other. Let one rose wither and the other flourish! If you keep fighting, a thousand lives must wither."},{"t":"sp","who":"SON","lines":["How will my mother, for a father's death,","Take on with me and ne'er be satisfied!"],"id":364,"m":"How my mother will grieve at me for my father's death, and never be consoled!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FATHER","lines":["How will my wife, for slaughter of my son,","Shed seas of tears and ne'er be satisfied!"],"id":365,"m":"How my wife will shed seas of tears for the killing of my son, and never be consoled!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["How will the country, for these woeful chances,","Misthink the king and not be satisfied!"],"id":366,"m":"How the country will think badly of the king for these sad events, and never be satisfied!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SON","lines":["Was ever son so rued a father's death?"],"id":367,"m":"Did a son ever regret a father's death so much?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FATHER","lines":["Was ever father so bemoan'd his son?"],"id":368,"m":"Did a father ever mourn his son so much?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Was ever king so griev'd for subjects' woe?","Much is your sorrow, mine ten times so much."],"id":369,"m":"Did a king ever grieve so much for his subjects' sorrow? Your sorrow is great; mine is ten times greater."},{"t":"sp","who":"SON","lines":["I'll bear thee hence, where I may weep my fill."],"id":370,"m":"I'll carry you away to where I can weep my fill."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit with the body.","id":371},{"t":"sp","who":"FATHER","lines":["These arms of mine shall be thy winding-sheet;","My heart, sweet boy, shall be thy sepulchre,","For from my heart thine image ne'er shall go;","My sighing breast shall be thy funeral bell;","And so obsequious will thy father be,","Even for the loss of thee, having no more,","As Priam was for all his valiant sons.","I'll bear thee hence; and let them fight that will,","For I have murder'd where I should not kill."],"id":372,"m":"These arms of mine will be your shroud; my heart, sweet boy, will be your tomb, for your image will never leave my heart. My sighing chest will be your funeral bell; and your father will mourn you as devotedly, losing you, his only son, as Priam mourned all his brave sons. I'll carry you away; let those fight who want to, for I have murdered where I should not have killed."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit with the body.","id":373},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Sad-hearted men, much overgone with care,","Here sits a king more woeful than you are."],"id":374,"m":"Sad-hearted men, overwhelmed with sorrow, here sits a king more miserable than you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarums. Excursions. Enter QUEEN MARGARET, PRINCE OF WALES, and EXETER.","id":375},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE","lines":["Fly, father, fly! for all your friends are fled,","And Warwick rages like a chafed bull.","Away! for death doth hold us in pursuit."],"id":376,"m":"Run, father, run! All your friends have run, and Warwick is raging like an angry bull. Away! Death is chasing us."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Mount you, my lord; towards Berwick post amain.","Edward and Richard, like a brace of greyhounds,","Having the fearful flying hare in sight,","With fiery eyes, sparkling for very wrath,","And bloody steel grasp'd in their ireful hands,","Are at our backs; and therefore hence amain."],"id":377,"m":"Get on your horse, my lord, and ride fast to Berwick. Edward and Richard, like a pair of greyhounds with the frightened hare in sight, eyes flashing with anger and bloody swords in their furious hands, are right behind us; so away, quickly."},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["Away! for vengeance comes along with them.","Nay, stay not to expostulate; make speed,","Or else come after; I'll away before."],"id":378,"m":"Away! Revenge is coming with them. No, don't stop to argue; hurry, or else follow on; I'll go ahead."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Nay, take me with thee, good sweet Exeter;","Not that I fear to stay, but love to go","Whither the queen intends. Forward! away!"],"id":379,"m":"No, take me with you, good sweet Exeter. Not that I'm afraid to stay, but I want to go wherever the queen is going. Forward! Away!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":380},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"VI","loc":"Another Part of the Field.","id":381},{"t":"sd","text":"A loud alarum. Enter CLIFFORD, wounded.","id":382},{"t":"sp","who":"CLIFFORD","lines":["Here burns my candle out; ay, here it dies,","Which whiles it lasted gave King Henry light.","O Lancaster! I fear thy overthrow","More than my body's parting with my soul!","My love and fear glued many friends to thee;","And, now I fall, thy tough commixtures melt,","Impairing Henry, strengthening mis-proud York.","The common people swarm like summer flies;","And whither fly the gnats but to the sun?","And who shines now but Henry's enemies?","O Phoebus, hadst thou never given consent","That Phaethon should check thy fiery steeds,","Thy burning car never had scorch'd the earth!","And, Henry, hadst thou sway'd as kings should do,","Or as thy father and his father did,","Giving no ground unto the house of York,","They never then had sprung like summer flies;","I, and ten thousand in this luckless realm,","Had left no mourning widows for our death,","And thou this day hadst kept thy chair in peace.","For what doth cherish weeds but gentle air?","And what makes robbers bold but too much lenity?","Bootless are plaints, and cureless are my wounds;","No way to fly, nor strength to hold out flight.","The foe is merciless and will not pity,","For at their hands I have deserv'd no pity.","The air hath got into my deadly wounds,","And much effuse of blood doth make me faint.—","Come, York and Richard, Warwick, and the rest;","I stabb'd your fathers' bosoms, split my breast."],"id":383,"m":"Here my candle burns out; yes, here it dies, the candle that gave King Henry light while it lasted. O Lancaster! I fear your downfall more than my soul's parting from my body! Love and fear of me held many friends to you; and now that I fall, those strong bonds melt, weakening Henry and strengthening arrogant York. The common people swarm like summer flies; and where do gnats fly but to the sun? And who is the sun now but Henry's enemies? O Phoebus, if you had never let Phaethon drive your fiery horses, your burning chariot would never have scorched the earth! And Henry, if you had ruled as kings should, or as your father and his father did, giving no ground to the house of York, they would never have sprung up like summer flies; I and ten thousand others in this unlucky kingdom would have left no widows mourning our deaths, and you would have kept your throne in peace today. For what makes weeds grow but mild weather? And what makes robbers bold but too much leniency? Complaints are useless, and my wounds can't be cured; there's no way to escape, and no strength to keep running. The enemy is merciless and will show no pity, for I've deserved no pity from them. The air has got into my deadly wounds, and losing so much blood makes me faint. Come, York and Richard, Warwick and the rest; I stabbed your fathers' chests; now split mine."},{"t":"sd","text":"He faints.","id":384},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum and retreat. Enter EDWARD, GEORGE, RICHARD, MONTAGUE, WARWICK, and Soldiers.","id":385},{"t":"sp","who":"EDWARD","lines":["Now breathe we, lords; good fortune bids us pause,","And smooth the frowns of war with peaceful looks.—","Some troops pursue the bloody-minded queen","That led calm Henry, though he were a king,","As doth a sail, fill'd with a fretting gust,","Command an argosy to stem the waves.","But think you, lords, that Clifford fled with them?"],"id":386,"m":"Now let's catch our breath, lords. Good fortune tells us to pause and smooth war's frowns with peaceful looks. Some troops are chasing the bloodthirsty queen, who led calm Henry, though he was the king, the way a sail filled with a gusty wind forces a great ship to plough against the waves. But do you think, lords, that Clifford escaped with them?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["No, 't is impossible he should escape;","For, though before his face I speak the words,","Your brother Richard mark'd him for the grave,","And whereso'er he is he's surely dead."],"id":387,"m":"No, it's impossible he escaped; for, though I say it to his face, your brother Richard marked him for the grave, and wherever he is, he's surely dead."},{"t":"sd","text":"Clifford groans and dies.","id":388},{"t":"sp","who":"EDWARD","lines":["Whose soul is that which takes her heavy leave?"],"id":389,"m":"Whose soul is that, taking its heavy leave?"},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["A deadly groan, like life and death's departing."],"id":390,"m":"A deadly groan, like life parting from death."},{"t":"sp","who":"EDWARD","lines":["See who it is; and, now the battle's ended,","If friend or foe, let him be gently us'd."],"id":391,"m":"See who it is; and now the battle is over, friend or enemy, let him be treated kindly."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Revoke that doom of mercy, for 't is Clifford,","Who, not contented that he lopp'd the branch,","In hewing Rutland when his leaves put forth,","But set his murthering knife unto the root","From whence that tender spray did sweetly spring;","I mean our princely father, Duke of York."],"id":392,"m":"Take back that merciful order, for it's Clifford, who, not satisfied with lopping off the branch by cutting down Rutland just as his leaves were budding, put his murdering knife to the root that tender shoot grew from, I mean our princely father, the Duke of York."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["From off the gates of York fetch down the head,","Your father's head, which Clifford placed there;","Instead whereof, let this supply the room.","Measure for measure must be answered."],"id":393,"m":"Take down the head from the gates of York, your father's head, which Clifford put there, and let this one take its place. Measure must be paid back with measure."},{"t":"sp","who":"EDWARD","lines":["Bring forth that fatal screech-owl to our house,","That nothing sung but death to us and ours;","Now death shall stop his dismal threatening sound,","And his ill-boding tongue no more shall speak."],"id":394,"m":"Bring out that deadly screech-owl to our family, who sang nothing but death to us and ours. Now death will stop his dismal threatening noise, and his tongue that foretold evil will speak no more."},{"t":"sd","text":"Soldiers bring the body forward.","id":395},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["I think his understanding is bereft.—","Speak, Clifford, dost thou know who speaks to thee?—","Dark cloudy death o'ershades his beams of life,","And he nor sees nor hears us, what we say."],"id":396,"m":"I think he has lost his senses. Speak, Clifford: do you know who's speaking to you? Dark, cloudy death is overshadowing the light of his life, and he neither sees us nor hears what we say."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["O, would he did! and so, perhaps, he doth;","'T is but his policy to counterfeit,","Because he would avoid such bitter taunts","Which in the time of death he gave our father."],"id":397,"m":"O, I wish he did! And perhaps he does. It's just his trick to pretend, to avoid the kind of bitter taunts he gave our father as he died."},{"t":"sp","who":"GEORGE","lines":["If so thou think'st, vex him with eager words."],"id":398,"m":"If you think so, torment him with sharp words."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Clifford, ask mercy, and obtain no grace."],"id":399,"m":"Clifford, beg for mercy, and get no pardon."},{"t":"sp","who":"EDWARD","lines":["Clifford, repent in bootless penitence."],"id":400,"m":"Clifford, repent with useless repentance."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Clifford, devise excuses for thy faults."],"id":401,"m":"Clifford, think up excuses for your crimes."},{"t":"sp","who":"GEORGE","lines":["While we devise fell tortures for thy faults."],"id":402,"m":"While we think up cruel tortures for your crimes."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Thou didst love York, and I am son to York."],"id":403,"m":"You loved York, and I am York's son."},{"t":"sp","who":"EDWARD","lines":["Thou pitiedst Rutland, I will pity thee."],"id":404,"m":"You pitied Rutland; I will pity you."},{"t":"sp","who":"GEORGE","lines":["Where's Captain Margaret to fence you now?"],"id":405,"m":"Where's Captain Margaret to defend you now?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["They mock thee, Clifford; swear as thou wast wont."],"id":406,"m":"They're mocking you, Clifford; swear as you used to."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["What! not an oath? nay then, the world goes hard","When Clifford cannot spare his friends an oath.—","I know by that he's dead; and, by my soul,","If this right hand would buy two hours' life,","That I in all despite might rail at him,","This hand should chop it off, and with the issuing blood","Stifle the villain whose unstanched thirst","York and young Rutland could not satisfy."],"id":407,"m":"What! Not one oath? Well, things are bad when Clifford can't spare his friends an oath. That tells me he's dead; and by my soul, if this right hand could buy him two hours of life so that I could insult him with all my scorn, I'd chop it off and choke the villain with the gushing blood, the villain whose endless thirst York and young Rutland couldn't satisfy."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Ay, but he's dead. Off with the traitor's head,","And rear it in the place your father's stands.—","And now to London with triumphant march,","There to be crowned England's royal king;","From whence shall Warwick cut the sea to France,","And ask the Lady Bona for thy queen.","So shalt thou sinew both these lands together,","And, having France thy friend, thou shalt not dread","The scatt'red foe that hopes to rise again;","For though they cannot greatly sting to hurt,","Yet look to have them buzz to offend thine ears.","First will I see the coronation,","And then to Brittany I'll cross the sea","To effect this marriage, so it please my lord."],"id":408,"m":"Yes, but he's dead. Off with the traitor's head, and put it up where your father's is. And now to London in a triumphant march, to be crowned England's royal king. From there Warwick will cross the sea to France and ask for Lady Bona as your queen. That way you'll bind both countries together, and with France as your friend you won't have to fear the scattered enemy who hope to rise again; for though they can't sting hard enough to hurt you, expect them to buzz in your ears and annoy you. First I'll see the coronation, and then I'll cross the sea to Brittany to arrange this marriage, if my lord agrees."},{"t":"sp","who":"EDWARD","lines":["Even as thou wilt, sweet Warwick, let it be;","For in thy shoulder do I build my seat,","And never will I undertake the thing","Wherein thy counsel and consent is wanting.—","Richard, I will create thee Duke of Gloster;—","And George, of Clarence.—Warwick, as ourself,","Shall do and undo as him pleaseth best."],"id":409,"m":"Let it be just as you wish, sweet Warwick; for I build my throne on your shoulder, and I'll never undertake anything without your advice and consent. Richard, I'll make you Duke of Gloucester, and George, Duke of Clarence. Warwick, like ourself, will do and undo whatever he thinks best."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Let me be Duke of Clarence, George of Gloster,","For Gloster's dukedom is too ominous."],"id":410,"m":"Let me be Duke of Clarence and George Duke of Gloucester, for the dukedom of Gloucester is too unlucky."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Tut! that's a foolish observation;","Richard, be Duke of Gloster. Now to London,","To see these honours in possession."],"id":411,"m":"Nonsense! That's a foolish remark. Richard, be Duke of Gloucester. Now to London, to take possession of these honours."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":412},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"I","loc":"A Forest in the North of England.","id":413},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter two Keepers, with crossbows in their hands.","id":414},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST KEEPER","lines":["Under this thick-grown brake we'll shroud ourselves,","For through this laund anon the deer will come;","And in this covert will we make our stand,","Culling the principal of all the deer."],"id":415,"m":"We'll hide under this thick undergrowth, because the deer will come through this clearing soon; we'll take our position in this cover and pick out the best of the herd."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND KEEPER","lines":["I'll stay above the hill, so both may shoot."],"id":416,"m":"I'll stay up on the hill, so we can both shoot."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST KEEPER","lines":["That cannot be; the noise of thy crossbow","Will scare the herd, and so my shoot is lost.","Here stand we both, and aim we at the best;","And, for the time shall not seem tedious,","I'll tell thee what befell me on a day","In this self place where now we mean to stand."],"id":417,"m":"That won't work; the noise of your crossbow will scare the herd and my shot will be wasted. Let's both stand here and aim at the best one; and so the time doesn't drag, I'll tell you what happened to me one day in this very spot where we're about to stand."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND KEEPER","lines":["Here comes a man; let's stay till he be past."],"id":418,"m":"Here comes a man; let's wait until he's gone past."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter KING HENRY, disguised, with a prayer-book.","id":419},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["From Scotland am I stolen, even of pure love,","To greet mine own land with my wishful sight.","No, Harry, Harry, 't is no land of thine;","Thy place is fill'd, thy sceptre wrung from thee,","Thy balm wash'd off wherewith thou wast anointed.","No bending knee will call thee Caesar now,","No humble suitors press to speak for right;","No, not a man comes for redress of thee,","For how can I help them, and not myself?"],"id":420,"m":"I've slipped away from Scotland, purely out of love, to greet my own country with longing eyes. No, Harry, Harry, it's not your country; your place is taken, your sceptre wrenched from you, the holy oil you were anointed with washed off. No bending knee will call you Caesar now, no humble petitioners crowd to ask for justice; no, not one man comes to you for help, for how can I help them when I can't help myself?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST KEEPER","lines":["Ay, here's a deer whose skin's a keeper's fee.","This is the quondam king; let's seize upon him."],"id":421,"m":"Yes, here's a deer whose skin is a gamekeeper's prize. This is the former king; let's seize him."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Let me embrace thee, sour adversity;","For wise men say it is the wisest course."],"id":422,"m":"Let me embrace you, bitter adversity, for wise men say that's the wisest course."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND KEEPER","lines":["Why linger we? let us lay hands upon him."],"id":423,"m":"Why are we waiting? Let's grab him."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST KEEPER","lines":["Forbear awhile; we'll hear a little more."],"id":424,"m":"Hold on a moment; we'll hear a bit more."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["My queen and son are gone to France for aid;","And, as I hear, the great commanding Warwick","Is thither gone to crave the French king's sister","To wife for Edward. If this news be true,","Poor queen and son, your labour is but lost,","For Warwick is a subtle orator,","And Lewis a prince soon won with moving words.","By this account then Margaret may win him,","For she's a woman to be pitied much.","Her sighs will make a batt'ry in his breast,","Her tears will pierce into a marble heart;","The tiger will be mild whiles she doth mourn,","And Nero will be tainted with remorse","To hear and see her plaints, her brinish tears.","Ay, but she's come to beg, Warwick to give;","She on his left side craving aid for Henry,","He on his right asking a wife for Edward.","She weeps and says her Henry is depos'd,","He smiles and says his Edward is install'd;","That she, poor wretch, for grief can speak no more;","Whiles Warwick tells his title, smooths the wrong,","Inferreth arguments of mighty strength,","And, in conclusion, wins the king from her,","With promise of his sister, and what else,","To strengthen and support King Edward's place.","O Margaret, thus 't will be! and thou, poor soul,","Art then forsaken, as thou went'st forlorn!"],"id":425,"m":"My queen and son have gone to France for help; and I hear the great, commanding Warwick has gone there to ask for the French king's sister as a wife for Edward. If this news is true, poor queen and son, your effort is wasted, for Warwick is a clever speaker and Lewis is a prince easily won by moving words. Then again, by this reckoning Margaret may win him, for she's a woman to be greatly pitied. Her sighs will batter at his heart, her tears will pierce a heart of marble; the tiger will be gentle while she mourns, and Nero would be touched with pity to hear her complaints and see her salt tears. Yes, but she's come to beg, and Warwick to give: she on his left side begging help for Henry, he on his right asking a wife for Edward. She weeps and says her Henry is deposed; he smiles and says his Edward is crowned; so that she, poor wretch, can't speak any more for grief, while Warwick explains Edward's claim, smooths over the wrong, offers powerful arguments, and in the end wins the king away from her with the promise of his sister and whatever else will strengthen and support King Edward's position. O Margaret, that's how it will be! And you, poor soul, will be abandoned, just as you went, hopeless!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND KEEPER","lines":["Say, what art thou, that talk'st of kings and queens?"],"id":426,"m":"Tell me, who are you, talking about kings and queens?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["More than I seem, and less than I was born to;","A man at least, for less I should not be;","And men may talk of kings, and why not I?"],"id":427,"m":"More than I seem, and less than I was born to be; a man at least, for I can't be less; and men may talk about kings, so why not me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND KEEPER","lines":["Ay, but thou talk'st as if thou wert a king."],"id":428,"m":"Yes, but you talk as if you were a king."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Why, so I am, in mind; and that's enough."],"id":429,"m":"Well, so I am, in my mind, and that's enough."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND KEEPER","lines":["But, if thou be a king, where is thy crown?"],"id":430,"m":"But if you're a king, where's your crown?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["My crown is in my heart, not on my head,","Not deck'd with diamonds and Indian stones,","Not to be seen; my crown is call'd content,","A crown it is that seldom kings enjoy."],"id":431,"m":"My crown is in my heart, not on my head; not decorated with diamonds and Indian gems, not visible. My crown is called contentment, a crown that kings seldom enjoy."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND KEEPER","lines":["Well, if you be a king crown'd with content,","Your crown content and you must be contented","To go along with us; for, as we think,","You are the king King Edward hath depos'd,","And we his subjects, sworn in all allegiance,","Will apprehend you as his enemy."],"id":432,"m":"Well, if you're a king crowned with contentment, your crown, contentment, and you must be content to come with us; for we think you're the king King Edward has deposed, and we, his subjects, sworn to loyalty, will arrest you as his enemy."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["But did you never swear, and break an oath?"],"id":433,"m":"But have you never sworn an oath and broken it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND KEEPER","lines":["No, never such an oath; nor will not now."],"id":434,"m":"No, never an oath like that; and we won't now."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Where did you dwell when I was King of England?"],"id":435,"m":"Where did you live when I was King of England?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND KEEPER","lines":["Here in this country, where we now remain."],"id":436,"m":"Here in this region, where we live now."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["I was anointed king at nine months old,","My father and my grandfather were kings,","And you were sworn true subjects unto me;","And tell me, then, have you not broke your oaths?"],"id":437,"m":"I was anointed king at nine months old; my father and grandfather were kings; and you swore to be my true subjects. So tell me, haven't you broken your oaths?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST KEEPER","lines":["No;","For we were subjects but while you were king."],"id":438,"m":"No; we were your subjects only while you were king."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Why, am I dead? do I not breathe, a man?","Ah, simple men! you know not what you swear.","Look, as I blow this feather from my face,","And as the air blows it to me again,","Obeying with my wind when I do blow,","And yielding to another when it blows,","Commanded always by the greater gust,","Such is the lightness of you common men.","But do not break your oaths; for of that sin","My mild entreaty shall not make you guilty.","Go where you will, the king shall be commanded;","And be you kings, command, and I'll obey."],"id":439,"m":"Why, am I dead? Don't I still breathe, as a man? Ah, simple men! You don't know what you're swearing. Look, as I blow this feather away from my face and the air blows it back to me, obeying my breath when I blow and giving way to another when that blows, always ruled by the stronger gust: that's how fickle you common people are. But don't break your oaths; my gentle plea won't make you guilty of that sin. Go where you like; the king will obey orders. You be kings: command, and I'll obey."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST KEEPER","lines":["We are true subjects to the king,—King Edward."],"id":440,"m":"We're true subjects of the king, King Edward."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["So would you be again to Henry","If he were seated as King Edward is."],"id":441,"m":"And so you'd be again to Henry, if he were on the throne as King Edward is."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST KEEPER","lines":["We charge you, in God's name and the king's","To go with us unto the officers."],"id":442,"m":"We order you, in God's name and the king's, to come with us to the officers."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["In God's name lead; your king's name be obey'd;","And what God will, that let your king perform;","And what he will, I humbly yield unto."],"id":443,"m":"Lead on in God's name; let your king's name be obeyed. Let your king do what God wants, and I humbly submit to what he wants."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":444},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"II","loc":"The palace.","id":445},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter KING EDWARD, GLOSTER, CLARENCE, and LADY GREY.","id":446},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Brother of Gloster, at Saint Alban's field","This lady's husband, Sir John Grey, was slain,","His land then seiz'd on by the conqueror;","Her suit is now to repossess those lands,","Which we in justice cannot well deny,","Because in quarrel of the house of York","The worthy gentleman did lose his life."],"id":447,"m":"Brother Gloucester, this lady's husband, Sir John Grey, was killed at the battle of St Albans, and his land was then seized by the victor. She is now asking to have those lands back, which in fairness we can hardly refuse, since the good gentleman lost his life fighting for the house of York."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Your highness shall do well to grant her suit;","It were dishonour to deny it her."],"id":448,"m":"Your highness would do well to grant her request; it would be dishonourable to refuse."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["It were no less; but yet I'll make a pause."],"id":449,"m":"It would indeed; but still, I'll take my time."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["[Aside to Clarence] Yea; is it so?","I see the lady hath a thing to grant","Before the king will grant her humble suit."],"id":450,"m":"(Aside to Clarence) Oh, is that how it is? I see the lady has something to grant before the king will grant her humble request."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["[Aside to Gloster] He knows the game;","how true he keeps the wind!"],"id":451,"m":"(Aside to Gloucester) He knows the hunt; see how he keeps downwind of his prey!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["[Aside to Clarence] Silence!"],"id":452,"m":"(Aside to Clarence) Quiet!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Widow, we will consider of your suit,","And come some other time to know our mind."],"id":453,"m":"Widow, we'll consider your request; come back another time to hear our decision."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY GREY","lines":["Right gracious lord, I cannot brook delay;","May it please your highness to resolve me now,","And what your pleasure is shall satisfy me."],"id":454,"m":"Most gracious lord, I can't bear delay. Please decide now, your highness, and whatever pleases you will satisfy me."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["[Aside to Clarence] Ay, widow?","then I'll warrant you all your lands,","An if what pleases him shall pleasure you.","Fight closer, or, good faith, you'll catch a blow."],"id":455,"m":"(Aside to Clarence) Oh, will it, widow? Then I'll guarantee you all your lands, if what pleases him gives you pleasure. Guard yourself closer, or, believe me, you'll take a hit."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["[Aside to Gloster] I fear her not, unless she chance to fall."],"id":456,"m":"(Aside to Gloucester) I'm not worried for her, unless she happens to fall."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["[Aside to CLARENCE] God forbid that, for he'll take vantages."],"id":457,"m":"(Aside to Clarence) God forbid! He'll take advantage."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["How many children hast thou, widow? tell me."],"id":458,"m":"How many children do you have, widow? Tell me."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["[Aside to Gloster] I think he means to beg a child of her."],"id":459,"m":"(Aside to Gloucester) I think he means to ask her for a child."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["[Aside to Clarence] Nay, whip me then; he'll rather","give her two."],"id":460,"m":"(Aside to Clarence) No, whip me if he does; he'd rather give her two."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY GREY","lines":["Three, my most gracious lord."],"id":461,"m":"Three, my most gracious lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["[Aside to Clarence] You shall have four if you'll be","rul'd by him."],"id":462,"m":"(Aside to Clarence) You'll have four if you do as he says."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["'T were pity they should lose their father's lands."],"id":463,"m":"It would be a pity if they lost their father's lands."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY GREY","lines":["Be pitiful, dread lord, and grant it then."],"id":464,"m":"Then be merciful, dread lord, and grant them."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Lords, give us leave; I'll try this widow's wit."],"id":465,"m":"Lords, leave us alone; I'll test this widow's wits."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["[Aside to Clarence] Ay, good leave have you;","for you will have leave","Till youth take leave and leave you to the crutch."],"id":466,"m":"(Aside to Clarence) Yes, take your leave; you'll take liberties until youth takes leave of you and leaves you to your crutch."},{"t":"sd","text":"Gloster and Clarence stand apart.","id":467},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Now tell me, madam, do you love your children?"],"id":468,"m":"Now tell me, madam, do you love your children?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY GREY","lines":["Ay, full as dearly as I love myself."],"id":469,"m":"Yes, as dearly as I love myself."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["And would you not do much to do them good?"],"id":470,"m":"And wouldn't you do a lot to do them good?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY GREY","lines":["To do them good I would sustain some harm."],"id":471,"m":"To do them good I'd put up with some harm."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Then get your husband's lands to do them good."],"id":472,"m":"Then get your husband's lands back to do them good."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY GREY","lines":["Therefore I came unto your majesty."],"id":473,"m":"That's why I came to your majesty."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["I'll tell you how these lands are to be got."],"id":474,"m":"I'll tell you how these lands can be got."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY GREY","lines":["So shall you bind me to your highness' service."],"id":475,"m":"Then you'll bind me to your highness's service."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["What service wilt thou do me if I give them?"],"id":476,"m":"What service will you do me if I give them to you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY GREY","lines":["What you command that rests in me to do."],"id":477,"m":"Whatever you command that's in my power to do."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["But you will take exceptions to my boon."],"id":478,"m":"But you'll object to what I ask."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY GREY","lines":["No, gracious lord, except I cannot do it."],"id":479,"m":"No, gracious lord, unless I can't do it."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Ay, but thou canst do what I mean to ask."],"id":480,"m":"Yes, but you can do what I mean to ask."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY GREY","lines":["Why, then, I will do what your grace commands."],"id":481,"m":"Why, then, I'll do what your grace commands."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["He plies her hard; and much rain wears the marble."],"id":482,"m":"He's pressing her hard; and a lot of rain wears down marble."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["As red as fire! nay, then her wax must melt."],"id":483,"m":"She's as red as fire! Well, then her wax must melt."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY GREY","lines":["Why stops my lord? shall I not hear my task?"],"id":484,"m":"Why does my lord stop? Won't I hear my task?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["An easy task; 't is but to love a king."],"id":485,"m":"An easy task: it's only to love a king."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY GREY","lines":["That's soon perform'd, because I am a subject."],"id":486,"m":"That's easily done, since I'm his subject."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Why, then, thy husband's lands I freely give thee."],"id":487,"m":"Why, then, I freely give you your husband's lands."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY GREY","lines":["I take my leave with many thousand thanks."],"id":488,"m":"I take my leave with many thousand thanks."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["The match is made; she seals it with a curtsy."],"id":489,"m":"The deal is done; she seals it with a curtsy."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["But stay thee; 't is the fruits of love I mean."],"id":490,"m":"But wait; it's the fruits of love I mean."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY GREY","lines":["The fruits of love I mean, my loving liege."],"id":491,"m":"I mean the fruits of love, my loving lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Ay, but, I fear me, in another sense.","What love, thinkst thou, I sue so much to get?"],"id":492,"m":"Yes, but I'm afraid in a different sense. What love do you think I'm so eager to get?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY GREY","lines":["My love till death, my humble thanks, my prayers;","That love which virtue begs, and virtue grants."],"id":493,"m":"My love till death, my humble thanks, my prayers; the love that virtue asks for and virtue gives."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["No, by my troth, I did not mean such love."],"id":494,"m":"No, I swear, I didn't mean that kind of love."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY GREY","lines":["Why, then, you mean not as I thought you did."],"id":495,"m":"Why, then, you don't mean what I thought you did."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["But now you partly may perceive my mind."],"id":496,"m":"But now you may partly understand what I have in mind."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY GREY","lines":["My mind will never grant what I perceive","Your Highness aims at, if I aim aright."],"id":497,"m":"My mind will never agree to what I see your highness is aiming at, if I've guessed right."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["To tell thee plain, I aim to lie with thee."],"id":498,"m":"To put it plainly, I want to sleep with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY GREY","lines":["To tell you plain, I had rather lie in prison."],"id":499,"m":"To put it plainly, I'd rather sleep in prison."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Why, then thou shalt not have thy husband's lands."],"id":500,"m":"Why, then you won't have your husband's lands."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY GREY","lines":["Why, then mine honesty shall be my dower,","For by that loss I will not purchase them."],"id":501,"m":"Why, then my chastity will be my dowry, for I won't buy them by losing it."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Therein thou wrong'st thy children mightily."],"id":502,"m":"In that you do your children a great wrong."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY GREY","lines":["Herein your highness wrongs both them and me.","But, mighty lord, this merry inclination","Accords not with the sadness of my suit;","Please you dismiss me either with ay or no."],"id":503,"m":"In this your highness wrongs both them and me. But, mighty lord, this playful mood doesn't suit the seriousness of my request. Please dismiss me with either yes or no."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Ay, if thou wilt say ay to my request.","No, if thou dost say no to my demand."],"id":504,"m":"Yes, if you'll say yes to my request; no, if you say no to my demand."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY GREY","lines":["Then no, my lord. My suit is at an end."],"id":505,"m":"Then no, my lord. My suit is finished."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["The widow likes him not, she knits her brows."],"id":506,"m":"The widow doesn't like him; she's frowning."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["He is the bluntest wooer in Christendom."],"id":507,"m":"He's the bluntest wooer in Christendom."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["[Aside] Her looks doth argue her replete with modesty,","Her words doth show her wit incomparable,","All her perfections challenge sovereignty;","One way or other she is for a king,","And she shall be my love, or else my queen.—","Say that King Edward take thee for his queen?"],"id":508,"m":"(Aside) Her looks show she's full of modesty; her words show her wit is beyond compare; all her perfections claim the right to rule. One way or another she's fit for a king, and she'll be my mistress or else my queen. (Aloud) What if King Edward took you as his queen?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY GREY","lines":["'T is better said than done, my gracious lord;","I am a subject fit to jest withal,","But far unfit to be a sovereign."],"id":509,"m":"That's easier said than done, my gracious lord. I'm a subject fit to joke with, but quite unfit to be a sovereign."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Sweet widow, by my state I swear to thee,","I speak no more than what my soul intends;","And that is to enjoy thee for my love."],"id":510,"m":"Sweet widow, I swear to you by my crown that I mean exactly what I say, which is to have you as my love."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY GREY","lines":["And that is more than I will yield unto.","I know I am too mean to be your queen,","And yet too good to be your concubine."],"id":511,"m":"And that is more than I'll agree to. I know I'm too lowly to be your queen, and yet too good to be your mistress."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["You cavil, widow; I did mean my queen."],"id":512,"m":"You're quibbling, widow; I meant my queen."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY GREY","lines":["'T will grieve your grace my sons should call you father."],"id":513,"m":"It would upset your grace to have my sons call you father."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["No more than when my daughters call thee mother.","Thou art a widow, and thou hast some children;","And, by God's mother, I, being but a bachelor,","Have other some; why, 't is a happy thing","To be the father unto many sons.","Answer no more, for thou shalt be my queen."],"id":514,"m":"No more than it will upset you when my daughters call you mother. You're a widow and you have some children; and, by God's mother, I, though only a bachelor, have some others. Why, it's a happy thing to be father to many sons. Say no more, for you will be my queen."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["The ghostly father now hath done his shrift."],"id":515,"m":"The father confessor has finished hearing her confession."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["When he was made a shriver, 't was for shift."],"id":516,"m":"When he became a confessor, it was for a trick, to get her out of her shift."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Brothers, you muse what chat we two have had."],"id":517,"m":"Brothers, you're wondering what we two have been talking about."},{"t":"sd","text":"Gloster and Clarence come forward.","id":518},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["The widow likes it not, for she looks very sad."],"id":519,"m":"The widow doesn't like it; she looks very sad."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["You'd think it strange if I should marry her."],"id":520,"m":"You'd think it strange if I married her."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["To whom, my lord?"],"id":521,"m":"To whom, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Why, Clarence, to myself."],"id":522,"m":"Why, Clarence, to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["That would be ten days' wonder, at the least."],"id":523,"m":"That would be a ten days' wonder at least."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["That's a day longer than a wonder lasts."],"id":524,"m":"That's a day longer than a wonder usually lasts."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["By so much is the wonder in extremes."],"id":525,"m":"That just shows how extreme the wonder is."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Well, jest on, brothers; I can tell you both,","Her suit is granted for her husband's lands."],"id":526,"m":"Well, keep joking, brothers. I can tell you both her request for her husband's lands is granted."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Nobleman.","id":527},{"t":"sp","who":"NOBLEMAN","lines":["My gracious lord, Henry your foe is taken,","And brought your prisoner to your palace gate."],"id":528,"m":"My gracious lord, your enemy Henry has been captured and brought as your prisoner to your palace gate."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["See that he be convey'd unto the Tower.—","And go we, brothers, to the man that took him,","To question of his apprehension.—","Widow, go you along.—Lords, use her honourably."],"id":529,"m":"See that he's taken to the Tower. And let's go, brothers, to the man who captured him, to ask him about the arrest. Widow, you come along. Lords, treat her with honour."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt King Edward, Lady Grey, Clarence, and Nobleman.","id":530},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Ay, Edward will use women honourably.","Would he were wasted, marrow, bones, and all,","That from his loins no hopeful branch may spring,","To cross me from the golden time I look for!","And yet, between my soul's desire and me—","The lustful Edward's title buried—","Is Clarence, Henry, and his son young Edward,","And all the unlook'd-for issue of their bodies,","To take their rooms ere I can place myself;","A cold premeditation for my purpose!","Why, then I do but dream on sovereignty,","Like one that stands upon a promontory,","And spies a far-off shore where he would tread,","Wishing his foot were equal with his eye,","And chides the sea that sunders him from thence,","Saying, he'll lade it dry to have his way.","So do I wish the crown, being so far off,","And so I chide the means that keeps me from it;","And so I say I'll cut the causes off,","Flattering me with impossibilities.—","My eye's too quick, my heart o'erweens too much,","Unless my hand and strength could equal them.","Well, say there is no kingdom then for Richard,","What other pleasure can the world afford?","I'll make my heaven in a lady's lap,","And deck my body in gay ornaments,","And witch sweet ladies with my words and looks.","O miserable thought! and more unlikely","Than to accomplish twenty golden crowns.","Why, love forswore me in my mother's womb;","And, for I should not deal in her soft laws,","She did corrupt frail nature with some bribe","To shrink mine arm up like a wither'd shrub;","To make an envious mountain on my back,","Where sits deformity to mock my body;","To shape my legs of an unequal size;","To disproportion me in every part,","Like to a chaos, or an unlick'd bear-whelp","That carries no impression like the dam.","And am I then a man to be belov'd?","O, monstrous fault, to harbour such a thought!","Then, since this earth affords no joy to me","But to command, to check, to o'erbear such","As are of better person than myself,","I'll make my heaven to dream upon the crown,","And, whiles I live, to account this world but hell","Until my mis-shap'd trunk that bear this head","Be round impaled with a glorious crown.","And yet I know not how to get the crown,","For many lives stand between me and home,","And I, like one lost in a thorny wood,","That rends the thorns, and is rent with the thorns,","Seeking a way, and straying from the way,","Not knowing how to find the open air,","But toiling desperately to find it out,","Torment myself to catch the English crown;","And from that torment I will free myself,","Or hew my way out with a bloody axe.","Why, I can smile, and murther while I smile,","And cry 'Content!' to that which grieves my heart,","And wet my cheeks with artificial tears,","And frame my face to all occasions.","I'll drown more sailors than the mermaid shall,","I'll slay more gazers than the basilisk;","I'll play the orator as well as Nestor,","Deceive more slyly than Ulysses could,","And like a Sinon take another Troy.","I can add colours to the chameleon,","Change shapes with Protheus for advantages,","And set the murtherous Machiavel to school.","Can I do this, and cannot get a crown?","Tut, were it farther off, I'll pluck it down."],"id":531,"m":"Yes, Edward will treat women honourably. I wish he were wasted away, marrow, bones and all, so that no promising offspring could spring from his loins to block my way to the golden time I'm hoping for! And yet, between my soul's desire and me, even with lustful Edward's claim buried, stand Clarence, Henry and his son young Edward, and all the unexpected children they may have, to take their places before I can take mine. A chilling forecast for my plans! Why, then I'm only dreaming of the crown, like someone standing on a headland who spots a distant shore he'd like to walk on, wishes his feet could go as far as his eyes, and scolds the sea that separates him from it, saying he'll bail it dry to get his way. That's how I long for the crown, so far away; that's how I scold whatever keeps me from it; and that's how I say I'll cut away the obstacles, flattering myself with impossibilities. My eye is too quick and my heart too ambitious unless my hand and strength can match them. Well, suppose there's no kingdom for Richard; what other pleasure can the world give? I'll make my heaven in a lady's lap, dress myself in fine clothes and bewitch sweet ladies with my words and looks. O miserable thought, and less likely than winning twenty golden crowns! Why, love rejected me in my mother's womb; and so that I'd have nothing to do with her gentle laws, she bribed weak nature to shrivel my arm like a withered shrub, to build a spiteful mountain on my back where deformity sits to mock my body, to shape my legs unequal in size, to put me out of proportion in every part, like chaos, or an unlicked bear cub that doesn't take after its mother. So am I a man to be loved? O monstrous mistake, even to think it! Then, since this earth gives me no joy except to command, rebuke and overpower people better-looking than me, I'll make my heaven dreaming of the crown, and as long as I live I'll count this world nothing but hell until the misshapen body that carries this head is circled with a glorious crown. And yet I don't know how to get the crown, for many lives stand between me and my goal; and like someone lost in a thorny wood, tearing at the thorns and torn by them, looking for a path and straying from it, not knowing how to reach open air but struggling desperately to find it, I torment myself to catch the English crown. And I'll free myself from that torment or hack my way out with a bloody axe. Why, I can smile, and murder while I smile, and cry 'Wonderful!' at what grieves my heart, and wet my cheeks with fake tears, and fit my face to any occasion. I'll drown more sailors than the mermaid, kill more onlookers than the basilisk; I'll speak as well as Nestor, deceive more slyly than Ulysses, and take another Troy like Sinon. I can add colours to the chameleon, change shape with Proteus when it suits me, and teach the murderous Machiavelli a lesson. Can I do all this and not get a crown? Tut, even if it were further off, I'll pull it down."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":532},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"III","loc":"France. The King's Palace.","id":533},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. Enter LEWIS, the French King, and LADY BONA, attended: the King takes his state. Then enter QUEEN MARGARET, PRINCE EDWARD, and the EARL OF OXFORD; LEWIS rising as she enters.","id":534},{"t":"sp","who":"KING LEWIS","lines":["Fair Queen of England, worthy Margaret,","Sit down with us; it ill befits thy state","And birth that thou shouldst stand while Lewis doth sit."],"id":535,"m":"Fair Queen of England, noble Margaret, sit down with us. It doesn't suit your rank and birth that you should stand while Lewis sits."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["No, mighty King of France; now Margaret","Must strike her sail and learn a while to serve","Where kings command. I was, I must confess,","Great Albion's queen in former golden days;","But now mischance hath trod my title down","And with dishonour laid me on the ground,","Where I must take like seat unto my fortune,","And to my humble seat conform myself."],"id":536,"m":"No, mighty King of France; now Margaret must lower her sails and learn to serve for a while where kings command. I confess I was queen of great England in former golden days; but now misfortune has trampled my title and laid me on the ground in disgrace, where I must take a seat that fits my fortune and adapt myself to my humble place."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING LEWIS","lines":["Why, say, fair queen, whence springs this deep despair?"],"id":537,"m":"Tell me, fair queen, where does this deep despair come from?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["From such a cause as fills mine eyes with tears","And stops my tongue, while heart is drown'd in cares."],"id":538,"m":"From a cause that fills my eyes with tears and stops my tongue, while my heart is drowned in troubles."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING LEWIS","lines":["Whate'er it be, be thou still like thyself,","And sit thee by our side; yield not thy neck"],"id":539,"m":"Whatever it is, still be yourself, and sit beside us; don't bow your neck"},{"t":"sd","text":"Seats her by him.","id":540},{"t":"sp","who":"KING LEWIS","cont":1,"lines":["To fortune's yoke, but let thy dauntless mind","Still ride in triumph over all mischance.","Be plain, Queen Margaret, and tell thy grief;","It shall be eas'd if France can yield relief."],"id":541,"m":"to fortune's yoke, but let your fearless mind still ride in triumph over every misfortune. Speak plainly, Queen Margaret, and tell me your grief; it will be eased if France can give relief."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Those gracious words revive my drooping thoughts,","And give my tongue-tied sorrows leave to speak.","Now, therefore, be it known to noble Lewis","That Henry, sole possessor of my love,","Is of a king become a banish'd man","And forc'd to live in Scotland a forlorn,","While proud ambitious Edward, Duke of York,","Usurps the regal title and the seat","Of England's true-anointed lawful king.","This is the cause that I, poor Margaret,","With this my son, Prince Edward, Henry's heir,","Am come to crave thy just and lawful aid;","And if thou fail us, all our hope is done.","Scotland hath will to help, but cannot help;","Our people and our peers are both misled,","Our treasure seiz'd, our soldiers put to flight,","And, as thou seest, ourselves in heavy plight."],"id":542,"m":"Those kind words revive my drooping spirits and let my tongue-tied sorrows speak. So let noble Lewis know that Henry, the only owner of my love, has gone from being a king to being an exile, forced to live wretchedly in Scotland, while proud, ambitious Edward, Duke of York, usurps the royal title and throne of England's true, anointed, lawful king. That's why I, poor Margaret, have come with my son, Prince Edward, Henry's heir, to beg for your just and lawful help; and if you fail us, all our hope is finished. Scotland wants to help but can't; our people and our nobles are both misled, our treasure seized, our soldiers routed, and, as you see, we ourselves are in a sad state."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING LEWIS","lines":["Renowned queen, with patience calm the storm","While we bethink a means to break it off."],"id":543,"m":"Famous queen, calm the storm with patience while we think of a way to end it."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["The more we stay, the stronger grows our foe."],"id":544,"m":"The longer we wait, the stronger our enemy grows."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING LEWIS","lines":["The more I stay, the more I'll succour thee."],"id":545,"m":"The longer I wait, the more I'll help you."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["O, but impatience waiteth on true sorrow!—","And see where comes the breeder of my sorrow."],"id":546,"m":"O, but impatience always goes with true sorrow! And look, here comes the cause of my sorrow."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter WARWICK, attended.","id":547},{"t":"sp","who":"KING LEWIS","lines":["What's he approacheth boldly to our presence?"],"id":548,"m":"Who is this who comes so boldly into our presence?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Our Earl of Warwick, Edward's greatest friend."],"id":549,"m":"Our Earl of Warwick, Edward's greatest friend."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING LEWIS","lines":["Welcome, brave Warwick. What brings thee to France?"],"id":550,"m":"Welcome, brave Warwick. What brings you to France?"},{"t":"sd","text":"He descends. Queen Margaret rises.","id":551},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Ay, now begins a second storm to rise,","For this is he that moves both wind and tide."],"id":552,"m":"Yes, now a second storm begins to rise, for this is the man who moves both wind and tide."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["From worthy Edward, king of Albion,","My lord and sovereign, and thy vowed friend,","I come, in kindness and unfeigned love,","First, to do greetings to thy royal person;","And then, to crave a league of amity;","And lastly, to confirm that amity","With nuptial knot, if thou vouchsafe to grant","That virtuous Lady Bona, thy fair sister,","To England's king in lawful marriage."],"id":553,"m":"I come from noble Edward, King of England, my lord and sovereign and your sworn friend, in kindness and sincere love: first to greet your royal person; then to ask for a treaty of friendship; and lastly to confirm that friendship with a marriage, if you'll agree to give the virtuous Lady Bona, your fair sister, to England's king in lawful marriage."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["[Aside] If that go forward, Henry's hope is done."],"id":554,"m":"(Aside) If that goes ahead, Henry's hope is finished."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["[To BONA] And, gracious madam, in our king's behalf,","I am commanded, with your leave and favour,","Humbly to kiss your hand, and with my tongue","To tell the passion of my sovereign's heart,","Where fame, late entering at his heedful ears,","Hath plac'd thy beauty's image and thy virtue."],"id":555,"m":"(To Bona) And, gracious madam, on our king's behalf I'm ordered, with your permission, to kiss your hand humbly and tell you of the love in my sovereign's heart, where report, lately reaching his attentive ears, has placed the image of your beauty and virtue."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["King Lewis,—and Lady Bona,—hear me speak","Before you answer Warwick. His demand","Springs not from Edward's well-meant honest love,","But from deceit, bred by necessity;","For how can tyrants safely govern home","Unless abroad they purchase great alliance?","To prove him tyrant this reason may suffice,—","That Henry liveth still; but were he dead,","Yet here Prince Edward stands, King Henry's son.","Look therefore, Lewis, that by this league and marriage","Thou draw not on thy danger and dishonour;","For though usurpers sway the rule awhile,","Yet heavens are just, and time suppresseth wrongs."],"id":556,"m":"King Lewis, and Lady Bona, hear me before you answer Warwick. His request doesn't come from Edward's honest, well-meant love, but from deceit born of need; for how can tyrants rule safely at home unless they buy powerful alliances abroad? This reason is enough to prove he's a tyrant: Henry is still alive; and even if he were dead, here stands Prince Edward, King Henry's son. So take care, Lewis, that by this treaty and marriage you don't bring danger and dishonour on yourself; for though usurpers may rule for a while, heaven is just and time puts down wrongs."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Injurious Margaret!"],"id":557,"m":"Insulting Margaret!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE","lines":["And why not queen?"],"id":558,"m":"And why not Queen Margaret?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Because thy father Henry did usurp,","And thou no more art prince than she is queen."],"id":559,"m":"Because your father Henry was a usurper, and you're no more a prince than she's a queen."},{"t":"sp","who":"OXFORD","lines":["Then Warwick disannuls great John of Gaunt,","Which did subdue the greatest part of Spain;","And, after John of Gaunt, Henry the Fourth,","Whose wisdom was a mirror to the wisest;","And, after that wise prince, Henry the Fifth,","Who by his prowess conquered all France.","From these our Henry lineally descends."],"id":560,"m":"Then Warwick cancels out great John of Gaunt, who conquered most of Spain; and after John of Gaunt, Henry the Fourth, whose wisdom was a model for the wisest; and after that wise prince, Henry the Fifth, whose valour conquered all France. Our Henry is descended in a direct line from these."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Oxford, how haps it in this smooth discourse,","You told not how Henry the Sixth hath lost","All that which Henry the Fifth had gotten?","Methinks these peers of France should smile at that.","But for the rest, you tell a pedigree","Of threescore and two years,—a silly time","To make prescription for a kingdom's worth."],"id":561,"m":"Oxford, how is it that in this smooth speech you didn't mention how Henry the Sixth has lost everything Henry the Fifth won? I think these French lords would smile at that. As for the rest, you list a family tree of sixty-two years, a feeble length of time to establish a right to a kingdom."},{"t":"sp","who":"OXFORD","lines":["Why, Warwick, canst thou speak against thy liege,","Whom thou obeyedst thirty and six years,","And not bewray thy treason with a blush?"],"id":562,"m":"Why, Warwick, can you speak against your king, whom you obeyed for thirty-six years, without a blush giving away your treason?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Can Oxford, that did ever fence the right,","Now buckler falsehood with a pedigree?","For shame Leave Henry, and call Edward king."],"id":563,"m":"Can Oxford, who always defended what was right, now shield a lie with a family tree? For shame! Leave Henry and call Edward king."},{"t":"sp","who":"OXFORD","lines":["Call him my king by whose injurious doom","My elder brother, the Lord Aubrey Vere,","Was done to death? and more than so, my father,","Even in the downfall of his mellow'd years,","When nature brought him to the door of death?","No, Warwick, no; while life upholds this arm,","This arm upholds the house of Lancaster."],"id":564,"m":"Call him my king, by whose unjust sentence my elder brother, Lord Aubrey Vere, was put to death? And worse than that, my father too, in the decline of his ripe old age, when nature had brought him to death's door? No, Warwick, no; while there's life in this arm, this arm supports the house of Lancaster."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["And I the house of York."],"id":565,"m":"And I the house of York."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING LEWIS","lines":["Queen Margaret, Prince Edward, and Oxford,","Vouchsafe at our request to stand aside","While I use further conference with Warwick."],"id":566,"m":"Queen Margaret, Prince Edward and Oxford, please stand aside at our request while I talk further with Warwick."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Heavens grant that Warwick's words bewitch him not!"],"id":567,"m":"Heaven grant that Warwick's words don't bewitch him!"},{"t":"sd","text":"They stand aloof.","id":568},{"t":"sp","who":"KING LEWIS","lines":["Now, Warwick, tell me, even upon thy conscience,","Is Edward your true king? for I were loath","To link with him that were not lawful chosen."],"id":569,"m":"Now, Warwick, tell me honestly, on your conscience: is Edward your true king? I'd hate to ally myself with someone who wasn't lawfully chosen."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Thereon I pawn my credit and mine honour."],"id":570,"m":"I stake my reputation and my honour on it."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING LEWIS","lines":["But is he gracious in the people's eye?"],"id":571,"m":"But is he popular with the people?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["The more that Henry was unfortunate."],"id":572,"m":"All the more because Henry was unlucky."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING LEWIS","lines":["Then further, all dissembling set aside,","Tell me for truth the measure of his love","Unto our sister Bona."],"id":573,"m":"Then, further, setting all pretence aside, tell me truly how much he loves our sister Bona."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Such it seems","As may beseem a monarch like himself.","Myself have often heard him say and swear","That this his love was an eternal plant,","Whereof the root was fix'd in virtue's ground,","The leaves and fruit maintain'd with beauty's sun,","Exempt from envy, but not from disdain,","Unless the Lady Bona quit his pain."],"id":574,"m":"His love seems to be what befits a monarch like him. I've often heard him say and swear that this love of his was an eternal plant, rooted in the ground of virtue, its leaves and fruit nourished by the sun of beauty, free from malice but not from scorn, unless Lady Bona relieves his pain."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING LEWIS","lines":["Now, sister, let us hear your firm resolve."],"id":575,"m":"Now, sister, let's hear your firm decision."},{"t":"sp","who":"BONA","lines":["Your grant or your denial shall be mine.","Yet I confess [to Warwick] that often ere this day,","When I have heard your king's desert recounted,","Mine ear hath tempted judgment to desire."],"id":576,"m":"Your consent or your refusal will be mine. (To Warwick) Yet I confess that often before today, when I've heard your king's merits described, my ear has tempted my judgment to desire him."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING LEWIS","lines":["Then, Warwick, thus: our sister shall be Edward's;","And now forthwith shall articles be drawn","Touching the jointure that your king must make,","Which with her dowry shall be counterpois'd.—","Draw near, Queen Margaret, and be a witness","That Bona shall be wife to the English king."],"id":577,"m":"Then, Warwick, it's settled: our sister will be Edward's. And now the terms will be drawn up at once about the settlement your king must make, to be balanced against her dowry. Come here, Queen Margaret, and witness that Bona will be the wife of the English king."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE","lines":["To Edward, but not to the English king."],"id":578,"m":"Of Edward, but not of the English king."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Deceitful Warwick! it was thy device","By this alliance to make void my suit.","Before thy coming Lewis was Henry's friend."],"id":579,"m":"Treacherous Warwick! It was your plan to cancel my request with this alliance. Before you came, Lewis was Henry's friend."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING LEWIS","lines":["And still is friend to him and Margaret;","But if your title to the crown be weak,","As may appear by Edward's good success,","Then 't is but reason that I be releas'd","From giving aid which late I promised.","Yet shall you have all kindness at my hand","That your estate requires and mine can yield."],"id":580,"m":"And I'm still a friend to him and Margaret; but if your claim to the crown is weak, as Edward's success suggests, then it's only reasonable that I should be released from giving the help I recently promised. Still, you'll have every kindness from me that your position needs and mine can give."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Henry now lives in Scotland, at his ease,","Where, having nothing, nothing can he lose.","And as for you yourself, our quondam queen,","You have a father able to maintain you,","And better 't were you troubled him than France."],"id":581,"m":"Henry now lives comfortably in Scotland, where, having nothing, he can lose nothing. And as for you, our former queen, you have a father who can support you, and it would be better to trouble him than France."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Peace, impudent and shameless Warwick,","Proud setter-up and puller-down of kings!","I will not hence, till, with my talk and tears,","Both full of truth, I make King Lewis behold","Thy sly conveyance and thy lord's false love;","For both of you are birds of selfsame feather."],"id":582,"m":"Quiet, impudent, shameless Warwick, proud setter-up and puller-down of kings! I won't leave until, with words and tears both full of truth, I make King Lewis see your sly trickery and your lord's false love; for you're both birds of a feather."},{"t":"sd","text":"A horn sounded within.","id":583},{"t":"sp","who":"KING LEWIS","lines":["Warwick, this is some post to us or thee."],"id":584,"m":"Warwick, this is some messenger for us or for you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Post.","id":585},{"t":"sp","who":"POST","lines":["My lord ambassador, these letters are for you.","Sent from your brother Marquess Montague.—","These from our king unto your majesty.—","And, madam, these for you, from whom I know not."],"id":586,"m":"My lord ambassador, these letters are for you, sent from your brother, the Marquess of Montague. These are from our king to your majesty. And these, madam, are for you, from whom I don't know."},{"t":"sd","text":"They all read their letters.","id":587},{"t":"sp","who":"OXFORD","lines":["I like it well that our fair queen and mistress","Smiles at her news while Warwick frowns at his."],"id":588,"m":"I'm glad our fair queen and mistress smiles at her news while Warwick frowns at his."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE","lines":["Nay, mark how Lewis stamps as he were nettled;","I hope all's for the best."],"id":589,"m":"And look how Lewis stamps as if he'd been stung. I hope it's all for the best."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING LEWIS","lines":["Warwick, what are thy news?—and yours, fair queen?"],"id":590,"m":"Warwick, what's your news? And yours, fair queen?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Mine, such as fill my heart with unhop'd joys."],"id":591,"m":"Mine is the kind that fills my heart with joy I never hoped for."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Mine, full of sorrow and heart's discontent."],"id":592,"m":"Mine is full of sorrow and heartache."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING LEWIS","lines":["What! has your king married the Lady Grey,","And now, to soothe your forgery and his,","Sends me a paper to persuade me patience?","Is this the alliance that he seeks with France?","Dare he presume to scorn us in this manner?"],"id":593,"m":"What! Has your king married Lady Grey, and now, to cover up your deceit and his, sends me a note to persuade me to be patient? Is this the alliance he wants with France? Does he dare to mock us like this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["I told your majesty as much before;","This proveth Edward's love and Warwick's honesty."],"id":594,"m":"I told your majesty as much before. This proves Edward's love and Warwick's honesty."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["King Lewis, I here protest, in sight of heaven,","And by the hope I have of heavenly bliss,","That I am clear from this misdeed of Edward's;","No more my king, for he dishonours me,","But most himself, if he could see his shame.","Did I forget that by the house of York","My father came untimely to his death?","Did I let pass the abuse done to my niece?","Did I impale him with the regal crown?","Did I put Henry from his native right?","And am I guerdon'd at the last with shame?","Shame on himself! for my desert is honour;","And to repair my honour lost for him,","I here renounce him and return to Henry.—","My noble queen, let former grudges pass,","And henceforth I am thy true servitor.","I will revenge his wrong to Lady Bona,","And replant Henry in his former state."],"id":595,"m":"King Lewis, I declare here before heaven, and by my hope of heavenly bliss, that I'm innocent of Edward's wrongdoing. He's no longer my king, for he dishonours me, but himself most of all, if he could see his own shame. Did I forget that my father died before his time because of the house of York? Did I let the insult to my niece pass? Did I crown him with the royal crown? Did I put Henry out of his birthright? And am I rewarded in the end with shame? Shame on him! I deserve honour; and to repair the honour I've lost for his sake, I here renounce him and go back to Henry. My noble queen, let old grudges go; from now on I am your true servant. I'll avenge his wrong to Lady Bona and put Henry back in his former place."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Warwick, these words have turn'd my hate to love;","And I forgive and quite forget old faults,","And joy that thou becom'st King Henry's friend."],"id":596,"m":"Warwick, these words have turned my hate to love; I forgive and completely forget old offences, and I'm glad you're becoming King Henry's friend."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["So much his friend, ay, his unfeigned friend,","That if King Lewis vouchsafe to furnish us","With some few bands of chosen soldiers,","I'll undertake to land them on our coast","And force the tyrant from his seat by war.","'T is not his new-made bride shall succour him;","And as for Clarence,—as my letters tell me,—","He's very likely now to fall from him,","For matching more for wanton lust than honour,","Or than for strength and safety of our country."],"id":597,"m":"So much his friend, yes, his sincere friend, that if King Lewis will provide us with a few companies of picked soldiers, I'll undertake to land them on our coast and drive the tyrant from his throne by war. His new bride won't be able to help him; and as for Clarence, my letters tell me he's very likely to break with him now, for marrying out of lust rather than for honour or the strength and safety of our country."},{"t":"sp","who":"BONA","lines":["Dear brother, how shall Bona be reveng'd","But by thy help to this distressed queen?"],"id":598,"m":"Dear brother, how can Bona be avenged except by your helping this distressed queen?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Renowned prince, how shall poor Henry live","Unless thou rescue him from foul despair?"],"id":599,"m":"Famous prince, how can poor Henry live unless you rescue him from black despair?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BONA","lines":["My quarrel and this English queen's are one."],"id":600,"m":"My quarrel and this English queen's are one."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["And mine, fair Lady Bona, joins with yours."],"id":601,"m":"And mine, fair Lady Bona, joins with yours."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING LEWIS","lines":["And mine with hers, and thine, and Margaret's.","Therefore, at last, I firmly am resolv'd","You shall have aid."],"id":602,"m":"And mine with hers, and yours, and Margaret's. So at last I'm firmly decided: you'll have help."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Let me give humble thanks for all at once."],"id":603,"m":"Let me give humble thanks for everything at once."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING LEWIS","lines":["Then, England's messenger, return in post","And tell false Edward, thy supposed king,","That Lewis of France is sending over maskers","To revel it with him and his new bride.","Thou seest what's past; go fear thy king withal."],"id":604,"m":"Then, English messenger, go back at full speed and tell false Edward, your so-called king, that Lewis of France is sending over masked dancers to revel with him and his new bride. You've seen what has happened; go and frighten your king with it."},{"t":"sp","who":"BONA","lines":["Tell him, in hope he'll prove a widower shortly,","I'll wear the willow garland for his sake."],"id":605,"m":"Tell him that, in the hope he'll soon be a widower, I'll wear the willow garland of a jilted lover for his sake."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Tell him my mourning weeds are laid aside,","And I am ready to put armour on."],"id":606,"m":"Tell him my mourning clothes are put away and I'm ready to put on armour."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Tell him from me that he hath done me wrong,","And therefore I'll uncrown him ere 't be long.","There's thy reward; be gone."],"id":607,"m":"Tell him from me that he has wronged me, and so I'll uncrown him before long. There's your tip; be off."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Post.","id":608},{"t":"sp","who":"KING LEWIS","lines":["But, Warwick,","Thou and Oxford, with five thousand men,","Shall cross the seas and bid false Edward battle;","And, as occasion serves, this noble queen","And prince shall follow with a fresh supply.","Yet, ere thou go, but answer me one doubt:","What pledge have we of thy firm loyalty?"],"id":609,"m":"But, Warwick, you and Oxford, with five thousand men, will cross the sea and give false Edward battle; and when the moment is right, this noble queen and prince will follow with fresh troops. But before you go, answer me one doubt: what guarantee do we have of your firm loyalty?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["This shall assure my constant loyalty,—","That if our queen and this young prince agree,","I'll join mine eldest daughter and my joy","To him forthwith in holy wedlock bands."],"id":610,"m":"This will guarantee my constant loyalty: if our queen and this young prince agree, I'll marry my eldest daughter, my joy, to him at once in holy wedlock."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Yes, I agree, and thank you for your motion.—","Son Edward, she is fair and virtuous;","Therefore delay not, give thy hand to Warwick,","And with thy hand thy faith irrevocable","That only Warwick's daughter shall be thine."],"id":611,"m":"Yes, I agree, and thank you for the proposal. Son Edward, she's fair and virtuous, so don't delay: give your hand to Warwick, and with your hand your unbreakable promise that only Warwick's daughter will be your wife."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE","lines":["Yes, I accept her, for she well deserves it;","And here, to pledge my vow, I give my hand."],"id":612,"m":"Yes, I accept her, for she well deserves it; and here, to pledge my vow, I give my hand."},{"t":"sd","text":"He gives his hand to Warwick.","id":613},{"t":"sp","who":"KING LEWIS","lines":["Why stay we now? These soldiers shall be levied,","And thou, Lord Bourbon, our high admiral,","Shall waft them over with our royal fleet.—","I long till Edward fall by war's mischance","For mocking marriage with a dame of France."],"id":614,"m":"Why are we waiting now? These soldiers will be raised, and you, Lord Bourbon, our high admiral, will carry them over with our royal fleet. I long for Edward to fall by the chances of war for making a mockery of marriage with a lady of France."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt all but Warwick.","id":615},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["I came from Edward as ambassador,","But I return his sworn and mortal foe;","Matter of marriage was the charge he gave me,","But dreadful war shall answer his demand.","Had he none else to make a stale but me?","Then none but I shall turn his jest to sorrow.","I was the chief that rais'd him to the crown,","And I'll be chief to bring him down again;","Not that I pity Henry's misery,","But seek revenge on Edward's mockery."],"id":616,"m":"I came from Edward as an ambassador, but I go back his sworn and deadly enemy. He sent me on a matter of marriage, but dreadful war will answer his request. Had he no one else to make a fool of but me? Then no one but me will turn his joke to sorrow. I was the main one who raised him to the crown, and I'll be the main one to bring him down again; not because I pity Henry's misery, but because I want revenge for Edward's mockery."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":617},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"I","loc":"London. The Palace.","id":618},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter GLOSTER, CLARENCE, SOMERSET, and MONTAGUE.","id":619},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Now tell me, brother Clarence, what think you","Of this new marriage with the Lady Grey?","Hath not our brother made a worthy choice?"],"id":620,"m":"Now tell me, brother Clarence, what do you think of this new marriage to Lady Grey? Hasn't our brother made a fine choice?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["Alas! you know 't is far from hence to France;","How could he stay till Warwick made return?"],"id":621,"m":"Alas, you know it's a long way from here to France; how could he wait until Warwick came back?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["My lords, forbear this talk; here comes the King."],"id":622,"m":"My lords, stop this talk; here comes the king."},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. Enter KING EDWARD, attended; LADY GREY, as Queen; PEMBROKE, STAFFORD, HASTINGS, and others.","id":623},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["And his well-chosen bride."],"id":624,"m":"And his well-chosen bride."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["I mind to tell him plainly what I think."],"id":625,"m":"I intend to tell him plainly what I think."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Now, brother Clarence, how like you our choice","That you stand pensive as half malcontent?"],"id":626,"m":"Now, brother Clarence, how do you like our choice, that you stand there brooding, half discontented?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["As well as Lewis of France, or the Earl of Warwick,","Which are so weak of courage and in judgment","That they'll take no offence at our abuse."],"id":627,"m":"As much as Lewis of France or the Earl of Warwick do, who are so lacking in courage and judgment that they won't take offence at the way we've insulted them."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Suppose they take offence without a cause,","They are but Lewis and Warwick: I am Edward,","Your King and Warwick's, and must have my will."],"id":628,"m":"Suppose they do take offence without cause: they're only Lewis and Warwick. I'm Edward, your king and Warwick's, and I must have my way."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["And shall have your will, because our King;","Yet hasty marriage seldom proveth well."],"id":629,"m":"And you shall have your way, because you're our king; but hasty marriages seldom turn out well."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Yea, brother Richard, are you offended too?"],"id":630,"m":"Oh, brother Richard, are you offended too?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Not I.","No; God forbid that I should wish them sever'd","Whom God hath join'd together; ay, and 't were pity","To sunder them that yoke so well together."],"id":631,"m":"Not me. No; God forbid I should want to separate those whom God has joined together; yes, and it would be a pity to part two who are so well yoked together."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Setting your scorns and your mislike aside,","Tell me some reason why the Lady Grey","Should not become my wife and England's queen.—","And you too, Somerset and Montague,","Speak freely what you think."],"id":632,"m":"Putting your mockery and your dislike aside, give me some reason why Lady Grey shouldn't become my wife and England's queen. And you too, Somerset and Montague, say freely what you think."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["Then this is mine opinion,—that King Lewis","Becomes your enemy, for mocking him","About the marriage of the Lady Bona."],"id":633,"m":"Then this is my opinion: King Lewis becomes your enemy for being made a fool of over the marriage to Lady Bona."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["And Warwick, doing what you gave in charge,","Is now dishonoured by this new marriage."],"id":634,"m":"And Warwick, who did what you told him to, is now dishonoured by this new marriage."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["What if both Lewis and Warwick be appeas'd","By such invention as I can devise?"],"id":635,"m":"What if both Lewis and Warwick can be pacified by some scheme I can think up?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MONTAGUE","lines":["Yet to have join'd with France in such alliance","Would more have strength'ned this our commonwealth","'Gainst foreign storms than any home-bred marriage."],"id":636,"m":"Still, an alliance like that with France would have strengthened our country against foreign storms more than any marriage at home."},{"t":"sp","who":"HASTINGS","lines":["Why, knows not Montague that of itself","England is safe if true within itself?"],"id":637,"m":"Why, doesn't Montague know that England is safe on its own if it's loyal to itself?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MONTAGUE","lines":["But the safer when 't is back'd with France."],"id":638,"m":"But safer when France backs it."},{"t":"sp","who":"HASTINGS","lines":["'T is better using France than trusting France.","Let us be back'd with God, and with the seas","Which he hath giv'n for fence impregnable,","And with their helps only defend ourselves;","In them and in ourselves our safety lies."],"id":639,"m":"It's better to use France than to trust France. Let God be our backing, and the seas he has given us as an unbreakable wall; let us defend ourselves with their help alone. Our safety lies in them and in ourselves."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["For this one speech Lord Hastings well deserves","To have the heir of the Lord Hungerford."],"id":640,"m":"For that speech alone Lord Hastings well deserves to marry Lord Hungerford's heiress."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Ay, what of that? it was my will and grant;","And for this once my will shall stand for law."],"id":641,"m":"Yes, what about it? It was my wish and my grant, and this once my wish will stand as law."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["And yet, methinks, your grace hath not done well","To give the heir and daughter of Lord Scales","Unto the brother of your loving bride.","She better would have fitted me or Clarence;","But in your bride you bury brotherhood."],"id":642,"m":"And yet, I think your grace hasn't done well to give Lord Scales's daughter and heiress to your loving bride's brother. She would have suited me or Clarence better; but in your bride you bury brotherhood."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["Or else you would not have bestow'd the heir","Of the Lord Bonville on your new wife's son,","And leave your brothers to go speed elsewhere."],"id":643,"m":"Or else you wouldn't have given Lord Bonville's heiress to your new wife's son and left your brothers to try their luck elsewhere."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Alas, poor Clarence! is it for a wife","That thou art malcontent? I will provide thee."],"id":644,"m":"Alas, poor Clarence! Is it a wife you're unhappy about? I'll provide for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["In choosing for yourself you show'd your judgment,","Which being shallow you shall give me leave","To play the broker in mine own behalf;","And to that end I shortly mind to leave you."],"id":645,"m":"In choosing for yourself you showed your judgment, which is shallow, so you'll allow me to act as matchmaker on my own behalf; and for that purpose I intend to leave you soon."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Leave me or tarry, Edward will be king,","And not be tied unto his brother's will."],"id":646,"m":"Leave me or stay, Edward will be king and not be tied to his brother's wishes."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","lines":["My lords, before it pleas'd his majesty","To raise my state to title of a queen,","Do me but right, and you must all confess","That I was not ignoble of descent,","And meaner than myself have had like fortune.","But as this title honours me and mine,","So your dislikes, to whom I would be pleasing,","Doth cloud my joys with danger and with sorrow."],"id":647,"m":"My lords, before it pleased his majesty to raise me to the title of queen, you must all admit, if you're fair to me, that I was not of low birth, and women of lower rank than me have had the same good fortune. But just as this title honours me and my family, so your disapproval, when I want to please you, clouds my happiness with danger and sorrow."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["My love, forbear to fawn upon their frowns.","What danger or what sorrow can befall thee","So long as Edward is thy constant friend","And their true sovereign, whom they must obey?","Nay, whom they shall obey, and love thee too,","Unless they seek for hatred at my hands;","Which if they do, yet will I keep thee safe,","And they shall feel the vengeance of my wrath."],"id":648,"m":"My love, don't try to win over their frowns. What danger or sorrow can come to you as long as Edward is your constant friend and their true sovereign, whom they must obey? Indeed, whom they will obey, and love you too, unless they want me to hate them; and if they do, I'll still keep you safe, and they'll feel my angry revenge."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["[Aside] I hear, yet say not much, but think the more."],"id":649,"m":"(Aside) I hear, and say little, but think all the more."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Messenger.","id":650},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Now, messenger, what letters or what news","From France?"],"id":651,"m":"Now, messenger, what letters or news from France?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["My sovereign liege, no letters, and few words,","But such as I, without your special pardon,","Dare not relate."],"id":652,"m":"My sovereign lord, no letters and only a few words, but ones I don't dare repeat without your special pardon."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Go to, we pardon thee; therefore, in brief,","Tell me their words as near as thou canst guess them.","What answer makes King Lewis unto our letters?"],"id":653,"m":"Go on, we pardon you. So tell me briefly their words, as closely as you can remember them. What answer does King Lewis give to our letters?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["At my depart these were his very words:","'Go tell false Edward, thy supposed king,","That Lewis of France is sending over maskers","To revel it with him and his new bride.'"],"id":654,"m":"When I left, these were his exact words: 'Go and tell false Edward, your so-called king, that Lewis of France is sending over masked dancers to revel with him and his new bride.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Is Lewis so brave? belike he thinks me Henry.","But what said Lady Bona to my marriage?"],"id":655,"m":"Is Lewis so bold? He must think I'm Henry. But what did Lady Bona say about my marriage?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["These were her words, utt'red with mild disdain:","'Tell him, in hope he'll prove a widower shortly,","I'll wear the willow garland for his sake.'"],"id":656,"m":"These were her words, spoken with mild scorn: 'Tell him that, in the hope he'll soon be a widower, I'll wear the willow garland for his sake.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["I blame not her, she could say little less,","She had the wrong; but what said Henry's queen?","For I have heard that she was there in place."],"id":657,"m":"I don't blame her; she could hardly say less, since she was the one wronged. But what did Henry's queen say? I've heard she was there."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["'Tell him' quoth she 'my mourning weeds are done,","And I am ready to put armour on.'"],"id":658,"m":"'Tell him,' she said, 'my mourning clothes are finished with, and I'm ready to put on armour.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Belike she minds to play the Amazon.","But what said Warwick to these injuries?"],"id":659,"m":"It seems she means to play the Amazon. But what did Warwick say to these insults?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["He, more incens'd against your majesty","Than all the rest, discharg'd me with these words:","'Tell him from me that he hath done me wrong,","And therefore I'll uncrown him ere 't be long.'"],"id":660,"m":"He was more furious with your majesty than all the rest, and sent me off with these words: 'Tell him from me that he has wronged me, and so I'll uncrown him before long.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Ha! durst the traitor breathe out so proud words?","Well, I will arm me, being thus forewarn'd;","They shall have wars, and pay for their presumption.","But say, is Warwick friends with Margaret?"],"id":661,"m":"Ha! Does the traitor dare breathe such proud words? Well, being warned like this, I'll arm myself. They'll have war and pay for their arrogance. But tell me, is Warwick friends with Margaret?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["Ay, gracious sovereign; they are so link'd in friendship","That young Prince Edward marries Warwick's daughter."],"id":662,"m":"Yes, gracious sovereign; they're so bound in friendship that young Prince Edward is marrying Warwick's daughter."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["Belike the elder; Clarence will have the younger.","Now, brother king, farewell, and sit you fast,","For I will hence to Warwick's other daughter;","That, though I want a kingdom, yet in marriage","I may not prove inferior to yourself.—","You that love me and Warwick, follow me."],"id":663,"m":"The elder one, presumably; Clarence will have the younger. Now, brother king, goodbye, and hold on tight to your throne, for I'm going to Warwick's other daughter, so that, though I lack a kingdom, at least I won't be worse off than you in marriage. Those of you who love me and Warwick, follow me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Clarence, and Somerset follows.","id":664},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["[Aside] Not I.","My thoughts aim at a further matter; I","Stay not for the love of Edward, but the crown."],"id":665,"m":"(Aside) Not me. My thoughts aim at something further. I stay not out of love for Edward, but for the crown."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Clarence and Somerset both gone to Warwick!","Yet am I arm'd against the worst can happen,","And haste is needful in this desperate case.—","Pembroke and Stafford, you in our behalf","Go levy men and make prepare for war;","They are already, or quickly will be landed.","Myself in person will straight follow you.—"],"id":666,"m":"Clarence and Somerset both gone over to Warwick! Still, I'm armed against the worst that can happen, and speed is needed in this desperate situation. Pembroke and Stafford, go and raise troops for us and prepare for war. They've already landed, or soon will. I'll follow you myself at once."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Pembroke and Stafford.","id":667},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","cont":1,"lines":["But, ere I go, Hastings and Montague,","Resolve my doubt. You twain, of all the rest,","Are near to Warwick by blood and by alliance;","Tell me if you love Warwick more than me?","If it be so, then both depart to him.","I rather wish you foes than hollow friends;","But if you mind to hold your true obedience,","Give me assurance with some friendly vow,","That I may never have you in suspect."],"id":668,"m":"But before I go, Hastings and Montague, settle my doubt. You two, more than the rest, are close to Warwick by blood and marriage. Tell me, do you love Warwick more than me? If so, both of you go to him. I'd rather have you as enemies than as false friends; but if you mean to stay truly loyal, give me some friendly vow as a guarantee, so I'll never have to suspect you."},{"t":"sp","who":"MONTAGUE","lines":["So God help Montague as he proves true!"],"id":669,"m":"May God help Montague as he proves true!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HASTINGS","lines":["And Hastings as he favours Edward's cause!"],"id":670,"m":"And Hastings, as he supports Edward's cause!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Now, brother Richard, will you stand by us?"],"id":671,"m":"Now, brother Richard, will you stand by us?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Ay, in despite of all that shall withstand you."],"id":672,"m":"Yes, in spite of everyone who stands against you."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Why, so! then am I sure of victory.","Now, therefore, let us hence; and lose no hour","Till we meet Warwick with his foreign pow'r."],"id":673,"m":"Why, good! Then I'm sure of victory. So let's go now and not lose an hour until we meet Warwick and his foreign army."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":674},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"II","loc":"A Plain in Warwickshire.","id":675},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter WARWICK and OXFORD with French and other Forces.","id":676},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Trust me, my lord, all hitherto goes well;","The common people by numbers swarm to us.","But see where Somerset and Clarence comes!—"],"id":677,"m":"Believe me, my lord, everything's gone well so far. The common people are swarming to us in great numbers. But look, here come Somerset and Clarence!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter CLARENCE and SOMERSET.","id":678},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","cont":1,"lines":["Speak suddenly, my lords, are we all friends?"],"id":679,"m":"Tell me quickly, my lords, are we all friends?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["Fear not that, my lord."],"id":680,"m":"Don't worry about that, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Then, gentle Clarence, welcome unto Warwick;—","And welcome, Somerset.—I hold it cowardice","To rest mistrustful where a noble heart","Hath pawn'd an open hand in sign of love;","Else might I think that Clarence, Edward's brother,","Were but a feigned friend to our proceedings.","But welcome, sweet Clarence; my daughter shall be thine.","And now what rests but, in night's coverture,","Thy brother being carelessly encamp'd,","His soldiers lurking in the towns about,","And but attended by a simple guard,","We may surprise and take him at our pleasure?","Our scouts have found the adventure very easy;","That as Ulysses and stout Diomede","With sleight and manhood stole to Rhesus' tents,","And brought from thence the Thracian fatal steeds,","So we, well cover'd with the night's black mantle,","At unawares may beat down Edward's guard,","And seize himself,—I say not slaughter him,","For I intend but only to surprise him.—","You that will follow me to this attempt,","Applaud the name of Henry with your leader."],"id":681,"m":"Then, gentle Clarence, welcome to Warwick; and welcome, Somerset. I think it's cowardly to stay suspicious when a noble heart has offered an open hand as a sign of love; otherwise I might think that Clarence, Edward's brother, was only pretending to support our cause. But welcome, sweet Clarence; my daughter will be yours. And now all that remains is this: since your brother is camped carelessly, his soldiers scattered in the towns around and only a small guard with him, we can surprise him under cover of night and take him as we please. Our scouts have found the venture very easy; so that, just as Ulysses and brave Diomedes crept by skill and courage into Rhesus's tents and brought away the fateful Thracian horses, we, well covered by night's black cloak, can catch Edward's guard unawares, beat them down and seize him. I don't say kill him; I only mean to take him by surprise. Those of you who'll follow me in this attempt, cheer the name of Henry with your leader."},{"t":"sd","text":"They all cry, 'Henry!'.","id":682},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","cont":1,"lines":["Why then, let's on our way in silent sort;","For Warwick and his friends, God and Saint George!"],"id":683,"m":"Why then, let's go on our way quietly. For Warwick and his friends, God and Saint George!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":684},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"III","loc":"Edward's Camp near Warwick.","id":685},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter certain Watchmen, to guard the KING'S tent.","id":686},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST WATCHMAN","lines":["Come on, my masters, each man take his stand;","The king by this is set him down to sleep."],"id":687,"m":"Come on, gentlemen, each man take his position. The king will have sat down to sleep by now."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND WATCHMAN","lines":["What, will he not to bed?"],"id":688,"m":"What, won't he go to bed?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST WATCHMAN","lines":["Why, no; for he hath made a solemn vow","Never to lie and take his natural rest","Till Warwick or himself be quite suppress'd."],"id":689,"m":"Why, no; he's made a solemn vow never to lie down and sleep properly until either Warwick or he himself is completely beaten."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND WATCHMAN","lines":["To-morrow, then, belike shall be the day,","If Warwick be so near as men report."],"id":690,"m":"Then tomorrow will probably be the day, if Warwick is as close as people say."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD WATCHMAN","lines":["But say, I pray, what nobleman is that","That with the king here resteth in his tent?"],"id":691,"m":"But tell me, please, what nobleman is that resting in the tent with the king?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST WATCHMAN","lines":["'T is the Lord Hastings, the king's chiefest friend."],"id":692,"m":"It's Lord Hastings, the king's closest friend."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD WATCHMAN","lines":["O, is it So? But why commands the king","That his chief followers lodge in towns about him,","While he himself keeps in the cold field?"],"id":693,"m":"Oh, is it? But why does the king order his main followers to stay in the towns around him, while he stays out in the cold field?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND WATCHMAN","lines":["'T is the more honour, because more dangerous."],"id":694,"m":"There's more honour in it, because it's more dangerous."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD WATCHMAN","lines":["Ay, but give me worship and quietness;","I like it better than dangerous honour.","If Warwick knew in what estate he stands,","'T is to be doubted he would waken him."],"id":695,"m":"Yes, but give me dignity and quiet; I like that better than dangerous honour. If Warwick knew what position he's in, he'd probably come and wake him up."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST WATCHMAN","lines":["Unless our halberds did shut up his passage."],"id":696,"m":"Unless our spears blocked his way."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND WATCHMAN","lines":["Ay; wherefore else guard we his royal tent","But to defend his person from night-foes?"],"id":697,"m":"Yes; why else do we guard his royal tent, except to protect him from enemies at night?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter WARWICK, CLARENCE, OXFORD, SOMERSET, and Forces silently.","id":698},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["This is his tent; and see where, stand his guard.","Courage, my masters! honour now or never!","But follow me, and Edward shall be ours."],"id":699,"m":"This is his tent; and look, there stand his guards. Courage, gentlemen! Honour now or never! Just follow me, and Edward will be ours."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST WATCHMAN","lines":["Who goes there?"],"id":700,"m":"Who goes there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND WATCHMAN","lines":["Stay, or thou diest."],"id":701,"m":"Stop, or you die."},{"t":"sd","text":"Warwick and the rest cry all, 'Warwick! Warwick!' and set upon the guard, who fly, crying 'Arm! Arm!' Warwick and the rest following them.","id":702},{"t":"sd","text":"Drum beating and trumpet sounding; enter WARWICK and the rest, bringing the KING out in his gown sitting in a chair. GLOSTER and HASTINGS fly over the stage.","id":703},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["What are they that fly there?"],"id":704,"m":"Who are those running away there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Richard and Hastings. Let them go; here is the duke."],"id":705,"m":"Richard and Hastings. Let them go; here's the duke."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["The duke! why, Warwick, when we parted,","Thou call'dst me king?"],"id":706,"m":"The duke! Why, Warwick, when we parted you called me king."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Ay, but the case is alter'd;","When you disgrac'd me in my embassade,","Then I degraded you from being king,","And come now to create you Duke of York.","Alas! how should you govern any kingdom","That know not how to use ambassadors,","Nor how to be contented with one wife,","Nor how to use your brothers brotherly,","Nor how to study for the people's welfare,","Nor how to shroud yourself from enemies?"],"id":707,"m":"Yes, but things have changed. When you disgraced me during my embassy, I demoted you from being king, and now I've come to make you Duke of York. Alas! How could you govern any kingdom when you don't know how to treat ambassadors, or how to be content with one wife, or how to treat your brothers like a brother, or how to work for the people's welfare, or how to protect yourself from enemies?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Yea, brother of Clarence, art thou here too?","Nay, then I see that Edward needs must down.—","Yet, Warwick, in despite of all mischance","Of thee thyself and all thy complices,","Edward will always bear himself as king;","Though fortune's malice overthrow my state,","My mind exceeds the compass of her wheel."],"id":708,"m":"Oh, brother Clarence, are you here too? Well, then I see Edward has to fall. Still, Warwick, in spite of all the bad luck you and your accomplices bring, Edward will always behave like a king. Though fortune's spite may overthrow my position, my mind is beyond the reach of her wheel."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Then for his mind be Edward England's king;"],"id":709,"m":"Then let Edward be England's king in his mind;"},{"t":"sd","text":"Takes off his crown.","id":710},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","cont":1,"lines":["But Henry now shall wear the English crown","And be true king indeed, thou but the shadow.—","My Lord of Somerset, at my request,","See that forthwith Duke Edward be convey'd","Unto my brother, Archbishop of York.","When I have fought with Pembroke and his fellows,","I'll follow you and tell what answer","Lewis and the Lady Bona send to him.—","Now, for a while farewell, good Duke of York."],"id":711,"m":"but Henry will now wear the English crown and be the real king, and you only the shadow. My Lord of Somerset, at my request see that Duke Edward is taken at once to my brother, the Archbishop of York. When I've fought Pembroke and his companions, I'll follow you and tell him what answer Lewis and Lady Bona send him. Now, goodbye for a while, good Duke of York."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["What fates impose, that men must needs abide;","It boots not to resist both wind and tide."],"id":712,"m":"Men must put up with what the fates impose; there's no use resisting both wind and tide."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit King Edward, led out; Somerset with him.","id":713},{"t":"sp","who":"OXFORD","lines":["What now remains, my lords, for us to do,","But march to London with our soldiers?"],"id":714,"m":"What's left for us to do now, my lords, except march to London with our soldiers?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Ay, that's the first thing that we have to do,—","To free King Henry from imprisonment","And see him seated in the regal throne."],"id":715,"m":"Yes, that's the first thing we have to do: free King Henry from prison and see him sitting on the royal throne."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":716},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"IV","loc":"London. The Palace.","id":717},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter QUEEN ELIZABETH and RIVERS.","id":718},{"t":"sp","who":"RIVERS","lines":["Madam, what makes you in this sudden change?"],"id":719,"m":"Madam, what has caused this sudden change in you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","lines":["Why, brother Rivers, are you yet to learn","What late misfortune is befallen King Edward?"],"id":720,"m":"Why, brother Rivers, haven't you heard yet what misfortune has just happened to King Edward?"},{"t":"sp","who":"RIVERS","lines":["What! loss of some pitch'd battle against Warwick?"],"id":721,"m":"What! Has he lost a pitched battle against Warwick?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","lines":["No, but the loss of his own royal person."],"id":722,"m":"No, but he's lost his royal person."},{"t":"sp","who":"RIVERS","lines":["Then is my sovereign slain?"],"id":723,"m":"Then is my sovereign dead?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","lines":["Ay, almost slain, for he is taken prisoner,","Either betray'd by falsehood of his guard","Or by his foe surpris'd at unawares,","And, as I further have to understand,","Is new committed to the Bishop of York,","Fell Warwick's brother and by that our foe."],"id":724,"m":"Yes, almost dead, for he's been taken prisoner, either betrayed by a disloyal guard or surprised by his enemy; and, as I've also been told, he has just been handed over to the Archbishop of York, cruel Warwick's brother and therefore our enemy."},{"t":"sp","who":"RIVERS","lines":["These news, I must confess, are full of grief;","Yet, gracious madam, bear it as you may.","Warwick may lose, that now hath won the day."],"id":725,"m":"This news is very sad, I must admit; but, gracious madam, bear it as well as you can. Warwick, who has won today, may lose."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","lines":["Till then, fair hope must hinder life's decay;","And I the rather wean me from despair,","For love of Edward's offspring in my womb.","This is it that makes me bridle passion","And bear with mildness my misfortune's cross;","Ay, ay, for this I draw in many a tear,","And stop the rising of blood-sucking sighs,","Lest with my sighs or tears I blast or drown","King Edward's fruit, true heir to the English crown."],"id":726,"m":"Until then, hope must keep my life from failing; and I wean myself from despair all the more out of love for Edward's child in my womb. This is what makes me rein in my passion and bear the cross of my misfortune calmly. Yes, yes, for this I hold back many a tear and stop the blood-draining sighs, in case my sighs or tears should blight or drown King Edward's child, the true heir to the English crown."},{"t":"sp","who":"RIVERS","lines":["But, madam, where is Warwick then become?"],"id":727,"m":"But, madam, what has become of Warwick?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","lines":["I am inform'd that he comes towards London,","To set the crown once more on Henry's head.","Guess thou the rest: King Edward's friends must down;","But to prevent the tyrant's violence,—","For trust not him that hath once broken faith,—","I'll hence forthwith unto the sanctuary,","To save at least the heir of Edward's right.","There shall I rest secure from force and fraud.","Come therefore, let us fly while we may fly;","If Warwick take us, we are sure to die."],"id":728,"m":"I'm told he's coming towards London to put the crown on Henry's head again. You can guess the rest: King Edward's friends must fall. But to escape the tyrant's violence, for never trust someone who has once broken his word, I'll go at once to sanctuary, to save at least the heir to Edward's right. There I'll be safe from force and trickery. Come, then, let's run while we can; if Warwick catches us, we're sure to die."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":729},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"V","loc":"A park near Middleham Castle in Yorkshire.","id":730},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter GLOSTER, HASTINGS, SIR WILLIAM STANLEY, and others.","id":731},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Now, my Lord Hastings, and Sir William Stanley,","Leave off to wonder why I drew you hither","Into this chiefest thicket of the park.","Thus stands the case: you know our King, my brother,","Is prisoner to the Bishop here, at whose hands","He hath good usage and great liberty,","And often, but attended with weak guard,","Comes hunting this way to disport himself.","I have advertis'd him by secret means","That if about this hour he make this way,","Under the colour of his usual game,","He shall here find his friends, with horse and men,","To set him free from his captivity."],"id":732,"m":"Now, my Lord Hastings and Sir William Stanley, stop wondering why I brought you here into the thickest part of the park. This is how things stand: you know our king, my brother, is the archbishop's prisoner here; he's treated well and given a lot of freedom, and he often comes hunting this way for amusement with only a weak guard. I've secretly let him know that if he comes this way at about this time, pretending to be at his usual sport, he'll find his friends here with horses and men to free him from captivity."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter KING EDWARD and a Huntsman.","id":733},{"t":"sp","who":"HUNTSMAN","lines":["This way, my lord, for this way lies the game."],"id":734,"m":"This way, my lord, for the game is this way."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Nay, this way, man; see, where the huntsmen stand.—","Now, brother of Gloster, Lord Hastings, and the rest,","Stand you thus close to steal the bishop's deer?"],"id":735,"m":"No, this way, man; look where the huntsmen are standing. Now, brother Gloucester, Lord Hastings and the rest, are you hiding here to steal the archbishop's deer?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Brother, the time and case requireth haste;","Your horse stands ready at the park corner."],"id":736,"m":"Brother, the time and situation call for speed. Your horse is ready at the corner of the park."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["But whither shall we then?"],"id":737,"m":"But where shall we go then?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HASTINGS","lines":["To Lynn, my lord, and shipt from thence to Flanders."],"id":738,"m":"To King's Lynn, my lord, and from there by ship to Flanders."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Well guess'd, believe me, for that was my meaning."],"id":739,"m":"Well guessed, believe me; that was my plan."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Stanley, I will requite thy forwardness."],"id":740,"m":"Stanley, I'll reward your eagerness."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["But wherefore stay we? 't is no time to talk."],"id":741,"m":"But why are we waiting? This is no time to talk."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Huntsman, what say'st thou? wilt thou go along?"],"id":742,"m":"Huntsman, what do you say? Will you come along?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HUNTSMAN","lines":["Better do so than tarry and be hang'd."],"id":743,"m":"Better that than stay and be hanged."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Come then; away! let's have no more ado."],"id":744,"m":"Come on, then; away! Let's not fuss any more."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Bishop, farewell; shield thee from Warwick's frown,","And pray that I may repossess the crown."],"id":745,"m":"Goodbye, archbishop; protect yourself from Warwick's anger, and pray that I may get the crown back."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":746},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"VI","loc":"London. The Tower.","id":747},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter KING HENRY, CLARENCE, WARWICK, SOMERSET, Young RICHMOND, OXFORD, MONTAGUE, Lieutenant of the Tower, and Attendants.","id":748},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Master Lieutenant, now that God and friends","Have shaken Edward from the regal seat","And turn'd my captive state to liberty,","My fear to hope, my sorrows unto joys,","At our enlargement what are thy due fees?"],"id":749,"m":"Master Lieutenant, now that God and my friends have shaken Edward off the royal throne and turned my imprisonment into freedom, my fear into hope and my sorrows into joys, what fees do we owe you on our release?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LIEUTENANT","lines":["Subjects may challenge nothing of their sovereigns;","But if an humble prayer may prevail,","I then crave pardon of your Majesty."],"id":750,"m":"Subjects can claim nothing from their sovereigns; but if a humble request may succeed, I ask your majesty's pardon."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["For what, lieutenant? for well using me?","Nay, be thou sure I'll well requite thy kindness,","For that it made my imprisonment a pleasure;","Ay, such a pleasure as incaged birds","Conceive when, after many moody thoughts,","At last by notes of household harmony","They quite forget their loss of liberty.—","But, Warwick, after God thou sett'st me free,","And chiefly therefore I thank God and thee;","He was the author, thou the instrument.","Therefore, that I may conquer fortune's spite,","By living low where fortune cannot hurt me,","And that the people of this blessed land","May not be punish'd with my thwarting stars,","Warwick, although my head still wear the crown,","I here resign my government to thee,","For thou art fortunate in all thy deeds."],"id":751,"m":"For what, lieutenant? For treating me well? No, you can be sure I'll reward your kindness well, since it made my imprisonment a pleasure; yes, the kind of pleasure caged birds feel when, after many gloomy thoughts, they finally forget their lost freedom in the sound of household music. But Warwick, after God, it's you who set me free, so I thank God and you above all; he was the author, you the instrument. So, to beat fortune's spite by living humbly where fortune can't hurt me, and so that the people of this blessed land are not punished by my unlucky stars, Warwick, although my head still wears the crown, I here hand over the government to you, for you are lucky in everything you do."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Your grace hath still been fam'd for virtuous,","And now may seem as wise as virtuous","By spying and avoiding fortune's malice,","For few men rightly temper with the stars;","Yet in this one thing let me blame your grace,","For choosing me when Clarence is in place."],"id":752,"m":"Your grace has always been famous for virtue, and now you may seem as wise as you are virtuous by spotting fortune's malice and avoiding it, for few men adapt themselves rightly to their stars. Yet in this one thing let me find fault with your grace: for choosing me when Clarence is here."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["No, Warwick, thou art worthy of the sway,","To whom the heavens in thy nativity","Adjudg'd an olive branch and laurel crown,","As likely to be blest in peace and war;","And therefore, I yield thee my free consent."],"id":753,"m":"No, Warwick, you're worthy of the power; at your birth the heavens awarded you an olive branch and a laurel crown, as one likely to be blessed in peace and war. So I freely give you my consent."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["And I choose Clarence only for protector."],"id":754,"m":"And I choose Clarence alone as Protector."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Warwick and Clarence, give me both your hands.","Now join your hands, and with your hands your hearts,","That no dissension hinder government.","I make you both protectors of this land,","While I myself will lead a private life","And in devotion spend my latter days,","To sin's rebuke and my Creator's praise."],"id":755,"m":"Warwick and Clarence, give me both your hands. Now join your hands, and with your hands your hearts, so no quarrel hinders the government. I make you both Protectors of this land, while I myself will lead a private life and spend my last days in devotion, rebuking sin and praising my Creator."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["What answers Clarence to his sovereign's will?"],"id":756,"m":"What does Clarence answer to his sovereign's wish?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["That he consents if Warwick yield consent,","For on thy fortune I repose myself."],"id":757,"m":"That he agrees if Warwick agrees, for I rely on your good fortune."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Why, then, though loath, yet I must be content.","We'll yoke together, like a double shadow","To Henry's body, and supply his place,—","I mean in bearing weight of government","While he enjoys the honour and his ease.","And, Clarence, now then it is more than needful","Forthwith that Edward be pronounc'd a traitor,","And all his lands and goods confiscated."],"id":758,"m":"Why, then, though I'm reluctant, I must accept. We'll be yoked together like a double shadow to Henry's body and take his place: I mean in carrying the weight of government while he enjoys the honour and his ease. And, Clarence, it's now essential that Edward be proclaimed a traitor at once and all his lands and goods confiscated."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["What else? and that succession be determin'd."],"id":759,"m":"Of course; and that the succession be settled."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Ay, therein Clarence shall not want his part."],"id":760,"m":"Yes, and Clarence won't lack his share in that."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["But with the first of all your chief affairs,","Let me entreat—for I command no more—","That Margaret your queen, and my son Edward,","Be sent for to return from France with speed;","For, till I see them here, by doubtful fear","My joy of liberty is half eclips'd."],"id":761,"m":"But as the first of all your main business, let me ask, for I command no longer, that Margaret your queen and my son Edward be sent for to return quickly from France; for until I see them here, anxious fear half darkens my joy at being free."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["It shall be done, my sovereign, with all speed."],"id":762,"m":"It will be done, my sovereign, with all speed."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["My Lord of Somerset, what youth is that","Of whom you seem to have so tender care?"],"id":763,"m":"My Lord of Somerset, who is that youth you seem to take such tender care of?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["My liege, it is young Henry, Earl of Richmond."],"id":764,"m":"My lord, it's young Henry, Earl of Richmond."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Come hither, England's hope.—If secret powers"],"id":765,"m":"Come here, England's hope. If secret powers"},{"t":"sd","text":"Lays his hand on his head.","id":766},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","cont":1,"lines":["Suggest but truth to my divining thoughts,","This pretty lad will prove our country's bliss.","His looks are full of peaceful majesty,","His head by nature fram'd to wear a crown,","His hand to wield a sceptre, and himself","Likely in time to bless a regal throne.","Make much of him, my lords; for this is he","Must help you more than you are hurt by me."],"id":767,"m":"are telling my prophetic thoughts the truth, this fine boy will turn out to be our country's blessing. His looks are full of peaceful majesty; his head is shaped by nature to wear a crown, his hand to hold a sceptre, and he himself is likely in time to grace a royal throne. Look after him well, my lords; for this is the one who must help you more than I have harmed you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Messenger.","id":768},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["What news, my friend?"],"id":769,"m":"What news, my friend?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["That Edward is escaped from your brother,","And fled, as he hears since, to Burgundy."],"id":770,"m":"That Edward has escaped from your brother and fled, as he has heard since, to Burgundy."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Unsavoury news! but how made he escape?"],"id":771,"m":"Unwelcome news! But how did he escape?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["He was convey'd by Richard Duke of Gloster","And the Lord Hastings, who attended him","In secret ambush on the forest side,","And from the bishop's huntsmen rescued him,","For hunting was his daily exercise."],"id":772,"m":"He was taken away by Richard, Duke of Gloucester, and Lord Hastings, who waited for him in a secret ambush at the edge of the forest and rescued him from the archbishop's huntsmen; for hunting was his daily exercise."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["My brother was too careless of his charge.—","But let us hence, my sovereign, to provide","A salve for any sore that may betide."],"id":773,"m":"My brother was too careless with his prisoner. But let's go, my sovereign, and provide a remedy for any trouble that may come."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt King Henry, Warwick, Clarence, Lieutenant, and attendants.","id":774},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["My lord, I like not of this flight of Edward's,","For doubtless Burgundy will yield him help,","And we shall have more wars before 't be long.","As Henry's late presaging prophecy","Did glad my heart with hope of this young Richmond,","So doth my heart misgive me, in these conflicts","What may befall him, to his harm and ours;","Therefore, Lord Oxford, to prevent the worst,","Forthwith we'll send him hence to Brittany","Till storms be past of civil enmity."],"id":775,"m":"My lord, I don't like this escape of Edward's, for Burgundy will certainly give him help, and we'll have more wars before long. Just as Henry's prophecy just now gladdened my heart with hope for young Richmond, so my heart fears what may happen to him in these conflicts, to his harm and ours. So, Lord Oxford, to prevent the worst, we'll send him away at once to Brittany until the storms of civil war are over."},{"t":"sp","who":"OXFORD","lines":["Ay; for if Edward repossess the crown,","'T is like that Richmond with the rest shall down."],"id":776,"m":"Yes; for if Edward gets the crown back, Richmond is likely to fall with the rest."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["It shall be so; he shall to Brittany.","Come therefore, let's about it speedily."],"id":777,"m":"So be it; he'll go to Brittany. Come, then, let's see to it quickly."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":778},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"VII","loc":"Before York.","id":779},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter KING EDWARD, GLOSTER, HASTINGS, and Forces.","id":780},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Now, brother Richard, Lord Hastings, and the rest,","Yet thus far fortune maketh us amends,","And says that once more I shall interchange","My waned state for Henry's regal crown.","Well have we pass'd and now repass'd the seas,","And brought desired help from Burgundy.","What then remains, we being thus arriv'd","From Ravenspurg haven before the gates of York,","But that we enter as into our dukedom?"],"id":781,"m":"Now, brother Richard, Lord Hastings and the rest, so far fortune is making it up to us and says I'll once more exchange my fallen state for Henry's royal crown. We've crossed the sea and back safely and brought the help we wanted from Burgundy. Now that we've arrived from the harbour at Ravenspurgh at the gates of York, all that remains is to enter it as our own dukedom."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["The gates made fast!—Brother, I like not this;","For many men that stumble at the threshold","Are well foretold that danger lurks within."],"id":782,"m":"The gates are locked! Brother, I don't like this; many men who stumble on the doorstep are being warned that danger lies inside."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Tush, man! abodements must not now affright us;","By fair or foul means we must enter in,","For hither will our friends repair to us."],"id":783,"m":"Nonsense, man! We mustn't be scared by omens now. By fair means or foul we must get in, for our friends will come to us here."},{"t":"sp","who":"HASTINGS","lines":["My liege, I'll knock once more to summon them."],"id":784,"m":"My lord, I'll knock once more to call them."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter on the walls, the Mayor of York and his Brethren.","id":785},{"t":"sp","who":"MAYOR","lines":["My lords, we were forewarned of your coming","And shut the gates for safety of ourselves,","For now we owe allegiance unto Henry."],"id":786,"m":"My lords, we were warned you were coming and shut the gates for our own safety, for we now owe allegiance to Henry."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["But master mayor, if Henry be your king,","Yet Edward, at the least, is Duke of York."],"id":787,"m":"But, Master Mayor, if Henry is your king, Edward is at least Duke of York."},{"t":"sp","who":"MAYOR","lines":["True, my good lord; I know you for no less."],"id":788,"m":"True, my good lord; I know you to be no less."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Why, and I challenge nothing but my dukedom,","As being well content with that alone."],"id":789,"m":"Why, and I claim nothing but my dukedom, being quite content with that alone."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["[Aside] But when the fox hath once got in his nose,","He'll soon find means to make the body follow."],"id":790,"m":"(Aside) But once the fox has got his nose in, he'll soon find a way to make the body follow."},{"t":"sp","who":"HASTINGS","lines":["Why, master mayor, why stand you in a doubt?","Open the gates; we are King Henry's friends."],"id":791,"m":"Why, Master Mayor, why are you hesitating? Open the gates; we're King Henry's friends."},{"t":"sp","who":"MAYOR","lines":["Ay, say you so? the gates shall then be open'd."],"id":792,"m":"Oh, is that so? Then the gates will be opened."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt from above.","id":793},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["A wise, stout captain, and soon persuaded."],"id":794,"m":"A wise, brave commander, and easily persuaded!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HASTINGS","lines":["The good old man would fain that all were well,","So 't were not long of him; but, being enter'd,","I doubt not, I, but we shall soon persuade","Both him and all his brothers unto reason."],"id":795,"m":"The good old man would like everything to be all right, so long as he isn't to blame; but once we're in, I have no doubt we'll soon persuade him and all his fellow aldermen to see sense."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Mayor and two Aldermen, below.","id":796},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["So, master mayor; these gates must not be shut","But in the night or in the time of war.","What! fear not, man, but yield me up the keys;"],"id":797,"m":"So, Master Mayor, these gates must be shut only at night or in wartime. What! Don't be afraid, man; hand me the keys;"},{"t":"sd","text":"Takes his keys.","id":798},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","cont":1,"lines":["For Edward will defend the town and thee,","And all those friends that deign to follow me."],"id":799,"m":"for Edward will defend the town and you, and all those friends who choose to follow me."},{"t":"sd","text":"March. Enter MONTGOMERY and Forces.","id":800},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Brother, this is Sir John Montgomery,","Our trusty friend unless I be deceiv'd."],"id":801,"m":"Brother, this is Sir John Montgomery, our loyal friend, unless I'm mistaken."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Welcome, Sir John; but why come you in arms?"],"id":802,"m":"Welcome, Sir John; but why have you come armed?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MONTGOMERY","lines":["To help King Edward in his time of storm,","As every loyal subject ought to do."],"id":803,"m":"To help King Edward in his stormy time, as every loyal subject should."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Thanks, good Montgomery; but we now forget","Our title to the crown, and only claim","Our dukedom till God please to send the rest."],"id":804,"m":"Thank you, good Montgomery; but we're now forgetting our claim to the crown and claiming only our dukedom, until God chooses to send the rest."},{"t":"sp","who":"MONTGOMERY","lines":["Then fare you well, for I will hence again;","I came to serve a king, and not a duke.—","Drummer, strike up, and let us march away."],"id":805,"m":"Then goodbye, for I'll go home again. I came to serve a king, not a duke. Drummer, strike up, and let's march away."},{"t":"sd","text":"A march begun.","id":806},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Nay, stay, Sir John, awhile, and we'll debate","By what safe means the crown may be recover'd."],"id":807,"m":"No, wait a moment, Sir John, and we'll discuss safe ways to get the crown back."},{"t":"sp","who":"MONTGOMERY","lines":["What talk you of debating? in few words,","If you'll not here proclaim yourself our king,","I'll leave you to your fortune and begone","To keep them back that come to succour you.","Why shall we fight if you pretend no title?"],"id":808,"m":"Why talk about discussing? In short, if you won't proclaim yourself our king here, I'll leave you to your fate and go, and hold back those who are coming to help you. Why should we fight if you claim no title?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Why, brother, wherefore stand you on nice points?"],"id":809,"m":"Why, brother, why are you fussing over fine points?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["When we grow stronger, then we'll make our claim;","Till then 't is wisdom to conceal our meaning."],"id":810,"m":"When we're stronger, we'll make our claim; until then it's wise to hide our intentions."},{"t":"sp","who":"HASTINGS","lines":["Away with scrupulous wit! now arms must rule."],"id":811,"m":"Away with cautious cleverness! Now weapons must rule."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["And fearless minds climb soonest unto crowns.—","Brother, we will proclaim you out of hand;","The bruit thereof will bring you many friends."],"id":812,"m":"And fearless minds climb to crowns fastest. Brother, we'll proclaim you at once; the news of it will bring you many friends."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Then be it as you will; for 't is my right,","And Henry but usurps the diadem."],"id":813,"m":"Then have it your way; for it's my right, and Henry only usurps the crown."},{"t":"sp","who":"MONTGOMERY","lines":["Ay, now my sovereign speaketh like himself,","And now will I be Edward's champion."],"id":814,"m":"Yes, now my sovereign speaks like himself, and now I'll be Edward's champion."},{"t":"sp","who":"HASTINGS","lines":["Sound, trumpet; Edward shall be here proclaim'd.—","Come, fellow-soldier, make thou proclamation."],"id":815,"m":"Sound, trumpet; Edward will be proclaimed here. Come, fellow soldier, read the proclamation."},{"t":"sd","text":"Gives him a paper. Flourish.","id":816},{"t":"sp","who":"SOLDIER","lines":["[Reads] 'Edward the Fourth, by the grace of God,","King of England and France, and Lord of Ireland,' etc."],"id":817,"m":"(Reads) 'Edward the Fourth, by the grace of God, King of England and France, and Lord of Ireland,' and so on."},{"t":"sp","who":"MONTGOMERY","lines":["And whoso'er gainsays King Edward's right,","By this I challenge him to single fight."],"id":818,"m":"And whoever denies King Edward's right, I challenge him to single combat with this."},{"t":"sd","text":"Throws down gauntlet.","id":819},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["Long live Edward the Fourth!"],"id":820,"m":"Long live Edward the Fourth!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Thanks, brave Montgomery, and thanks unto you all;","If fortune serve me, I'll requite this kindness.","Now for this night let's harbour here in York;","And when the morning sun shall raise his car","Above the border of this horizon","We'll forward towards Warwick and his mates,","For well I wot that Henry is no soldier.—","Ah, froward Clarence! how evil it beseems thee","To flatter Henry and forsake thy brother!","Yet, as we may, we'll meet both thee and Warwick.—","Come on, brave soldiers; doubt not of the day,","And, that once gotten, doubt not of large pay."],"id":821,"m":"Thank you, brave Montgomery, and thanks to you all; if fortune favours me, I'll repay this kindness. Now let's stay here in York tonight; and when the morning sun lifts his chariot above the edge of the horizon, we'll go on towards Warwick and his allies, for I know Henry is no soldier. Ah, wayward Clarence! How badly it suits you to flatter Henry and abandon your brother! Still, we'll face both you and Warwick as best we can. Come on, brave soldiers; don't doubt we'll win the day, and once it's won, don't doubt you'll be well paid."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":822},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"VIII","loc":"London. The Palace.","id":823},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. Enter KING HENRY, WARWICK, CLARENCE, MONTAGUE, EXETER, and OXFORD.","id":824},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["What counsel, lords? Edward from Belgia,","With hasty Germans and blunt Hollanders,","Hath pass'd in safety through the narrow seas,","And with his troops doth march amain to London;","And many giddy people flock to him."],"id":825,"m":"What do you advise, lords? Edward has crossed the Channel safely from the Low Countries with impetuous Germans and rough Dutchmen, and is marching at full speed on London with his troops, and many fickle people are flocking to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Let's levy men and beat him back again."],"id":826,"m":"Let's raise troops and drive him back again."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["A little fire is quickly trodden out,","Which, being suffer'd, rivers cannot quench."],"id":827,"m":"A little fire is quickly stamped out, which, if it's allowed to burn, rivers can't put out."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["In Warwickshire I have true-hearted friends,","Not mutinous in peace, yet bold in war.","Those will I muster up;—and thou, son Clarence,","Shalt stir up in Suffolk, Norfolk, and in Kent","The knights and gentlemen to come with thee.—","Thou, brother Montague, in Buckingham,","Northampton, and in Leicestershire shalt find","Men well inclin'd to hear what thou command'st.—","And thou, brave Oxford, wondrous well belov'd,","In Oxfordshire shalt muster up thy friends.—","My sovereign, with the loving citizens,","Like to his island girt in with the ocean,","Or modest Dian circled with her nymphs,","Shall rest in London till we come to him.—","Fair lords, take leave and stand not to reply.—","Farewell, my sovereign."],"id":828,"m":"In Warwickshire I have loyal friends, peaceful in peacetime but bold in war; I'll call them up. And you, son Clarence, will rouse the knights and gentlemen in Suffolk, Norfolk and Kent to come with you. You, brother Montague, will find men in Buckingham, Northampton and Leicestershire who are willing to obey your orders. And you, brave Oxford, so wonderfully well loved, will gather your friends in Oxfordshire. My sovereign, with the loyal citizens, like his island surrounded by the ocean, or chaste Diana circled by her nymphs, will stay in London until we come back to him. Fair lords, say goodbye and don't stop to answer. Farewell, my sovereign."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Farewell, my Hector, and my Troy's true hope."],"id":829,"m":"Farewell, my Hector, the true hope of my Troy."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["In sign of truth I kiss your highness' hand."],"id":830,"m":"As a sign of my loyalty I kiss your highness's hand."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Well-minded Clarence, be thou fortunate!"],"id":831,"m":"Well-meaning Clarence, may you have good fortune!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MONTAGUE","lines":["Comfort, my lord;—and so I take my leave."],"id":832,"m":"Courage, my lord; and so I take my leave."},{"t":"sp","who":"OXFORD","lines":["And thus [kissing Henry's hand] I seal my truth, and bid adieu."],"id":833,"m":"And so (kissing Henry's hand) I seal my loyalty and say goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Sweet Oxford, and my loving Montague,","And all at once, once more a happy farewell."],"id":834,"m":"Sweet Oxford, and my loving Montague, and all of you together, once more a happy farewell."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Farewell, sweet lords; let's meet at Coventry."],"id":835,"m":"Farewell, sweet lords; let's meet at Coventry."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Warwick, Clarendon, Oxford, and Montague.","id":836},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Here at the palace will I rest a while.—","Cousin of Exeter, what thinks your lordship?","Methinks the power that Edward hath in field","Should not be able to encounter mine."],"id":837,"m":"I'll rest here at the palace for a while. Cousin Exeter, what does your lordship think? I think the army Edward has in the field shouldn't be able to stand up to mine."},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["The doubt is that he will seduce the rest."],"id":838,"m":"The worry is that he'll win over the rest."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["That's not my fear; my meed hath got me fame.","I have not stopp'd mine ears to their demands,","Nor posted off their suits with slow delays;","My pity hath been balm to heal their wounds,","My mildness hath allay'd their swelling griefs,","My mercy dried their water-flowing tears.","I have not been desirous of their wealth","Nor much oppress'd them with great subsidies,","Nor forward of revenge, though they much err'd;","Then, why should they love Edward more than me?","No, Exeter, these graces challenge grace;","And when the lion fawns upon the lamb","The lamb will never cease to follow him."],"id":839,"m":"That's not what I fear. My good deeds have made me well known. I haven't closed my ears to the people's requests, or put off their petitions with long delays. My pity has been a balm to heal their wounds, my gentleness has eased their swelling griefs, my mercy has dried their streaming tears. I haven't wanted their wealth, or burdened them much with heavy taxes, or been quick to take revenge, though they did much wrong. So why should they love Edward more than me? No, Exeter, such kindness earns kindness; and when the lion is gentle to the lamb, the lamb will never stop following him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Shout within 'A Lancaster! A Lancaster!'.","id":840},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["Hark, hark, my lord! what shouts are these?"],"id":841,"m":"Listen, listen, my lord! What shouts are these?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter KING EDWARD, GLOSTER, and Soldiers.","id":842},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Seize on the shame-fac'd Henry! bear him hence,","And once again proclaim us king of England.—","You are the fount that makes small brooks to flow.","Now stops thy spring; my sea shall suck them dry","And swell so much the higher by their ebb.—","Hence with him to the Tower! let him not speak.—"],"id":843,"m":"Seize the shamefaced Henry! Take him away, and proclaim us King of England once again. You are the spring that makes the little streams flow; now your spring is stopped, my sea will suck them dry and swell all the higher as they ebb. Off to the Tower with him! Don't let him speak."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt some with King Henry.","id":844},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","cont":1,"lines":["And, lords, towards Coventry bend we our course,","Where peremptory Warwick now remains.","The sun shines hot, and, if we use delay,","Cold biting winter mars our hop'd-for hay."],"id":845,"m":"And, lords, let's head for Coventry, where overbearing Warwick is now. The sun is hot, and if we delay, cold biting winter will spoil the hay we're hoping for."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Away betimes, before his forces join,","And take the great-grown traitor unawares.","Brave warriors, march amain towards Coventry."],"id":846,"m":"Let's go early, before his forces join up, and take the overgrown traitor by surprise. Brave warriors, march at full speed to Coventry."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":847},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"I","loc":"Coventry.","id":848},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter, upon the walls, WARWICK, the Mayor of Coventry, two Messengers, and others.","id":849},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Where is the post that came from valiant Oxford?—","How far hence is thy lord, mine honest fellow?"],"id":850,"m":"Where's the messenger who came from brave Oxford? How far away is your lord, my good fellow?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST MESSENGER","lines":["By this at Dunsmore, marching hitherward."],"id":851,"m":"At Dunsmore by now, marching this way."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["How far off is our brother Montague?","Where is the post that came from Montague?"],"id":852,"m":"How far away is our brother Montague? Where's the messenger who came from Montague?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND MESSENGER","lines":["By this at Daintry, with a puissant troop."],"id":853,"m":"At Daventry by now, with a powerful army."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter SIR JOHN SOMERVILLE.","id":854},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Say, Somerville, what says my loving son?","And, by thy guess, how nigh is Clarence now?"],"id":855,"m":"Tell me, Somerville, what does my loving son-in-law say? And how near do you guess Clarence is now?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERVILLE","lines":["At Southam I did leave him with his forces","And do expect him here some two hours hence."],"id":856,"m":"I left him at Southam with his forces, and I expect him here in about two hours."},{"t":"sd","text":"Drum heard.","id":857},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Then Clarence is at hand; I hear his drum."],"id":858,"m":"Then Clarence is close; I can hear his drum."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERVILLE","lines":["It is not his, my lord; here Southam lies.","The drum your honour hears marcheth from Warwick."],"id":859,"m":"It isn't his, my lord; Southam lies this way. The drum your honour hears is marching from Warwick."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Who should that be? belike, unlook'd-for friends."],"id":860,"m":"Who could that be? Friends we didn't expect, probably."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERVILLE","lines":["They are at hand, and you shall quickly know."],"id":861,"m":"They're close, and you'll soon find out."},{"t":"sd","text":"March. Flourish. Enter KING EDWARD, GLOSTER, and Forces.","id":862},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Go, trumpet, to the walls and sound a parle."],"id":863,"m":"Go, trumpeter, to the walls and sound for a parley."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["See how the surly Warwick mans the wall."],"id":864,"m":"Look how surly Warwick has manned the wall."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["O, unbid spite! Is sportful Edward come?","Where slept our scouts, or how are they seduc'd,","That we could hear no news of his repair?"],"id":865,"m":"O, unwelcome trouble! Has playboy Edward come? Where were our scouts sleeping, or how have they been bribed, that we heard no news of his coming?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Now, Warwick, wilt thou ope the city gates?","Speak gentle words and humbly bend thy knee,","Call Edward king and at his hands beg mercy?","And he shall pardon thee these outrages."],"id":866,"m":"Now, Warwick, will you open the city gates, speak politely, kneel humbly, call Edward king and beg him for mercy? Then he'll pardon you for these outrages."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Nay, rather, wilt thou draw thy forces hence,","Confess who set thee up and pluck'd thee down?","Call Warwick patron and be penitent,","And thou shalt still remain the Duke of York."],"id":867,"m":"No, rather will you take your forces away, admit who set you up and pulled you down, call Warwick your patron and repent? Then you can still remain Duke of York."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["I thought, at least, he would have said the king;","Or did he make the jest against his will?"],"id":868,"m":"I thought at least he'd say 'king'; or did he make the joke without meaning to?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Is not a dukedom, sir, a goodly gift?"],"id":869,"m":"Isn't a dukedom a fine gift, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Ay, by my faith, for a poor earl to give;","I'll do thee service for so good a gift."],"id":870,"m":"Yes, indeed, for a poor earl to give. I'll do you a favour in return for such a good gift."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["'T was I that gave the kingdom to thy brother."],"id":871,"m":"It was I who gave the kingdom to your brother."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Why, then, 't is mine, if but by Warwick's gift."],"id":872,"m":"Why, then, it's mine, if only by Warwick's gift."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Thou art no Atlas for so great a weight,","And, weakling, Warwick takes his gift again;","And Henry is my king, Warwick his subject."],"id":873,"m":"You're no Atlas to carry such a great weight; so, weakling, Warwick takes his gift back; and Henry is my king, and Warwick is his subject."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["But Warwick's king is Edward's prisoner;","And, gallant Warwick, do but answer this:","What is the body when the head is off?"],"id":874,"m":"But Warwick's king is Edward's prisoner. And, gallant Warwick, just answer this: what is the body when the head is cut off?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Alas! that Warwick had no more forecast,","But, whiles he thought to steal the single ten,","The king was slily finger'd from the deck!","You left poor Henry at the Bishop's palace,","And ten to one, you'll meet him in the Tower."],"id":875,"m":"Alas, that Warwick didn't think further ahead! While he was trying to steal a mere ten, the king was slyly taken from the deck! You left poor Henry at the bishop's palace, and ten to one you'll meet him in the Tower."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["'T is even so; yet you are Warwick still."],"id":876,"m":"That's exactly it; yet you're still Warwick."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Come, Warwick, take the time; kneel down, kneel down.","Nay, when? strike now, or else the iron cools."],"id":877,"m":"Come, Warwick, seize the moment; kneel, kneel. Well, when? Strike now, or the iron will cool."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["I had rather chop this hand off at a blow,","And with the other fling it at thy face,","Than bear so low a sail, to strike to thee."],"id":878,"m":"I'd rather chop this hand off with one blow and throw it in your face with the other than lower my sail so far as to surrender to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Sail how thou canst, have wind and tide thy friend,","This hand, fast wound about thy coal-black hair,","Shall, whiles thy head is warm and new cut off,","Write in the dust this sentence with thy blood,","'Wind-changing Warwick now can change no more.'"],"id":879,"m":"Sail however you can, with wind and tide on your side: this hand, wound tightly in your coal-black hair, will, while your head is still warm and freshly cut off, write this sentence in the dust with your blood: 'Warwick, who changed with the wind, can change no more.'"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter OXFORD, with Forces.","id":880},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["O cheerful colours! see where Oxford comes."],"id":881,"m":"O cheering flags! Look, here comes Oxford."},{"t":"sp","who":"OXFORD","lines":["Oxford, Oxford, for Lancaster!"],"id":882,"m":"Oxford, Oxford, for Lancaster!"},{"t":"sd","text":"He and his forces enter the city.","id":883},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["The gates are open; let us enter too."],"id":884,"m":"The gates are open; let's go in too."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["So other foes may set upon our backs.","Stand we in good array, for they no doubt","Will issue out again and bid us battle;","If not, the city being but of small defence,","We'll quietly rouse the traitors in the same."],"id":885,"m":"So other enemies can attack us from behind? Let's stay in good order, for they'll no doubt come out again and offer battle; if not, since the city has weak defences, we'll soon drive the traitors out of it."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["O, welcome, Oxford, for we want thy help."],"id":886,"m":"O, welcome, Oxford, for we need your help."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter MONTAGUE, with Forces.","id":887},{"t":"sp","who":"MONTAGUE","lines":["Montague, Montague, for Lancaster!"],"id":888,"m":"Montague, Montague, for Lancaster!"},{"t":"sd","text":"He and his forces enter the city.","id":889},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Thou and thy brother both shall buy this treason,","Even with the dearest blood your bodies bear."],"id":890,"m":"You and your brother will both pay for this treason with the most precious blood in your bodies."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["The harder match'd, the greater victory;","My mind presageth happy gain and conquest."],"id":891,"m":"The harder the match, the greater the victory. My mind foretells happy gain and conquest."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter SOMERSET, with forces.","id":892},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["Somerset, Somerset, for Lancaster!"],"id":893,"m":"Somerset, Somerset, for Lancaster!"},{"t":"sd","text":"He and his forces enter the city.","id":894},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Two of thy name, both Dukes of Somerset,","Have sold their lives unto the House of York;","And thou shalt be the third if this sword hold."],"id":895,"m":"Two of your name, both Dukes of Somerset, have paid with their lives to the house of York; and you'll be the third, if this sword holds out."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter CLARENCE, with Forces.","id":896},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["And lo, where George of Clarence sweeps along,","Of force enough to bid his brother battle;","With whom an upright zeal to right prevails,","More than the nature of a brother's love!—"],"id":897,"m":"And look, here comes George of Clarence, sweeping along with enough troops to fight his brother; in him an honest zeal for what is right outweighs a brother's natural love!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Gloster and Clarence whisper.","id":898},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","cont":1,"lines":["Come, Clarence, come; thou wilt if Warwick call."],"id":899,"m":"Come, Clarence, come; you will if Warwick calls."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["Father of Warwick, know you what this means?"],"id":900,"m":"Father-in-law Warwick, do you know what this means?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Taking the red rose out of his hat.","id":901},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","cont":1,"lines":["Look here, I throw my infamy at thee;","I will not ruinate my father's house,","Who gave his blood to lime the stones together,","And set up Lancaster. Why, trow'st thou, Warwick,","That Clarence is so harsh, so blunt, unnatural,","To bend the fatal instruments of war","Against his brother and his lawful king?","Perhaps thou wilt object my holy oath;","To keep that oath were more impiety","Than Jephtha's when he sacrific'd his daughter.","I am so sorry for my trespass made","That, to deserve well at my brother's hands,","I here proclaim myself thy mortal foe,","With resolution, whereso'er I meet thee—","As I will meet thee, if thou stir abroad—","To plague thee for thy foul misleading me.","And so, proud-hearted Warwick, I defy thee,","And to my brother turn my blushing cheeks.—","Pardon me, Edward, I will make amends;—","And, Richard, do not frown upon my faults,","For I will henceforth be no more unconstant."],"id":902,"m":"Look, I throw my disgrace at you. I will not ruin my father's house, which he cemented together with his blood, to set up Lancaster. Why, do you really think, Warwick, that Clarence is so harsh, blunt and unnatural as to turn the deadly weapons of war against his brother and lawful king? Perhaps you'll bring up my holy oath; to keep that oath would be more wicked than Jephthah's keeping his when he sacrificed his daughter. I'm so sorry for the wrong I've done that, to earn my brother's favour, I here declare myself your deadly enemy, determined, wherever I meet you, and I will meet you if you come out, to punish you for foully misleading me. And so, proud-hearted Warwick, I defy you, and turn my blushing cheeks to my brother. Forgive me, Edward, I'll make amends; and Richard, don't frown on my faults, for from now on I'll never be fickle again."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Now, welcome more, and ten times more belov'd,","Than if thou never hadst deserv'd our hate."],"id":903,"m":"Now you're more welcome, and ten times more loved, than if you'd never earned our hatred."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Welcome, good Clarence; this is brother-like."],"id":904,"m":"Welcome, good Clarence; this is how a brother should behave."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["O passing traitor, perjur'd and unjust!"],"id":905,"m":"O utter traitor, oath-breaker and unjust!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["What, Warwick, wilt thou leave the town and fight,","Or shall we beat the stones about thine ears?"],"id":906,"m":"What, Warwick, will you leave the town and fight, or shall we knock the stones down around your ears?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Alas! I am not coop'd here for defence;","I will away towards Barnet presently,","And bid thee battle, Edward, if thou dar'st."],"id":907,"m":"Alas! I'm not shut in here to defend myself. I'll go towards Barnet at once and give you battle, Edward, if you dare."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Yes, Warwick, Edward dares and leads the way.—","Lords, to the field! Saint George and victory!"],"id":908,"m":"Yes, Warwick, Edward dares, and leads the way. Lords, to the field! Saint George and victory!"},{"t":"sd","text":"March. Exeunt.","id":909},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"II","loc":"A Field of Battle near Barnet.","id":910},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum and excursions. Enter KING EDWARD, bringing in WARWICK wounded.","id":911},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["So, lie thou there; die thou, and die our fear,","For Warwick was a bug that fear'd us all.—","Now, Montague, sit fast; I seek for thee,","That Warwick's bones may keep thine company."],"id":912,"m":"So, lie there; die, and let our fear die with you, for Warwick was a bogeyman that frightened us all. Now, Montague, hold on tight; I'm coming for you, so Warwick's bones can keep you company."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":913},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Ah! who is nigh? come to me, friend or foe,","And tell me who is victor, York or Warwick.","Why ask I that? my mangled body shows;","My blood, my want of strength, my sick heart shows","That I must yield my body to the earth","And, by my fall, the conquest to my foe.","Thus yields the cedar to the axe's edge,","Whose arms gave shelter to the princely eagle,","Under whose shade the ramping lion slept,","Whose top-branch overpeer'd Jove's spreading tree,","And kept low shrubs from winter's pow'rful wind.","These eyes, that now are dimm'd with death's black veil,","Have been as piercing as the midday sun,","To search the secret treasons of the world;","The wrinkles in my brows, now fill'd with blood,","Were liken'd oft to kingly sepulchres,","For who liv'd king but I could dig his grave?","And who durst smile when Warwick bent his brow?","Lo, now my glory smear'd in dust and blood!","My parks, my walks, my manors that I had,","Even now forsake me, and of all my lands","Is nothing left me but my body's length.","Why, what is pomp, rule, reign, but earth and dust?","And live we how we can, yet die we must."],"id":914,"m":"Ah! Who's near? Come to me, friend or enemy, and tell me who has won, York or Warwick. Why do I ask? My mangled body shows it; my blood, my lack of strength, my sick heart show that I must give my body to the earth and, by my fall, the victory to my enemy. So the cedar falls to the axe's edge, whose arms sheltered the princely eagle, under whose shade the rearing lion slept, whose top branch looked down on Jove's spreading oak and protected low shrubs from winter's powerful wind. These eyes, now dimmed by death's black veil, have been as piercing as the midday sun in seeking out the world's secret treasons. The wrinkles on my brow, now filled with blood, were often compared to the graves of kings; for what living king was there whose grave I couldn't dig? And who dared smile when Warwick frowned? Look, now my glory is smeared with dust and blood! My parks, my walks, the manors I owned, are leaving me even now, and of all my lands nothing is left me but the length of my body. Why, what is pomp, rule and power but earth and dust? However we live, we must die."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter OXFORD and SOMERSET.","id":915},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["Ah, Warwick, Warwick! wert thou as we are,","We might recover all our loss again.","The queen from France hath brought a puissant power;","Even now we heard the news. Ah, couldst thou fly!"],"id":916,"m":"Ah, Warwick, Warwick! If you were as well as we are, we could still win back all we've lost. The queen has brought a powerful army from France; we heard the news just now. Ah, if only you could run!"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Why, then I would not fly.—Ah, Montague!","If thou be there, sweet brother, take my hand","And with thy lips keep in my soul awhile.","Thou lov'st me not; for, brother, if thou did'st,","Thy tears would wash this cold, congealed blood","That glues my lips and will not let me speak.","Come quickly, Montague, or I am dead."],"id":917,"m":"Why, even then I wouldn't run. Ah, Montague! If you're there, sweet brother, take my hand and hold my soul in a while longer with your lips. You don't love me; for, brother, if you did, your tears would wash away this cold, clotted blood that glues my lips shut and won't let me speak. Come quickly, Montague, or I'll be dead."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["Ah, Warwick, Montague hath breath'd his last,","And to the latest gasp cried out for Warwick,","And said 'Commend me to my valiant brother.'","And more he would have said, and more he spoke,","Which sounded like a clamour in a vault,","That might not be distinguish'd; but at last","I well might hear, delivered with a groan,—","'O farewell, Warwick!'"],"id":918,"m":"Ah, Warwick, Montague has breathed his last, and to his final gasp he called out for Warwick and said, 'Give my regards to my brave brother.' He would have said more, and did say more, but it sounded like noise echoing in a vault and couldn't be made out; but at last I clearly heard him say, with a groan, 'O farewell, Warwick!'"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Sweet rest his soul!—Fly, lords, and save yourselves;","For Warwick bids you all farewell, to meet in heaven."],"id":919,"m":"Rest his soul! Run, lords, and save yourselves; for Warwick bids you all farewell, to meet again in heaven."},{"t":"sd","text":"Dies.","id":920},{"t":"sp","who":"OXFORD","lines":["Away, away, to meet the queen's great power!"],"id":921,"m":"Away, away, to meet the queen's great army!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt bearing off Warwick's body.","id":922},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"III","loc":"Another Part of the Field.","id":923},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. Enter KING EDWARD in triumph; with CLARENCE, GLOSTER, and the rest.","id":924},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Thus far our fortune keeps an upward course,","And we are grac'd with wreaths of victory.","But, in the midst of this bright-shining day,","I spy a black, suspicious, threatening cloud,","That will encounter with our glorious sun","Ere he attain his easeful western bed.","I mean, my lords, those powers that the Queen","Hath rais'd in Gallia have arriv'd our coast","And, as we hear, march on to fight with us."],"id":925,"m":"So far our fortune keeps rising, and we're honoured with the garlands of victory. But in the middle of this bright day I can see a black, suspicious, threatening cloud that will clash with our glorious sun before he reaches his restful bed in the west. I mean, my lords, that the army the queen has raised in France has landed on our coast and, we hear, is marching to fight us."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["A little gale will soon disperse that cloud","And blow it to the source from whence it came;","Thy very beams will dry those vapours up,","For every cloud engenders not a storm."],"id":926,"m":"A little breeze will soon scatter that cloud and blow it back where it came from. Your own beams will dry up those mists, for not every cloud brings a storm."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["The queen is valued thirty thousand strong,","And Somerset, with Oxford, fled to her;","If she have time to breathe, be well assur'd,","Her faction will be full as strong as ours."],"id":927,"m":"The queen's army is reckoned at thirty thousand, and Somerset and Oxford have fled to her. If she has time to catch her breath, you can be sure her side will be just as strong as ours."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["We are advertis'd by our loving friends","That they do hold their course toward Tewkesbury.","We, having now the best at Barnet field,","Will thither straight, for willingness rids way;","And, as we march, our strength will be augmented","In every county as we go along.—","Strike up the drum! cry 'Courage!' and away."],"id":928,"m":"Our loyal friends tell us they're heading for Tewkesbury. Having won at Barnet, we'll go there at once, for eagerness shortens the road; and as we march, our forces will grow in every county we pass through. Strike up the drum! Shout 'Courage!' and away."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":929},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"IV","loc":"Plains near Tewkesbury.","id":930},{"t":"sd","text":"March. Enter QUEEN MARGARET, PRINCE EDWARD, SOMERSET, OXFORD, and Soldiers.","id":931},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Great lords, wise men ne'er sit and wail their loss,","But cheerly seek how to redress their harms.","What though the mast be now blown overboard,","The cable broke, the holding-anchor lost,","And half our sailors swallow'd in the flood?","Yet lives our pilot still. Is 't meet that he","Should leave the helm, and like a fearful lad","With tearful eyes add water to the sea,","And give more strength to that which hath too much,","Whiles in his moan the ship splits on the rock,","Which industry and courage might have sav'd?","Ah, what a shame! ah, what a fault were this!","Say Warwick was our anchor; what of that?","And Montague our topmast; what of him?","Our slaught'red friends the tackles; what of these?","Why, is not Oxford here another anchor,","And Somerset another goodly mast?","The friends of France our shrouds and tacklings?","And, though unskilful, why not Ned and I","For once allow'd the skilful pilot's charge?","We will not from the helm to sit and weep,","But keep our course, though the rough wind say no,","From shelves and rocks that threaten us with wrack,","As good to chide the waves as speak them fair.","And what is Edward but a ruthless sea?","What Clarence but a quicksand of deceit?","And Richard but a ragged fatal rock?","All these the enemies to our poor bark?","Say you can swim; alas, 't is but a while!","Tread on the sand; why, there you quickly sink;","Bestride the rock; the tide will wash you off,","Or else you famish,—that's a threefold death.","This speak I, lords, to let you understand,","If case some one of you would fly from us,","That there's no hop'd-for mercy with the brothers","More than with ruthless waves, with sands, and rocks.","Why, courage then! what cannot be avoided","'T were childish weakness to lament or fear."],"id":932,"m":"Great lords, wise men never sit and wail over their losses, but cheerfully look for ways to repair the damage. What if the mast is blown overboard, the cable broken, the anchor lost and half our sailors swallowed by the sea? Our pilot is still alive. Is it right for him to leave the helm and, like a frightened boy, add his tears to the sea, giving more strength to what already has too much, while the ship splits on the rock as he moans, when hard work and courage might have saved it? Ah, what a shame! Ah, what a fault that would be! Say Warwick was our anchor; what of it? And Montague our topmast; what of him? Our slaughtered friends the rigging; what of them? Why, isn't Oxford here another anchor, and Somerset another fine mast, and our French friends the ropes and rigging? And though we're unskilled, why shouldn't Ned and I be allowed to take the skilled pilot's place for once? We won't leave the helm to sit and weep, but keep our course, though the rough wind says no, away from the sandbanks and rocks that threaten to wreck us. Scolding the waves does as much good as speaking kindly to them. And what is Edward but a merciless sea? What is Clarence but a quicksand of deceit? And Richard but a jagged deadly rock? All of them enemies of our poor ship. Say you can swim: alas, only for a while! Step on the sand: you'll quickly sink. Climb on the rock: the tide will wash you off, or else you'll starve; that's a triple death. I say this, lords, to make you understand, in case any of you should want to desert us, that there's no more mercy to be hoped for from those brothers than from merciless waves, sands and rocks. So, courage! It would be childish weakness to mourn or fear what can't be avoided."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE","lines":["Methinks, a woman of this valiant spirit","Should, if a coward heard her speak these words,","Infuse his breast with magnanimity,","And make him, naked, foil a man at arms.","I speak not this as doubting any here;","For, did I but suspect a fearful man,","He should have leave to go away betimes,","Lest in our need he might infect another","And make him of the like spirit to himself.","If any such be here—as God forbid!—","Let him depart before we need his help."],"id":933,"m":"I think a woman of such brave spirit, if a coward heard her speak these words, would fill his heart with courage and make him, unarmed, defeat an armed man. I don't say this because I doubt anyone here; for if I suspected a single frightened man, he'd be allowed to leave in good time, so that when we need him he doesn't infect another and make him as fearful as himself. If there's anyone like that here, which God forbid, let him leave before we need his help."},{"t":"sp","who":"OXFORD","lines":["Women and children of so high a courage,","And warriors faint! why, 't were perpetual shame.—","O, brave young prince! thy famous grandfather","Doth live again in thee; long mayst thou live","To bear his image and renew his glories!"],"id":934,"m":"Women and children with such great courage, and warriors fainting! Why, that would be an everlasting shame. O brave young prince! Your famous grandfather lives again in you; may you live long to carry his likeness and renew his glories!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["And he that will not fight for such a hope,","Go home to bed, and like the owl by day,","If he arise, be mock'd and wonder'd at."],"id":935,"m":"And anyone who won't fight for such a hope, let him go home to bed, and if he gets up, be mocked and stared at like an owl by day."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Thanks, gentle Somerset.—Sweet Oxford, thanks."],"id":936,"m":"Thank you, gentle Somerset. Sweet Oxford, thank you."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE","lines":["And take his thanks that yet hath nothing else."],"id":937,"m":"And take the thanks of one who has nothing else yet."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Messenger.","id":938},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["Prepare you, lords, for Edward is at hand","Ready to fight; therefore be resolute."],"id":939,"m":"Get ready, lords, for Edward is close and ready to fight; so be resolute."},{"t":"sp","who":"OXFORD","lines":["I thought no less; it is his policy","To haste thus fast, to find us unprovided."],"id":940,"m":"I expected as much. It's his tactic to hurry like this, to catch us unprepared."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["But he's deceiv'd; we are in readiness."],"id":941,"m":"But he's mistaken; we're ready."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["This cheers my heart, to see your forwardness."],"id":942,"m":"It cheers my heart to see your eagerness."},{"t":"sp","who":"OXFORD","lines":["Here pitch our battle; hence we will not budge."],"id":943,"m":"Set out our battle lines here; we won't move from this spot."},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish and march. Enter KING EDWARD, CLARENCE, GLOSTER, and Forces.","id":944},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Brave followers, yonder stands the thorny wood","Which, by the heaven's assistance and your strength,","Must by the roots be hewn up yet ere night.","I need not add more fuel to your fire,","For, well I wot, ye blaze to burn them out.","Give signal to the fight, and to it, lords."],"id":945,"m":"Brave followers, over there stands the thorny wood which, with heaven's help and your strength, must be pulled up by the roots before nightfall. I don't need to add fuel to your fire, for I know well you're blazing to burn them out. Give the signal to fight, and at them, lords."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Lords, knights, and gentlemen, what I should say,","My tears gainsay; for every word I speak,","Ye see I drink the water of my eyes.","Therefore, no more but this: Henry, your sovereign,","Is prisoner to the foe, his state usurp'd,","His realm a slaughter-house, his subjects slain,","His statutes cancell'd, and his treasure spent;","And yonder is the wolf that makes this spoil.","You fight in justice; then, in God's name, lords,","Be valiant and give signal to the fight."],"id":946,"m":"Lords, knights and gentlemen, my tears contradict what I ought to say; for with every word I speak, you can see I'm drinking the water of my eyes. So, only this: Henry, your sovereign, is the enemy's prisoner, his throne usurped, his kingdom a slaughterhouse, his subjects killed, his laws cancelled and his treasure spent; and over there is the wolf that does this damage. You're fighting for justice; so, in God's name, lords, be brave and give the signal to fight."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt both armies.","id":947},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"V","loc":"Another part of the Field.","id":948},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. Enter KING EDWARD, CLARENCE, GLOSTER, and Forces; With QUEEN MARGARET, OXFORD, and SOMERSET, as prisoners.","id":949},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Now, here a period of tumultuous broils.","Away with Oxford to Hames Castle straight;","For Somerset, off with his guilty head.","Go, bear them hence; I will not hear them speak."],"id":950,"m":"Now here is the end of these violent quarrels. Take Oxford away to Hammes Castle at once; as for Somerset, off with his guilty head. Go, take them away; I won't hear them speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"OXFORD","lines":["For my part, I'll not trouble thee with words."],"id":951,"m":"For my part, I won't trouble you with words."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["Nor I, but stoop with patience to my fortune."],"id":952,"m":"Nor will I; I'll bow patiently to my fortune."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Oxford and Somerset, guarded.","id":953},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["So part we sadly in this troublous world,","To meet with joy in sweet Jerusalem."],"id":954,"m":"So we part sadly in this troubled world, to meet with joy in the heavenly Jerusalem."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Is proclamation made that who finds Edward","Shall have a high reward, and he his life?"],"id":955,"m":"Has it been proclaimed that whoever finds Edward will have a large reward, and he will have his life?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["It is; and lo, where youthful Edward comes!"],"id":956,"m":"It has; and look, here comes young Edward!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter soldiers with PRINCE EDWARD.","id":957},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Bring forth the gallant; let us hear him speak.","What! can so young a man begin to prick?—","Edward, what satisfaction canst thou make","For bearing arms, for stirring up my subjects,","And all the trouble thou hast turn'd me to?"],"id":958,"m":"Bring out the young hero; let's hear him speak. What! Can someone so young start to sprout thorns already? Edward, what amends can you make for taking up arms, stirring up my subjects and all the trouble you've caused me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE","lines":["Speak like a subject, proud, ambitious York!","Suppose that I am now my father's mouth;","Resign thy chair, and where I stand kneel thou,","Whilst I propose the selfsame words to thee","Which, traitor, thou wouldst have me answer to."],"id":959,"m":"Speak like a subject, proud, ambitious York! Imagine I'm now speaking for my father: give up your throne, and kneel where I stand, while I put to you the very questions, traitor, that you want me to answer."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Ah, thy father had been so resolv'd!"],"id":960,"m":"Ah, if only your father had been so resolute!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["That you might still have worn the petticoat,","And ne'er have stol'n the breech from Lancaster."],"id":961,"m":"Then you could have kept wearing the petticoat and never stolen the trousers from Lancaster."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE","lines":["Let Aesop fable in a winter's night;","His currish riddle sorts not with this place."],"id":962,"m":"Let Aesop tell his fables on a winter's night; his dog's riddles don't suit this place."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["By heaven, brat, I'll plague you for that word."],"id":963,"m":"By heaven, brat, I'll plague you for that word."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Ay, thou wast born to be a plague to men."],"id":964,"m":"Yes, you were born to be a plague to men."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["For God's sake, take away this captive scold."],"id":965,"m":"For God's sake, take away this captive nag."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE","lines":["Nay, take away this scolding crook-back rather."],"id":966,"m":"No, take away this nagging hunchback instead."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Peace, wilful boy, or I will charm your tongue."],"id":967,"m":"Quiet, stubborn boy, or I'll silence your tongue."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["Untutor'd lad, thou art too malapert."],"id":968,"m":"Ill-taught boy, you're too impudent."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE","lines":["I know my duty; you are all undutiful.","Lascivious Edward,—and thou perjur'd George,—","And thou misshapen Dick,—I tell ye all,","I am your better, traitors as ye are;—","And thou usurp'st my father's right and mine."],"id":969,"m":"I know my duty; you're all disloyal. Lustful Edward, and you, oath-breaking George, and you, misshapen Dick, I tell you all I'm your superior, traitors that you are; and you are stealing my father's right and mine."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Take that, the likeness of this railer here."],"id":970,"m":"Take that, you image of this railing woman here."},{"t":"sd","text":"Stabs him.","id":971},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Sprawl'st thou? take that, to end thy agony."],"id":972,"m":"Are you sprawling? Take that, to end your agony."},{"t":"sd","text":"Stabs him.","id":973},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["And there's for twitting me with perjury."],"id":974,"m":"And that's for taunting me with breaking my oath."},{"t":"sd","text":"Stabs him.","id":975},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["O, kill me too!"],"id":976,"m":"O, kill me too!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Marry, and shall."],"id":977,"m":"Indeed, I will."},{"t":"sd","text":"Offers to kill her.","id":978},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Hold, Richard, hold! for we have done too much."],"id":979,"m":"Stop, Richard, stop! We've done too much."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Why should she live to fill the world with words?"],"id":980,"m":"Why should she live to fill the world with words?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["What! doth she swoon? use means for her recovery."],"id":981,"m":"What! Has she fainted? Do what you can to revive her."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Clarence, excuse me to the king, my brother.","I'll hence to London on a serious matter;","Ere ye come there, be sure to hear some news."],"id":982,"m":"Clarence, make my excuses to the king, my brother. I'm going to London on serious business; before you get there, you can be sure you'll hear some news."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["What? what?"],"id":983,"m":"What? What?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["The Tower! the Tower!"],"id":984,"m":"The Tower! The Tower!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":985},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["O Ned! sweet Ned! speak to thy mother, boy.","Canst thou not speak?—O traitors! murtherers!","They that stabb'd Caesar shed no blood at all,","Did not offend, nor were not worthy blame,","If this foul deed were by to equal it.","He was a man: this, in respect, a child,","And men ne'er spend their fury on a child.","What's worse than murtherer, that I may name it?","No, no, my heart will burst, an if I speak;","And I will speak, that so my heart may burst.—","Butchers and villains! bloody cannibals!","How sweet a plant have you untimely cropp'd!","You have no children, butchers! if you had,","The thought of them would have stirr'd up remorse;","But, if you ever chance to have a child,","Look in his youth to have him so cut off","As, deathsmen, you have rid this sweet young prince!"],"id":986,"m":"O Ned! Sweet Ned! Speak to your mother, boy. Can't you speak? O traitors! Murderers! Those who stabbed Caesar shed no blood at all, did no wrong and deserved no blame, if this foul deed were set beside it. He was a man; this, by comparison, a child, and men never vent their fury on a child. What's worse than a murderer, so I can call you it? No, no, my heart will burst if I speak; and I will speak, so that my heart will burst. Butchers and villains! Bloody cannibals! What a sweet plant you've cut down before its time! You have no children, butchers! If you had, the thought of them would have stirred up pity; but if you ever happen to have a child, expect to see him cut off in his youth, as you, executioners, have disposed of this sweet young prince!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Away with her! go, bear her hence perforce."],"id":987,"m":"Away with her! Go, take her away by force."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Nay, never bear me hence, dispatch me here;","Here sheathe thy sword, I'll pardon thee my death.","What! wilt thou not?—then, Clarence, do it thou."],"id":988,"m":"No, don't take me away; finish me here. Sheathe your sword here; I'll forgive you my death. What! Won't you? Then, Clarence, you do it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["By heaven, I will not do thee so much ease."],"id":989,"m":"By heaven, I won't give you that much comfort."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Good Clarence, do; sweet Clarence, do thou do it."],"id":990,"m":"Good Clarence, do; sweet Clarence, do it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["Didst thou not hear me swear I would not do it?"],"id":991,"m":"Didn't you hear me swear I wouldn't do it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["Ay, but thou usest to forswear thyself;","'T was sin before, but now 't is charity.","What! wilt thou not? where is that devil's butcher,","Hard-favour'd Richard?—Richard, where art thou?","Thou art not here; murther is thy alms-deed,","Petitioners for blood thou ne'er putt'st back."],"id":992,"m":"Yes, but you're in the habit of breaking your oaths. It was a sin before, but now it would be a kindness. What! Won't you? Where's that devil's butcher, ugly Richard? Richard, where are you? You're not here; murder is your act of charity; you never turn away anyone who asks for blood."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Away, I say! I charge ye, bear her hence."],"id":993,"m":"Away, I say! I order you, take her away."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","lines":["So come to you and yours as to this prince!"],"id":994,"m":"May the same happen to you and yours as to this prince!"},{"t":"sd","text":"She is taken out.","id":995},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Where's Richard gone?"],"id":996,"m":"Where has Richard gone?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["To London, all in post, and, as I guess,","To make a bloody supper in the Tower."],"id":997,"m":"To London, at full speed; and, I guess, to make a bloody supper in the Tower."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["He's sudden if a thing comes in his head.","Now march we hence; discharge the common sort","With pay and thanks, and let's away to London,","And see our gentle queen how well she fares.","By this, I hope, she hath a son for me."],"id":998,"m":"He acts suddenly when a thing comes into his head. Now let's march away. Dismiss the common soldiers with pay and thanks, and let's go to London to see how our gentle queen is. By now, I hope, she has had a son for me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":999},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"VI","loc":"London. The Tower.","id":1000},{"t":"sd","text":"KING HENRY is discovered sitting with a book in his hand, the Lieutenant attending. Enter GLOSTER.","id":1001},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Good day, my lord. What! at your book so hard?"],"id":1002,"m":"Good day, my lord. What! Studying your book so hard?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Ay, my good lord;—my lord, I should say rather.","'T is sin to flatter; 'good' was little better.","Good Gloster and good devil were alike,","And both preposterous; therefore, not good lord."],"id":1003,"m":"Yes, my good lord; my lord, I should say rather. It's a sin to flatter, and 'good' was little better. 'Good Gloucester' and 'good devil' would be the same, and both absurd; so not 'good lord'."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Sirrah, leave us to ourselves; we must confer."],"id":1004,"m":"Fellow, leave us alone; we must talk."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Lieutenant.","id":1005},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["So flies the reckless shepherd from the wolf;","So first the harmless sheep doth yield his fleece,","And next his throat unto the butcher's knife.—","What scene of death hath Roscius now to act?"],"id":1006,"m":"So the careless shepherd runs away from the wolf; so the harmless sheep first gives up his fleece, and next his throat to the butcher's knife. What death scene has Roscius come to act now?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Suspicion always haunts the guilty mind;","The thief doth fear each bush an officer."],"id":1007,"m":"Suspicion always haunts the guilty mind; the thief fears every bush is a constable."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["The bird that hath been limed in a bush","With trembling wings misdoubteth every bush;","And I, the hapless male to one sweet bird,","Have now the fatal object in my eye","Where my poor young was lim'd, was caught, and kill'd."],"id":1008,"m":"The bird that has been caught with birdlime in a bush fears every bush with trembling wings; and I, the unhappy father of one sweet bird, now have before my eyes the deadly thing by which my poor young one was trapped, caught and killed."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Why, what a peevish fool was that of Crete","That taught his son the office of a fowl!","And yet, for all his wings, the fool was drown'd."],"id":1009,"m":"Why, what a foolish idiot that man from Crete was, who taught his son to fly like a bird! And for all his wings, the fool was drowned."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["I, Daedalus; my poor boy, Icarus;","Thy father, Minos, that denied our course;","The sun that sear'd the wings of my sweet boy,","Thy brother Edward; and thyself, the sea","Whose envious gulf did swallow up his life.","Ah, kill me with thy weapon, not with words!","My breast can better brook thy dagger's point","Than can my ears that tragic history.","But wherefore dost thou come? is 't for my life?"],"id":1010,"m":"I am Daedalus; my poor boy, Icarus; your father, Minos, who blocked our way; the sun that scorched my sweet boy's wings, your brother Edward; and you yourself the sea, whose spiteful depths swallowed up his life. Ah, kill me with your weapon, not with words! My heart can stand your dagger's point better than my ears can stand that tragic story. But why have you come? Is it for my life?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Think'st thou I am an executioner?"],"id":1011,"m":"Do you think I'm an executioner?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["A persecutor, I am sure, thou art;","If murdering innocents be executing,","Why, then thou art an executioner."],"id":1012,"m":"A persecutor, I'm sure you are. If murdering innocent people is execution, then you're an executioner."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["Thy son I kill'd for his presumption."],"id":1013,"m":"I killed your son for his arrogance."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Hadst thou been kill'd when first thou didst presume,","Thou hadst not liv'd to kill a son of mine.","And thus I prophesy,—that many a thousand,","Which now mistrust no parcel of my fear,","And many an old man's sigh and many a widow's,","And many an orphan's water-standing eye,—","Men for their sons', wives for their husbands' fate,","And orphans for their parents' timeless death,—","Shall rue the hour that ever thou wast born.","The owl shriek'd at thy birth, an evil sign;","The night-crow cried, aboding luckless time;","Dogs howl'd, and hideous tempest shook down trees;","The raven rook'd her on the chimney's top,","And chatt'ring pies in dismal discord sung.","Thy mother felt more than a mother's pain,","And yet brought forth less than a mother's hope,","An indigested and deformed lump,","Not like the fruit of such a goodly tree.","Teeth hadst thou in thy head when thou wast born,","To signify thou cam'st to bite the world;","And, if the rest be true which I have heard,","Thou cam'st—"],"id":1014,"m":"If you had been killed when you first acted arrogantly, you wouldn't have lived to kill a son of mine. And so I prophesy that many thousands, who now don't suspect any of what I fear, and many an old man's sigh and many a widow's, and many an orphan's tear-filled eye, men for their sons', wives for their husbands' fate, and orphans for their parents' untimely death, will regret the hour you were ever born. The owl shrieked at your birth, an evil sign; the night-crow cried, foretelling unlucky times; dogs howled, and a hideous storm shook down trees; the raven perched on the chimney top, and chattering magpies sang in dismal discord. Your mother felt more than a mother's pain, and yet gave birth to less than a mother hopes for: a shapeless, deformed lump, not like the fruit of such a fine tree. You had teeth in your head when you were born, to show you came to bite the world; and if the rest is true that I've heard, you came—"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["I'll hear no more. Die, prophet, in thy speech."],"id":1015,"m":"I'll hear no more. Die, prophet, in the middle of your speech."},{"t":"sd","text":"Stabs him.","id":1016},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","cont":1,"lines":["For this, amongst the rest, was I ordain'd."],"id":1017,"m":"I was destined for this, among other things."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Ay, and for much more slaughter after this.","O, God forgive my sins, and pardon thee!"],"id":1018,"m":"Yes, and for much more slaughter after this. O God, forgive my sins, and pardon you!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Dies.","id":1019},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["What! will the aspiring blood of Lancaster","Sink in the ground? I thought it would have mounted.","See, how my sword weeps for the poor King's death!","O, may such purple tears be always shed","From those that wish the downfall of our house!—","If any spark of life be yet remaining,","Down, down to hell; and say I sent thee thither,"],"id":1020,"m":"What! Will the ambitious blood of Lancaster sink into the ground? I thought it would rise. See how my sword weeps for the poor king's death! O, may such red tears always be shed from those who wish for the downfall of our house! If any spark of life is still left, down, down to hell, and say I sent you there,"},{"t":"sd","text":"Stabs him again.","id":1021},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","cont":1,"lines":["I, that have neither pity, love, nor fear.","Indeed, 't is true that Henry told me of;","For I have often heard my mother say","I came into the world with my legs forward.","Had I not reason, think ye, to make haste","And seek their ruin that usurp'd our right?","The midwife wonder'd; and the women cried","'O, Jesus bless us, he is born with teeth!'","And so I was, which plainly signified","That I should snarl and bite and play the dog.","Then, since the heavens have shap'd my body so,","Let hell make crook'd my mind to answer it.","I have no brother, I am like no brother,","And this word 'love,' which greybeards call divine,","Be resident in men like one another,","And not in me! I am myself alone.—","Clarence, beware! thou keep'st me from the light;","But I will sort a pitchy day for thee;","For I will buzz abroad such prophecies","That Edward shall be fearful of his life,","And then, to purge his fear, I'll be thy death.","King Henry and the prince his son are gone;","Clarence, thy turn is next, and then the rest,","Counting myself but bad till I be best.","I'll throw thy body in another room,","And triumph, Henry, in thy day of doom."],"id":1022,"m":"I, who have neither pity, love nor fear. Indeed, what Henry told me is true; for I've often heard my mother say I came into the world feet first. Don't you think I had reason to hurry and seek the ruin of those who stole our right? The midwife was amazed, and the women cried, 'O Jesus bless us, he's born with teeth!' And so I was, which clearly meant that I should snarl and bite and play the dog. Then, since the heavens have shaped my body like this, let hell twist my mind to match it. I have no brother; I'm like no brother; and let this word 'love', which old men call divine, live in men who are like one another, and not in me! I am myself alone. Clarence, beware! You keep me from the light; but I'll arrange a pitch-black day for you; for I'll spread such prophecies about that Edward will fear for his life, and then, to cure his fear, I'll be your death. King Henry and his son the prince are gone; Clarence, your turn is next, and then the rest, for I count myself nothing until I'm the best. I'll throw your body in another room, and gloat, Henry, on your day of doom."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit with the body.","id":1023},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"VII","loc":"London. The Palace.","id":1024},{"t":"sd","text":"KING EDWARD is discovered sitting on his throne; QUEEN ELIZABETH with the infant Prince, CLARENCE, Gloster, HASTINGS, and others, near him.","id":1025},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Once more we sit in England's royal throne,","Re-purchas'd with the blood of enemies.","What valiant foemen, like to autumn's corn,","Have we mow'd down in tops of all their pride!","Three Dukes of Somerset, threefold renown'd","For hardy and undoubted champions;","Two Cliffords, as the father and the son;","And two Northumberlands,—two braver men","Ne'er spurr'd their coursers at the trumpet's sound;","With them the two brave bears, Warwick and Montague,","That in their chains fetter'd the kingly lion","And made the forest tremble when they roar'd.","Thus have we swept suspicion from our seat","And made our footstool of security.—","Come hither, Bess, and let me kiss my boy.—","Young Ned, for thee thine uncles and myself","Have in our armours watch'd the winter's night,","Went all afoot in summer's scalding heat,","That thou mightst repossess the crown in peace;","And of our labours thou shalt reap the gain."],"id":1026,"m":"Once more we sit on England's royal throne, bought back with our enemies' blood. What brave enemies we have mown down at the height of their pride, like corn in autumn! Three Dukes of Somerset, three times famous as bold and unquestioned champions; two Cliffords, father and son; and two Northumberlands: two braver men never spurred their horses at the sound of the trumpet; and with them the two brave bears, Warwick and Montague, who chained up the royal lion and made the forest tremble when they roared. So we've swept all threats from our throne and made security our footstool. Come here, Bess, and let me kiss my boy. Young Ned, for your sake your uncles and I have kept watch in our armour through winter nights and marched on foot in the scalding summer heat, so that you could inherit the crown in peace; and you'll reap the profit of our labours."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["[Aside] I'll blast his harvest if your head were laid;","For yet I am not look'd on in the world.","This shoulder was ordain'd so thick to heave;","And heave it shall some weight or break my back.—","Work thou the way,—and that shall execute."],"id":1027,"m":"(Aside) I'll blight his harvest once your head is laid low; for people don't take notice of me yet. This shoulder was made so thick to lift, and it will lift some weight or break my back. (Touching his head) You plan the way, (touching his hand) and this will carry it out."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Clarence and Gloster, love my lovely queen;","And kiss your princely nephew, brothers both."],"id":1028,"m":"Clarence and Gloucester, love my lovely queen; and kiss your princely nephew, both of you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["The duty that I owe unto your Majesty","I seal upon the lips of this sweet babe."],"id":1029,"m":"I seal the duty I owe your majesty on the lips of this sweet baby."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","lines":["Thanks, noble Clarence; worthy brother, thanks."],"id":1030,"m":"Thank you, noble Clarence; worthy brother, thank you."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOSTER","lines":["And, that I love the tree from whence thou sprang'st,","Witness the loving kiss I give the fruit.—","[Aside] To say the truth, so Judas kiss'd his Master,","And cried, all hail! when as he meant all harm."],"id":1031,"m":"And as proof that I love the tree you grew from, see the loving kiss I give its fruit. (Aside) To tell the truth, that's how Judas kissed his master and cried 'All hail!' when he meant nothing but harm."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Now am I seated as my soul delights;","Having my country's peace and brothers' loves."],"id":1032,"m":"Now I'm on the throne just as my soul desires, with my country at peace and my brothers' love."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["What will your Grace have done with Margaret?","Reignier, her father, to the King of France","Hath pawn'd the Sicils and Jerusalem,","And hither have they sent it for her ransom."],"id":1033,"m":"What does your grace want done with Margaret? Reignier, her father, has mortgaged Sicily and Jerusalem to the King of France and sent the money here for her ransom."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING EDWARD","lines":["Away with her and waft her hence to France.—","And now what rests but that we spend the time","With stately triumphs, mirthful comic shows,","Such as befits the pleasure of the court?","Sound drums and trumpets!—farewell sour annoy!","For here, I hope, begins our lasting joy."],"id":1034,"m":"Away with her; ship her off to France. And now what's left but to spend the time in grand celebrations and merry comic shows, as befits the pleasures of the court? Sound drums and trumpets! Goodbye, bitter trouble! For here, I hope, our lasting joy begins."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1035}];
const STUDY={"chars":[{"name":"King Henry VI","group":"The house of Lancaster","desc":"Gentle, pious and unable to rule. Faced with York on his throne, he admits to himself 'my title's weak' and buys peace by disinheriting his own son, which drives Margaret to leave him. Sent away from the battle at Towton, he sits on a molehill wishing he were a shepherd and watches a son who has killed his father and a father who has killed his son. Captured in a northern forest, freed by Warwick, he hands the government to Warwick and Clarence, blesses young Richmond as 'England's hope', and is taken again by Edward. In the Tower he prophesies the misery Richard will cause, and Richard stabs him."},{"name":"Queen Margaret","group":"The house of Lancaster","desc":"Henry's French wife, who leads Lancaster's armies while he prays. She divorces herself from his bed until the entail is repealed, wins at Wakefield and St Albans, and torments the captured York with a paper crown and a napkin soaked in his son Rutland's blood before stabbing him. She seeks help in France, is outdone by Warwick until Edward's marriage turns him to her side, and returns to fight at Tewkesbury, rallying her lords with the image of a ship without its mast. Captured, she watches her son stabbed to death, begs to be killed, and is ransomed back to France."},{"name":"Earl of Warwick","group":"The house of York, then Lancaster","desc":"The 'setter-up and plucker-down of kings'. He puts York on the throne in the first scene, makes Edward king after Towton, and goes to France to win him Lady Bona. When Edward marries Lady Grey instead, Warwick turns, joins Margaret, seals the alliance by betrothing his daughter to Prince Edward, captures Edward in his tent and restores Henry. Edward escapes, outmanoeuvres him at Coventry and wins Clarence back. Dying at Barnet, Warwick compares himself to a fallen cedar: 'what is pomp, rule, reign, but earth and dust?'"},{"name":"Richard Plantagenet, Duke of York","group":"The house of York","desc":"Claimant to the crown through Lionel, Duke of Clarence. He sits on Henry's throne and accepts the entail, then lets his son Richard argue him out of his oath. Defeated at Wakefield, he is set on a molehill by Margaret, crowned with paper and given Rutland's blood to dry his tears. His great speech of defiance ('She-wolf of France') moves even Northumberland to tears before Clifford and Margaret stab him. His head is set on the gates of York."},{"name":"Edward, Earl of March, later King Edward IV","group":"The house of York","desc":"York's eldest son: brave in battle, easily led by desire. Warwick makes him king after Towton, but he throws away the French match to marry Lady Grey and gives her family the best heirs, losing Warwick, Clarence and France at once. Captured and uncrowned, he escapes, returns with help from Burgundy, claims only his dukedom at York until Montgomery forces him to proclaim himself king, and wins at Barnet and Tewkesbury. He is first to stab Prince Edward. The play ends with his feast of 'lasting joy', which Richard is already plotting to poison."},{"name":"Richard, later Duke of Gloucester","group":"The house of York","desc":"York's crooked-backed son, the future Richard III. He kills Somerset, talks his father out of his oath, swears to avenge York and Rutland and hunts Clifford. Created Duke of Gloucester, he watches Edward's wooing with scorn and, in a long soliloquy (3.2), lays out his road to the crown: he can 'smile, and murther while I smile'. He stays with Edward only for the crown, rescues him from captivity, wins Clarence back, stabs Prince Edward and murders Henry in the Tower. Alone, he says 'I am myself alone' and names Clarence as next."},{"name":"George, later Duke of Clarence","group":"The house of York","desc":"York's third son, sent from Burgundy to join his brothers before Towton. Offended by Edward's marriage and by the heiresses given to the queen's family, he goes over to Warwick and marries his younger daughter, and is named joint Protector with Warwick. At Coventry, after a whispered word with Richard, he pulls the red rose from his hat and returns to Edward. He stabs Prince Edward. Richard marks him as the next to die."},{"name":"Lord Clifford","group":"The house of Lancaster","desc":"Son of the Clifford whom York killed at St Albans, he has sworn to wipe out York's line. He murders the boy Rutland ('Thy father slew my father; therefore, die') and helps kill York. He tells Henry that even beasts defend their young. Wounded at Towton, he dies blaming Henry's softness for the war; the Yorkists mock his corpse and set his head where York's had been."},{"name":"Edward, Prince of Wales","group":"The house of Lancaster","desc":"Henry and Margaret's son. He refuses to stay with the father who disinherited him, is knighted before Towton, and is betrothed to Warwick's daughter in France. At Tewkesbury his courage moves Oxford to call him his grandfather Henry V reborn. Captured, he tells the three brothers to kneel to him and calls them lascivious, perjured and misshapen; Edward, Richard and Clarence stab him in turn in front of his mother."},{"name":"Lady Grey, later Queen Elizabeth","group":"The house of York","desc":"A widow whose husband died fighting for York at St Albans. She asks Edward for her husband's lands; he offers them for her body; she refuses ('I had rather lie in prison'), and he marries her instead. As queen she asks the lords to accept her. When Edward is captured she takes sanctuary to protect the child she is carrying. She appears at the end with the infant prince."},{"name":"Earl of Rutland","group":"The house of York","desc":"York's youngest son, a boy with his tutor. He begs Clifford for his life, offering to live in prison, and is stabbed. His blood on Margaret's napkin becomes the symbol of the play's cruelty."},{"name":"King Lewis XI of France","group":"The French","desc":"He welcomes Margaret but agrees to give his sister Bona to Edward after Warwick's assurances. When the post brings news of Edward's marriage, he joins Warwick and Margaret and sends troops to England."},{"name":"Lady Bona","group":"The French","desc":"King Lewis's sister, offered to Edward through Warwick. Jilted, she joins the alliance against Edward and sends word that she will 'wear the willow garland for his sake'."},{"name":"Marquess of Montague","group":"The house of York, then Lancaster","desc":"Warwick's brother. He fights for York at first and swears loyalty to Edward after Clarence leaves, but follows Warwick back to Lancaster without explanation and dies at Barnet calling for his brother."},{"name":"Earl of Oxford","group":"The house of Lancaster","desc":"A loyal Lancastrian whose father and brother were executed by Edward. He argues Henry's descent before King Lewis, fights at Barnet and Tewkesbury, and is sent prisoner to Hammes Castle."},{"name":"Duke of Somerset","group":"The house of Lancaster","desc":"He leaves Edward's court with Clarence after the marriage, guards the captured Edward, protects young Richmond and sends him to Brittany. Captured at Tewkesbury, he is beheaded: Richard calls him the third Duke of Somerset to die by the house of York."},{"name":"Duke of Exeter","group":"The house of Lancaster","desc":"Henry's kinsman. In 1.1 his conscience tells him York is the lawful king, but he stays with Henry, escapes with him after Towton, and is with him when Edward seizes him in the palace."},{"name":"Earl of Northumberland","group":"The house of Lancaster","desc":"A northern lord whose father York killed. He helps capture York but is moved to tears by his grief, and is rebuked by Margaret for it."},{"name":"Lord Hastings","group":"The house of York","desc":"Edward's chief friend. He argues that England is safe 'if true within itself', helps rescue Edward from the archbishop's park, and tricks the Mayor of York into opening the gates."},{"name":"Henry, Earl of Richmond","group":"The house of Lancaster","desc":"A boy (no lines) whom Henry blesses as 'England's hope', saying his head was made to wear a crown. Somerset sends him to Brittany for safety. He is the future Henry VII, who defeats Richard in Richard III."},{"name":"The Son and the Father","group":"The commons","desc":"Two soldiers at Towton: one finds he has killed his father, the other that he has killed his only son. Henry, watching, sees the red rose and the white in the dead son's face."},{"name":"Sir John Montgomery","group":"The house of York","desc":"He brings troops to Edward at York but will serve only a king, not a duke, and forces Edward to proclaim himself."},{"name":"Earl Rivers","group":"The house of York","desc":"Lady Grey's brother, who stands by her when Edward is captured."}],"scenes":{"a1s1":{"sum":"Fresh from victory at St Albans, York, his sons, Warwick and their soldiers break into the Parliament House and Warwick seats York on the throne. King Henry arrives with Clifford, Northumberland, Westmoreland and Exeter. Henry refuses bloodshed, argues his title, and privately admits it is weak; Exeter decides York is the lawful king. When Warwick's soldiers show themselves, Henry agrees that he will reign for life and the crown will then pass to York and his heirs; York swears to end the war. Henry's lords leave in disgust. Margaret, furious that her son is disinherited, separates herself from Henry and goes to raise the north.","key":["The play opens with a broken succession: a king who admits his title is weak and buys peace with his son's birthright.","Warwick's force, not the lawyers' arguments, settles the matter.","Margaret's anger turns the war into a mother's war for her son."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Henry admit in an aside during the debate?","o":["That he is afraid of Warwick","That his title is weak","That he never wanted to be king","That Exeter is a traitor"],"a":1},{"q":"What deal does Henry make with York?","o":["He gives up the crown at once and goes into a monastery","He will reign for life and the crown then passes to York's heirs","He divides the kingdom in two","York will be Protector"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Margaret respond to the agreement?","o":["She accepts it to keep the peace, as long as her son is made Prince of Wales by Parliament and the lords","She leaves for France","She divorces herself from Henry's table and bed until the act is repealed","She tries to kill York"],"a":2},{"q":"Why does Henry's compromise satisfy no one?","o":["It hands the crown to York at once and ends Henry's reign","It disinherits his own son to buy peace for his lifetime","It gives all of the English lands in France back to Lewis","It makes Margaret the ruler of all England while he lives"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Henry's private admission about his title reveal?","o":["He is certain that York is a traitor","He plans to give up the crown that day","He thinks Margaret has a better claim","He doubts his own right to the throne"],"a":3}]},"a1s2":{"sum":"At Sandal Castle, Edward, Richard and Montague argue over who will persuade York to take the crown now. Richard argues that York's oath means nothing, since Henry had no lawful authority to take it, and paints the sweetness of a crown. York agrees at once and sends his sons and brother to raise support. A messenger reports that Margaret is coming with twenty thousand men. York's uncles, the Mortimers, arrive; though outnumbered, York goes out to fight.","key":["Richard's first big speech is a clever argument for breaking an oath.","York gives up his promise within minutes, so both houses are now oath-breakers.","York's confidence against a 'woman-general' sets up his fall."],"quiz":[{"q":"How does Richard argue that York's oath is not binding?","o":["It was sworn under threat of death in the Parliament house, so it does not count","It was not sworn before a true and lawful magistrate, since Henry usurped","It was never written down or witnessed by a bishop or by the lords in Parliament","The Pope cancelled it"],"a":1},{"q":"How large is Margaret's army, according to the messenger?","o":["Five thousand","Ten thousand","Twenty thousand","Thirty thousand"],"a":2},{"q":"What does Richard say about fighting the queen?","o":["They should wait for Warwick and his army before they fight","A woman-general is nothing to fear","They should make peace with the Queen","They must flee to Ireland at once"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Richard argue that York's oath is void?","o":["Henry had no lawful authority to receive it","An oath sworn in Parliament is never binding","York swore it while under threat of death","The Pope has already freed York from the oath"],"a":0},{"q":"What does the speed of York's agreement suggest?","o":["He is a cautious man who weighs every word","He has been forced to agree by Queen Margaret","He does not really want the crown for himself","His ambition was only waiting for an excuse"],"a":3}]},"a1s3":{"sum":"During the battle of Wakefield, young Rutland and his tutor try to escape. Clifford sends the chaplain away and, although the boy begs for pity and offers to live in prison, stabs him to revenge his father's death at York's hands. He goes to find York with Rutland's blood still on his blade.","key":["Rutland's murder makes the chain of revenge personal and merciless.","Clifford says no killing could satisfy him while one of York's line lives.","Rutland's warning that Clifford's own son may be killed the same way is a prophecy of the revenge cycle."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Clifford kill Rutland?","o":["Rutland attacked him","To avenge his father, whom York killed","Margaret ordered it","Rutland was carrying letters"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Rutland offer Clifford for his life?","o":["Money and jewels from his father","To live in prison all his days","To change sides","To give up his title"],"a":1},{"q":"What happens to the tutor?","o":["He is killed with Rutland on the spot by Clifford's men","He is dragged away; his priesthood saves his life","He escapes to warn York","He betrays Rutland"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Clifford's killing of Rutland show about the war?","o":["Revenge now spares no one, not even children","The war is fought by strict rules of honour","Clifford is a merciful and gentle soldier","The tutor has betrayed the boy to the enemy"],"a":0},{"q":"Why does Clifford kill Rutland?","o":["To win a reward from Queen Margaret's treasury","To stop Rutland from becoming king in future","To revenge his father's death at York's hands","By mistake, thinking him to be a grown soldier"],"a":2}]},"a1s4":{"sum":"York, beaten and alone, remembers his sons' bravery and knows he will die. Margaret, Clifford and Northumberland capture him. Margaret stands him on a molehill, mocks his ambitions, gives him a napkin stained with Rutland's blood to dry his tears and puts a paper crown on his head. York answers with a furious speech against the 'she-wolf of France' and weeps for Rutland, moving Northumberland to tears. Clifford and Margaret stab him, and Margaret orders his head set on the gates of York.","key":["Margaret's cruelty is theatrical: the molehill, the napkin and the paper crown turn York's ambition into a mock coronation.","York's 'tiger's heart wrapp'd in a woman's hide' attacks Margaret for being unwomanly.","Northumberland's tears show that even enemies can feel pity, which Margaret refuses."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Margaret give York to dry his tears?","o":["His own handkerchief, wet with his tears","A napkin stained with Rutland's blood","A torn piece of the royal standard from the field","A paper crown to wear on his head"],"a":1},{"q":"What does York call Margaret?","o":["A tiger's heart wrapp'd in a woman's hide","The flower of all France","A gentle queen","A fair warrior"],"a":0},{"q":"Who stabs York?","o":["Northumberland and Clifford","Clifford and Margaret","Margaret alone","Prince Edward"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Margaret give York a paper crown and molehill?","o":["To honour him as a brave and worthy enemy","To mock his ambition before she kills him","To show that she has forgiven his rebellion","To prove that Henry is the only true king"],"a":1},{"q":"What is the effect of York's 'she-wolf' speech on Northumberland?","o":["It makes him laugh aloud","It makes him very angry","It changes his allegiance","It moves him to tears"],"a":3}]},"a2s1":{"sum":"Edward and Richard, near Mortimer's Cross, wonder what has happened to their father. Three suns appear in the sky and join into one; Edward takes it as a sign for the three brothers and adopts the suns as his badge, and Richard makes a bawdy joke. A messenger describes York's death in full. Edward grieves; Richard says he cannot weep, only revenge. Warwick and Montague arrive: Warwick has lost the second battle of St Albans, where Henry's presence and Margaret's success took the heart from his men, and George has come from Burgundy. Warwick rouses them to march on London and proclaim Edward king.","key":["The three suns become an omen of the three brothers, and Edward's badge.","Edward weeps; Richard turns grief into rage: 'Tears, then, for babes; blows and revenge for me!'","Warwick, defeated but undaunted, makes Edward Duke of York and promises him the throne."],"quiz":[{"q":"What do Edward and Richard see in the sky?","o":["A great comet trailing fire across the sky","Three suns that join into one","An eclipse","A bloody moon"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Warwick say his soldiers lost at St Albans?","o":["They were outnumbered ten to one by the Queen's army and the northern lords who came south","They fought half-heartedly, perhaps because of the king's gentleness or fear of Clifford","Clarence betrayed them","The weather"],"a":1},{"q":"Where has George come from?","o":["Ireland, where he was hiding from Clifford","France, at the court of King Lewis","Burgundy, sent by his aunt","Scotland, with the Queen's allies"],"a":2},{"q":"How do Edward and Richard react differently to their father's death?","o":["Edward grieves, while Richard can only think of revenge","Edward wants revenge, while Richard weeps for many days","Both brothers decide to make peace with Queen Margaret","Both brothers flee at once to Burgundy for their safety"],"a":0},{"q":"What does Edward make of the three suns?","o":["A warning that the war is lost and they should go home","A sign for the three brothers, which he takes as his badge","A sign that the King will soon die in the north","A sign that Warwick will betray them in the end"],"a":1}]},"a2s2":{"sum":"Before York, Margaret shows Henry York's head on the gates; Henry is sickened. Clifford urges him to protect his son as even beasts protect their young; Henry answers that ill-gotten goods bring no happiness. Henry knights Prince Edward. The Yorkists arrive. In a parley the two sides trade insults: Edward claims the crown, Richard calls Clifford a butcher, Margaret calls Richard a 'foul misshapen stigmatic', Edward blames her pride for the war. Henry tries to speak but is silenced by both sides, and the armies part to fight.","key":["Clifford's nature speech presents Henry's mercy as unnatural weakness.","The parley shows Henry silenced by his own wife and lords.","Edward blames Margaret's pride, not Henry, for the Yorkists' claim."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Margaret show Henry outside York?","o":["The Yorkist army","York's head on the city gates","Rutland's grave","Warwick's banner"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Henry do for Prince Edward before the battle?","o":["Disinherits him again","Knights him","Sends him to France","Gives him command of the army"],"a":1},{"q":"Whom does Edward blame for the war?","o":["Clifford, who killed his brother","Henry","Margaret and her pride","Warwick"],"a":2},{"q":"What is Henry's answer to Clifford's argument about protecting his son?","o":["A king must protect his heir at any cost","Ill-gotten goods bring no happiness","Beasts are wiser than men in such things","The Prince must learn to fight for himself"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Henry's silencing at the parley show?","o":["He is the most respected man present","He has no real power on either side","He has lost his voice in the cold","He refuses to speak to the rebels"],"a":1}]},"a2s3":{"sum":"At the battle of Towton the Yorkists are losing. Warwick, exhausted, rests; Edward, George and Richard arrive with bad news, and Richard reports that Warwick's brother has been killed by Clifford, crying for revenge. Warwick swears to kill his horse rather than fly and never rest until revenge is won; Edward kneels with him in the vow. They embrace and return to the fight.","key":["The Yorkists' fortunes are at their lowest point in the battle.","Warwick's vow on his knees turns the tide of feeling.","Richard, who never wept, now 'melt[s] with woe'."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Richard report about Warwick's brother?","o":["He has deserted to Margaret's side with all of his northern men","He was killed by Clifford's lance, crying for revenge","He has been captured by the Queen and her lords","He has reached London"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Warwick vow to do?","o":["Kill his horse rather than fly, and never rest until revenge","Make peace with Henry and go home to Warwick Castle for good, and leave the war","Go to France for help from King Lewis and his lords at Paris","Become a monk and pray for all the men he has lost"],"a":0},{"q":"What does George suggest they promise the troops?","o":["Land in France","Rewards like those of Olympic victors","Pardons for all crimes","The queen's jewels"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Warwick's vow to kill his horse mean?","o":["He has no food left for his men","He thinks the horse is cursed","He will never flee from the battle","He wants to fight on foot like Edward"],"a":2},{"q":"What drives Warwick back into the battle?","o":["A messenger with urgent news from the King","His brother's death and a vow of revenge","Margaret's sudden offer of a peace treaty","Fear that Edward will leave the field without him"],"a":1}]},"a2s4":{"sum":"Richard finally corners Clifford alone and they exchange taunts about York and Rutland. They fight; when Warwick comes in, Clifford flees. Richard tells Warwick to find other game: he will hunt this wolf himself.","key":["The two avengers meet face to face: each claims a father's death as his right.","Clifford's flight denies Richard his revenge in combat."],"quiz":[{"q":"What happens when Warwick arrives during the fight?","o":["Clifford surrenders","Clifford flees","Richard is wounded","Warwick kills Clifford"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Richard call Clifford?","o":["A fox who hides from the hounds","A wolf he will hunt to death","A lion","A snake"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Richard tell Warwick to find other game?","o":["He thinks Warwick is too weak to fight","He wants Warwick to chase the King","He is afraid of losing the battle","He wants to kill Clifford himself"],"a":3},{"q":"What is the cause of the hatred between Richard and Clifford?","o":["A quarrel over a horse","An insult at court","Margaret's love for Clifford","The deaths of York and Rutland"],"a":3},{"q":"What happens when Warwick joins the fight?","o":["Clifford dies","Clifford flees","Richard flees","Warwick is hurt"],"a":1}]},"a2s5":{"sum":"Henry, sent from the field by Margaret and Clifford, sits on a molehill and compares the battle to a tide. He wishes he were a shepherd, measuring his life by sundials and seasons rather than crowns and treasons. A soldier enters with a man he has killed and finds it is his father; another enters with a body and finds it is his only son. Henry grieves with them, seeing the red and white roses in the dead boy's face. Margaret, the Prince and Exeter tell him to flee to Berwick, for the battle is lost.","key":["Henry's shepherd speech shows what he wants: order, time and peace instead of rule.","The son and the father turn civil war into family murder.","Henry watches and grieves but cannot act."],"quiz":[{"q":"Where does Henry sit during the battle?","o":["On a throne","On a molehill","On his horse","On the city walls"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Henry wish he could be?","o":["A monk in his cell","A homely shepherd","A soldier","A merchant"],"a":1},{"q":"What do the two soldiers discover?","o":["They are brothers who were parted as boys and never met again until now","One has killed his father and the other his son","They fought on the same side","Their lords have changed sides"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the son who killed his father, and the father who killed his son, show?","o":["Soldiers often get lost on the battlefield","The King's army is badly trained and led","Both men are spies for the Duke of York","Civil war turns families against themselves"],"a":3},{"q":"Why does Henry long to be a shepherd?","o":["Shepherds are far richer than any king","A simple life is happier than a king's","He wants to hide himself from Margaret","He has always loved the sheep of the north"],"a":1}]},"a2s6":{"sum":"Clifford, wounded, says his candle that lit Henry is going out and blames Henry's mildness for the rise of York. He faints. The victorious Yorkists enter; Clifford groans and dies. They mock his body and Warwick orders his head placed where York's was. Warwick plans Edward's coronation and his own embassy to France to win Lady Bona. Edward creates Richard Duke of Gloucester and George Duke of Clarence; Richard thinks Gloucester an ominous title.","key":["Clifford's dying speech blames weak kingship, not York, for the war.","The mockery of his corpse repeats Margaret's mockery of York: revenge copies revenge.","Warwick is now running the new reign, planning both the crowning and the marriage."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Warwick order done with Clifford's head?","o":["Bury it at St Albans","Set it on York's gates in place of York's","Send it to Margaret","Throw it in the river"],"a":1},{"q":"Whom does Warwick plan to win as Edward's bride?","o":["Lady Grey, the widow of a Lancastrian knight","Lady Bona, sister of the French king","Warwick's own daughter","Margaret of Anjou"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Richard dislike the title Duke of Gloucester?","o":["It is too poor","It is ominous","It belongs to George","It is French"],"a":1},{"q":"Whom does the dying Clifford blame for York's rise?","o":["Margaret, for her pride","Henry, for his mildness","Warwick, for his greed","Northumberland, for his fear"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Richard dislike the title of Gloucester?","o":["It is too small","It is French","It is ominous","It is Clarence's"],"a":2}]},"a3s1":{"sum":"Two keepers hiding to shoot deer in a northern forest overhear Henry, who has slipped back from Scotland in disguise. He imagines Margaret and Warwick both pleading at the French court. The keepers recognise him and ask where his crown is; he says it is in his heart, a crown called content. They arrest him for King Edward, and when he asks whether they have not broken their oaths to him, they say they were his subjects only while he was king. Henry compares the common people to a feather blown by the strongest wind and goes with them.","key":["Henry's 'crown is called content' shows his true kingdom is inward.","The keepers' logic makes loyalty depend on who holds power.","The feather image sums up the fickleness of allegiance in the whole play."],"quiz":[{"q":"Where does Henry say his crown is?","o":["Locked away in the Tower of London","In his heart, called content","With Margaret in France","Buried at Towton"],"a":1},{"q":"Why do the keepers say they have not broken their oath?","o":["They never swore one to him in the first place","They were his subjects only while he was king","The Pope released them from all their oaths","Henry released them"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Henry compare the common people to?","o":["A river","A feather blown by the strongest gust","A flock of sheep","Leaves in autumn"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Henry's 'crown called content' suggest?","o":["Inner peace matters more to him than power","He has hidden the real crown in his cloak","He plans to win back his crown by force","He wants the keepers to crown him again"],"a":0},{"q":"What do the keepers' views on their oaths reveal?","o":["Common people never break their oaths","Loyalty follows whoever holds power","The keepers are secret Lancastrians","Oaths matter more than kings do"],"a":1}]},"a3s2":{"sum":"Lady Grey asks King Edward to restore her dead husband's lands. Gloucester and Clarence make bawdy asides as Edward sends them aside and offers the lands for her love. She refuses to be his mistress, and Edward, struck by her virtue and wit, proposes marriage. He tells his astonished brothers. News comes that Henry has been captured and Edward orders him to the Tower. Alone, Gloucester wishes Edward childless, counts the lives between himself and the crown, says love forswore him in the womb, and vows to win the crown by any means: he can 'smile, and murther while I smile'.","key":["Edward's wooing puts private desire above the state just as Warwick is wooing Bona for him.","The brothers' asides turn the scene into a bawdy comedy with a dangerous outcome.","Richard's soliloquy reveals the future Richard III: a self-made actor and murderer."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Lady Grey want from Edward?","o":["A pardon for her sons, who fought against him","Her dead husband's lands","A place at court","Revenge on Clifford"],"a":1},{"q":"Who stands between Gloucester and the crown, by his own count?","o":["Only Edward, now that the old King is locked up in the Tower of London","Edward, Clarence, Henry, Prince Edward and any children they may have","Warwick and Margaret","Nobody"],"a":1},{"q":"Which figure does Gloucester say he will 'set to school'?","o":["Old Nestor","Ulysses","Machiavel","Sinon the Greek"],"a":2},{"q":"Why does Edward propose marriage to Lady Grey?","o":["Her refusal to be his mistress wins him","Her lands are the richest in all of England","His brothers have urged him to marry quickly","Warwick has already arranged the whole match"],"a":0},{"q":"What does Gloucester's soliloquy reveal for the first time?","o":["He plans to marry Lady Grey's daughter in secret","He wants to retire to the country and live alone","He is loyal to Edward and will die in his service","He will win the crown by any means, however bloody"],"a":3}]},"a3s3":{"sum":"At the French court Margaret begs King Lewis for help. Warwick arrives to ask for Lady Bona for Edward; Margaret and Oxford argue against him, and Warwick and Oxford dispute Henry's pedigree. Lewis speaks privately with Warwick, is satisfied, and agrees to the match. A post brings letters: Edward has married Lady Grey. Lewis is insulted; Warwick, shamed, renounces Edward and swears to restore Henry. Margaret forgives him, Lewis promises troops, and Warwick offers his eldest daughter to Prince Edward. Alone, Warwick says he will bring Edward down to revenge his mockery.","key":["The scene is a reversal: Margaret seems beaten, then wins everything when the letters arrive.","Warwick's change of side comes from wounded honour, not principle.","The marriage of Warwick's daughter to Prince Edward seals the new alliance."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why has Warwick come to France?","o":["To ask for troops","To ask for Lady Bona's hand for Edward","To arrest Margaret","To make peace for Henry"],"a":1},{"q":"What news do the letters bring?","o":["Henry has died","Edward has married Lady Grey","Clarence has rebelled","York's head has been taken down"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Warwick guarantee his new loyalty?","o":["He gives Lewis the town of Calais and its harbour","He offers his eldest daughter to Prince Edward","He swears on the Bible","He sends his brother as a hostage"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Warwick turn against Edward?","o":["Edward's secret marriage shames him at the French court","Edward refuses to pay him for all his help in the wars","Margaret promises him the crown of England for himself","Lewis threatens to have him killed if he does not change"],"a":0},{"q":"How does the letter change the balance at the French court?","o":["It makes Lewis side with Edward at once","It ends the war between France and England","It turns Lewis and Warwick against Edward","It makes Margaret give up her claim forever"],"a":2}]},"a4s1":{"sum":"Gloucester, Clarence, Somerset and Montague discuss the marriage. Edward enters with his new queen and challenges his brothers. Clarence warns that Lewis and Warwick are now enemies; Montague says a French alliance would have been stronger; Hastings says England is safe if true within itself. Clarence and Gloucester complain that the best heirs have gone to the queen's family. The messenger brings the French replies, and news that Warwick's daughter will marry Prince Edward. Clarence leaves to marry Warwick's other daughter, and Somerset follows. Gloucester stays, for the crown. Edward orders troops raised and makes Hastings and Montague swear loyalty.","key":["Edward's 'I am Edward' shows him behaving as an absolute king before he is secure.","The quarrel over heiresses shows how marriage is politics.","Gloucester's aside 'Not I' shows that he stays only for his own ends."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Hastings argue?","o":["England needs a French alliance more than ever now","England is safe if true within itself","The marriage should be annulled","Warwick should be pardoned"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Clarence leave Edward?","o":["He is banished from court by Edward for speaking against the marriage","He goes to marry Warwick's younger daughter","He is going to France as ambassador","He wants to be Protector"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Gloucester stay with Edward?","o":["Out of love for him","For the crown","Because Warwick hates him","Because he is ill"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Hastings mean by saying England is safe 'if true within itself'?","o":["England needs the help of France to survive at all","The King must punish all of his disloyal subjects","Unity at home matters more than foreign allies","England should stay out of all of the foreign wars"],"a":2},{"q":"Why does Gloucester stay with Edward?","o":["He has his eye on the crown","He loves his brother dearly","He fears Warwick's anger","He has nowhere else to go"],"a":0}]},"a4s2":{"sum":"Warwick and Oxford land with French troops and find the common people flocking to them. Clarence and Somerset join them, and Warwick promises Clarence his daughter. He plans to surprise Edward at night in his lightly guarded camp, like Ulysses and Diomedes stealing Rhesus's horses, and his men cry 'Henry!'.","key":["Clarence's change of side is welcomed with a marriage promise.","Warwick's plan is a night raid, not an open battle."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Warwick plan to do?","o":["Besiege London","Surprise Edward at night in his camp","Challenge Edward to single combat","Wait for Margaret"],"a":1},{"q":"Which classical raid does Warwick compare his plan to?","o":["The Greeks hiding inside the wooden horse at the gates of Troy","Ulysses and Diomedes stealing Rhesus's horses","Jason and the golden fleece","Hercules and the Hydra"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the Rhesus comparison suggest about Warwick's plan?","o":["A long siege to starve the enemy out","A peaceful meeting to end the war","A trick to make Edward surrender","A bold night raid on a sleeping enemy"],"a":3},{"q":"What does Clarence's change of side show?","o":["Clarence is the most loyal of brothers","Warwick has forced him to join him","Clarence wants to marry Lady Bona","Loyalty shifts with self-interest"],"a":3},{"q":"Why do the common people flock to Warwick?","o":["They have been paid in French gold","They fear the French army","They welcome a change from Edward","They want Clarence as king"],"a":2}]},"a4s3":{"sum":"Watchmen guard Edward's tent; he has vowed not to sleep in a bed until Warwick or he is beaten, while his lords lodge in nearby towns. Warwick, Clarence, Oxford and Somerset attack; Gloucester and Hastings escape, and Edward is carried out in his gown. Warwick takes off his crown, calls him Duke of York again and lists his failings as a king. Edward says his mind is still a king's. Somerset takes him to Warwick's brother the Archbishop of York, and Warwick sets off to free Henry.","key":["Edward's carelessness, lodging his lords away from him, costs him the crown.","Warwick's list of Edward's faults is a summary of bad kingship.","For the second time in the play a king is uncrowned by Warwick."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who escapes when Edward is captured?","o":["Clarence and the Duke of Somerset","Gloucester and Hastings","Montague and Rivers","Nobody"],"a":1},{"q":"Where is Edward sent?","o":["To the Tower of London to wait for the King's judgement","To Warwick's brother, the Archbishop of York","To France as a prisoner of King Lewis","To Coventry"],"a":1},{"q":"Which fault does Warwick NOT list?","o":["He misused ambassadors","He could not be content with one wife","He did not treat his brothers like a brother","He was too cruel to Henry"],"a":3},{"q":"What does Edward's reply 'my mind is still a king's' show?","o":["He has gone mad in captivity","He keeps his pride even in defeat","He plans to escape that very night","He agrees to give up the crown"],"a":1},{"q":"Why is Edward easy to capture?","o":["He is drunk after a long feast with his lords","His guards have been bribed by Warwick's men","His lords lodge in towns away from his tent","He has gone out hunting in the park alone"],"a":2}]},"a4s4":{"sum":"Queen Elizabeth tells her brother Rivers that Edward has been captured and is held by the Archbishop of York. She is pregnant with Edward's heir, and to protect the child she goes into sanctuary before Warwick reaches London.","key":["The queen's pregnancy introduces the next heir of York.","Sanctuary is her only protection, showing how exposed Edward's family is."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the queen control her grief?","o":["She hopes Warwick will marry her","She is carrying Edward's child","She plans revenge","She does not love Edward"],"a":1},{"q":"Where does she go for safety?","o":["To France","Into sanctuary","To the Tower","To Burgundy"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Elizabeth take sanctuary?","o":["To protect Edward's unborn heir","To hide Edward's treasure from Warwick","To plan Edward's escape from prison","To pray for Warwick's soul in peace"],"a":0},{"q":"What does the scene suggest about Edward's cause?","o":["It is lost for good now and forever","It is backed by all the citizens of London","It no longer matters to anyone at all","It depends on his heir's survival"],"a":3},{"q":"Who holds Edward captive?","o":["Warwick himself","The Archbishop of York","King Lewis","Margaret"],"a":1}]},"a4s5":{"sum":"Gloucester, Hastings and Sir William Stanley wait in a park where the captive Edward is allowed to hunt. Edward arrives with a huntsman, and they ride for Lynn and a ship to Flanders. Edward thanks Stanley and mocks the archbishop.","key":["Gloucester plans and carries out the rescue, proving himself indispensable.","The light, quick scene shows how fast fortune turns."],"quiz":[{"q":"How is Edward rescued?","o":["By an army from the north under Hastings","While hunting in the archbishop's park","By bribing the jailer","By Clarence"],"a":1},{"q":"Where do they plan to sail?","o":["To France, from Dover","From Lynn to Flanders","To Ireland, by way of Wales","To Scotland"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Edward's escape show about his captors?","o":["They planned to let him go","They guarded him too lightly","They were in Edward's pay","They fled from Warwick's army"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Edward's mood change on escaping?","o":["He becomes confident and mocking","He becomes afraid and silent on the road","He becomes sad and tearful for his queen","He becomes humble and quiet with his friends"],"a":0},{"q":"Where do Edward and his friends head?","o":["London, to free the Queen","Lynn, for a ship to Flanders","York, to raise the north","Wales, to join the Tudors"],"a":1}]},"a4s6":{"sum":"In the Tower, the freed Henry thanks the Lieutenant and Warwick. He hands government to Warwick and Clarence as joint Protectors while he lives privately, and asks for Margaret and his son to be sent for. Warwick wants Edward proclaimed a traitor. Henry lays his hand on young Richmond's head and calls him 'England's hope'. News comes that Edward has escaped to Burgundy. Somerset and Oxford decide to send Richmond to Brittany for safety.","key":["Henry gives up power again, keeping only the name of king.","The blessing of Richmond looks forward to Henry VII and the end of the wars.","Edward's escape undoes the restoration at once."],"quiz":[{"q":"To whom does Henry hand the government?","o":["Margaret and her son, the Prince","Warwick and Clarence","Oxford and his men","Somerset, his old friend"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Henry call young Richmond?","o":["England's hope","My heir","A Tudor rose","The new Talbot"],"a":0},{"q":"Where does Edward flee?","o":["To Scotland","To Burgundy","To Ireland","To Brittany"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Henry hand government to Warwick and Clarence?","o":["He prefers a private life to ruling","He is too ill to leave his room in the Tower","He wants to test their loyalty to his family","He has been ordered to do so by Margaret"],"a":0},{"q":"What does Henry's blessing of Richmond foreshadow?","o":["Richmond will die young in Brittany","Richmond will one day end the wars","Richmond will betray the Lancastrians","Richmond will marry Margaret's sister"],"a":1}]},"a4s7":{"sum":"Edward, back from Burgundy with Gloucester and Hastings, finds the gates of York shut. He tells the Mayor he claims only his dukedom, and Hastings says they are King Henry's friends, so the gates open. Montgomery arrives with troops but will leave unless Edward proclaims himself king. Urged by Gloucester and Hastings, Edward is proclaimed King Edward the Fourth, and Montgomery throws down his gauntlet as his champion.","key":["Edward repeats the trick of Bolingbroke: he claims only his dukedom to get in.","Montgomery forces Edward to declare himself king: soldiers will only fight for a crown.","Gloucester's 'fearless minds climb soonest unto crowns' describes his own method."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Edward first claim at York?","o":["The crown","Only his dukedom","Nothing at all","The earldom of March"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Montgomery threaten to leave?","o":["Edward will not pay him","He came to serve a king, not a duke","He has been insulted by Hastings","He fears Warwick"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Montgomery do after the proclamation?","o":["Kneels and weeps","Throws down his gauntlet as Edward's champion","Rides off to warn Warwick and Clarence at Coventry","Opens the gates"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Edward get into York?","o":["He breaks down the gates by force","He bribes the Mayor with French gold","He disguises himself as a merchant","He claims to want only his dukedom"],"a":3},{"q":"What finally makes Edward proclaim himself king?","o":["The Mayor of York begs him to take the crown","Warwick is reported dead at Coventry","Henry sends a letter giving up the crown","Montgomery threatens to leave unless he does"],"a":3}]},"a4s8":{"sum":"In London, Warwick reports that Edward has landed and is marching on London. He sends Clarence, Montague and Oxford to raise troops and leaves Henry in London. Henry tells Exeter he has no fear, since he has been merciful and kind and the lamb will follow the lion that fawns on it. Shouts are heard; Edward and Gloucester burst in, seize Henry and send him to the Tower, then march on Warwick at Coventry.","key":["Henry's belief that mercy earns loyalty is proved wrong at once.","The kingdom changes hands again with almost no fighting."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Henry say he does not fear Edward?","o":["Warwick has a huge army of loyal men in the north and Midlands","His mercy and mildness have won the people","God has promised him victory in a dream","Edward is ill"],"a":1},{"q":"What happens to Henry at the end of the scene?","o":["He escapes to Scotland with a few loyal friends in the night","He is seized and sent to the Tower","He abdicates in favour of his son","He goes to Coventry"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Henry's trust in his own mercy show?","o":["A clever plan to trap Edward in London","A gentle faith that blinds him to danger","A fear that Edward will kill him soon","A belief that Warwick will protect him"],"a":1},{"q":"What is ironic about Henry's lamb-and-lion image?","o":["He has never seen a lion","Edward is kind to him after","Exeter laughs at it","He is seized moments later"],"a":3},{"q":"Where does Warwick go to meet Edward?","o":["Barnet","Coventry","Tewkesbury","Lynn"],"a":1}]},"a5s1":{"sum":"On the walls of Coventry, Warwick waits for his allies. Edward and Gloucester arrive first and the two sides exchange offers: Warwick offers Edward his dukedom, Edward offers Warwick pardon. Gloucester reminds Warwick that Henry is already in the Tower. Oxford, Montague and Somerset each march in for Lancaster. Clarence arrives, whispers with Gloucester, tears the red rose from his hat and returns to Edward, defying Warwick. Warwick leaves to fight at Barnet.","key":["Clarence's change of sides, prompted by Richard's whisper, is the turning point of the last act.","Warwick, the kingmaker, is outplayed: Henry is lost and Clarence turns.","Edward's threat to write in the dust with Warwick's blood shows his growing hardness."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Warwick offer Edward from the walls?","o":["The crown back","His dukedom, if he repents","Exile in France","A duel"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Clarence throw at Warwick?","o":["His glove of mail, as a challenge","The red rose from his hat","His sword, broken in two","A letter"],"a":1},{"q":"Whom does Clarence compare his oath-keeping to?","o":["Judas, who betrayed his master for silver","Jephthah, who sacrificed his daughter","Brutus, who killed his friend Caesar","Ulysses, who tricked the Trojans"],"a":1},{"q":"Why is Clarence's return to Edward a turning point?","o":["Edward loses his brother and his best soldiers","Warwick loses a key ally at the decisive moment","The Lancastrians gain control of Coventry","Henry escapes from the Tower to join Warwick"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Clarence's tearing of the red rose show?","o":["He has been wounded in battle","He hates Warwick's daughter","He is giving it to Edward","He switches back to York"],"a":3}]},"a5s2":{"sum":"At Barnet, Edward drags in the wounded Warwick and leaves him to die, going to find Montague. Warwick, dying, compares himself to a fallen cedar and reflects that of all his lands nothing is left but his body's length. Oxford and Somerset bring news that Margaret has landed with an army and that Montague is dead, calling for Warwick. Warwick dies, and they go to join the queen.","key":["Warwick's dying speech is a meditation on the emptiness of power.","The kingmaker dies as a cedar falls: the play's great tree image.","Even in defeat, Margaret's arrival keeps Lancaster's hope alive."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Warwick compare himself to as he dies?","o":["A lion","A cedar felled by the axe","A sinking ship","A setting sun"],"a":1},{"q":"What news do Oxford and Somerset bring?","o":["Henry is dead in the Tower, killed by Richard's own hand","Margaret has landed with an army, and Montague is dead","Clarence has returned to them","Edward is wounded"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Warwick's dying speech say about power?","o":["A strong lord can never be beaten in war","All worldly greatness ends in the grave","Power should always be handed to sons","Land is worth more than any king's crown"],"a":1},{"q":"Why is Warwick compared to a cedar?","o":["He was great, and now is fallen","He is old and wise like a tree","He comes from the forests of Lebanon","He stood still while others ran"],"a":0},{"q":"What news comes as Warwick dies?","o":["Edward has been killed in the fighting at Barnet","Margaret has landed and Montague is dead","Henry has escaped from the Tower to Scotland","Clarence has come back to Warwick's side at last"],"a":1}]},"a5s3":{"sum":"Edward, victorious at Barnet, sees one more cloud: Margaret's army from France. Clarence is confident; Gloucester warns that she has thirty thousand men. Edward hears they are heading for Tewkesbury and marches at once.","key":["A brief interval of triumph, already shadowed by the next threat."],"quiz":[{"q":"Where is Margaret's army heading?","o":["London Bridge","Tewkesbury","York Castle","Coventry"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Edward compare Margaret's army to?","o":["A black, threatening cloud","A flood","A wolf pack","A fire"],"a":0},{"q":"What does Edward's 'one more cloud' image suggest?","o":["A storm is coming to ruin the harvest","A last threat stands between him and peace","He fears his brother Gloucester most","He is ready to make peace with Margaret"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Edward march at once?","o":["To return to London and see his son born","To bury Warwick at the Abbey with honour","To meet King Lewis on the coast of France","To catch Margaret before she grows stronger"],"a":3},{"q":"How many men does Gloucester say Margaret has?","o":["Ten thousand","Thirty thousand","Five thousand","Fifty thousand"],"a":1}]},"a5s4":{"sum":"Near Tewkesbury Margaret rallies her lords: though Warwick, the anchor, and Montague, the topmast, are lost, the ship can still be steered, and the York brothers are only sea, quicksand and rock. Prince Edward echoes her and Oxford praises the boy as his grandfather reborn. A messenger brings word that Edward is coming. The two sides make their speeches, Margaret in tears, and the battle begins.","key":["Margaret's ship speech is her finest moment as a leader.","Prince Edward shows the courage his father lacks.","Both sides call the other the wolf or the thorny wood."],"quiz":[{"q":"In Margaret's speech, what is Warwick?","o":["The anchor","The broken mast","The pilot","A sail"],"a":0},{"q":"What is Richard in her speech?","o":["A quicksand under the waves","A ragged fatal rock","A ruthless sea that swallows ships","A storm off the coast"],"a":1},{"q":"Whom does Oxford say lives again in Prince Edward?","o":["Henry the Fourth","His grandfather Henry the Fifth","Talbot","John of Gaunt"],"a":1},{"q":"What is the purpose of Margaret's ship speech?","o":["To explain how she escaped from France by sea","To keep her men's spirits up despite their losses","To persuade the Prince to flee to safety","To show that she has given up all hope of victory"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Oxford's praise of the Prince do?","o":["Proves that the Prince is a coward like Henry","Mocks the Prince for his youth and weakness","Suggests that Oxford will betray the Prince","Shows the Lancastrian hope in the next generation"],"a":3}]},"a5s5":{"sum":"After Tewkesbury, Edward sends Oxford to Hammes Castle and Somerset to execution. Prince Edward is brought in and defies the brothers, calling them lascivious, perjured and misshapen. Edward, Gloucester and Clarence each stab him in front of Margaret. Gloucester offers to kill her too but Edward stops him; Gloucester slips away to 'the Tower'. Margaret curses the 'butchers' and begs Clarence to kill her, but he refuses, and she is taken away. Edward heads for London, hoping his queen has borne a son.","key":["The killing of Prince Edward echoes Rutland's murder: one child for another.","All three brothers stab: the Yorkist victory is shared guilt.","Margaret's curse ('You have no children, butchers!') looks forward to Richard III."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who stabs Prince Edward?","o":["Gloucester alone, with his dagger, in front of his mother","Edward, Gloucester and Clarence in turn","Clarence and Hastings","A soldier"],"a":1},{"q":"Where does Gloucester go after the murder?","o":["To France","To London and the Tower","To York","To Coventry"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Margaret ask Clarence to do?","o":["Free her","Kill her","Take her to Henry","Bury her son"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the killing of Prince Edward show about the York brothers?","o":["They are merciful and kind in their victory","They are divided over whether to kill the Prince","They are as ruthless as their enemies were","They regret the war and want peace with Margaret"],"a":2},{"q":"Why does Gloucester hurry to 'the Tower'?","o":["To free Henry","To see his son","To hide from Edward","To murder Henry"],"a":3}]},"a5s6":{"sum":"In the Tower, Gloucester sends the Lieutenant away. Henry compares himself to a sheep before the butcher and to Daedalus mourning Icarus, with Edward as the sun and Gloucester as the sea. He prophesies that thousands will curse Gloucester's birth, recalls the evil omens at it and says he was born with teeth to bite the world. Gloucester stabs him mid-sentence, then again, and alone embraces his monstrous birth: he has no brother, he is 'myself alone', and Clarence will be next.","key":["Henry's prophecy is cut short: Richard silences the play's last voice of conscience.","'I am myself alone' rejects every family bond the play has broken.","The scene sets up Richard III: Clarence is the next target."],"quiz":[{"q":"Which myth does Henry use for himself and his son?","o":["Phaethon and the chariot of the sun","Daedalus and Icarus","Orpheus","Prometheus"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Henry say Gloucester was born with?","o":["A crown","Teeth","A caul","A crooked sword"],"a":1},{"q":"Whom does Gloucester name as his next victim?","o":["Edward","Clarence","Hastings","The baby prince"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Henry's Daedalus and Icarus image mean?","o":["He mourns his son, with Edward and Gloucester as his killers","He hopes to fly out of the Tower and escape to the coast of France","He thinks his son is still alive and hiding somewhere in Wales","He believes that Gloucester has come to set him free at last"],"a":0},{"q":"What does 'myself alone' reveal about Gloucester?","o":["He is lonely and wants friends","He is the only son of York left","He wants to live as a hermit","He recognises no bond of family"],"a":3}]},"a5s7":{"sum":"Edward sits on the throne again with Queen Elizabeth and his infant son. He lists the enemies he has mown down and tells the baby the crown has been won for him. Gloucester, aside, says he will blast this harvest. Clarence and Gloucester kiss the baby; Gloucester compares his kiss to Judas's. Margaret is to be ransomed to France, and Edward calls for triumphs and shows: 'here, I hope, begins our lasting joy'.","key":["The ending looks like peace but is full of dramatic irony.","Richard's Judas kiss undermines Edward's joy.","The infant prince links to the murdered princes of Richard III."],"quiz":[{"q":"Whom does Gloucester compare himself to when he kisses the prince?","o":["Brutus","Judas","Cain","Herod"],"a":1},{"q":"What happens to Margaret?","o":["She is executed","She is ransomed to France","She enters a convent","She is imprisoned in the Tower"],"a":1},{"q":"What are Edward's last words about?","o":["Revenge on all his enemies in France","The beginning of lasting joy","His brother's treachery","A crusade"],"a":1},{"q":"What is ironic about Edward's 'lasting joy'?","o":["The baby is not really Edward's son","Margaret has already raised a new army","Gloucester already plots to destroy it","The crown has been stolen from the Tower"],"a":2},{"q":"What does Gloucester's Judas comparison reveal?","o":["He has sold the crown for silver","He is sorry for what he has done","He fears that Clarence will betray him","His kiss for the baby is a betrayal"],"a":3}]}},"notes":{"2":"The play begins straight after the first battle of St Albans (1455), where the Yorkists won and King Henry escaped. Shakespeare telescopes the five years between St Albans and the Parliament of 1460.","3":"The northern lords were the Lancastrian backbone. Clifford's father and Northumberland's father both died at St Albans, which explains their vows of revenge.","9":"Richard throws down the head of the Duke of Somerset he claims to have killed. Historically Richard was only two at St Albans; Shakespeare makes him a warrior from the start.","12":"John of Gaunt, fourth son of Edward III, was the ancestor of the Lancastrian kings and of the Beauforts (Somerset).","22":"Warwick names this 'the bloody parliament', but in fact it ends in a legal compromise, the Act of Accord of October 1460.","24":"Falcons wore bells on their legs. Warwick pictures himself as the hawk whose bells terrify smaller birds.","29":"'Mourn in steel': Clifford wears armour instead of black mourning clothes because his grief can only be eased by revenge.","56":"Henry recites his descent from Henry IV and Henry V. York's claim came through his mother from Lionel, Duke of Clarence, Edward III's third son, elder than John of Gaunt.","58":"Henry became king in 1422, at nine months old, when Henry V died. His uncles Bedford and Gloucester governed; France was largely lost during his reign (see 1 Henry VI).","68":"Henry IV (Bolingbroke) deposed Richard II in 1399, as shown in Richard II.","70":"Henry's aside is a rare moment of private honesty from a king: he knows his claim rests on a deposition.","107":"This is the 'entail': Henry settles the crown on York and his heirs, cutting out his own son. It is the legal knot the rest of the play tries to untie with swords.","130":"Falconbridge (the Bastard of Fauconberg) commanded ships in the Channel. Calais was England's last foothold in France and Warwick's base.","160":"Richard's argument is lawyer's logic: an oath is valid only if sworn before a lawful authority, and Henry, a usurper, was none.","161":"Lord Cobham and 'the Kentishmen' recall Jack Cade's Kentish rebels of 2 Henry VI.","169":"Sandal Castle near Wakefield in Yorkshire was York's stronghold. The battle of Wakefield was fought on 30 December 1460.","172":"Richard's scorn of a 'woman-general' is the first of many attacks on Margaret for taking a man's role.","190":"The historical Rutland was seventeen. Shakespeare makes him a child to deepen the horror of his murder.","197":"A Latin line from Ovid's Heroides: 'May the gods grant that this be the height of your glory.' Rutland, a schoolboy, dies quoting his lessons.","208":"Phaethon, son of the sun god, drove his father's chariot, lost control and was struck down by Jove. York is cast as an overreacher.","209":"The phoenix rose from its own ashes. York means that one of his sons will avenge him, as Edward and Richard do.","221":"The molehill and the paper crown mock York's ambition: a man who 'raught at mountains' is set on a mound of earth and crowned with paper.","226":"York attacks Margaret's father, René of Anjou, who held the titles King of Naples, Sicily and Jerusalem but no real kingdoms or wealth.","228":"Hyrcania, by the Caspian Sea, was famous in classical writing for its tigers.","237":"Historically York's head was set on Micklegate Bar in York with a paper crown on it.","241":"Mortimer's Cross in Herefordshire was fought in February 1461.","243":"The appearance of three suns (a 'parhelion', caused by ice crystals) before Mortimer's Cross is recorded in the chronicles. Edward took the 'sun in splendour' as his badge.","246":"Richard puns on 'sun' and 'son' and mocks Edward's appetite for women: he prefers 'the breeder' to the male.","252":"Hector, the 'hope of Troy', defended the city against the Greeks.","261":"Warwick describes the second battle of St Albans (17 February 1461), where the Lancastrians won and recovered King Henry.","263":"Edward's aunt Isabel of Portugal was Duchess of Burgundy. Historically George and Richard were sent to Burgundy for safety after Wakefield.","270":"Warwick hails Edward as Duke of York and King in the same breath. Edward was proclaimed king in London in March 1461.","282":"Clifford's argument from nature (lions, bears, doves) treats Henry's mercy as unnatural.","283":"Henry's reply rests on a proverb: 'ill-gotten goods never thrive'. He wishes his father had left him only virtue.","289":"'Darraign your battle': set your army in battle order.","324":"'Iron of Naples hid with English gilt': Margaret is base metal gilded by her English marriage.","325":"A 'wisp of straw' was put on a scold as a public shame. Edward compares Margaret to Helen of Troy, and Henry, by implication, to Menelaus, the betrayed husband.","333":"Towton (29 March 1461), in a snowstorm, was probably the bloodiest battle ever fought in England.","341":"Historically the man killed was Warwick's illegitimate half-brother, the Bastard of Salisbury, who died at Ferrybridge the day before Towton. The text says only 'Thy brother'.","357":"Henry's molehill echoes the molehill on which York was mocked and killed (1.4).","359":"The son was 'pressed' (conscripted) by the king's officers in London; his father was pressed by Warwick. The scene shows ordinary men forced to fight in their lords' quarrel.","363":"The red and white roses, chosen in the Temple Garden in 1 Henry VI, now appear in a dead boy's blood and pale face.","372":"Priam, King of Troy, lost almost all his fifty sons in the Trojan war.","383":"Clifford dies blaming Henry's weakness. In the Folio stage direction he enters 'with an arrow in his neck'; this edition says only 'wounded'.","393":"'Measure for measure' (Matthew 7:2): as you judge, so you will be judged.","408":"Warwick plans a marriage to Bona of Savoy, sister-in-law of Louis XI; Shakespeare follows the chronicles in calling her the French king's sister.","410":"The previous Dukes of Gloucester (Thomas of Woodstock and Humphrey, in 2 Henry VI) both died violently, which is why Richard finds the title ominous.","415":"'Laund': a glade. The keepers are poachers or gamekeepers waiting for deer in a northern chase.","420":"After Towton Henry fled to Scotland. Historically he was captured in 1465 in Lancashire.","431":"Henry's 'crown called content' recalls the classical and Christian ideal of the contented mind as true riches.","439":"The feather image makes fickleness visible: allegiance blows whichever way the stronger wind blows.","447":"Sir John Grey died fighting for Lancaster at the second battle of St Albans; Shakespeare follows the chronicles in saying he died for York.","451":"'Keeps the wind': a hunting term; a hunter stays downwind of the deer so it cannot scent him.","466":"A chain of puns on 'leave' (permission, freedom, departure): Edward will take liberties until age leaves him on a crutch.","515":"The 'ghostly father' is a priest hearing confession ('shrift'). Clarence puns: he became a confessor 'for shift', as a trick, or to get at her smock (shift).","531":"Richard's soliloquy introduces the character of Richard III: his deformity, his self-pity, his gift for acting and his ruthless ambition.","536":"'Strike her sail': lower the sails in submission, as a ship does to a superior.","553":"Albion is an old poetic name for England.","560":"Oxford's pedigree: John of Gaunt (who campaigned in Spain), Henry IV and Henry V.","561":"Warwick counts the Lancastrian reign as sixty-two years (1399 to 1461), too short to establish a title by long possession ('prescription').","564":"The Earl of Oxford and his son Aubrey de Vere were executed for treason in 1462.","574":"Warwick is caught in a lie: he praises a love that Edward has already abandoned.","576":"Bona's aside to Warwick shows she is already half in love with the reports of Edward.","595":"'The abuse done to my niece': the chroniclers report that Edward made advances to a young woman of Warwick's family, one of the grievances behind Warwick's revolt.","605":"A willow garland was the sign of a forsaken lover.","610":"Warwick's daughter Anne married Prince Edward in 1470. In Richard III, as the widowed Lady Anne, she is wooed by Richard over Henry's coffin.","640":"Edward has given Lord Hungerford's heiress to Hastings, the heir of Lord Scales to the queen's brother, and Lord Bonville's heiress to the queen's son: marriages that made the Woodvilles powerful.","663":"Clarence married Isabel, Warwick's elder daughter, in 1469. The play, following the chronicles loosely, makes her the younger.","681":"In the Iliad, Ulysses and Diomedes raided the Thracian camp at night, killed King Rhesus and took his horses before they could drink from the Xanthus.","705":"Edward was in fact captured at Olney in 1469 and held by Warwick's brother George Neville, Archbishop of York.","711":"Warwick physically takes the crown from Edward's head, as Margaret mockingly crowned York.","726":"The child Elizabeth carries is the future Edward V, one of the princes later murdered in the Tower in Richard III.","728":"Sanctuary: a church where fugitives could not lawfully be seized. Elizabeth Woodville took sanctuary in Westminster Abbey in 1470.","751":"Henry's 'readeption' (restoration) lasted from October 1470 to April 1471.","765":"Henry Tudor, Earl of Richmond, was the future Henry VII, grandfather of Elizabeth I. Henry's blessing gives the Tudors a prophetic stamp of approval.","781":"Ravenspurgh, on the Humber, is where Bolingbroke landed in Richard II to claim 'only his dukedom'. Edward lands there in 1471 and does the same.","817":"The proclamation formula claims France too: English kings kept the title 'King of France' until 1801.","825":"Edward returned with Burgundian help: 'hasty Germans and blunt Hollanders'.","843":"Edward's image: Henry is the spring that feeds the streams (his supporters); Edward's sea will swallow them.","875":"A card-game image: while Warwick tried to steal a ten, the king was taken from the pack.","879":"'Wind-changing Warwick' sums up the kingmaker who has changed sides.","895":"Edmund Beaufort (killed at St Albans, 2 Henry VI) and Henry Beaufort (executed 1464) were the previous Dukes of Somerset. This one, Edmund Beaufort the younger, will be beheaded after Tewkesbury.","902":"Jephthah (Judges 11) vowed to sacrifice the first thing that met him on his return and had to sacrifice his daughter: an example of a vow it is wicked to keep.","914":"The cedar was the king of trees; Jove's 'spreading tree' is the oak, sacred to him.","932":"Margaret's ship metaphor (anchor, topmast, tackle, pilot) was a stock image of the state in Elizabethan writing.","950":"Hammes (Hames) Castle, near Calais. Oxford historically escaped from it.","962":"Prince Edward calls Richard's jibe a beast fable, like Aesop's, unfit for a royal occasion; Aesop was also said to be deformed.","970":"Historically it is uncertain how Prince Edward died at Tewkesbury (4 May 1471). The chronicles of Hall and Holinshed describe the brothers stabbing him.","986":"Margaret's cry 'You have no children, butchers!' is echoed in Macbeth's 'He has no children'. Her curse on Edward's line comes true in Richard III.","997":"Henry VI died in the Tower on 21 May 1471, probably murdered. Tudor writers blamed Richard.","1006":"Roscius was the most famous actor of ancient Rome. Henry calls Richard an actor arriving to play a death scene.","1008":"Birdlime was a sticky paste spread on twigs to catch birds.","1010":"Daedalus made wings for himself and his son Icarus to escape King Minos of Crete; Icarus flew too near the sun, the wax melted and he drowned.","1014":"Births with ill omens and babies born with teeth were believed to signal monsters. Thomas More's history of Richard III reports the teeth.","1022":"'I am myself alone' is Richard's rejection of kinship. Richard III opens with him alone on stage, continuing this soliloquy.","1026":"Edward lists the dead: three Dukes of Somerset, two Cliffords, two Earls of Northumberland, and Warwick and Montague, whose badge was the bear and ragged staff.","1031":"Judas betrayed Christ with a kiss and the greeting 'All hail' (Matthew 26:49).","1033":"René of Anjou ('Reignier') paid Margaret's ransom in 1475; she died in France in 1482."},"gloss":{"beaver":"visor of a helmet","perforce":"by force","bywords":"objects of scorn","poltroons":"cowards","shambles":"slaughterhouse","factious":"rebellious, quarrelsome","sith":"since","cavilling":"quibbling","winding sheet":"burial shroud","unconstrain'd":"of his own free will","prejudicial":"damaging","entail":"settle (the inheritance) on","bewray":"reveal","environed":"surrounded","Tire":"feed greedily, tear (as a hawk)","presently":"immediately","whet on":"urge on","privily":"secretly","Witty":"intelligent","post":"haste; messenger","Whenas":"when","brat":"child (contemptuous)","mean":"lowly","Dii faciant laudis summa sit ista tuae":"may the gods grant this be the height of your glory (Ovid)","Congeal'd":"clotted","falchion":"curved sword","bootless":"useless","overmatching":"too strong","butt":"target","Phaethon":"son of the sun god who crashed his father's chariot","noontide prick":"the mark of noon on a sundial","invectives":"abuse","bandy":"exchange (words)","buckle":"grapple","grin":"bare its teeth","spurn":"kick","impeach":"discredit","gin":"trap","cony":"rabbit","raught":"reached","preachment":"sermon","mess":"group of four","wanton":"lustful, playful","prodigy":"monster, ill omen","napkin":"handkerchief","withal":"with","feed":"paid","pale":"enclose, encircle","orisons":"prayers","trull":"whore","vizard":"mask","assay":"try","type":"title","boots":"profits","Septentrion":"the north","obdurate":"hard-hearted","flinty":"hard as flint","obsequies":"funeral rites","fell":"cruel","Beshrew me":"curse me","inly":"inwardly","weeping-ripe":"ready to weep","neat":"cattle","younker":"young gallant","meeds":"merits","target":"shield","hap":"luck","baleful":"deadly, sorrowful","poniards":"daggers","depart":"departure","advertised":"informed","spleen":"anger","thrasher":"thresher (of grain)","marches":"border country (of Wales)","Making another head":"raising another army","haught":"haughty","moe":"more","Via":"forward!","amain":"at full speed","forfend":"forbid","puissant":"powerful","it sorts":"it falls out well","lenity":"leniency","level at":"aim at","fondly":"foolishly","toward":"promising","Darraign":"set in order","rate":"scold","minions":"favourites","stigmatic":"one branded by deformity","extraught":"descended","detect":"reveal","callat":"scold, trollop","broach'd":"started","slipp'd":"let pass","Forspent":"exhausted","spite of spite":"whatever happens","Bootless":"useless","clangor":"trumpet-blast","Forslow":"delay","swain":"country lad, shepherd","dials":"sundials","quaintly":"skilfully","ean":"give birth (of sheep)","silly":"innocent, simple","curds":"curdled milk","viands":"food","curious":"elaborately made","press'd":"conscripted","obsequious":"dutiful in mourning","overgone":"overwhelmed","chafed":"enraged","brace":"pair","expostulate":"argue","commixtures":"alliances","mis-proud":"wrongly proud","effuse":"outpouring","argosy":"large merchant ship","doom":"sentence","screech-owl":"bird of ill omen","bereft":"taken away","policy":"cunning","bootless penitence":"useless repentance","unstanched":"unquenched","sinew":"bind firmly","brake":"thicket","laund":"glade","covert":"hiding place","Culling":"picking out","quondam":"former","balm":"holy oil (of coronation)","batt'ry":"bombardment","brinish":"salty","Inferreth":"brings forward","lightness":"fickleness","brook":"endure","vantages":"advantages","shrift":"confession","shriver":"confessor","ten days' wonder":"a short-lived sensation","promontory":"headland","lade":"bail, empty","o'erweens":"presumes","unlick'd bear-whelp":"bear cub not yet licked into shape (bears were thought to lick cubs into form)","dam":"mother","impaled":"encircled","basilisk":"mythical serpent that killed with its look","Sinon":"the Greek who tricked the Trojans into taking in the wooden horse","Protheus":"Proteus, sea god who could change his shape","Machiavel":"cunning politician (after Machiavelli)","strike her sail":"lower her sails, submit","Albion":"England","Gallia":"France","embassade":"embassy","disannuls":"cancels","prescription":"right gained by long possession","buckler":"shield, defend","mellow'd":"ripe, aged","quit":"requite, relieve","jointure":"estate settled on a wife","counterpois'd":"balanced","conveyance":"trickery","nettled":"stung","unhop'd":"unexpected","guerdon'd":"rewarded","stale":"dupe, laughing-stock","willow garland":"sign of a forsaken lover","maskers":"masked revellers","waft":"carry by sea","abodements":"omens","malcontent":"discontented","fawn":"court, flatter","Belike":"probably","presumption":"arrogance","coverture":"cover","adventure":"enterprise","halberds":"long-handled axes","case is alter'd":"the situation has changed","complices":"accomplices","compass":"range","sanctuary":"church refuge where one could not be arrested","disport":"amuse","colour":"pretext","requite":"repay","forwardness":"eagerness","enlargement":"release","temper":"come to terms","Adjudg'd":"awarded","presaging":"prophetic","misgive":"fill with doubt","waned":"declined","Ravenspurg":"port on the Humber where Bolingbroke landed","scrupulous wit":"over-cautious reasoning","out of hand":"at once","bruit":"report, rumour","harbour":"lodge","giddy":"fickle","meed":"merit","allay'd":"eased","subsidies":"taxes","shame-fac'd":"bashful","peremptory":"overbearing","parle":"parley","unbid":"unwelcome","repair":"coming","Atlas":"the giant who held up the sky","forecast":"foresight","ruinate":"ruin","lime":"cement","trow'st":"believe","Jephtha":"biblical judge who sacrificed his daughter to keep a vow","passing":"utter","bug":"bugbear, terror","ramping":"rearing","overpeer'd":"looked down on","shelves":"sandbanks","wrack":"wreck","bark":"ship","famish":"starve","magnanimity":"great courage","unprovided":"unprepared","period":"end","broils":"conflicts","prick":"sprout thorns, be troublesome","currish":"dog-like, mean","malapert":"impudent","railer":"abuser","charm":"silence (by spell)","untimely":"too early","deathsmen":"executioners","alms-deed":"act of charity","Roscius":"famous Roman actor","limed":"caught with birdlime","peevish":"silly","parcel":"part","timeless":"untimely","rook'd":"crouched, perched","pies":"magpies","indigested":"shapeless","sort":"arrange","pitchy":"black","triumphs":"public festivities","annoy":"trouble, grief"}};
const PARTS={"127":[[0,10,"Who can be calm in such extremes? Ah, miserable man! I wish I'd died a virgin and never seen you or borne you a son, since you've turned out such an unnatural father! Has he done anything to deserve losing his birthright like this? If you had loved him half as much as I do, or felt the pain I felt for him once, or fed him with my blood as I did, you would have given your own heart's blood rather than make that savage duke your heir and disinherit your only son."]],"130":[[0,12,"Forced you! Are you a king and you let yourself be forced? I'm ashamed to hear you speak. Ah, timid wretch! You've ruined yourself, your son and me, and given the house of York so much power that you'll reign only because they allow it. To settle the crown on him and his heirs is just digging your own grave and crawling into it long before your time. Warwick is Chancellor and lord of Calais; stern Falconbridge controls the Channel; the duke is made Protector of the realm; and you think you'll be safe? That's the safety of a trembling lamb surrounded by wolves."],[13,20,"If I'd been there, silly woman that I am, the soldiers would have tossed me on their spears before I agreed to that act. But you value your life above your honour; and since you do, I here separate myself from your table and your bed, Henry, until that act of parliament that disinherits my son is repealed."],[21,26,"The northern lords who have abandoned your flag will follow mine once they see it unfurled; and it will be unfurled, to your shame and the complete ruin of the house of York. So I leave you. Come, son, let's go. Our army is ready; come, we'll go after them."]],"160":[[0,5,"An oath means nothing unless it's sworn before a true and lawful authority with power over the one who swears. Henry had no such power; he stole the place. So, since he was the one you swore to, your oath, my lord, is empty and worthless."],[6,12,"So, to arms! And father, just think how sweet it is to wear a crown; inside its circle lies paradise and every bliss and joy the poets invent. Why are we hanging about? I can't rest until the white rose I wear is dyed in the warm blood of Henry's heart."]],"161":[[0,12,"Enough, Richard; I will be king or die. Brother, go to London at once and spur Warwick on to this enterprise. Richard, go to the Duke of Norfolk and tell him our plan secretly. Edward, go to my Lord Cobham; the men of Kent will gladly rise with him. I trust them, for they're soldiers: clever, courteous, generous and full of spirit. While you're busy with this, all that's left for me is to find an opportunity to rise up, without the king or anyone of the house of Lancaster knowing my plan."]],"190":[[0,9,"If I had your brothers here, their lives and yours would not be revenge enough for me. No; if I dug up your ancestors' graves and hung their rotting coffins up in chains, it couldn't quench my anger or ease my heart. The sight of anyone from the house of York is like a Fury tormenting my soul, and until I root out their cursed family and leave not one alive, I'm living in hell. So—"]],"202":[[0,7,"The queen's army has won the field. Both my uncles are killed trying to rescue me, and all my followers turn their backs on the eager enemy and run, like ships before the wind or lambs chased by starving wolves. My sons: God knows what has happened to them; but I do know they have behaved like men born to win fame in life or death."],[8,16,"Three times Richard cut a path through to me and three times shouted, 'Courage, father! Fight it out!' And just as often Edward came to my side with his sword stained red to the hilt with the blood of the men who had fought him. And when the toughest soldiers fell back, Richard shouted, 'Charge! Don't give an inch!' and 'A crown, or else a glorious tomb! A sceptre, or a grave on earth!'"],[17,20,"With that, we charged again; but, alas, we were pushed back again, like a swan I've seen swimming uselessly against the tide and wearing herself out against waves too strong for her."]],"221":[[0,12,"Brave warriors, Clifford and Northumberland, come and make him stand on this molehill here, the man who reached for mountains with outstretched arms but only grasped their shadow. What! Was it you who wanted to be King of England? Was it you who made merry in our parliament and preached a sermon about your noble descent? Where's your pack of sons to back you now? The wanton Edward and the lusty George? And where's that brave hunchbacked freak, your boy Dicky, who used to cheer his dad on in rebellions with his growling voice? Or, along with the rest, where's your darling Rutland?"],[13,23,"Look, York: I stained this handkerchief with the blood that brave Clifford, with his sword's point, made flow from the boy's chest; and if your eyes can weep for his death, I give you this to dry your cheeks with. Alas, poor York! If I didn't hate you to death, I'd pity your miserable state. Please, grieve to make me merry, York; stamp, rave and fret, so I can sing and dance. What, has your fiery heart so dried up your insides that not a tear can fall for Rutland's death?"],[24,29,"Why are you so calm, man? You should be mad; and I'm mocking you like this to make you mad. I see you want to be paid to entertain me. York can't speak unless he wears a crown. A crown for York! And, lords, bow low to him. Hold his hands while I put it on."]],"223":[[0,12,"Yes, indeed, sir, now he looks like a king. Yes, this is the man who took King Henry's throne, and this is the one who was his adopted heir. But how is it that great Plantagenet is crowned so soon and has broken his solemn oath? As I remember, you weren't to be king until our King Henry had shaken hands with Death. And will you wrap your head in Henry's glory and rob his brows of the crown now, while he's alive, against your holy oath? O, it's a fault far too unforgivable. Off with the crown, and with the crown his head! And while we catch our breath, let's take time to kill him."]],"226":[[0,9,"She-wolf of France, but worse than the wolves of France, whose tongue is more poisonous than an adder's fang! How unfitting it is for your sex to gloat like an Amazon whore over the sorrows of those whom fortune has taken prisoner! If your face weren't like a mask, unchanging and hardened shameless by habitual evil, I'd try to make you blush, proud queen. Just to tell you where you came from and who your family is would shame you, if you weren't shameless."],[10,16,"Your father has the title of King of Naples, of both Sicilies and Jerusalem, yet he's not as rich as an English farmer. Did that poor monarch teach you to gloat? There's no need for it, and it does you no good, proud queen, unless it's to prove the saying that a beggar on horseback rides his horse to death."],[17,25,"It's beauty that often makes women proud, but God knows you have little of it. It's virtue that makes them most admired; the lack of it makes you a freak. It's self-control that makes them seem divine; the lack of it makes you hateful. You're as opposite to every good thing as the other side of the world is to us, or as the south is to the north."],[26,31,"O tiger's heart wrapped in a woman's skin! How could you draw the child's life-blood and tell the father to wipe his eyes with it, and still have the face of a woman? Women are soft, mild, compassionate and yielding; you are stern, hard, flinty, rough and pitiless."],[32,38,"You tell me to rage? Well, now you have your wish. You want me to weep? Well, now you have your way; for raging wind whips up endless showers, and when the rage dies down the rain begins. These tears are my sweet Rutland's funeral rites, and every drop cries for revenge for his death, against you, cruel Clifford, and you, treacherous Frenchwoman."]],"228":[[0,11,"Even starving cannibals wouldn't have touched that face of his or stained it with blood; but you are more inhuman, more pitiless, ten times more than the tigers of Hyrcania. See, ruthless queen, an unlucky father's tears. You dipped this cloth in my sweet boy's blood, and I'm washing the blood away with tears. Keep the handkerchief and go and boast about this; and if you tell the sad story truthfully, I swear the listeners will weep; yes, even my enemies will shed streams of tears and say, 'Alas, it was a pitiful deed!'"],[12,16,"There, take the crown, and with the crown my curse; and when you're in need, may you get the same comfort I'm now getting from your cruel hand! Hard-hearted Clifford, take me out of this world; my soul to heaven, my blood on your heads!"]],"242":[[0,11,"I can't be happy until I know what has become of our brave father. I saw him ranging about in the battle and watched him single out Clifford. I thought he carried himself in the thick of the troops like a lion among a herd of cattle, or like a bear surrounded by dogs: after he has bitten a few and made them yelp, the rest keep their distance and bark at him. That's how our father dealt with his enemies; that's how his enemies fled from my warlike father. I think it's honour enough to be his son."],[12,15,"Look how the morning opens her golden gates and says goodbye to the glorious sun. How like it is to the springtime of youth, dressed up like a young gallant prancing off to his girl!"]],"252":[[0,8,"He was surrounded by many enemies and stood against them as Hector, the hope of Troy, stood against the Greeks who would have entered Troy. But even Hercules must give way when outnumbered, and many strokes, even with a small axe, cut down the hardest oak. Your father was overpowered by many hands, but killed only by the angry arm of merciless Clifford and the queen."],[9,17,"She crowned the gracious duke in mockery and laughed in his face, and when he wept with grief the ruthless queen gave him a handkerchief soaked in the innocent blood of sweet young Rutland, killed by rough Clifford, to dry his cheeks. And after many insults and foul taunts, they took his head and put it on the gates of York, and there it remains, the saddest sight I've ever seen."]],"253":[[0,10,"Sweet Duke of York, our support to lean on! Now you're gone, we have no staff, no prop. O Clifford, violent Clifford! You've killed the flower of Europe's knighthood; and you beat him treacherously, for in single combat he would have beaten you. Now my body, the palace of my soul, has become a prison. Ah, if only my soul would break out, so my body could be shut away in the ground to rest! For I'll never be happy again; never, O never, will I see joy again!"]],"254":[[0,9,"I can't weep, for all the moisture in my body is hardly enough to cool my furnace-hot heart; nor can my tongue unload my heart's great burden, because the very breath I should speak with is fanning the coals that burn in my chest and consume me with flames that tears would put out. To weep is to make grief less deep. So tears are for babies; blows and revenge are for me! Richard, I bear your name; I'll avenge your death or die famous in the attempt."]],"261":[[0,15,"Ten days ago I drowned this news in tears; and now, to add to your sorrows, I've come to tell you what has happened since. After the bloody battle at Wakefield, where your brave father breathed his last, news of your loss and his death was brought to me as fast as messengers could ride. I was in London, guarding the king. I gathered my soldiers and crowds of friends and, well equipped as I thought, marched towards St Albans to cut off the queen, bringing the king along on my side; my scouts had told me she was coming with every intention of overturning our recent decree in parliament about King Henry's oath and your succession."],[16,28,"To cut a long story short, we met at St Albans, our armies engaged and both sides fought fiercely. But whether it was the king's coolness, looking very gently on his warlike queen, that robbed my soldiers of their hot anger, or the news of her success, or an unusual fear of Clifford's cruelty, who thunders blood and death at his prisoners, I can't say; but to tell the truth, their weapons came and went like lightning, while our soldiers' weapons, like an owl's lazy flight or a lazy thresher's flail, fell gently, as if they were hitting their friends."],[29,37,"I encouraged them with the justice of our cause and promises of high pay and great rewards, but it was no use. They had no heart to fight, and we had no hope of winning with them, so we ran: the king to the queen; Lord George your brother, Norfolk and I have rushed here at top speed to join you, since we heard you were here in the Welsh borders raising another army to fight again."]],"267":[[0,10,"Why, that's why Warwick came to find you, and why my brother Montague is here. Listen, lords. The proud, insolent queen, with Clifford and the arrogant Northumberland and many more proud birds of their kind, have melted the soft king like wax. He swore to agree to your succession, and his oath is recorded in parliament; and now that whole gang has gone to London to cancel both his oath and anything else that works against the house of Lancaster."],[11,19,"Their army is about thirty thousand strong, I think. Now, if Norfolk's help and mine, together with all the friends you can raise among the loyal Welsh, brave Earl of March, comes to just twenty-five thousand, then forward! We'll march at full speed to London, mount our foaming horses once again, and once again shout, 'Charge the enemy!' but never again turn back and run."]],"282":[[0,9,"My gracious lord, this excessive leniency and harmful pity must be put aside. Who do lions look gently at? Not at the beast that would steal their den. Whose hand does the forest bear lick? Not the man who kills her cubs in front of her. Who escapes the hidden snake's deadly sting? Not the man who steps on its back. The smallest worm will turn when it's trodden on, and doves will peck to protect their young."],[10,16,"Ambitious York aimed at your crown, while you smiled as he frowned angrily. He, only a duke, wanted his son to be king and tried to raise up his children like a loving father; you, a king blessed with a fine son, agreed to disinherit him, which showed you to be a most unloving father."],[17,24,"Even animals without reason feed their young; and though they fear the sight of a man, who hasn't seen them use the very wings they sometimes flee with to attack someone climbing up to their nest, risking their own lives to defend their young? For shame, my lord! Let them be your example."],[25,33,"Wouldn't it be a pity if this fine boy lost his birthright through his father's fault, and in years to come said to his own child, 'What my great-grandfather and grandfather won, my careless father foolishly gave away'? Ah, what a disgrace that would be! Look at the boy, and let his manly face, which promises good fortune, harden your melting heart to keep what's yours and leave it to him."]],"283":[[0,10,"Clifford has played the speaker very well, presenting very powerful arguments. But, Clifford, tell me, haven't you ever heard that things got wrongly always turn out badly? And was it always lucky for the son whose father went to hell for his hoarding? I'll leave my son my good deeds, and I wish my father had left me nothing more; for everything else costs a thousand times more worry to keep than it gives even a scrap of pleasure to own."],[11,12,"Ah, cousin York! I wish your best friends knew how it grieves me that your head is here!"]],"325":[[0,9,"A bunch of straw, the badge of a scold, would be worth a thousand crowns to make this shameless trollop know what she is. Helen of Greece was far more beautiful than you, though your husband may be a Menelaus; and Agamemnon's brother was never wronged by that unfaithful woman as much as this king has been by you. His father made merry in the heart of France, tamed its king and made the Dauphin bow; and if he had married according to his rank, he might have kept that glory to this day."],[10,18,"But when he took a beggar to his bed and honoured your poor father with his wedding day, that sunshine brewed up a storm that washed his father's fortunes out of France and heaped rebellion on his crown at home. For what has started this uproar except your pride? If you'd been humble, our claim would still be asleep; and out of pity for the gentle king, we'd have let our claim pass to another generation."]],"342":[[0,9,"Then let the earth get drunk on our blood. I'll kill my horse, because I will not run. Why are we standing here like soft-hearted women, wailing over our losses while the enemy rages, and watching as if the tragedy were being acted for fun by pretend actors? Here on my knee I swear to God above that I'll never pause again or stand still until death has closed my eyes or fortune has given me my share of revenge."]],"343":[[0,10,"O Warwick, I kneel with you and chain my soul to yours in this vow! And before my knee rises from the cold earth, I lift my hands, eyes and heart to you, God, who sets up and pulls down kings, begging you, if it's your will that this body must be prey to my enemies, at least to open your bronze gates of heaven and let my sinful soul pass sweetly in. Now, lords, let's say goodbye until we meet again, wherever it may be, in heaven or on earth."]],"357":[[0,12,"This battle is like the struggle at dawn, when the fading clouds fight the growing light and the shepherd, blowing on his fingers, can't call it either full day or night. Now it sways this way, like a mighty sea forced by the tide to fight the wind; now it sways that way, like the same sea forced back by the fury of the wind. Sometimes the tide wins, then the wind; now one is ahead, then the other, both straining to win, chest to chest, yet neither conquers nor is conquered. That's how evenly balanced this cruel war is."],[13,19,"I'll sit down here on this molehill. Let God give the victory to whoever he wants! For Margaret my queen, and Clifford too, have scolded me away from the battle, both swearing they do best when I'm not there. I wish I were dead, if it were God's will; for what is there in this world but grief and sorrow?"],[20,39,"O God! I think it would be a happy life to be nothing more than a simple shepherd; to sit on a hill as I'm doing now, carving sundials carefully, line by line, to watch the minutes run: how many make a whole hour, how many hours make a day, how many days complete a year, how many years a man may live. Once I knew that, I'd divide up my time: so many hours I must tend my flock; so many hours I must rest; so many hours I must think; so many hours I must play; my ewes have been pregnant so many days; so many weeks before the poor things give birth; so many years before I shear the fleece. So minutes, hours, days, months and years, spent in the way they were made for, would bring white hair to a quiet grave."],[40,53,"Ah, what a life that would be! How sweet! How lovely! Doesn't the hawthorn bush give a sweeter shade to shepherds watching their innocent sheep than a rich embroidered canopy gives to kings who fear their subjects' treachery? O yes, it does, a thousand times sweeter! And in short, the shepherd's simple curds, his cold thin drink from his leather bottle, his usual sleep in the shade of a fresh tree, all of which he enjoys safely and sweetly, are far better than a prince's delicacies, his food served in a sparkling gold cup and his body laid in an elaborate bed, when worry, mistrust and treason wait on him."]],"359":[[0,5,"It's an ill wind that blows nobody any good. This man, whom I killed in hand-to-hand combat, may have some money on him; and I, who perhaps take it from him now, may before nightfall give up both my life and the money to someone else, as this dead man does to me."],[6,17,"Who is this? O God! It's my father's face, whom I've killed in this fight without knowing it. O terrible times, that produce such things! I was drafted out of London by the king; my father, being the Earl of Warwick's man, came to fight for York, drafted by his master; and I, who received my life from him, have taken his life with my own hands. Forgive me, God, I didn't know what I was doing; and forgive me, father, for I didn't know you. My tears will wash away these bloody marks, and I'll say no more until they've flowed their fill."]],"362":[[0,8,"You who resisted me so stubbornly, give me your gold if you have any, for I've paid for it with a hundred blows. But let me see: is this our enemy's face? Ah, no, no, no! It's my only son! Ah, boy, if there's any life left in you, lift up your eyes; see, see what showers rise, blown by the windy storm of my heart, onto your wounds, which kill my eyes and heart!"],[9,14,"O God, pity this miserable age! What cruel, butchering, unnatural, rebellious deeds this deadly quarrel produces every day! O boy, your father gave you life too soon, and took your life too late!"]],"383":[[0,9,"Here my candle burns out; yes, here it dies, the candle that gave King Henry light while it lasted. O Lancaster! I fear your downfall more than my soul's parting from my body! Love and fear of me held many friends to you; and now that I fall, those strong bonds melt, weakening Henry and strengthening arrogant York. The common people swarm like summer flies; and where do gnats fly but to the sun? And who is the sun now but Henry's enemies?"],[10,21,"O Phoebus, if you had never let Phaethon drive your fiery horses, your burning chariot would never have scorched the earth! And Henry, if you had ruled as kings should, or as your father and his father did, giving no ground to the house of York, they would never have sprung up like summer flies; I and ten thousand others in this unlucky kingdom would have left no widows mourning our deaths, and you would have kept your throne in peace today. For what makes weeds grow but mild weather? And what makes robbers bold but too much leniency?"],[22,29,"Complaints are useless, and my wounds can't be cured; there's no way to escape, and no strength to keep running. The enemy is merciless and will show no pity, for I've deserved no pity from them. The air has got into my deadly wounds, and losing so much blood makes me faint. Come, York and Richard, Warwick and the rest; I stabbed your fathers' chests; now split mine."]],"408":[[0,10,"Yes, but he's dead. Off with the traitor's head, and put it up where your father's is. And now to London in a triumphant march, to be crowned England's royal king. From there Warwick will cross the sea to France and ask for Lady Bona as your queen. That way you'll bind both countries together, and with France as your friend you won't have to fear the scattered enemy who hope to rise again; for though they can't sting hard enough to hurt you, expect them to buzz in your ears and annoy you."],[11,13,"First I'll see the coronation, and then I'll cross the sea to Brittany to arrange this marriage, if my lord agrees."]],"425":[[0,13,"My queen and son have gone to France for help; and I hear the great, commanding Warwick has gone there to ask for the French king's sister as a wife for Edward. If this news is true, poor queen and son, your effort is wasted, for Warwick is a clever speaker and Lewis is a prince easily won by moving words. Then again, by this reckoning Margaret may win him, for she's a woman to be greatly pitied. Her sighs will batter at his heart, her tears will pierce a heart of marble; the tiger will be gentle while she mourns, and Nero would be touched with pity to hear her complaints and see her salt tears."],[14,26,"Yes, but she's come to beg, and Warwick to give: she on his left side begging help for Henry, he on his right asking a wife for Edward. She weeps and says her Henry is deposed; he smiles and says his Edward is crowned; so that she, poor wretch, can't speak any more for grief, while Warwick explains Edward's claim, smooths over the wrong, offers powerful arguments, and in the end wins the king away from her with the promise of his sister and whatever else will strengthen and support King Edward's position. O Margaret, that's how it will be! And you, poor soul, will be abandoned, just as you went, hopeless!"]],"439":[[0,11,"Why, am I dead? Don't I still breathe, as a man? Ah, simple men! You don't know what you're swearing. Look, as I blow this feather away from my face and the air blows it back to me, obeying my breath when I blow and giving way to another when that blows, always ruled by the stronger gust: that's how fickle you common people are. But don't break your oaths; my gentle plea won't make you guilty of that sin. Go where you like; the king will obey orders. You be kings: command, and I'll obey."]],"531":[[0,9,"Yes, Edward will treat women honourably. I wish he were wasted away, marrow, bones and all, so that no promising offspring could spring from his loins to block my way to the golden time I'm hoping for! And yet, between my soul's desire and me, even with lustful Edward's claim buried, stand Clarence, Henry and his son young Edward, and all the unexpected children they may have, to take their places before I can take mine. A chilling forecast for my plans!"],[10,21,"Why, then I'm only dreaming of the crown, like someone standing on a headland who spots a distant shore he'd like to walk on, wishes his feet could go as far as his eyes, and scolds the sea that separates him from it, saying he'll bail it dry to get his way. That's how I long for the crown, so far away; that's how I scold whatever keeps me from it; and that's how I say I'll cut away the obstacles, flattering myself with impossibilities. My eye is too quick and my heart too ambitious unless my hand and strength can match them."],[22,40,"Well, suppose there's no kingdom for Richard; what other pleasure can the world give? I'll make my heaven in a lady's lap, dress myself in fine clothes and bewitch sweet ladies with my words and looks. O miserable thought, and less likely than winning twenty golden crowns! Why, love rejected me in my mother's womb; and so that I'd have nothing to do with her gentle laws, she bribed weak nature to shrivel my arm like a withered shrub, to build a spiteful mountain on my back where deformity sits to mock my body, to shape my legs unequal in size, to put me out of proportion in every part, like chaos, or an unlicked bear cub that doesn't take after its mother. So am I a man to be loved? O monstrous mistake, even to think it!"],[41,57,"Then, since this earth gives me no joy except to command, rebuke and overpower people better-looking than me, I'll make my heaven dreaming of the crown, and as long as I live I'll count this world nothing but hell until the misshapen body that carries this head is circled with a glorious crown. And yet I don't know how to get the crown, for many lives stand between me and my goal; and like someone lost in a thorny wood, tearing at the thorns and torn by them, looking for a path and straying from it, not knowing how to reach open air but struggling desperately to find it, I torment myself to catch the English crown. And I'll free myself from that torment or hack my way out with a bloody axe."],[58,71,"Why, I can smile, and murder while I smile, and cry 'Wonderful!' at what grieves my heart, and wet my cheeks with fake tears, and fit my face to any occasion. I'll drown more sailors than the mermaid, kill more onlookers than the basilisk; I'll speak as well as Nestor, deceive more slyly than Ulysses, and take another Troy like Sinon. I can add colours to the chameleon, change shape with Proteus when it suits me, and teach the murderous Machiavelli a lesson. Can I do all this and not get a crown? Tut, even if it were further off, I'll pull it down."]],"542":[[0,8,"Those kind words revive my drooping spirits and let my tongue-tied sorrows speak. So let noble Lewis know that Henry, the only owner of my love, has gone from being a king to being an exile, forced to live wretchedly in Scotland, while proud, ambitious Edward, Duke of York, usurps the royal title and throne of England's true, anointed, lawful king."],[9,16,"That's why I, poor Margaret, have come with my son, Prince Edward, Henry's heir, to beg for your just and lawful help; and if you fail us, all our hope is finished. Scotland wants to help but can't; our people and our nobles are both misled, our treasure seized, our soldiers routed, and, as you see, we ourselves are in a sad state."]],"556":[[0,12,"King Lewis, and Lady Bona, hear me before you answer Warwick. His request doesn't come from Edward's honest, well-meant love, but from deceit born of need; for how can tyrants rule safely at home unless they buy powerful alliances abroad? This reason is enough to prove he's a tyrant: Henry is still alive; and even if he were dead, here stands Prince Edward, King Henry's son. So take care, Lewis, that by this treaty and marriage you don't bring danger and dishonour on yourself; for though usurpers may rule for a while, heaven is just and time puts down wrongs."]],"595":[[0,10,"King Lewis, I declare here before heaven, and by my hope of heavenly bliss, that I'm innocent of Edward's wrongdoing. He's no longer my king, for he dishonours me, but himself most of all, if he could see his own shame. Did I forget that my father died before his time because of the house of York? Did I let the insult to my niece pass? Did I crown him with the royal crown? Did I put Henry out of his birthright? And am I rewarded in the end with shame?"],[11,17,"Shame on him! I deserve honour; and to repair the honour I've lost for his sake, I here renounce him and go back to Henry. My noble queen, let old grudges go; from now on I am your true servant. I'll avenge his wrong to Lady Bona and put Henry back in his former place."]],"597":[[0,9,"So much his friend, yes, his sincere friend, that if King Lewis will provide us with a few companies of picked soldiers, I'll undertake to land them on our coast and drive the tyrant from his throne by war. His new bride won't be able to help him; and as for Clarence, my letters tell me he's very likely to break with him now, for marrying out of lust rather than for honour or the strength and safety of our country."]],"616":[[0,9,"I came from Edward as an ambassador, but I go back his sworn and deadly enemy. He sent me on a matter of marriage, but dreadful war will answer his request. Had he no one else to make a fool of but me? Then no one but me will turn his joke to sorrow. I was the main one who raised him to the crown, and I'll be the main one to bring him down again; not because I pity Henry's misery, but because I want revenge for Edward's mockery."]],"681":[[0,6,"Then, gentle Clarence, welcome to Warwick; and welcome, Somerset. I think it's cowardly to stay suspicious when a noble heart has offered an open hand as a sign of love; otherwise I might think that Clarence, Edward's brother, was only pretending to support our cause. But welcome, sweet Clarence; my daughter will be yours."],[7,21,"And now all that remains is this: since your brother is camped carelessly, his soldiers scattered in the towns around and only a small guard with him, we can surprise him under cover of night and take him as we please. Our scouts have found the venture very easy; so that, just as Ulysses and brave Diomedes crept by skill and courage into Rhesus's tents and brought away the fateful Thracian horses, we, well covered by night's black cloak, can catch Edward's guard unawares, beat them down and seize him. I don't say kill him; I only mean to take him by surprise. Those of you who'll follow me in this attempt, cheer the name of Henry with your leader."]],"707":[[0,9,"Yes, but things have changed. When you disgraced me during my embassy, I demoted you from being king, and now I've come to make you Duke of York. Alas! How could you govern any kingdom when you don't know how to treat ambassadors, or how to be content with one wife, or how to treat your brothers like a brother, or how to work for the people's welfare, or how to protect yourself from enemies?"]],"728":[[0,9,"I'm told he's coming towards London to put the crown on Henry's head again. You can guess the rest: King Edward's friends must fall. But to escape the tyrant's violence, for never trust someone who has once broken his word, I'll go at once to sanctuary, to save at least the heir to Edward's right. There I'll be safe from force and trickery. Come, then, let's run while we can; if Warwick catches us, we're sure to die."]],"732":[[0,12,"Now, my Lord Hastings and Sir William Stanley, stop wondering why I brought you here into the thickest part of the park. This is how things stand: you know our king, my brother, is the archbishop's prisoner here; he's treated well and given a lot of freedom, and he often comes hunting this way for amusement with only a weak guard. I've secretly let him know that if he comes this way at about this time, pretending to be at his usual sport, he'll find his friends here with horses and men to free him from captivity."]],"751":[[0,9,"For what, lieutenant? For treating me well? No, you can be sure I'll reward your kindness well, since it made my imprisonment a pleasure; yes, the kind of pleasure caged birds feel when, after many gloomy thoughts, they finally forget their lost freedom in the sound of household music. But Warwick, after God, it's you who set me free, so I thank God and you above all; he was the author, you the instrument."],[10,16,"So, to beat fortune's spite by living humbly where fortune can't hurt me, and so that the people of this blessed land are not punished by my unlucky stars, Warwick, although my head still wears the crown, I here hand over the government to you, for you are lucky in everything you do."]],"775":[[0,9,"My lord, I don't like this escape of Edward's, for Burgundy will certainly give him help, and we'll have more wars before long. Just as Henry's prophecy just now gladdened my heart with hope for young Richmond, so my heart fears what may happen to him in these conflicts, to his harm and ours. So, Lord Oxford, to prevent the worst, we'll send him away at once to Brittany until the storms of civil war are over."]],"821":[[0,11,"Thank you, brave Montgomery, and thanks to you all; if fortune favours me, I'll repay this kindness. Now let's stay here in York tonight; and when the morning sun lifts his chariot above the edge of the horizon, we'll go on towards Warwick and his allies, for I know Henry is no soldier. Ah, wayward Clarence! How badly it suits you to flatter Henry and abandon your brother! Still, we'll face both you and Warwick as best we can. Come on, brave soldiers; don't doubt we'll win the day, and once it's won, don't doubt you'll be well paid."]],"828":[[0,9,"In Warwickshire I have loyal friends, peaceful in peacetime but bold in war; I'll call them up. And you, son Clarence, will rouse the knights and gentlemen in Suffolk, Norfolk and Kent to come with you. You, brother Montague, will find men in Buckingham, Northampton and Leicestershire who are willing to obey your orders. And you, brave Oxford, so wonderfully well loved, will gather your friends in Oxfordshire."],[10,15,"My sovereign, with the loyal citizens, like his island surrounded by the ocean, or chaste Diana circled by her nymphs, will stay in London until we come back to him. Fair lords, say goodbye and don't stop to answer. Farewell, my sovereign."]],"839":[[0,12,"That's not what I fear. My good deeds have made me well known. I haven't closed my ears to the people's requests, or put off their petitions with long delays. My pity has been a balm to heal their wounds, my gentleness has eased their swelling griefs, my mercy has dried their streaming tears. I haven't wanted their wealth, or burdened them much with heavy taxes, or been quick to take revenge, though they did much wrong. So why should they love Edward more than me? No, Exeter, such kindness earns kindness; and when the lion is gentle to the lamb, the lamb will never stop following him."]],"902":[[0,9,"Look, I throw my disgrace at you. I will not ruin my father's house, which he cemented together with his blood, to set up Lancaster. Why, do you really think, Warwick, that Clarence is so harsh, blunt and unnatural as to turn the deadly weapons of war against his brother and lawful king? Perhaps you'll bring up my holy oath; to keep that oath would be more wicked than Jephthah's keeping his when he sacrificed his daughter."],[10,20,"I'm so sorry for the wrong I've done that, to earn my brother's favour, I here declare myself your deadly enemy, determined, wherever I meet you, and I will meet you if you come out, to punish you for foully misleading me. And so, proud-hearted Warwick, I defy you, and turn my blushing cheeks to my brother. Forgive me, Edward, I'll make amends; and Richard, don't frown on my faults, for from now on I'll never be fickle again."]],"914":[[0,5,"Ah! Who's near? Come to me, friend or enemy, and tell me who has won, York or Warwick. Why do I ask? My mangled body shows it; my blood, my lack of strength, my sick heart show that I must give my body to the earth and, by my fall, the victory to my enemy."],[6,17,"So the cedar falls to the axe's edge, whose arms sheltered the princely eagle, under whose shade the rearing lion slept, whose top branch looked down on Jove's spreading oak and protected low shrubs from winter's powerful wind. These eyes, now dimmed by death's black veil, have been as piercing as the midday sun in seeking out the world's secret treasons. The wrinkles on my brow, now filled with blood, were often compared to the graves of kings; for what living king was there whose grave I couldn't dig? And who dared smile when Warwick frowned?"],[18,23,"Look, now my glory is smeared with dust and blood! My parks, my walks, the manors I owned, are leaving me even now, and of all my lands nothing is left me but the length of my body. Why, what is pomp, rule and power but earth and dust? However we live, we must die."]],"932":[[0,11,"Great lords, wise men never sit and wail over their losses, but cheerfully look for ways to repair the damage. What if the mast is blown overboard, the cable broken, the anchor lost and half our sailors swallowed by the sea? Our pilot is still alive. Is it right for him to leave the helm and, like a frightened boy, add his tears to the sea, giving more strength to what already has too much, while the ship splits on the rock as he moans, when hard work and courage might have saved it? Ah, what a shame! Ah, what a fault that would be!"],[12,23,"Say Warwick was our anchor; what of it? And Montague our topmast; what of him? Our slaughtered friends the rigging; what of them? Why, isn't Oxford here another anchor, and Somerset another fine mast, and our French friends the ropes and rigging? And though we're unskilled, why shouldn't Ned and I be allowed to take the skilled pilot's place for once? We won't leave the helm to sit and weep, but keep our course, though the rough wind says no, away from the sandbanks and rocks that threaten to wreck us. Scolding the waves does as much good as speaking kindly to them."],[24,37,"And what is Edward but a merciless sea? What is Clarence but a quicksand of deceit? And Richard but a jagged deadly rock? All of them enemies of our poor ship. Say you can swim: alas, only for a while! Step on the sand: you'll quickly sink. Climb on the rock: the tide will wash you off, or else you'll starve; that's a triple death. I say this, lords, to make you understand, in case any of you should want to desert us, that there's no more mercy to be hoped for from those brothers than from merciless waves, sands and rocks. So, courage! It would be childish weakness to mourn or fear what can't be avoided."]],"933":[[0,10,"I think a woman of such brave spirit, if a coward heard her speak these words, would fill his heart with courage and make him, unarmed, defeat an armed man. I don't say this because I doubt anyone here; for if I suspected a single frightened man, he'd be allowed to leave in good time, so that when we need him he doesn't infect another and make him as fearful as himself. If there's anyone like that here, which God forbid, let him leave before we need his help."]],"946":[[0,9,"Lords, knights and gentlemen, my tears contradict what I ought to say; for with every word I speak, you can see I'm drinking the water of my eyes. So, only this: Henry, your sovereign, is the enemy's prisoner, his throne usurped, his kingdom a slaughterhouse, his subjects killed, his laws cancelled and his treasure spent; and over there is the wolf that does this damage. You're fighting for justice; so, in God's name, lords, be brave and give the signal to fight."]],"986":[[0,9,"O Ned! Sweet Ned! Speak to your mother, boy. Can't you speak? O traitors! Murderers! Those who stabbed Caesar shed no blood at all, did no wrong and deserved no blame, if this foul deed were set beside it. He was a man; this, by comparison, a child, and men never vent their fury on a child. What's worse than a murderer, so I can call you it? No, no, my heart will burst if I speak; and I will speak, so that my heart will burst."],[10,16,"Butchers and villains! Bloody cannibals! What a sweet plant you've cut down before its time! You have no children, butchers! If you had, the thought of them would have stirred up pity; but if you ever happen to have a child, expect to see him cut off in his youth, as you, executioners, have disposed of this sweet young prince!"]],"1014":[[0,8,"If you had been killed when you first acted arrogantly, you wouldn't have lived to kill a son of mine. And so I prophesy that many thousands, who now don't suspect any of what I fear, and many an old man's sigh and many a widow's, and many an orphan's tear-filled eye, men for their sons', wives for their husbands' fate, and orphans for their parents' untimely death, will regret the hour you were ever born."],[9,21,"The owl shrieked at your birth, an evil sign; the night-crow cried, foretelling unlucky times; dogs howled, and a hideous storm shook down trees; the raven perched on the chimney top, and chattering magpies sang in dismal discord. Your mother felt more than a mother's pain, and yet gave birth to less than a mother hopes for: a shapeless, deformed lump, not like the fruit of such a fine tree. You had teeth in your head when you were born, to show you came to bite the world; and if the rest is true that I've heard, you came—"]],"1022":[[0,11,"I, who have neither pity, love nor fear. Indeed, what Henry told me is true; for I've often heard my mother say I came into the world feet first. Don't you think I had reason to hurry and seek the ruin of those who stole our right? The midwife was amazed, and the women cried, 'O Jesus bless us, he's born with teeth!' And so I was, which clearly meant that I should snarl and bite and play the dog. Then, since the heavens have shaped my body like this, let hell twist my mind to match it."],[12,23,"I have no brother; I'm like no brother; and let this word 'love', which old men call divine, live in men who are like one another, and not in me! I am myself alone. Clarence, beware! You keep me from the light; but I'll arrange a pitch-black day for you; for I'll spread such prophecies about that Edward will fear for his life, and then, to cure his fear, I'll be your death. King Henry and his son the prince are gone; Clarence, your turn is next, and then the rest, for I count myself nothing until I'm the best."],[24,25,"I'll throw your body in another room, and gloat, Henry, on your day of doom."]],"1026":[[0,11,"Once more we sit on England's royal throne, bought back with our enemies' blood. What brave enemies we have mown down at the height of their pride, like corn in autumn! Three Dukes of Somerset, three times famous as bold and unquestioned champions; two Cliffords, father and son; and two Northumberlands: two braver men never spurred their horses at the sound of the trumpet; and with them the two brave bears, Warwick and Montague, who chained up the royal lion and made the forest tremble when they roared."],[12,19,"So we've swept all threats from our throne and made security our footstool. Come here, Bess, and let me kiss my boy. Young Ned, for your sake your uncles and I have kept watch in our armour through winter nights and marched on foot in the scalding summer heat, so that you could inherit the crown in peace; and you'll reap the profit of our labours."]]};
const QUOTES={"themes":{"crown":["The crown and the right to rule","Who is the lawful king? Titles, entails, proclamations and a crown that changes hands again and again."],"oaths":["Oaths and broken faith","Every side swears and forswears: York's oath, Henry's entail, the keepers' allegiance, Clarence's two betrayals."],"revenge":["Revenge","Father for father, son for son: Clifford, Rutland, York, Clifford again, Prince Edward and Henry."],"family":["Fathers, sons and brothers","Disinherited sons, murdered children, a son who killed his father, three brothers who will turn on each other."],"women":["Women and power","Margaret the warrior queen, Lady Grey's wooing, Bona's insult: women who change the course of the war."],"fortune":["Fortune, tides and turning sides","Battles swaying like the sea, a feather blown by the wind, Warwick the weathercock, the wheel of fortune."],"nature":["Nature and the unnatural","Tigers, wolves, lambs and a child born with teeth: what is natural for a king, a mother, a man?"],"self":["Ambition and the self","Richard's soliloquies: the actor, the Machiavel, 'myself alone'."]},"quotes":[{"id":"2","who":"WARWICK","text":"I wonder how the king escap'd our hands.","themes":["crown"],"note":"The first line: the king is a fugitive in his own war."},{"id":"14","who":"WARWICK","text":"This is the palace of the fearful king,","themes":["crown"],"note":"Warwick calls Henry 'fearful' (timid) in his own palace."},{"id":"22","who":"WARWICK","text":"The bloody parliament shall this be call'd,","themes":["crown","fortune"],"note":"Warwick threatens to turn law into slaughter."},{"id":"24","who":"WARWICK","text":"Dares stir a wing if Warwick shake his bells.","themes":["crown","fortune"],"note":"Warwick as the falcon who frightens other birds."},{"id":"24","who":"WARWICK","text":"I'll plant Plantagenet, root him up who dares.","themes":["crown"],"note":"The kingmaker's boast."},{"id":"29","who":"CLIFFORD","text":"The hope thereof makes Clifford mourn in steel.","themes":["revenge"],"note":"Clifford's grief takes the form of armour."},{"id":"32","who":"CLIFFORD","text":"Patience is for poltroons, such as he;","themes":["revenge","crown"],"note":"Clifford despises Henry's patience."},{"id":"36","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Far be the thought of this from Henry's heart,","themes":["crown"],"note":"Henry refuses to turn Parliament into a slaughterhouse."},{"id":"41","who":"YORK","text":"'T was my inheritance, as the earldom was.","themes":["crown","family"],"note":"York claims his dukedom by birth, not Henry's gift."},{"id":"58","who":"KING HENRY","text":"When I was crown'd I was but nine months old.","themes":["crown","family"],"note":"Henry the baby king."},{"id":"59","who":"RICHARD","text":"You are old enough now, and yet, methinks, you lose.","themes":["crown"],"note":"Richard's first sneer at Henry."},{"id":"66","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Shall be my winding sheet.","themes":["crown"],"note":"Henry's brief flash of defiance."},{"id":"70","who":"KING HENRY","text":"I know not what to say; my title's weak.","themes":["crown","oaths"],"note":"Henry's private admission."},{"id":"70","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Tell me, may not a king adopt an heir?","themes":["crown","family"],"note":"Henry's legal argument: Richard II adopted Henry IV."},{"id":"79","who":"EXETER","text":"My conscience tells me he is lawful king.","themes":["crown","oaths"],"note":"Exeter defects in conscience."},{"id":"80","who":"KING HENRY","text":"All will revolt from me and turn to him.","themes":["crown","fortune"],"note":"Henry's fear in an aside."},{"id":"84","who":"CLIFFORD","text":"King Henry, be thy title right or wrong,","themes":["crown","oaths"],"note":"Clifford's loyalty does not depend on legality."},{"id":"87","who":"WARWICK","text":"Write up his title with usurping blood.","themes":["crown"],"note":"Warwick's threat of force."},{"id":"89","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Let me for this my lifetime reign as king.","themes":["crown","family"],"note":"Henry's bargain."},{"id":"94","who":"WESTMORELAND","text":"Base, fearful, and despairing Henry!","themes":["crown"],"note":"Westmoreland's scorn."},{"id":"99","who":"WESTMORELAND","text":"Farewell, faint-hearted and degenerate king,","themes":["crown","family"],"note":"'Degenerate': unworthy of his father, Henry V."},{"id":"107","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Whom I unnaturally shall disinherit.","themes":["family","nature"],"note":"Henry himself calls the act unnatural."},{"id":"107","who":"KING HENRY","text":"The crown to thee, and to thine heirs for ever;","themes":["crown","oaths"],"note":"The entail."},{"id":"112","who":"YORK","text":"Now York and Lancaster are reconcil'd.","themes":["crown","fortune"],"note":"York's hollow peace."},{"id":"127","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","text":"Who can be patient in such extremes?","themes":["women","family"],"note":"Margaret's first line."},{"id":"127","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","text":"Ah, wretched man! would I had died a maid,","themes":["women","family"],"note":"Margaret wishes she had never married."},{"id":"130","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","text":"Enforc'd thee! art thou king, and wilt be forc'd?","themes":["crown","women"],"note":"Margaret's contempt for a king who can be forced."},{"id":"130","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","text":"The trembling lamb environed with wolves.","themes":["nature","crown"],"note":"Margaret's image for Henry among the Yorkists."},{"id":"130","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","text":"Both from thy table, Henry, and thy bed,","themes":["women","family"],"note":"Margaret's separation."},{"id":"138","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Poor queen! how love to me and to her son","themes":["women","family"],"note":"Henry's gentle understanding."},{"id":"138","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Tire on the flesh of me and of my son.","themes":["nature"],"note":"York as a starving eagle."},{"id":"155","who":"EDWARD","text":"I would break a thousand oaths to reign one year.","themes":["oaths","crown"],"note":"Edward's careless ambition."},{"id":"160","who":"RICHARD","text":"An oath is of no moment, being not took","themes":["oaths"],"note":"Richard's sophistry."},{"id":"160","who":"RICHARD","text":"How sweet a thing it is to wear a crown,","themes":["crown","self"],"note":"Richard's love of the crown."},{"id":"160","who":"RICHARD","text":"Within whose circuit is Elysium","themes":["crown","self"],"note":"The crown as paradise."},{"id":"161","who":"YORK","text":"Richard, enough; I will be king, or die.","themes":["crown","oaths"],"note":"York breaks his oath at once."},{"id":"172","who":"RICHARD","text":"A woman-general! what should we fear?","themes":["women"],"note":"Richard's contempt for Margaret."},{"id":"181","who":"CLIFFORD","text":"Whose father slew my father, he shall die.","themes":["revenge","family"],"note":"Clifford's logic of revenge."},{"id":"184","who":"TUTOR","text":"Ah, Clifford, murder not this innocent child,","themes":["revenge","family"],"note":"The tutor pleads for Rutland."},{"id":"188","who":"CLIFFORD","text":"Hath stopp'd the passage where thy words should enter.","themes":["revenge"],"note":"Clifford is deaf to pity."},{"id":"190","who":"CLIFFORD","text":"The sight of any of the house of York","themes":["revenge","family"],"note":"Clifford's hatred of a whole family."},{"id":"195","who":"RUTLAND","text":"Thou hast one son; for his sake pity me,","themes":["revenge","family"],"note":"Rutland's appeal to Clifford as a father."},{"id":"196","who":"CLIFFORD","text":"Thy father slew my father; therefore, die.","themes":["revenge","family"],"note":"Clifford kills Rutland."},{"id":"198","who":"CLIFFORD","text":"Plantagenet! I come, Plantagenet!","themes":["revenge"],"note":"Clifford goes to find York."},{"id":"202","who":"YORK","text":"The army of the queen hath got the field.","themes":["women","fortune"],"note":"York admits defeat."},{"id":"202","who":"YORK","text":"With bootless labour swim against the tide","themes":["fortune"],"note":"York as a swan swimming against the tide."},{"id":"204","who":"YORK","text":"The sands are number'd that make up my life;","themes":["fortune"],"note":"York accepts death."},{"id":"208","who":"CLIFFORD","text":"Now Phaethon hath tumbled from his car,","themes":["crown","fortune"],"note":"Clifford casts York as Phaethon."},{"id":"209","who":"YORK","text":"My ashes, as the phoenix, may bring forth","themes":["revenge","family"],"note":"York prophesies revenge through his sons."},{"id":"214","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","text":"It is war's prize to take all vantages,","themes":["fortune"],"note":"Northumberland's realism."},{"id":"221","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","text":"Come, make him stand upon this molehill here,","themes":["crown","women"],"note":"Margaret's mock throne."},{"id":"221","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","text":"That raught at mountains with outstretched arms,","themes":["crown","self"],"note":"Margaret on York's ambition."},{"id":"221","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","text":"Look, York; I stain'd this napkin with the blood","themes":["revenge","women"],"note":"Margaret's napkin."},{"id":"221","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","text":"York cannot speak unless he wear a crown.","themes":["crown","women"],"note":"Margaret's taunt."},{"id":"223","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","text":"Off with the crown, and with the crown his head!","themes":["crown","revenge"],"note":"Margaret's command."},{"id":"226","who":"YORK","text":"She-wolf of France, but worse than wolves of France,","themes":["women","nature"],"note":"York's attack on Margaret."},{"id":"226","who":"YORK","text":"O tiger's heart wrapp'd in a woman's hide!","themes":["women","nature"],"note":"York's most famous line."},{"id":"226","who":"YORK","text":"Women are soft, mild, pitiful, and flexible;","themes":["women","nature"],"note":"York's idea of womanhood."},{"id":"226","who":"YORK","text":"Thou stern, obdurate, flinty, rough, remorseless.","themes":["women","nature"],"note":"York's list of Margaret's qualities."},{"id":"228","who":"YORK","text":"Upon my soul, the hearers will shed tears,","themes":["revenge"],"note":"York appeals to future audiences."},{"id":"229","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","text":"Had he been slaughter-man to all my kin,","themes":["revenge","nature"],"note":"Northumberland's pity."},{"id":"235","who":"YORK","text":"Open thy gate of mercy, gracious God!","themes":["revenge"],"note":"York's dying prayer."},{"id":"237","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","text":"So York may overlook the town of York.","themes":["revenge","crown"],"note":"Margaret's final joke."},{"id":"242","who":"RICHARD","text":"Methinks 'tis pride enough to be his son.","themes":["family"],"note":"Richard's pride in York."},{"id":"243","who":"EDWARD","text":"Dazzle mine eyes, or do I see three suns?","themes":["family","fortune"],"note":"Edward sees the parhelion."},{"id":"244","who":"RICHARD","text":"Now are they but one lamp, one light, one sun.","themes":["family"],"note":"The suns join."},{"id":"246","who":"RICHARD","text":"You love the breeder better than the male.","themes":["women","family"],"note":"Richard's joke about Edward's lust."},{"id":"253","who":"EDWARD","text":"Now my soul's palace is become a prison.","themes":["family"],"note":"Edward's grief."},{"id":"254","who":"RICHARD","text":"Tears, then, for babes, blows and revenge for me!","themes":["revenge","self"],"note":"Richard's grief turned to rage."},{"id":"254","who":"RICHARD","text":"Richard, I bear thy name; I'll venge thy death,","themes":["revenge","family"],"note":"Richard vows revenge."},{"id":"256","who":"RICHARD","text":"Show thy descent by gazing 'gainst the sun;","themes":["crown","family"],"note":"Richard urges Edward to claim the crown."},{"id":"261","who":"WARWICK","text":"Who look'd full gently on his warlike queen,","themes":["women","crown"],"note":"Warwick on Henry at St Albans."},{"id":"265","who":"WARWICK","text":"Can pluck the diadem from faint Henry's head","themes":["crown"],"note":"Warwick's power."},{"id":"270","who":"WARWICK","text":"No longer Earl of March, but Duke of York.","themes":["crown","family"],"note":"Warwick names Edward."},{"id":"271","who":"RICHARD","text":"Then, Clifford, were thy heart as hard as steel,","themes":["revenge"],"note":"Richard's vow against Clifford."},{"id":"281","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Withhold revenge, dear God! 't is not my fault,","themes":["revenge","oaths"],"note":"Henry fears God's punishment."},{"id":"282","who":"CLIFFORD","text":"To whom do lions cast their gentle looks?","themes":["nature","family"],"note":"Clifford's natural-law argument."},{"id":"282","who":"CLIFFORD","text":"The smallest worm will turn, being trodden on,","themes":["nature"],"note":"Clifford's proverb."},{"id":"282","who":"CLIFFORD","text":"My careless father fondly gave away?","themes":["family","crown"],"note":"Clifford imagines the Prince's reproach."},{"id":"283","who":"KING HENRY","text":"That things ill got had ever bad success?","themes":["crown","oaths"],"note":"Henry's reply."},{"id":"283","who":"KING HENRY","text":"I'll leave my son my virtuous deeds behind,","themes":["family"],"note":"Henry's legacy."},{"id":"285","who":"KING HENRY","text":"And learn this lesson,—draw thy sword in right.","themes":["family","crown"],"note":"Henry knights his son."},{"id":"290","who":"CLIFFORD","text":"The queen hath best success when you are absent.","themes":["women","crown"],"note":"Clifford's blunt advice."},{"id":"292","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Why, that's my fortune too; therefore I'll stay.","themes":["fortune","crown"],"note":"Henry's quiet wit."},{"id":"311","who":"RICHARD","text":"Upon that Clifford, that cruel child-killer.","themes":["revenge"],"note":"Richard names Clifford."},{"id":"316","who":"KING HENRY","text":"I am a king, and privileg'd to speak.","themes":["crown"],"note":"Henry's protest."},{"id":"322","who":"RICHARD","text":"For, well I wot, thou hast thy mother's tongue.","themes":["women","family"],"note":"Richard insults the Prince."},{"id":"323","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","text":"But like a foul misshapen stigmatic,","themes":["nature","self"],"note":"Margaret on Richard's body."},{"id":"324","who":"RICHARD","text":"Iron of Naples hid with English gilt,","themes":["women"],"note":"Richard on Margaret's lowly origins."},{"id":"325","who":"EDWARD","text":"Hadst thou been meek, our title still had slept;","themes":["women","crown"],"note":"Edward blames Margaret."},{"id":"329","who":"EDWARD","text":"These words will cost ten thousand lives this day.","themes":["fortune"],"note":"Edward ends the parley."},{"id":"335","who":"EDWARD","text":"For this world frowns and Edward's sun is clouded.","themes":["fortune","family"],"note":"Edward in defeat."},{"id":"341","who":"RICHARD","text":"'Warwick, revenge! brother, revenge my death!'","themes":["revenge","family"],"note":"The dying cry of Warwick's brother."},{"id":"342","who":"WARWICK","text":"I'll kill my horse, because I will not fly.","themes":["revenge"],"note":"Warwick's vow."},{"id":"343","who":"EDWARD","text":"Thou setter-up and plucker-down of kings,","themes":["crown","fortune"],"note":"Edward addresses God."},{"id":"344","who":"RICHARD","text":"I, that did never weep, now melt with woe,","themes":["family","self"],"note":"Richard weeps."},{"id":"351","who":"CLIFFORD","text":"This is the hand that stabbed thy father York,","themes":["revenge"],"note":"Clifford's boast."},{"id":"353","who":"RICHARD","text":"For I myself will hunt this wolf to death.","themes":["revenge","self"],"note":"Richard claims Clifford."},{"id":"357","who":"KING HENRY","text":"This battle fares like to the morning's war,","themes":["fortune"],"note":"Henry's opening image."},{"id":"357","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Here on this molehill will I sit me down.","themes":["crown","fortune"],"note":"Henry on the molehill."},{"id":"357","who":"KING HENRY","text":"O God! methinks it were a happy life,","themes":["crown"],"note":"Henry's shepherd fantasy begins."},{"id":"357","who":"KING HENRY","text":"To be no better than a homely swain;","themes":["crown"],"note":"Henry wishes he were a shepherd."},{"id":"357","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Would bring white hairs unto a quiet grave.","themes":["crown","fortune"],"note":"The shepherd's peaceful end."},{"id":"357","who":"KING HENRY","text":"To kings that fear their subjects' treachery?","themes":["crown","oaths"],"note":"Kings fear betrayal."},{"id":"359","who":"SON","text":"Ill blows the wind that profits nobody.","themes":["fortune"],"note":"The son's proverb."},{"id":"359","who":"SON","text":"Who's this?—O God! it is my father's face,","themes":["family","nature"],"note":"The son recognises his father."},{"id":"359","who":"SON","text":"Pardon me, God, I knew not what I did;","themes":["family"],"note":"The son's prayer."},{"id":"360","who":"KING HENRY","text":"O piteous spectacle! O bloody times!","themes":["family","crown"],"note":"Henry's response."},{"id":"360","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Poor harmless lambs abide their enmity.","themes":["nature","crown"],"note":"The common people suffer."},{"id":"362","who":"FATHER","text":"Ah, no, no, no! it is mine only son!","themes":["family","nature"],"note":"The father recognises his son."},{"id":"362","who":"FATHER","text":"O boy, thy father gave thee life too soon,","themes":["family"],"note":"The father's lament."},{"id":"363","who":"KING HENRY","text":"The red rose and the white are on his face,","themes":["crown","family"],"note":"Henry sees the roses in the boy's face."},{"id":"363","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Wither one rose, and let the other flourish!","themes":["crown"],"note":"Henry wishes one side would win."},{"id":"369","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Was ever king so griev'd for subjects' woe?","themes":["crown","family"],"note":"Henry's grief."},{"id":"372","who":"FATHER","text":"For I have murder'd where I should not kill.","themes":["family","nature"],"note":"The father's guilt."},{"id":"383","who":"CLIFFORD","text":"Here burns my candle out; ay, here it dies,","themes":["fortune","crown"],"note":"Clifford dying."},{"id":"383","who":"CLIFFORD","text":"And whither fly the gnats but to the sun?","themes":["fortune"],"note":"Clifford on fickle followers."},{"id":"383","who":"CLIFFORD","text":"And, Henry, hadst thou sway'd as kings should do,","themes":["crown"],"note":"Clifford blames Henry."},{"id":"383","who":"CLIFFORD","text":"And what makes robbers bold but too much lenity?","themes":["crown"],"note":"Clifford's moral."},{"id":"393","who":"WARWICK","text":"Measure for measure must be answered.","themes":["revenge"],"note":"Warwick's biblical justice."},{"id":"403","who":"RICHARD","text":"Thou didst love York, and I am son to York.","themes":["revenge","family"],"note":"Richard mocks the corpse."},{"id":"404","who":"EDWARD","text":"Thou pitiedst Rutland, I will pity thee.","themes":["revenge"],"note":"Edward's sarcasm."},{"id":"408","who":"WARWICK","text":"And ask the Lady Bona for thy queen.","themes":["women","crown"],"note":"Warwick's plan for Edward's marriage."},{"id":"409","who":"EDWARD","text":"For in thy shoulder do I build my seat,","themes":["crown"],"note":"Edward depends on Warwick."},{"id":"410","who":"RICHARD","text":"For Gloster's dukedom is too ominous.","themes":["self","fortune"],"note":"Richard's unease."},{"id":"420","who":"KING HENRY","text":"No, Harry, Harry, 't is no land of thine;","themes":["crown"],"note":"Henry in exile."},{"id":"420","who":"KING HENRY","text":"For how can I help them, and not myself?","themes":["crown"],"note":"Henry's helplessness."},{"id":"422","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Let me embrace thee, sour adversity;","themes":["fortune"],"note":"Henry's acceptance."},{"id":"427","who":"KING HENRY","text":"More than I seem, and less than I was born to;","themes":["crown"],"note":"Henry's riddle."},{"id":"431","who":"KING HENRY","text":"My crown is in my heart, not on my head,","themes":["crown"],"note":"Henry's inner crown."},{"id":"431","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Not to be seen; my crown is call'd content,","themes":["crown"],"note":"The crown called content."},{"id":"438","who":"FIRST KEEPER","text":"For we were subjects but while you were king.","themes":["oaths","crown"],"note":"The keepers' logic."},{"id":"439","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Such is the lightness of you common men.","themes":["oaths","fortune"],"note":"Henry on fickleness."},{"id":"439","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Commanded always by the greater gust,","themes":["fortune","oaths"],"note":"The feather blown by the wind."},{"id":"450","who":"GLOSTER","text":"I see the lady hath a thing to grant","themes":["women"],"note":"Gloucester's bawdy aside."},{"id":"482","who":"GLOSTER","text":"He plies her hard; and much rain wears the marble.","themes":["women"],"note":"Gloucester's comment."},{"id":"485","who":"KING EDWARD","text":"An easy task; 't is but to love a king.","themes":["women","crown"],"note":"Edward's offer."},{"id":"498","who":"KING EDWARD","text":"To tell thee plain, I aim to lie with thee.","themes":["women"],"note":"Edward's bluntness."},{"id":"499","who":"LADY GREY","text":"To tell you plain, I had rather lie in prison.","themes":["women"],"note":"Lady Grey's reply."},{"id":"501","who":"LADY GREY","text":"Why, then mine honesty shall be my dower,","themes":["women"],"note":"Lady Grey's virtue."},{"id":"507","who":"CLARENCE","text":"He is the bluntest wooer in Christendom.","themes":["women"],"note":"Clarence on Edward."},{"id":"508","who":"KING EDWARD","text":"One way or other she is for a king,","themes":["women","crown"],"note":"Edward decides."},{"id":"511","who":"LADY GREY","text":"I know I am too mean to be your queen,","themes":["women","crown"],"note":"Lady Grey's answer."},{"id":"516","who":"CLARENCE","text":"When he was made a shriver, 't was for shift.","themes":["women"],"note":"Clarence's pun."},{"id":"531","who":"GLOSTER","text":"Would he were wasted, marrow, bones, and all,","themes":["self","family"],"note":"Richard wishes Edward childless."},{"id":"531","who":"GLOSTER","text":"Why, then I do but dream on sovereignty,","themes":["self","crown"],"note":"Richard's dream."},{"id":"531","who":"GLOSTER","text":"Why, love forswore me in my mother's womb;","themes":["self","nature"],"note":"Richard's view of his body."},{"id":"531","who":"GLOSTER","text":"To shrink mine arm up like a wither'd shrub;","themes":["self","nature"],"note":"Richard's withered arm."},{"id":"531","who":"GLOSTER","text":"Like to a chaos, or an unlick'd bear-whelp","themes":["self","nature"],"note":"Richard as unformed."},{"id":"531","who":"GLOSTER","text":"And am I then a man to be belov'd?","themes":["self"],"note":"Richard's question."},{"id":"531","who":"GLOSTER","text":"I'll make my heaven to dream upon the crown,","themes":["self","crown"],"note":"The crown as heaven."},{"id":"531","who":"GLOSTER","text":"And I, like one lost in a thorny wood,","themes":["self"],"note":"Richard lost in thorns."},{"id":"531","who":"GLOSTER","text":"Or hew my way out with a bloody axe.","themes":["self","revenge"],"note":"Richard's resolve."},{"id":"531","who":"GLOSTER","text":"Why, I can smile, and murther while I smile,","themes":["self"],"note":"Richard the actor."},{"id":"531","who":"GLOSTER","text":"And frame my face to all occasions.","themes":["self"],"note":"Richard's masks."},{"id":"531","who":"GLOSTER","text":"And set the murtherous Machiavel to school.","themes":["self"],"note":"Richard and Machiavelli."},{"id":"531","who":"GLOSTER","text":"Can I do this, and cannot get a crown?","themes":["self","crown"],"note":"Richard's confidence."},{"id":"536","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","text":"Must strike her sail and learn a while to serve","themes":["women","fortune"],"note":"Margaret humbled."},{"id":"546","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","text":"O, but impatience waiteth on true sorrow!","themes":["women"],"note":"Margaret on her impatience."},{"id":"552","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","text":"For this is he that moves both wind and tide.","themes":["fortune","crown"],"note":"Margaret on Warwick."},{"id":"556","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","text":"Yet heavens are just, and time suppresseth wrongs.","themes":["crown","oaths"],"note":"Margaret's appeal to justice."},{"id":"559","who":"WARWICK","text":"And thou no more art prince than she is queen.","themes":["crown"],"note":"Warwick to Prince Edward."},{"id":"561","who":"WARWICK","text":"Of threescore and two years,—a silly time","themes":["crown"],"note":"Warwick dismisses the Lancastrian line."},{"id":"580","who":"KING LEWIS","text":"But if your title to the crown be weak,","themes":["crown","fortune"],"note":"Lewis abandons Margaret."},{"id":"582","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","text":"Proud setter-up and puller-down of kings!","themes":["crown","fortune"],"note":"Margaret on Warwick."},{"id":"588","who":"OXFORD","text":"Smiles at her news while Warwick frowns at his.","themes":["fortune"],"note":"Oxford sees the turn."},{"id":"595","who":"WARWICK","text":"No more my king, for he dishonours me,","themes":["oaths","crown"],"note":"Warwick renounces Edward."},{"id":"595","who":"WARWICK","text":"I here renounce him and return to Henry.","themes":["oaths","fortune"],"note":"Warwick changes sides."},{"id":"596","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","text":"Warwick, these words have turn'd my hate to love;","themes":["women","fortune"],"note":"Margaret forgives Warwick."},{"id":"605","who":"BONA","text":"I'll wear the willow garland for his sake.","themes":["women"],"note":"Bona's revenge."},{"id":"606","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","text":"And I am ready to put armour on.","themes":["women"],"note":"Margaret's message."},{"id":"607","who":"WARWICK","text":"And therefore I'll uncrown him ere 't be long.","themes":["crown","revenge"],"note":"Warwick's threat."},{"id":"616","who":"WARWICK","text":"Had he none else to make a stale but me?","themes":["revenge","self"],"note":"Warwick's wounded pride."},{"id":"616","who":"WARWICK","text":"Not that I pity Henry's misery,","themes":["revenge","crown"],"note":"Warwick's honesty."},{"id":"628","who":"KING EDWARD","text":"They are but Lewis and Warwick: I am Edward,","themes":["crown"],"note":"Edward's arrogance."},{"id":"629","who":"GLOSTER","text":"Yet hasty marriage seldom proveth well.","themes":["women","family"],"note":"Gloucester's warning."},{"id":"631","who":"GLOSTER","text":"To sunder them that yoke so well together.","themes":["women"],"note":"Gloucester's sarcasm."},{"id":"639","who":"HASTINGS","text":"'T is better using France than trusting France.","themes":["crown"],"note":"Hastings' policy."},{"id":"642","who":"GLOSTER","text":"But in your bride you bury brotherhood.","themes":["family","women"],"note":"Gloucester's complaint."},{"id":"646","who":"KING EDWARD","text":"Leave me or tarry, Edward will be king,","themes":["crown","family"],"note":"Edward to Clarence."},{"id":"649","who":"GLOSTER","text":"I hear, yet say not much, but think the more.","themes":["self"],"note":"Gloucester's aside."},{"id":"659","who":"KING EDWARD","text":"Belike she minds to play the Amazon.","themes":["women"],"note":"Edward on Margaret."},{"id":"665","who":"GLOSTER","text":"Stay not for the love of Edward, but the crown.","themes":["self","crown"],"note":"Gloucester's real reason."},{"id":"681","who":"WARWICK","text":"Applaud the name of Henry with your leader.","themes":["crown","fortune"],"note":"Warwick rallies for Henry."},{"id":"695","who":"THIRD WATCHMAN","text":"Ay, but give me worship and quietness;","themes":["crown"],"note":"The watchman's preference."},{"id":"707","who":"WARWICK","text":"Ay, but the case is alter'd;","themes":["fortune","crown"],"note":"Warwick to the captured Edward."},{"id":"707","who":"WARWICK","text":"Nor how to be contented with one wife,","themes":["women","crown"],"note":"Warwick's charge against Edward."},{"id":"708","who":"KING EDWARD","text":"My mind exceeds the compass of her wheel.","themes":["fortune","crown"],"note":"Edward defies Fortune."},{"id":"709","who":"WARWICK","text":"Then for his mind be Edward England's king;","themes":["crown"],"note":"Warwick's mockery."},{"id":"711","who":"WARWICK","text":"And be true king indeed, thou but the shadow.","themes":["crown"],"note":"Warwick uncrowns Edward."},{"id":"712","who":"KING EDWARD","text":"What fates impose, that men must needs abide;","themes":["fortune"],"note":"Edward accepts his fate."},{"id":"726","who":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","text":"King Edward's fruit, true heir to the English crown.","themes":["family","crown"],"note":"Elizabeth on her unborn child."},{"id":"728","who":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","text":"For trust not him that hath once broken faith,","themes":["oaths"],"note":"Elizabeth on Warwick."},{"id":"745","who":"KING EDWARD","text":"And pray that I may repossess the crown.","themes":["crown","fortune"],"note":"Edward escapes."},{"id":"751","who":"KING HENRY","text":"He was the author, thou the instrument.","themes":["crown"],"note":"Henry thanks God and Warwick."},{"id":"751","who":"KING HENRY","text":"By living low where fortune cannot hurt me,","themes":["fortune","crown"],"note":"Henry's escape from fortune."},{"id":"751","who":"KING HENRY","text":"I here resign my government to thee,","themes":["crown"],"note":"Henry hands power to Warwick."},{"id":"758","who":"WARWICK","text":"We'll yoke together, like a double shadow","themes":["crown"],"note":"Warwick and Clarence as Protectors."},{"id":"765","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Come hither, England's hope.","themes":["crown","family"],"note":"Henry blesses Richmond."},{"id":"767","who":"KING HENRY","text":"His head by nature fram'd to wear a crown,","themes":["crown","nature"],"note":"Henry on Richmond."},{"id":"767","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Must help you more than you are hurt by me.","themes":["crown"],"note":"Henry's humility."},{"id":"782","who":"GLOSTER","text":"For many men that stumble at the threshold","themes":["fortune"],"note":"Gloucester's omen."},{"id":"790","who":"GLOSTER","text":"But when the fox hath once got in his nose,","themes":["self","crown"],"note":"Gloucester's aside."},{"id":"804","who":"KING EDWARD","text":"Our title to the crown, and only claim","themes":["crown","oaths"],"note":"Edward's false modesty."},{"id":"805","who":"MONTGOMERY","text":"I came to serve a king, and not a duke.","themes":["crown"],"note":"Montgomery's demand."},{"id":"811","who":"HASTINGS","text":"Away with scrupulous wit! now arms must rule.","themes":["crown","fortune"],"note":"Hastings' advice."},{"id":"812","who":"GLOSTER","text":"And fearless minds climb soonest unto crowns.","themes":["self","crown"],"note":"Gloucester's maxim."},{"id":"813","who":"KING EDWARD","text":"And Henry but usurps the diadem.","themes":["crown"],"note":"Edward's proclamation."},{"id":"827","who":"CLARENCE","text":"A little fire is quickly trodden out,","themes":["fortune"],"note":"Clarence's warning."},{"id":"829","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Farewell, my Hector, and my Troy's true hope.","themes":["crown"],"note":"Henry to Warwick."},{"id":"839","who":"KING HENRY","text":"My pity hath been balm to heal their wounds,","themes":["crown"],"note":"Henry's self-defence."},{"id":"839","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Then, why should they love Edward more than me?","themes":["crown","fortune"],"note":"Henry's question."},{"id":"839","who":"KING HENRY","text":"The lamb will never cease to follow him.","themes":["nature","crown"],"note":"Henry's lion and lamb."},{"id":"843","who":"KING EDWARD","text":"Seize on the shame-fac'd Henry! bear him hence,","themes":["crown"],"note":"Edward seizes Henry."},{"id":"845","who":"KING EDWARD","text":"Cold biting winter mars our hop'd-for hay.","themes":["fortune"],"note":"Edward urges speed."},{"id":"865","who":"WARWICK","text":"O, unbid spite! Is sportful Edward come?","themes":["fortune"],"note":"Warwick surprised."},{"id":"871","who":"WARWICK","text":"'T was I that gave the kingdom to thy brother.","themes":["crown"],"note":"Warwick's claim."},{"id":"873","who":"WARWICK","text":"And Henry is my king, Warwick his subject.","themes":["crown","oaths"],"note":"Warwick's new loyalty."},{"id":"874","who":"KING EDWARD","text":"What is the body when the head is off?","themes":["crown"],"note":"Edward's riddle."},{"id":"879","who":"KING EDWARD","text":"'Wind-changing Warwick now can change no more.'","themes":["fortune","revenge"],"note":"Edward's threat."},{"id":"891","who":"KING EDWARD","text":"The harder match'd, the greater victory;","themes":["fortune"],"note":"Edward's confidence."},{"id":"902","who":"CLARENCE","text":"Look here, I throw my infamy at thee;","themes":["oaths","family"],"note":"Clarence returns to Edward."},{"id":"902","who":"CLARENCE","text":"I will not ruinate my father's house,","themes":["family"],"note":"Clarence's reason."},{"id":"902","who":"CLARENCE","text":"Than Jephtha's when he sacrific'd his daughter.","themes":["oaths"],"note":"Clarence justifies breaking his oath."},{"id":"905","who":"WARWICK","text":"O passing traitor, perjur'd and unjust!","themes":["oaths"],"note":"Warwick on Clarence."},{"id":"912","who":"KING EDWARD","text":"For Warwick was a bug that fear'd us all.","themes":["fortune"],"note":"Edward over Warwick."},{"id":"914","who":"WARWICK","text":"Thus yields the cedar to the axe's edge,","themes":["fortune","crown"],"note":"Warwick as a fallen cedar."},{"id":"914","who":"WARWICK","text":"For who liv'd king but I could dig his grave?","themes":["crown"],"note":"Warwick's pride."},{"id":"914","who":"WARWICK","text":"Is nothing left me but my body's length.","themes":["fortune"],"note":"Warwick's lands reduced to a grave."},{"id":"914","who":"WARWICK","text":"Why, what is pomp, rule, reign, but earth and dust?","themes":["fortune","crown"],"note":"Warwick's moral."},{"id":"914","who":"WARWICK","text":"And live we how we can, yet die we must.","themes":["fortune"],"note":"Warwick's last couplet."},{"id":"919","who":"WARWICK","text":"For Warwick bids you all farewell, to meet in heaven.","themes":["fortune"],"note":"Warwick dies."},{"id":"925","who":"KING EDWARD","text":"I spy a black, suspicious, threatening cloud,","themes":["fortune"],"note":"Edward on Margaret's army."},{"id":"932","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","text":"Great lords, wise men ne'er sit and wail their loss,","themes":["women","fortune"],"note":"Margaret's speech begins."},{"id":"932","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","text":"Yet lives our pilot still. Is 't meet that he","themes":["crown","fortune"],"note":"The ship of state."},{"id":"932","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","text":"And what is Edward but a ruthless sea?","themes":["fortune"],"note":"Margaret's images of the brothers."},{"id":"932","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","text":"And Richard but a ragged fatal rock?","themes":["self","nature"],"note":"Richard as a rock."},{"id":"932","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","text":"'T were childish weakness to lament or fear.","themes":["women"],"note":"Margaret's courage."},{"id":"933","who":"PRINCE","text":"Methinks, a woman of this valiant spirit","themes":["women","family"],"note":"The Prince praises his mother."},{"id":"934","who":"OXFORD","text":"Doth live again in thee; long mayst thou live","themes":["family","crown"],"note":"Oxford on the Prince."},{"id":"945","who":"KING EDWARD","text":"Brave followers, yonder stands the thorny wood","themes":["fortune"],"note":"Edward's battle speech."},{"id":"946","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","text":"And yonder is the wolf that makes this spoil.","themes":["nature","crown"],"note":"Margaret on Edward."},{"id":"954","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","text":"To meet with joy in sweet Jerusalem.","themes":["fortune"],"note":"Margaret's farewell."},{"id":"959","who":"PRINCE","text":"Speak like a subject, proud, ambitious York!","themes":["crown","family"],"note":"Prince Edward's defiance."},{"id":"961","who":"GLOSTER","text":"And ne'er have stol'n the breech from Lancaster.","themes":["women"],"note":"Gloucester's jibe at Margaret."},{"id":"964","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","text":"Ay, thou wast born to be a plague to men.","themes":["self","women"],"note":"Margaret on Richard."},{"id":"969","who":"PRINCE","text":"Lascivious Edward,—and thou perjur'd George,—","themes":["oaths","family"],"note":"The Prince's insults."},{"id":"969","who":"PRINCE","text":"And thou misshapen Dick,—I tell ye all,","themes":["self","nature"],"note":"The Prince on Richard."},{"id":"979","who":"KING EDWARD","text":"Hold, Richard, hold! for we have done too much.","themes":["revenge"],"note":"Edward stops Richard."},{"id":"980","who":"GLOSTER","text":"Why should she live to fill the world with words?","themes":["women","self"],"note":"Richard on Margaret."},{"id":"984","who":"GLOSTER","text":"The Tower! the Tower!","themes":["self","revenge"],"note":"Richard's exit."},{"id":"986","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","text":"O Ned! sweet Ned! speak to thy mother, boy.","themes":["family","women"],"note":"Margaret's grief."},{"id":"986","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","text":"They that stabb'd Caesar shed no blood at all,","themes":["revenge"],"note":"Margaret on the murder."},{"id":"986","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","text":"You have no children, butchers! if you had,","themes":["family","revenge"],"note":"Margaret's curse."},{"id":"992","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","text":"'T was sin before, but now 't is charity.","themes":["oaths"],"note":"Margaret to Clarence."},{"id":"992","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","text":"Hard-favour'd Richard?—Richard, where art thou?","themes":["self"],"note":"Margaret calls for Richard."},{"id":"994","who":"QUEEN MARGARET","text":"So come to you and yours as to this prince!","themes":["revenge","family"],"note":"Margaret's curse."},{"id":"998","who":"KING EDWARD","text":"He's sudden if a thing comes in his head.","themes":["self"],"note":"Edward on Richard."},{"id":"1003","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Good Gloster and good devil were alike,","themes":["self"],"note":"Henry's wordplay."},{"id":"1006","who":"KING HENRY","text":"What scene of death hath Roscius now to act?","themes":["self"],"note":"Henry calls Richard an actor."},{"id":"1007","who":"GLOSTER","text":"Suspicion always haunts the guilty mind;","themes":["self"],"note":"Richard's proverb."},{"id":"1010","who":"KING HENRY","text":"I, Daedalus; my poor boy, Icarus;","themes":["family"],"note":"Henry's myth."},{"id":"1010","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Ah, kill me with thy weapon, not with words!","themes":["self","revenge"],"note":"Henry begs a quick death."},{"id":"1014","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Shall rue the hour that ever thou wast born.","themes":["self","nature"],"note":"Henry's prophecy."},{"id":"1014","who":"KING HENRY","text":"The owl shriek'd at thy birth, an evil sign;","themes":["nature","self"],"note":"Omens at Richard's birth."},{"id":"1014","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Teeth hadst thou in thy head when thou wast born,","themes":["nature","self"],"note":"Richard born with teeth."},{"id":"1014","who":"KING HENRY","text":"To signify thou cam'st to bite the world;","themes":["nature","self"],"note":"The meaning of the teeth."},{"id":"1015","who":"GLOSTER","text":"I'll hear no more. Die, prophet, in thy speech.","themes":["self"],"note":"Richard kills Henry."},{"id":"1018","who":"KING HENRY","text":"O, God forgive my sins, and pardon thee!","themes":["crown","revenge"],"note":"Henry's dying words."},{"id":"1020","who":"GLOSTER","text":"See, how my sword weeps for the poor King's death!","themes":["self"],"note":"Richard's mockery."},{"id":"1022","who":"GLOSTER","text":"I, that have neither pity, love, nor fear.","themes":["self"],"note":"Richard's self-definition."},{"id":"1022","who":"GLOSTER","text":"I came into the world with my legs forward.","themes":["self","nature"],"note":"Richard's breech birth."},{"id":"1022","who":"GLOSTER","text":"Let hell make crook'd my mind to answer it.","themes":["self","nature"],"note":"Body and mind matched."},{"id":"1022","who":"GLOSTER","text":"I have no brother, I am like no brother,","themes":["self","family"],"note":"Richard rejects family."},{"id":"1022","who":"GLOSTER","text":"And not in me! I am myself alone.","themes":["self","family"],"note":"'Myself alone'."},{"id":"1022","who":"GLOSTER","text":"Clarence, beware! thou keep'st me from the light;","themes":["self","family"],"note":"Richard targets Clarence."},{"id":"1022","who":"GLOSTER","text":"Counting myself but bad till I be best.","themes":["self","crown"],"note":"Richard's ambition."},{"id":"1026","who":"KING EDWARD","text":"Once more we sit in England's royal throne,","themes":["crown","fortune"],"note":"Edward restored."},{"id":"1026","who":"KING EDWARD","text":"Re-purchas'd with the blood of enemies.","themes":["crown","revenge"],"note":"The cost of the crown."},{"id":"1026","who":"KING EDWARD","text":"That thou mightst repossess the crown in peace;","themes":["crown","family"],"note":"Edward to his son."},{"id":"1027","who":"GLOSTER","text":"I'll blast his harvest if your head were laid;","themes":["self","family"],"note":"Richard's aside."},{"id":"1027","who":"GLOSTER","text":"And heave it shall some weight or break my back.","themes":["self"],"note":"Richard's back."},{"id":"1031","who":"GLOSTER","text":"To say the truth, so Judas kiss'd his Master,","themes":["self","family","oaths"],"note":"Richard's Judas kiss."},{"id":"1032","who":"KING EDWARD","text":"Having my country's peace and brothers' loves.","themes":["family","crown"],"note":"Edward's complacency."},{"id":"1034","who":"KING EDWARD","text":"For here, I hope, begins our lasting joy.","themes":["crown","fortune"],"note":"The last line."}]};
const EX={"why":["Opens with a missing king, the play's central absence.","Shows Warwick's contempt for Henry from the first scene.","Links political process and violence.","Introduces Warwick's power and pride.","Warwick literally plants a king: the image of the setter-up of kings.","Establishes Clifford's revenge motive at once.","Patience, Henry's virtue, is cowardice to his allies.","Shows Henry's peaceable nature and its political cost.","Inheritance is the language of both claims.","Explains Henry's weakness: he never grew into rule.","Richard's wit is cruel from his first lines.","Henry can rise to royal rhetoric, but it does not last.","The crucial aside: the king himself doubts his right.","Shows the shaky legal ground of the Lancastrian claim.","Even Henry's kinsman believes York's claim.","Fear of desertion drives his surrender.","Loyalty as personal, not legal.","Shows force deciding the 'legal' debate.","The moment Henry trades his son's right for his own peace.","Henry's own supporters abandon him.","Henry measured against his father and found wanting.","Introduces the theme of unnatural fatherhood.","The legal settlement that sets the whole war going.","Ironic: the peace lasts a single scene.","She rejects patience, Henry's virtue.","Shows the depth of her anger at Henry.","Questions what kingship means if it can be coerced.","Lamb and wolf imagery runs through the play.","She divorces herself in all but law.","Henry's sympathy contrasts with Margaret's rage.","Predatory bird imagery for York's ambition.","Edward will break faith easily, as the play shows.","Shows Richard's clever, amoral reasoning.","The first sign of the ambition of the future Richard III.","Richard's crown-worship sets up his later soliloquy.","Shows how easily the oath is set aside.","Underestimating Margaret is York's undoing.","The revenge chain in one line.","Rutland's innocence makes the murder an atrocity.","Revenge has made Clifford deaf to pleas.","Revenge widens from one man to his whole line.","Rutland foresees the revenge chain coming back on Clifford.","The bluntest statement of revenge logic in the play.","Clifford's bloodlust drives the scene onward.","Margaret's army, not Henry's, wins.","Tide imagery of fortune begins here.","An hourglass image of fate.","Classical image of the overreacher's fall.","York's defiance looks forward to Edward and Richard.","War has no chivalry left in this play.","The molehill parodies York's claim to the throne.","Mountain and molehill: ambition mocked by scale.","The most famous act of cruelty in the play.","Leads to the paper crown.","Crown and head are one: losing one means losing the other.","The defining insult of Margaret.","Accuses Margaret of being unwomanly and unnatural.","Shows the gender expectations Margaret breaks.","The harsh list contrasts with the soft one before it.","The theatre's audience becomes the 'hearers' who weep.","Pity survives even between enemies.","A dignified death after the mockery.","Her cruelty continues even after his death.","Richard begins as a loving son.","An omen of the three brothers.","The brothers' unity, which will not last.","Edward's weakness for women, which will cost him.","Edward mourns as Richard cannot.","Richard refuses tears, choosing action.","Revenge is inherited like a name.","Eagles were thought to stare at the sun; true sons claim the throne.","Henry's gentleness is a weapon against his own side.","Warwick the kingmaker in action.","Edward inherits his father's title and claim.","Revenge drives Richard into the next battle.","Henry feels guilt where others feel triumph.","Nature imagery is used to argue for violence.","A famous proverb used to urge Henry to act.","Henry's failure seen through future generations.","Henry's moral sense questions the whole Lancastrian title.","A pious but politically empty inheritance.","Henry's moral lesson to his son.","Henry is a liability on his own side.","A rare moment of resolve from Henry.","Clifford's murder of Rutland defines him.","No one listens to the king.","Questions the Prince's paternity.","Deformity used as an insult and a sign.","Margaret as base metal with a gold coating.","Puts the blame for the war on a woman's pride.","Words lead directly to slaughter.","The sun badge is clouded: fortune turns.","Revenge passes from the dead to the living.","Burns his bridges, literally, to stay and fight.","The phrase is later applied to Warwick.","Richard's only tears, for his family.","Clifford relishes his revenge.","Revenge becomes a personal hunt.","The battle as dawn: neither night nor day.","Echoes York's molehill and his mock crown.","Henry's longing for a simple life.","A king who does not want to be king.","Contrasts with the violent deaths of the play.","Kingship as anxiety, not glory.","A soldier's practical view of war.","Civil war as patricide.","Echoes Christ's 'they know not what they do'.","Henry can only watch and weep.","Lions fight; lambs die.","Civil war as filicide.","Paradox of grief: born too soon, killed too late.","The Wars of the Roses written on a dead body.","Henry would even give up the crown for peace.","Henry's sorrow is genuine but useless.","Sums up the guilt of civil war.","Clifford as the light that kept Henry going.","People follow whoever is shining.","A Lancastrian's verdict on Henry's rule.","Mercy breeds rebellion, says Clifford.","Revenge as exact repayment.","Revenge continues past death.","The Yorkists copy Margaret's cruelty.","The marriage that will split York.","Edward's throne rests on Warwick's support.","Foreshadows Richard's dark future.","Henry speaks to himself as an ordinary man.","A king who cannot help even himself.","Stoic acceptance of misfortune.","Identity without the crown.","His idea of true kingship.","Henry's best-known line.","Allegiance follows power.","The feather image of loyalty.","Loyalty depends on whoever is strongest.","The brothers see Edward's motive at once.","Edward's wooing as persistent pressure.","Royal power used for seduction.","Edward's desire stripped of disguise.","Her wit matches and defeats his.","Chastity as her only wealth.","Comic, but marks Edward's recklessness.","Desire decides policy.","She keeps her honour and gains a crown.","Bawdy commentary on the wooing.","Richard's first open wish against his brother.","Ambition as a dream of a distant shore.","Self-pity becomes self-justification.","His deformity described in detail.","A shapeless body, a shapeless morality.","His isolation leads to ambition.","Echoes his earlier 'Elysium' speech.","His frustration and violence.","Violence as the way to the crown.","The defining line of the future Richard III.","Theatre and politics joined.","He will out-plot the arch-plotter.","Ends the soliloquy with a challenge.","The proud queen as a ship lowering its sail.","Her impatience is the engine of her action.","Warwick as a force of nature.","A belief the play both confirms and denies.","Titles are only as real as power.","Sixty-two years are not enough to make a title.","Success proves right, in Lewis's eyes.","Warwick's famous title.","Fortune reverses in a single letter.","Honour, not principle, changes his allegiance.","The kingmaker unmakes his king.","Enemies become allies in a moment.","A jilted woman's mocking message.","The warrior queen returns.","A personal insult becomes a war aim.","His motive: humiliation, not justice.","He admits he does not act for Henry.","Edward behaves as an absolute king.","A proverb that proves true.","Mock blessing of the marriage.","English self-reliance against foreign alliance.","The marriage divides the brothers.","Edward's pride drives his brother away.","Richard watches and waits.","Margaret as warrior woman.","Loyalty as a mask.","Henry's name becomes a rallying cry, not a person.","Comic contrast to aristocratic honour.","Fortune's turn in four words.","Private faults as political failures.","The wheel of fortune image.","A king in mind only, like Henry.","Substance and shadow of kingship.","Briefly echoes Henry's patience.","The next heir, whom Richard will murder.","Distrust of turncoats.","The crown changes hands again.","Henry sees God behind events.","He gives up rule to avoid fortune's blows.","A king in name only.","Power without the crown.","Tudor prophecy.","Natural kingship, the opposite of Richard's 'unnatural' body.","Henry admits he has harmed England.","Superstition and foreboding.","Richard's fox-like cunning.","Echoes Bolingbroke's trick in Richard II.","Soldiers fight for kings, not claims.","Force over law.","His own creed in one line.","Both sides call the other usurper.","Fire imagery for rebellion.","Henry casts Warwick as his champion.","His idea of good rule.","Answered immediately by Edward's arrival.","His trust in gentleness, proved wrong.","The crown changes hands again.","Proverbial: make hay while the sun shines.","Warwick outmanoeuvred.","The kingmaker's pride.","Irony: Henry is already captured.","Without Henry, Warwick's cause is headless.","Sums up Warwick as a weathercock.","Edward grows as a leader.","The red rose thrown away.","Family loyalty, suddenly remembered.","An oath can be wickeder to keep than to break.","'Perjured' Clarence: the label Margaret will repeat.","The bogeyman is gone.","The great tree image of the play.","The kingmaker's power summed up.","Mortality levels all.","The vanity of power.","A proverbial ending.","The kingmaker's end.","Weather imagery for fortune.","Margaret as leader.","Henry is the pilot, absent; Margaret steers.","Each brother a danger at sea.","Margaret's image of Richard.","The opposite of Henry's tearful passivity.","Margaret's courage inspires her son.","Henry V reborn in his grandson.","Thorny wood: echoes Richard's soliloquy.","Wolf imagery reversed.","Heaven as the only peace.","The boy speaks like a king.","Margaret 'wears the trousers'.","Prophetic insult.","Accurate charges that cost him his life.","The insult that provokes the killing.","Even Edward knows they have gone too far.","Margaret's words as a weapon.","Prepares Henry's murder.","Echoes York's grief for Rutland.","Compares the murder to Caesar's.","Her curse anticipates the princes in the Tower.","She turns his perjury into a weapon.","She knows who the real killer is.","Comes true in Richard III.","Dramatic irony: Edward does not see the danger.","Henry sees through Richard.","Richard the performer.","Turns guilt back on Henry.","A father's grief for his son.","Words wound more than steel.","The curse that hangs over Richard III.","Nature itself protested.","A monster from birth.","Richard as predator.","Silences the prophecy.","Christian forgiveness against revenge.","Blood as tears: black humour.","The three human feelings he denies.","Born in a hurry, as he will live.","His deformity as excuse and destiny.","Kinship denied.","The most famous line of the soliloquy.","Leads directly into Richard III.","Only the crown will satisfy him.","The final turn of the crown.","Bought with blood.","An inheritance that Richard will steal.","Threat to the infant prince.","His deformity as strength.","The betrayer's kiss on the baby.","Heavy dramatic irony.","Ironic: the audience knows Richard III is coming."],"intro":["The Third Part of King Henry the Sixth carries the Wars of the Roses from the Parliament of 1460 to Edward IV's victory in 1471. It opens with the Duke of York sitting on Henry's throne and Henry agreeing to make York his heir, cutting out his own son. Queen Margaret refuses to accept it, raises an army and kills York at Wakefield, after taunting him with a paper crown and a napkin dipped in his young son Rutland's blood.","York's sons, Edward and Richard, with the Earl of Warwick, the 'kingmaker', win at Towton and make Edward king. Henry, who wishes he were a shepherd, is captured. But Edward marries the widow Lady Grey while Warwick is in France arranging a royal match, and Warwick changes sides, joins Margaret and restores Henry. The crown changes hands again and again until Edward wins at Barnet, where Warwick dies, and at Tewkesbury, where the three brothers stab Henry's son before his mother's eyes.","Through all of this the play watches Richard, Duke of Gloucester, grow from a loyal son into a self-declared Machiavel. He murders Henry in the Tower, says 'I am myself alone', and kisses his brother's baby son with a Judas kiss as Edward celebrates his 'lasting joy'. The play leads straight into Richard III."],"keyfacts":[["Full title","The Third Part of Henry the Sixth, with the death of the Duke of York (Folio title); first printed in 1595 as The True Tragedy of Richard Duke of York and the Good King Henry the Sixth"],["Written","About 1591; the 1595 octavo is a shorter version. The Folio text (1623) is about a thousand lines longer"],["Genre","History: one of the Histories in the 1623 First Folio, the third play of the first tetralogy (1, 2 and 3 Henry VI, Richard III)"],["Setting","England (London, Sandal Castle, Wakefield, Towton, York, Coventry, Barnet, Tewkesbury, the Tower) and the French court, from 1460 to 1471"],["Protagonist","No single hero: King Henry, Margaret, Warwick, Edward and Richard each dominate a part of the play"],["Antagonists","York against Lancaster; later Warwick against Edward; and Richard against everyone"],["Inciting incident","Henry entails the crown on York, disinheriting his son, and Margaret takes up arms"],["Turning point","Edward's marriage to Lady Grey (3.2), which turns Warwick, France and Clarence against him (3.3, 4.1)"],["Climax","Tewkesbury and the murder of Prince Edward (5.5), followed by Richard's murder of Henry (5.6)"],["Resolution","Edward enthroned with his queen and infant son; Margaret ransomed; Richard plotting with a Judas kiss"],["Time span","About eleven years of history (1460 to 1471), compressed into a rapid sequence of battles"],["Major conflicts","York against Lancaster; Clifford against York's family; Warwick against Edward; Richard against his brothers"],["Themes","The right to the crown, oaths and broken faith, revenge, fathers and sons, women and power, fortune and fickleness, nature and the unnatural, ambition"]],"timeline":[{"s":"a1s1","t":"York seated on Henry's throne in Parliament","id":24},{"s":"a1s1","t":"Henry admits 'my title's weak'","id":70},{"s":"a1s1","t":"Henry entails the crown on York, disinheriting his son","id":107},{"s":"a1s1","t":"Margaret divorces herself from Henry and raises the north","id":130},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Richard persuades York to break his oath","id":160},{"s":"a1s3","t":"Clifford murders Rutland","id":196},{"s":"a1s4","t":"Margaret crowns York with paper and gives him Rutland's blood","id":221},{"s":"a1s4","t":"York stabbed by Clifford and Margaret","id":231},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Three suns appear to Edward and Richard","id":243},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Warwick proclaims Edward Duke of York and future king","id":270},{"s":"a2s2","t":"The parley before York: Henry silenced","id":316},{"s":"a2s3","t":"Warwick's vow at Towton","id":342},{"s":"a2s5","t":"Henry on the molehill; the son and the father","id":363},{"s":"a2s6","t":"Clifford dies; Edward makes his brothers Gloucester and Clarence","id":409},{"s":"a3s1","t":"Henry captured by keepers: 'my crown is call'd content'","id":431},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Edward woos and marries Lady Grey","id":514},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Gloucester's first soliloquy","id":531},{"s":"a3s3","t":"Warwick renounces Edward in France","id":595},{"s":"a4s1","t":"Clarence leaves Edward for Warwick","id":663},{"s":"a4s3","t":"Warwick captures Edward and takes his crown","id":711},{"s":"a4s5","t":"Gloucester rescues Edward","id":745},{"s":"a4s6","t":"Henry restored; Warwick and Clarence Protectors; Richmond blessed","id":765},{"s":"a4s7","t":"Edward proclaimed king again at York","id":817},{"s":"a4s8","t":"Edward seizes Henry and sends him to the Tower","id":843},{"s":"a5s1","t":"Clarence returns to Edward at Coventry","id":902},{"s":"a5s2","t":"Warwick dies at Barnet","id":919},{"s":"a5s4","t":"Margaret's ship speech at Tewkesbury","id":932},{"s":"a5s5","t":"The three brothers stab Prince Edward","id":970},{"s":"a5s6","t":"Gloucester murders Henry: 'I am myself alone'","id":1022},{"s":"a5s7","t":"Edward's 'lasting joy'; Gloucester's Judas kiss","id":1031}],"irony":{"112":"York declares that York and Lancaster are reconciled; within one scene he has broken his oath.","155":"Edward says he would break a thousand oaths to reign a year; Warwick will later uncrown him for broken faith.","172":"Richard scorns a 'woman-general'; the woman-general wins and kills his father.","195":"Rutland warns Clifford that his own son may die the same way; the revenge chain later destroys Clifford too.","209":"York prophesies a phoenix to avenge him; Edward and Richard become kings, but Richard will destroy his own family.","244":"The three suns join 'as if they vow'd some league inviolable'; the three brothers will betray each other.","283":"Henry says ill-gotten goods have bad success; this applies to every crown in the play, including Edward's.","343":"Edward calls God the 'setter-up and plucker-down of kings'; Margaret gives Warwick the same title.","409":"Edward says he will never act without Warwick's counsel; he marries without it.","431":"Henry says his crown is 'content', which kings seldom enjoy; he is anything but content in this play.","553":"Warwick praises Edward's love for Bona just as Edward has married someone else.","574":"Warwick swears to Edward's 'eternal' love for Bona; the letters arrive minutes later.","648":"Edward promises his queen safety while he lives; within two scenes he is a prisoner and she in sanctuary.","751":"Henry gives up the government to escape fortune; fortune takes him back to the Tower in two scenes.","765":"Henry blesses Richmond as 'England's hope'; Richmond will defeat Richard, Henry's murderer, at Bosworth.","839":"Henry asks why the people should love Edward more than him; Edward bursts in at that moment.","873":"Warwick declares 'Henry is my king'; Henry is already Edward's prisoner.","998":"Edward calls Richard merely 'sudden'; he has gone to murder Henry.","1028":"Edward asks his brothers to love his queen and kiss his son; Richard will kill the son.","1032":"Edward boasts of his brothers' love; Richard has just named Clarence as his next victim.","1034":"'Here, I hope, begins our lasting joy': the audience knows that Richard III follows."},"devices":{"beast":"Beasts and hunting","sea":"Sea, tide and wind","sun":"Sun, light and fire","tree":"Trees, plants and harvest","blood":"Blood and the body","theatre":"Theatre, masks and acting"},"dev":{"24":[["beast","Warwick as a hawk whose bells frighten other birds.","Dares stir a wing if Warwick shake his bells"],["tree","Warwick plants a king like a tree.","I'll plant Plantagenet, root him up who dares"]],"130":[["beast","Henry as a lamb surrounded by wolves.","The trembling lamb environed with wolves"]],"138":[["beast","York as a starving eagle tearing at Henry's flesh.","like an empty eagle"]],"187":[["beast","Clifford as a lion over its prey.","So looks the pent-up lion o'er the wretch"]],"202":[["sea","York's army as a swan swimming against the tide.","With bootless labour swim against the tide"]],"208":[["sun","York as Phaethon, fallen from the sun's chariot.","Now Phaethon hath tumbled from his car"]],"209":[["sun","York's revenge reborn like the phoenix from fire.","My ashes, as the phoenix, may bring forth"]],"216":[["beast","York as a woodcock in a snare.","so strives the woodcock with the gin"]],"221":[["blood","The napkin stained with Rutland's blood.","I stain'd this napkin with the blood"],["theatre","Margaret stages a mock coronation.","A crown for York!"]],"226":[["beast","Margaret as she-wolf and tiger.","O tiger's heart wrapp'd in a woman's hide!"],["sea","York's rage and tears as wind and rain.","For raging wind blows up incessant showers"]],"244":[["sun","Three suns join into one: the three brothers.","Now are they but one lamp, one light, one sun"]],"254":[["sun","Richard's heart as a furnace.","my furnace-burning heart"]],"282":[["beast","Lions, bears, serpents, worms and doves defend their young.","To whom do lions cast their gentle looks?"]],"335":[["sun","Edward's sun is clouded.","Edward's sun is clouded"]],"357":[["sea","The battle sways like the sea against the wind.","Now sways it this way, like a mighty sea"],["sun","The battle as dawn's struggle of light and dark.","This battle fares like to the morning's war"]],"363":[["blood","The red and white roses in a dead boy's face.","The red rose and the white are on his face"]],"383":[["sun","Clifford's life as a candle; the Yorkists as the sun that draws the gnats.","Here burns my candle out"]],"439":[["sea","Common men as a feather blown by the wind.","Commanded always by the greater gust"]],"482":[["sea","Edward's wooing as rain wearing marble.","much rain wears the marble"]],"531":[["sea","Richard on a headland, wishing to bail the sea dry.","Like one that stands upon a promontory"],["tree","Richard lost in a thorny wood.","like one lost in a thorny wood"],["theatre","Richard as an actor who can change his face.","And frame my face to all occasions"],["beast","Richard compares himself to an unlicked bear cub.","an unlick'd bear-whelp"]],"536":[["sea","Margaret lowers her sail like a ship in submission.","Must strike her sail"]],"552":[["sea","Warwick moves both wind and tide.","moves both wind and tide"]],"708":[["sun","Fortune's wheel cannot contain Edward's mind.","My mind exceeds the compass of her wheel"]],"843":[["sea","Henry as a spring, Edward as the sea that swallows his streams.","You are the fount that makes small brooks to flow"]],"879":[["sea","Warwick as a weathercock.","Wind-changing Warwick"]],"914":[["tree","Warwick as a fallen cedar.","Thus yields the cedar to the axe's edge"],["beast","The eagle and the lion sheltered by the cedar.","Whose arms gave shelter to the princely eagle"]],"932":[["sea","The state as a ship: anchor, mast, pilot.","What though the mast be now blown overboard"]],"1006":[["theatre","Richard as the actor Roscius.","What scene of death hath Roscius now to act?"],["beast","Henry as a sheep before the wolf and butcher.","So flies the reckless shepherd from the wolf"]],"1014":[["beast","Omens at Richard's birth: owl, night-crow, raven, magpies.","The owl shriek'd at thy birth, an evil sign"]],"1022":[["blood","Richard's sword 'weeps' blood.","I, that have neither pity, love, nor fear"]],"1026":[["tree","Enemies mown down like autumn corn.","like to autumn's corn"]],"1027":[["tree","Richard will blast Edward's harvest.","I'll blast his harvest"]]},"stage":{"hall":"The Parliament House in London, with the throne","castle":"York's castle at Sandal, near Wakefield","field":"Open country before a battle","battle":"A battlefield: Wakefield, Towton, Barnet or Tewkesbury","gate":"Before the gates of the city of York","forest":"A forest or hunting park","palace":"The royal palace in London","france":"The French king's palace","camp":"Edward's camp at night, before his tent","dungeon":"The Tower of London","walls":"The walls of Coventry"},"stagerules":[["parliament","hall"],["plain","field"],["castle","castle"],["before york","gate"],["field","battle"],["forest|park","forest"],["france","france"],["tower","dungeon"],["camp","camp"],["coventry","walls"],["palace","palace"]],"place":{"a1s1":"The Parliament House, London","a1s2":"Sandal Castle, Yorkshire","a1s3":"The battlefield of Wakefield","a1s4":"The battlefield of Wakefield","a2s1":"Near Mortimer's Cross, Herefordshire","a2s2":"Before the city of York","a2s3":"The battlefield of Towton","a2s4":"The battlefield of Towton","a2s5":"The battlefield of Towton","a2s6":"The battlefield of Towton","a3s1":"A forest in the north of England","a3s2":"The royal palace, London","a3s3":"The French king's palace","a4s1":"The royal palace, London","a4s2":"A plain in Warwickshire","a4s3":"Edward's camp near Warwick","a4s4":"The royal palace, London","a4s5":"A park near Middleham Castle, Yorkshire","a4s6":"The Tower of London","a4s7":"Before the gates of York","a4s8":"The royal palace, London","a5s1":"Before the walls of Coventry","a5s2":"The battlefield of Barnet","a5s3":"The battlefield of Barnet","a5s4":"Plains near Tewkesbury","a5s5":"The battlefield of Tewkesbury","a5s6":"The Tower of London","a5s7":"The royal palace, London"},"arc":{"York's fortunes":[8,7,4,2,4,5,3,5,7,9,9,9,6,5,4,2,2,4,3,6,8,8,9,8,7,10,10,10],"Lancaster's fortunes":[3,4,7,9,7,6,7,5,3,1,1,1,5,6,7,9,9,8,9,6,3,4,2,4,5,1,0,0]},"arcnote":"The two lines cross again and again: the play is a see-saw. York begins on the throne, but the entail costs Lancaster only a promise, and Margaret's victory at Wakefield brings York to his lowest point with his death in 1.4. The Yorkists rise at Towton and stay high through Edward's coronation and Henry's capture. Edward's marriage is the hinge: Warwick turns in 3.3, and Lancaster climbs until Edward is captured and Henry restored (4.3 to 4.6). Then Edward escapes, is proclaimed at York, seizes Henry and wins at Barnet. Margaret's last army gives Lancaster one more rise before Tewkesbury, after which the Prince and Henry are killed and Lancaster falls to nothing. York ends at the top, but the audience knows that the next threat to the house of York comes from inside it.","iago":[["a1s1","Henry, threatened in his own Parliament, entails the crown on York and his heirs, disinheriting Prince Edward."],["a1s2","York breaks the agreement: persuaded by Richard, he decides to take the crown by war."],["a1s4","Margaret kills York at Wakefield and crowns his corpse's head with paper: the Yorkist claim passes to Edward."],["a2s1","Warwick hails Edward as Duke of York and promises to proclaim him king in every town."],["a2s6","After Towton, Warwick sends Edward to London to be crowned; Henry flees."],["a3s1","Henry, back from Scotland, is captured by keepers who have sworn to King Edward."],["a3s2","Edward marries Lady Grey, throwing away the French alliance that secured his crown."],["a3s3","Warwick renounces Edward and swears to Margaret and Lewis to restore Henry."],["a4s1","Clarence and Somerset leave Edward's court to join Warwick."],["a4s3","Warwick seizes Edward in his tent and takes the crown from his head."],["a4s6","Henry is freed and restored as king, but hands the government to Warwick and Clarence."],["a4s7","Edward, escaped and back from Burgundy, is proclaimed King Edward IV at York."],["a4s8","Edward seizes Henry in London and sends him back to the Tower."],["a5s1","Clarence deserts Warwick for Edward; Warwick's cause loses its head and its heir."],["a5s2","Warwick, the kingmaker, dies at Barnet."],["a5s5","At Tewkesbury, the Lancastrian heir Prince Edward is stabbed by the three brothers."],["a5s6","Richard murders Henry in the Tower: no Lancastrian king remains."],["a5s7","Edward sits on the throne with his infant heir; Richard plans to take the crown next."]],"rel":{"1":[["WARWICK","YORK","trust"],["YORK","KING HENRY","hate"],["CLIFFORD","YORK","hate"],["QUEEN MARGARET","KING HENRY","hate"],["KING HENRY","QUEEN MARGARET","love"],["RICHARD","YORK","love"],["CLIFFORD","RUTLAND","hate"],["QUEEN MARGARET","YORK","hate"],["NORTHUMBERLAND","YORK","love"],["EXETER","KING HENRY","trust"]],"2":[["RICHARD","CLIFFORD","hate"],["EDWARD","WARWICK","trust"],["WARWICK","EDWARD","trust"],["CLIFFORD","KING HENRY","trust"],["QUEEN MARGARET","PRINCE","love"],["EDWARD","QUEEN MARGARET","hate"],["RICHARD","QUEEN MARGARET","hate"],["KING HENRY","PRINCE","love"]],"3":[["KING EDWARD","LADY GREY","love"],["GLOSTER","KING EDWARD","deceive"],["CLARENCE","KING EDWARD","hate"],["WARWICK","KING EDWARD","hate"],["QUEEN MARGARET","WARWICK","trust"],["KING LEWIS","WARWICK","trust"],["BONA","KING EDWARD","hate"],["OXFORD","WARWICK","hate"]],"4":[["CLARENCE","WARWICK","trust"],["WARWICK","CLARENCE","trust"],["GLOSTER","KING EDWARD","deceive"],["HASTINGS","KING EDWARD","trust"],["KING HENRY","WARWICK","trust"],["WARWICK","KING EDWARD","hate"],["QUEEN ELIZABETH","KING EDWARD","love"],["MONTGOMERY","KING EDWARD","trust"]],"5":[["CLARENCE","WARWICK","deceive"],["GLOSTER","CLARENCE","deceive"],["KING EDWARD","WARWICK","hate"],["QUEEN MARGARET","PRINCE","love"],["PRINCE","KING EDWARD","hate"],["GLOSTER","KING HENRY","hate"],["KING HENRY","GLOSTER","hate"],["OXFORD","QUEEN MARGARET","trust"],["GLOSTER","KING EDWARD","deceive"]]},"context":[["Date and texts","The play was probably written about 1591. A shorter version was printed in 1595 as The True Tragedy of Richard Duke of York and the Good King Henry the Sixth (an octavo). The First Folio of 1623 printed the longer text as The Third Part of Henry the Sixth. Scholars debate whether the octavo is an early draft or a version reconstructed from performance; most modern editions follow the Folio."],["Sources","Shakespeare drew on Edward Hall's Union of the Two Noble and Illustre Families of Lancaster and York (1548) and Raphael Holinshed's Chronicles (2nd edition, 1587). The paper crown, the three suns, the son who killed his father and the stabbing of Prince Edward all come from these chronicles, sometimes rearranged."],["The real events","The play runs from the Parliament of 1460 (the Act of Accord) through Wakefield (December 1460), Mortimer's Cross and the second battle of St Albans (February 1461), Towton (March 1461), Henry's capture (1465), Edward's marriage to Elizabeth Woodville (1464), Warwick's rebellion (1469 to 1470), Henry's brief restoration (1470 to 1471), Barnet (April 1471) and Tewkesbury (May 1471). Henry died in the Tower in May 1471."],["Compression","Shakespeare telescopes eleven years into a rapid series of reversals. Rutland was seventeen, not a child; Richard was eight at Wakefield, not a soldier; Edward's marriage came before, not after, Warwick's embassy. These changes sharpen the themes of murdered children and broken faith."],["The first tetralogy","1, 2 and 3 Henry VI and Richard III form a four-play sequence on the loss of France and the Wars of the Roses. 3 Henry VI introduces Richard of Gloucester's ambitions, and its last scenes lead directly into the first speech of Richard III."],["Authorship","The play was printed as Shakespeare's in the Folio. Some scholars have suggested collaboration (Christopher Marlowe is the name most often proposed), and the New Oxford Shakespeare (2016) credits Marlowe as co-author. The question remains open."],["The Tudor view","The Tudor historians presented the Wars of the Roses as a punishment for the deposition of Richard II that ended only when Henry VII united the roses. Henry VI's blessing of the young Richmond in 4.6 nods to this view."],["Women at war","Margaret of Anjou really did lead the Lancastrian cause while Henry suffered periods of mental illness. The play makes her a warrior queen in armour, and both sides attack her for being unwomanly."],["Machiavelli","Niccolò Machiavelli's The Prince (1532) was notorious in England as a handbook of political wickedness. Richard's boast that he will 'set the murtherous Machiavel to school' draws on the stage figure of the scheming 'Machiavel'."],["Staging","The play needs large battle scenes with drums, alarums and excursions, and uses the upper stage for the walls of York and Coventry. Modern productions, such as the Royal Shakespeare Company's The Wars of the Roses (1963) and later cycles, have often combined it with the other Henry VI plays."]],"critics":{"crown":[["E. M. W. Tillyard, 'Shakespeare's History Plays' (1944)","Paraphrased: Tillyard read the Henry VI plays as showing England suffering for broken order. In Part 3 order collapses completely, and the chaos of the civil war is the punishment for disorder that began with the deposition of Richard II."],["Jan Kott, 'Shakespeare Our Contemporary' (1964)","Paraphrased: Kott saw the histories as a 'Grand Mechanism' in which every king climbs the same staircase to the throne and is pushed down again. The swings of 3 Henry VI fit this pattern exactly: the names change, but the mechanism stays the same."]],"revenge":[["Michael Hattaway, introduction to the New Cambridge edition (1993)","Paraphrased: Hattaway stresses that the play shows a society without a working centre, where chivalric honour has turned into vendetta and every killing calls for another."],["Ronald Knowles, introduction to the Arden edition of 2 Henry VI (1999)","Paraphrased: Knowles traces how the codes of honour in the Henry VI plays decay into revenge; 3 Henry VI completes that decline."]],"women":[["Jean E. Howard and Phyllis Rackin, 'Engendering a Nation' (1997)","Paraphrased: Howard and Rackin argue that the history plays link political disorder with women who move into men's roles. Margaret's leadership and her attack on York show this anxiety, and the men answer by calling her monstrous and unnatural."],["Randall Martin, introduction to the Oxford edition (2001)","Paraphrased: Martin gives close attention to Margaret as a commanding figure whose energy holds the Lancastrian side together, and to how the play complicates the stereotype of the cruel foreign queen."]],"self":[["A. P. Rossiter, 'Angel with Horns' (1961)","Paraphrased: Rossiter saw Richard as a figure of comic, theatrical energy who mocks the moral order of the histories. His soliloquies in 3 Henry VI make the audience his accomplices."],["Bernard Spivack, 'Shakespeare and the Allegory of Evil' (1958)","Paraphrased: Spivack traced Richard's descent from the Vice of the morality plays, the witty, self-explaining tempter who shares his plans with the audience."]],"family":[["John D. Cox and Eric Rasmussen, introduction to the Arden edition (2001)","Paraphrased: Cox and Rasmussen point to the play's repeated broken bonds between parents and children, from Henry's disinheriting of his son to the son and father at Towton, as the human centre of the war."],["Emrys Jones, 'The Origins of Shakespeare' (1977)","Paraphrased: Jones showed how the Henry VI plays build scenes of mourning parents and murdered children in patterns, so that each killing echoes and answers an earlier one."]],"fortune":[["Irving Ribner, 'The English History Play in the Age of Shakespeare' (1957)","Paraphrased: Ribner saw the play as showing history driven by human will and failure as much as by providence; fortune turns because men break faith."],["Robert Ornstein, 'A Kingdom for a Stage' (1972)","Paraphrased: Ornstein argued that the plays question rather than illustrate the Tudor idea of providence; the reversals of 3 Henry VI often look like chance and opportunism, not divine justice."]]},"symbols":{"Crown":"\\bcrown\\w*|\\bdiadem\\b","Rose":"\\broses?\\b","Sun":"\\bsuns?\\b","Molehill":"\\bmolehill\\b","Wolf":"\\bwol(f|ves)\\b|\\btiger\\w*","Lamb":"\\blambs?\\b|\\bsheep\\b","Blood":"\\bblood\\w*"},"symbols2":[["The crown","The play's prize and its joke: York sits on the throne, wears a paper crown on a molehill, and dies; Henry's crown is 'content'; Warwick takes Edward's crown off and puts Henry's back. By the end, the crown has changed hands so often that its value is in doubt."],["The molehill","York is mocked on a molehill as a would-be king; Henry sits on one to wish he were a shepherd. A tiny mound stands for the vanity of their claims."],["The paper crown and the napkin","Margaret's mock coronation and Rutland's blood turn York's ambition and his love for his son into instruments of torture."],["The three suns","The sign of the three York brothers united. Edward makes it his badge; Richard jokes about it. The brothers do not stay united."],["The red and white roses","Seen in the dead son's face at Towton: the badges of the two houses become blood and pallor on a dead body. Clarence throws away his red rose to rejoin York."],["Wolves, tigers and lambs","Margaret is a 'she-wolf' and a 'tiger's heart'; Henry is a lamb among wolves and sees the common people as harmless lambs. At the end Margaret calls Edward the wolf."],["The sea and the wind","Battles sway like the sea; men are feathers in the wind; Warwick is 'wind-changing'; Margaret's ship of state loses its anchor and mast. The weather of the play is fortune."],["The cedar","Warwick dies as a felled cedar that once sheltered eagles and lions: the fall of the kingmaker."]],"solo":{"138":1,"357":1,"383":1,"420":1,"531":1,"616":1,"914":1,"1022":1},"insults":[{"id":"32","f":"Patience is for poltroons","who":"Clifford","n":"Clifford calls Henry's patience cowardice."},{"id":"94","f":"Base, fearful, and despairing Henry!","who":"Westmoreland","n":"Henry's own lords abuse him."},{"id":"99","f":"faint-hearted and degenerate king","who":"Westmoreland","n":"'Degenerate': unworthy of his father."},{"id":"130","f":"Ah, timorous wretch!","who":"Margaret","n":"Margaret's contempt for her husband."},{"id":"181","f":"the brat of this accursed duke","who":"Clifford","n":"Clifford dehumanises Rutland."},{"id":"221","f":"that valiant crook-back prodigy","who":"Margaret","n":"The first mockery of Richard's body."},{"id":"221","f":"Dicky your boy","who":"Margaret","n":"Diminutive contempt."},{"id":"226","f":"She-wolf of France","who":"York","n":"York's most famous insult."},{"id":"226","f":"like an Amazonian trull","who":"York","n":"A warrior whore: gender and sex used as weapons."},{"id":"226","f":"O tiger's heart wrapp'd in a woman's hide!","who":"York","n":"Margaret as a beast in disguise."},{"id":"226","f":"false Frenchwoman","who":"York","n":"Nationality as insult."},{"id":"300","f":"Are you there, butcher?","who":"Richard","n":"Richard names Clifford's crime."},{"id":"306","f":"long-tongued Warwick","who":"Margaret","n":"Warwick as a windbag."},{"id":"311","f":"that cruel child-killer","who":"Richard","n":"The label Clifford earns at Wakefield."},{"id":"323","f":"a foul misshapen stigmatic","who":"Margaret","n":"Richard's deformity as a sign of evil."},{"id":"324","f":"Iron of Naples hid with English gilt","who":"Richard","n":"Margaret's poverty mocked."},{"id":"325","f":"this shameless callat","who":"Edward","n":"Edward calls the queen a scold."},{"id":"329","f":"wrangling woman","who":"Edward","n":"Edward dismisses Margaret."},{"id":"557","f":"Injurious Margaret!","who":"Warwick","n":"Warwick denies her the title of queen."},{"id":"582","f":"impudent and shameless Warwick","who":"Margaret","n":"Margaret before Warwick changes sides."},{"id":"865","f":"sportful Edward","who":"Warwick","n":"Edward as a pleasure-seeker."},{"id":"905","f":"O passing traitor, perjur'd and unjust!","who":"Warwick","n":"Warwick on Clarence's second betrayal."},{"id":"965","f":"this captive scold","who":"Gloucester","n":"Richard on Margaret."},{"id":"966","f":"this scolding crook-back","who":"Prince Edward","n":"The Prince turns 'scold' back on Richard."},{"id":"969","f":"Lascivious Edward","who":"Prince Edward","n":"The insults that cost the Prince his life."},{"id":"986","f":"Butchers and villains! bloody cannibals!","who":"Margaret","n":"Margaret over her son's body."},{"id":"992","f":"that devil's butcher","who":"Margaret","n":"Margaret names Richard the real killer."}],"pronouns":[{"id":"38","f":"Thou factious Duke of York, descend my throne","n":"Henry uses 'thou' as a king to a rebel subject."},{"id":"39","f":"I am thine.","n":"York turns 'thy sovereign' around: he claims to be Henry's king."},{"id":"129","f":"Pardon me, Margaret;—pardon me, sweet son","n":"Henry's pleading 'me' to wife and son."},{"id":"130","f":"I shame to hear thee speak.","n":"Margaret uses contemptuous 'thee' to her husband the king."},{"id":"468","f":"Now tell me, madam, do you love your children?","n":"Edward begins with polite 'you' to the widow."},{"id":"508","f":"Say that King Edward take thee for his queen?","n":"Edward switches to intimate 'thee' when he proposes."},{"id":"959","f":"Speak like a subject, proud, ambitious York!","n":"The Prince speaks to King Edward as to a subject, by his title of York."},{"id":"1003","f":"my lord, I should say rather","n":"Henry corrects 'good lord' to plain 'lord' to deny Richard goodness."}],"predict":{"a1s1":["Henry fights York for the throne","Henry gives up his son's right to keep the crown for life"],"a1s2":["York keeps his oath","Richard persuades him to break it at once"],"a1s3":["Clifford spares the boy","Clifford kills Rutland for his father's death"],"a1s4":["York escapes to his sons","York is captured, mocked with a paper crown and killed"],"a2s1":["Edward and Richard give up","Warwick rallies them to make Edward king"],"a2s2":["Henry makes peace with the Yorkists","The parley ends in insults and battle"],"a2s3":["The Yorkists flee Towton","Warwick vows to fight to the death and they return"],"a2s4":["Richard kills Clifford","Clifford flees before Warwick"],"a2s5":["Henry rides into battle","Henry sits on a molehill and watches a son and a father mourn"],"a2s6":["Clifford escapes with Margaret","Clifford dies and Edward will be crowned"],"a3s1":["Henry raises a new army in Scotland","Henry is caught by two gamekeepers"],"a3s2":["Edward grants the widow's suit and nothing more","Edward marries her, and Gloucester reveals his own ambitions"],"a3s3":["Warwick wins Bona for Edward","News of Edward's marriage turns Warwick against him"],"a4s1":["The brothers accept Edward's marriage","Clarence leaves to join Warwick"],"a4s2":["Warwick fights Edward in open battle","Warwick plans a night raid on Edward's camp"],"a4s3":["Edward beats off the raid","Edward is captured and uncrowned"],"a4s4":["The queen flees to France","She takes sanctuary to protect her unborn child"],"a4s5":["Edward stays a prisoner","Gloucester rescues him while he hunts"],"a4s6":["Henry rules strongly again","Henry hands power to Warwick and Clarence, and Edward escapes"],"a4s7":["York's gates stay shut","Edward gets in and is proclaimed king"],"a4s8":["Warwick defeats Edward","Edward seizes Henry in London"],"a5s1":["Clarence fights for Warwick","Clarence returns to Edward"],"a5s2":["Warwick escapes Barnet","Warwick dies on the field"],"a5s3":["Edward rests after Barnet","Edward marches at once to meet Margaret"],"a5s4":["Margaret's lords desert her","They stand and fight at Tewkesbury"],"a5s5":["Prince Edward is spared","The three brothers stab him"],"a5s6":["Gloucester frees Henry","Gloucester murders him"]},"contrast":[{"t":"Henry and Margaret","pts":["Henry wants peace and a shepherd's life; Margaret wants victory and her son's rights.","Henry disinherits his son; Margaret raises armies for him.","Henry forgives and blesses; Margaret mocks and curses.","Both lose their son, and both prophesy against the house of York."],"ids":[107,130,357,932]},{"t":"Edward and Richard","pts":["Both are brave sons of York.","Edward grieves and weeps; Richard turns grief to rage.","Edward follows his desires; Richard plans and acts a part.","Edward wins the crown; Richard is already plotting to take it."],"ids":[253,254,508,531]},{"t":"Clifford and Richard","pts":["Both are sons sworn to avenge a father.","Clifford kills a child, Rutland; Richard helps kill a young man, Prince Edward, and kills Henry.","Clifford dies blaming his king's weakness; Richard survives and rejects every bond."],"ids":[196,254,383,1022]},{"t":"The son and the father at Towton","pts":["Each has killed his own flesh and blood unknowingly.","Each fears how the women at home will grieve.","Their mirror speeches show civil war as a family destroying itself."],"ids":[359,362,364,365]},{"t":"Warwick and Clarence","pts":["Both change sides because of Edward's marriage.","Warwick changes once, for honour, and dies for it; Clarence changes twice and survives.","Warwick is called 'wind-changing'; Clarence 'perjur'd'."],"ids":[595,663,902,905]},{"t":"York's death and Henry's death","pts":["Both are taunted before being killed.","York answers with rage and curses; Henry with prophecy.","Both are killed by a son of the other house.","Both die praying."],"ids":[226,235,1014,1018]}],"analysis":{"KING HENRY":["Henry is the gentlest king in Shakespeare and the least able to rule. In the first scene he refuses to turn Parliament into a 'shambles', privately admits 'my title's weak', and buys his own peace by disinheriting his son. His queen and lords call him faint-hearted and degenerate, and the play largely agrees: Clifford's dying verdict is that if Henry had 'sway'd as kings should do', the war would never have happened.","Yet Henry is also the play's moral voice. Sent away from Towton, he sits on a molehill and imagines the life of a shepherd measured by sundials and seasons; then he watches a son who has killed his father and a father who has killed his son, and sees the red and white roses in the dead boy's face. He grieves for his subjects as no other character does. Captured, he says his crown is 'content'; restored, he hands power to others and blesses Richmond as 'England's hope'.","His last scene gives him unexpected strength. Facing Richard in the Tower he refuses flattery, calls Richard an actor come to play a death scene, and delivers a prophecy that Richard cuts off with a dagger. He dies forgiving his murderer. Henry shows the cost of goodness without power, but also the poverty of a world that has no use for it."],"QUEEN MARGARET":["Margaret takes over the Lancastrian cause because Henry will not fight. Her first speech attacks him as an unnatural father, and she separates herself from his bed until the entail is repealed. She then raises the north, defeats York at Wakefield and wins again at St Albans, while her enemies call her a 'woman-general', 'she-wolf of France' and 'Amazonian trull'.","Her cruelty to York is the most shocking scene of the first half: the molehill, the napkin dipped in Rutland's blood and the paper crown turn his ambition into a cruel joke. But the play also gives her real courage and eloquence. In France she pleads, loses, and then wins everything when Edward's marriage turns Warwick. At Tewkesbury her ship speech makes her the only true leader on her side.","The play then turns her cruelty back on her. She is made to watch her own son stabbed, as York was made to weep for Rutland, and her lament ('You have no children, butchers!') echoes York's. She begs to die and is sent back to France. Her curses look forward to Richard III, where she returns as a prophetess of doom."],"WARWICK":["Warwick is the 'setter-up and plucker-down of kings'. He seats York on the throne, makes Edward king after Towton and plans his marriage. He is a superb orator and a soldier who vows to kill his horse rather than fly. His power is personal: the crown seems to rest on his shoulder, as Edward admits.","His change of side in France reveals his motive. He renounces Edward because he has been made a 'stale', a laughing-stock; he admits he does not pity Henry's misery but seeks revenge on Edward's mockery. He captures Edward in his tent, lists his failings as a king and literally takes the crown off his head.","At Coventry he is outmanoeuvred: Henry is already captured and Clarence turns. Dying at Barnet, he compares himself to a felled cedar and asks 'what is pomp, rule, reign, but earth and dust?' The kingmaker ends with nothing but his body's length of earth."],"YORK":["York dominates the first act. He takes Henry's throne, accepts the entail and swears to keep the peace, then lets his son talk him out of the oath within a scene. His confidence against a woman-general leads him to fight at Wakefield against the odds.","His death scene is one of the most powerful in the early plays. Captured, set on a molehill and crowned with paper, he is given his son's blood to dry his tears. He answers with a speech that moves from contempt to grief: 'O tiger's heart wrapp'd in a woman's hide!' He weeps for Rutland and makes Northumberland weep too. He dies praying, and his head is set on the gates of York.","York's claim passes to his sons. His defiance ('My ashes, as the phoenix, may bring forth a bird that will revenge') comes true in Edward and Richard, but it brings his house the same violence that destroyed him."],"KING EDWARD":["Edward is a fighter and a lover. He grieves openly for his father, adopts the three suns as his badge, and becomes king through Warwick's power. His weakness is appetite: Richard jokes that he 'loves the breeder better than the male', and his first act of private rule is to proposition a widow and then, when she refuses, to marry her.","The marriage shows him governing by will ('I am Edward, your king and Warwick's, and must have my will'). It costs him France, Warwick, Clarence and, briefly, the crown. Taken in his gown, he still insists his mind 'exceeds the compass' of fortune's wheel. Rescued, he learns cunning: he claims only his dukedom to get into York, and becomes ruthless, threatening to write in the dust with Warwick's blood.","At Tewkesbury he is the first to stab Prince Edward. He ends the play enthroned with his queen and infant son, proud of his 'brothers' loves', blind to Richard's Judas kiss. The 'lasting joy' he announces is the play's last irony."],"GLOSTER":["Richard (named Gloucester from 3.2) begins as a fiercely loyal son. He throws down Somerset's head, argues his father out of his oath and, when York is killed, refuses tears: 'blows and revenge for me!' He hunts Clifford through Towton and mocks his corpse.","In his great soliloquy after Edward's wooing (3.2), he reveals the man who will become Richard III. He counts the lives between himself and the crown, says love forswore him in the womb and gave him a withered arm and crooked back, and decides that since no one will love him, the crown will be his heaven. He can 'smile, and murther while I smile', 'frame my face to all occasions' and 'set the murtherous Machiavel to school'. From now on he stays with Edward 'not for the love of Edward, but the crown'.","He acts on it. He rescues Edward, wins Clarence back, stabs Prince Edward, leaves for 'the Tower' and kills Henry mid-prophecy. Alone with the body he rejects every bond: 'I have no brother, I am like no brother... I am myself alone.' He names Clarence as next, and kisses Edward's son as Judas kissed Christ. The play ends with him already plotting the next play."],"CLARENCE":["George, Duke of Clarence, is the brother who changes sides. He joins his brothers from Burgundy before Towton, but Edward's marriage and the heiresses given to the queen's family drive him to Warwick, whose younger daughter he marries. He is named joint Protector under Henry.","At Coventry, after a whispered word with Richard, he pulls the red rose from his hat and returns to Edward, claiming that keeping his oath to Warwick would be worse than Jephthah's sacrifice. Warwick calls him 'perjur'd', the word the Prince and Margaret will throw back at him.","He stabs Prince Edward but refuses to kill Margaret. His fickleness makes him useful and doomed: Richard's last soliloquy names him as the next to die."]},"motifs":[["The crown","The play's prize changes hands again and again: entailed, mocked in paper, taken off Edward's head, put back on Henry's, claimed and disclaimed. Henry's 'crown call'd content' is the only one no one can take.",[22,53,54,129,130,156,189,277,284]],["Molehills, thrones and the shepherd","York is crowned on a molehill; Henry sits on one and wishes he were a shepherd. The great and small images make kingship look like a child's game of 'king of the castle'.",[50,51,99,100,101,102]],["Beasts: wolves, tigers, lambs","The Yorkists call Margaret a she-wolf and tiger; Henry is a lamb among wolves; Clifford argues from lions and bears; Richard is a ragged rock, a dog born with teeth. The human world has become a forest.",[27,55,56,76,108,212,241,266]],["Sea, tide and wind","Battles sway like the sea, men are feathers in the wind, Warwick moves wind and tide, Margaret steers a broken ship. Fortune is weather.",[45,98,133,159,219,233,234]],["Fathers and sons","Henry disinherits his son; Clifford kills a son for a father; the son and the father at Towton; Henry as Daedalus mourning Icarus. Every bond between generations breaks.",[21,42,105,109,110,261,251]],["Theatre and masks","Margaret stages a mock coronation; Richard boasts of acting and wears a mask; Henry calls him Roscius. The play is aware of itself as a performance of history.",[53,153,154,259,282]]],"bigq":[["Who is the rightful king?",["Henry admits his title is weak and Exeter agrees with York","Warwick's soldiers, not the law, decide in 1.1","Every king in the play is called a usurper by someone","By the end, right seems to belong to whoever holds the crown"],[12,13,14,17,131,163,207]],["Is Henry a good man and a bad king?",["He refuses violence and grieves for his subjects","His weakness lets the war happen, as Clifford says","He gives away his son's birthright and his own power","He dies forgiving his murderer"],[7,18,113,117,129,196,268]],["Is Margaret a monster or a mother?",["She torments York with Rutland's blood","She fights for her son when his father will not","Her enemies call her unnatural for doing a man's job","She suffers exactly what she inflicted"],[24,52,56,57,233,251,253]],["Why does Warwick change sides?",["Edward's marriage makes him a 'stale' in France","He admits he does not pity Henry","Honour and pride, not principle, drive him","He dies having made and unmade two kings"],[166,167,172,173,226,229]],["How does the play prepare for Richard III?",["Richard's soliloquy in 3.2 reveals his plan","He murders Henry and rejects his family","Margaret and Henry curse and prophesy","The Judas kiss and Edward's 'lasting joy' end on irony"],[144,153,155,263,274,275,282,284]],["Is anyone in this play loyal?",["Oaths are broken by York, Henry, Edward, Warwick and Clarence","The keepers say allegiance lasts only while a king rules","Clifford is loyal to Henry 'right or wrong'","Richard's loyalty to Edward is a mask"],[16,31,32,131,132,182,224]]],"examq":[["How does Shakespeare present the struggle for the crown in Henry VI, Part 3?",[12,22,129,189,207,277,284]],["Explore the presentation of Queen Margaret.",[24,26,52,56,233,251,253]],["How is Richard of Gloucester presented as a dangerous figure?",[144,146,153,155,272,274,282]],["How does the play present the cost of civil war for ordinary people?",[104,105,107,108,109,111,114]],["Explore the theme of revenge in the play.",[37,42,48,68,119,121,253]],["How does Shakespeare present King Henry as a ruler?",[12,18,100,117,129,210,268]]],"check":["Answered the exact question in the first sentence","Made a clear argument (a thesis) and kept to it","Used short, precise quotations, embedded in your sentences","Analysed words and images, not just the story","Linked the play to its context (the Wars of the Roses, Tudor history) where it helps","Compared at least two moments from different parts of the play","Considered how an audience might react","Mentioned the dramatic effect: staging, irony, soliloquy","Used terms such as soliloquy, aside, dramatic irony, imagery","Ended with a conclusion that answers the question"],"models":[[129,"Henry's line redefines what a crown is.","'My crown is in my heart, not on my head' moves kingship from the outward symbol to the inner state.","The crown 'call'd content' is one 'that seldom kings enjoy', so he suggests that real kings are never content.","The irony is that he says this while being arrested, a king with no kingdom: his inner crown cannot protect him."],[56,"York's insult defines Margaret for her enemies.","'O tiger's heart wrapp'd in a woman's hide!' sets the animal inside against the woman outside.","The image accuses her of disguise: she looks like a woman but has the cruelty of a beast.","Yet she is fighting for her son, so the play invites us to question whether her 'unnatural' violence is really a mother's love turned hard."],[153,"Richard reveals himself as an actor.","'Why, I can smile, and murther while I smile' places smiling and murdering side by side as equal skills.","The repetition of 'smile' makes the hypocrisy chilling and almost comic.","Speaking in soliloquy, he makes the audience his confidants, so we share the knowledge that the other characters lack."],[105,"The son's discovery puts civil war into a single face.","'O God! it is my father's face' turns an anonymous enemy into a family member.","The broken line and exclamation show the shock of recognition.","Henry watches as a helpless spectator, as we do, which makes the scene a symbol of the whole war."],[42,"Clifford's line states the logic of revenge.","'Thy father slew my father; therefore, die' is built as a syllogism: a reason and a conclusion.","The neat shape makes murder sound like justice, which is exactly the horror.","Rutland's earlier warning about Clifford's own son shows that the 'therefore' will never end."],[117,"Clifford's dying words judge Henry.","'hadst thou sway'd as kings should do' sets an ideal of kingship that Henry has failed.","Coming from Henry's most loyal follower, the criticism has special weight.","Yet the play also shows that the strong kings (Edward, later Richard) bring no peace, so the question of good kingship stays open."]],"lang":{"un":[["226","She-wolf of France, but worse than wolves of France,","Margaret as a savage beast from a foreign land."],["531","Why, I can smile, and murther while I smile,","Richard can hide murder behind a pleasant face."],["431","Not to be seen; my crown is call'd content,","True kingship is an inner peace no one can see."],["914","Why, what is pomp, rule, reign, but earth and dust?","Power counts for nothing in death."],["1022","I have no brother, I am like no brother,","Richard denies all family bonds."]],"pun":[["246","Nay, bear three daughters","daughters","pun","Suns and sons: Edward should bear daughters on his shield, since he loves women."],["466","Ay, good leave have you","leave","pun","'Leave' as permission, liberty and departure."],["516","When he was made a shriver, 't was for shift.","shift","pun","Shift as a trick and as a woman's smock."],["229","Had he been slaughter-man to all my kin","slaughter-man","wordplay","An ugly compound for a killer."],["1003","'good' was little better","good","wordplay","Henry refuses to call Richard 'good'."]],"st":[["531","Why, I can smile, and murther while I smile,",[["smile","Repeated: the smile is his weapon."],["murther","Placed between two smiles, hidden by them."]]],["1022","And not in me! I am myself alone.",[["alone","The last word: total isolation."],["myself","Self as the only loyalty."]]],["196","Thy father slew my father; therefore, die.",[["father","Repeated: revenge passed between generations."],["therefore","Murder as logical conclusion."]]]]},"dir":[["221","Look, York; I stain'd this napkin with the blood","How should Margaret present the napkin?",[["Slowly, savouring York's pain","Stress her cruelty and enjoyment."],["Fast and furious, almost hysterical","Show her as driven by grief and rage of her own."],["Coolly, as a political display to her soldiers","Make her a commander, not a monster."]]],["357","Here on this molehill will I sit me down.","How should Henry play the molehill speech?",[["Wistful and dreamy, almost happy","Make the audience share his longing."],["Exhausted and broken","Show him as a man destroyed by war."],["Ironically aware of the molehill's echo of York","Bring out the play's pattern."]]],["508","Say that King Edward take thee for his queen?","How should Edward propose?",[["Impulsively, surprised by himself","Edward acts on desire."],["Calculatingly, to win her body","The proposal is a trick."],["With genuine admiration","He is moved by her virtue and wit."]]],["531","Why, I can smile, and murther while I smile,","How should Gloucester deliver this line?",[["Smiling at the audience","Make us complicit in his charm."],["Quietly, as a discovery","He is realising his power."],["Bitterly, still angry about his body","Show the self-pity behind the threat."]]],["970","Take that, the likeness of this railer here.","How should the stabbing of Prince Edward be staged?",[["Quickly and chaotically","A crime of sudden rage."],["Slowly, one brother after another","A ritual of shared guilt."],["With Margaret held so she must watch","Echo York and the napkin."]]],["1031","To say the truth, so Judas kiss'd his Master,","How should the Judas kiss be staged?",[["A long, tender kiss on the baby","Make the audience shudder."],["Quickly, with a wink to the audience","Richard as comic villain."],["Edward smiling, unaware","Stress the irony of the ending."]]]],"ev":{"ctx":[["243","1006"],["243","351","41"],["261","711","959"],["184","351","155"],["531","1006","1015"],["588","959"],["107","765","1015"],["226","536","606"],["531","812"],["174","363","372"]],"cr":{"crown":[["66","107","360"],["270","531","812"]],"revenge":[["181","184","196"],["196","404","979"]],"women":[["226","606","964"],["226","536","964"]],"self":[["531","1015","1027"],["531","1007","1027"]],"family":[["107","184","372"],["184","372","979"]],"fortune":[["360","422","439"],["360","439","1006"]]}}};
